[Event "New Jersey Open 1957"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1957.??.??"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Sherwin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Too little, too late"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "13"]

{ Although Sherwin makes no serious errors in the opening, he misses several equalizing opportunities. Demonstrating the technica1 virtuosity that is to become his hallmark, Fischer, with astonishing maturity, gradually strengthens his grip by accumulating small advantages: the better center and the two Bishops. Sherwin, meanwhile, attempts to consolidate his position – only to see his 14-year-old opponent shatter it with a thunderbolt (18 Nxh7). It brings to mind Alekhine's combinations, which also seemed to spring from nowhere. Sherwin, lashing back, refuses to fall. However, his defense finally disintegrates under a series of acute blows to his wobbly King. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d3
{ This used to be my favorite. I thought it led to a favorable variation of the King's Indian reversed, particularly after Black has committed himself with ...e6. }
3... Nc6 4. g3 Nf6
{ Fischer-Ivkov, Santa Monica 1966 continued 4...d5 5 Nbd2 Bd6 6 Bg2 Nge7 7 0-0 0-0 8 Nh4! with chances for initiative. }
5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O
{ More usual is 6...d5; but Black has purposely delayed placing his center Pawns. Has he a new idea in mind? }
7. Nbd2 { After 7 e5 Nd5 8 Nbd2 f6 9 exf6 Nxf6 10 Re1 gives White an edge. }
7... Rb8
{ Sherwin slid the Rook here with his pinky, as if to emphasize the cunning of this mysterious move. 7...d5 8 Re1 b5 9 e5 Nd7 10 Nfl b4 11 h4 a5 12 Bf4 a4 13 a3! Fischer-Mjagmasuren, Sousse Interzona1 1967, leads to double-edged play where Black's chances on the Q-side countervail White's K-side attack – but White usually comes first. }
8. Re1 d6 9. c3 b6
{ Not bad. But I had expected 9...b5 10 d4 cxd4! (if 10...b4 11 e5! bxc3? [11...dxe5 12 dxe5 Nd7 13 c4 holds the advantage] 12 exf6 cxd2 13 Nxd2! wins a piece) 11 cxd4 d5 with equality. }
10. d4 Qc7 $2
{ This leads to trouble. Black should strive for counterplay by opening the c-file: 10...cxd4 11 cxd4 d512 e5 Nd7, etc. }
11. e5 $1 Nd5
{ Worse is 11...Nd7 12 exd6 Bxd6 13 Ne4 cxd4 14 Nxd6 Qxd6 15 Bf4 e5 16 Nxe5! Ndxe5 17 cxd4 winning a Pawn. The best try is 11...dxe5 12 dxe5 Nd7 13 Qe2 Bb7 14 h4 with a bind, but Black's game may be tenable. }
12. exd6 Bxd6 13. Ne4 $1 c4
{ An unpleasant choice, since it releases the pressure in the center and gives White a free hand to start operations on the K-side. However, other moves lose material:
A] 13...Be7 14 c4 Nf6 15 Bf4, etc. B] 13...cxd4 14 Nxd6 Qxd6 15 c4! Nf6 16 Bf4 and again the lineup on this diagonal is unfortunate. }
14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Ng5 $1 Nce7 $2
{ A bad mistake. Black's game is still tenable after 15...h6 16 Ne4 Qd8. } 16.
Qc2 $1 Ng6
{ On 16...f5? 17 Qe2 picks off a Pawn. Had Sherwin seen what was coming, however, he might have chosen 16...g6 17 Ne4 Qc7 18 Bh6 Rd8, though 19 Qd2 prepares to exploit his weakened dark squares. }
17. h4 Nf6
{ Apparently everything's defended now. Unappetizing is 17...Rd8 (17...h6 18 h5 hxg5 19 hxg6 f6? 20 Qe2-h5) 18 Nxh7! Kxh7 19 h5 f5 20 hxg6+ Kxg6 21 Re5! with a bind. }
18. Nxh7 $1
{ Throwing a monkey wrench into Black's carefully contrived setup! As usual, tactics flow from a positionally superior game. }
18... Nxh7 { Not 18...Kxh7? 19 Bf4. } 19. h5 Nh4 $1
{ The best fighting chance. Not 19...Ne7 20 Bf4 wins a clear exchange. } 20.
Bf4 Qd8 21. gxh4 { 21 Bxb8? Nxg2 22 Kxg2 Bb7+ 23 f3 Qxb8. } 21... Rb7 $1 22. h6
$1
{ He's hoping for 22 Bxb7 Bxb7 and, suddenly, the initiative passes to Black despite his material deficit. }
22... Qxh4
{ Once again, time-pressure had Sherwin burying his thumbs in his ears. Instead of trying to mix it up, Black should keep his King sheltered as long as possible with 22...g6 23 h5! g5 (if 23...gxh5 24 Qe2), but 24 Be5 stays a Pawn ahead with two Bishops. It might still be a fight, though. }
23. hxg7 Kxg7 $2
{ Suicidal. The last hope would have been 23...Rd8 24 Bg3 Qh6, though 25 Qe2 is hard to meet (if 25...b5 26 a4 a6 27 axb5 axb5 28 Ra8). }
24. Re4 $1 { Threatening 25 Be5+. } 24... Qh5 25. Re3 $1
{ Now the Rook joins the King hunt – and it's murder. The immediate threat is 26 Rh3 Qg6 27 Rg3. }
25... f5 26. Rh3 Qe8 { 26...Qg6? 27 Rg3. } 27. Be5+
{ White can pick off a couple of exchanges with 27 Bh6+ Kg8 28 Bxf8 Qxf8 29 Bxb7, etc. But by now I felt there was more in the offing. }
27... Nf6 { 27...Kg8? 28 Rg3+ Kf7 29 Rg7 mate. } 28. Qd2 $1 Kf7 29. Qg5 Qe7
{ On 29...Ke7 30 Rh7+ is devastating. } 30. Bxf6 Qxf6 31. Rh7+ Ke8 32. Qxf6
Rxh7 { On 32...Rxf6 33 Bxb7 nets a whole Rook. } 33. Bc6+
{ If 33...Bd7 34 Qxe6+. } 1-0

[Event "Portoroz 1958"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1958.??.??"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Larsen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Slaying the dragon"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "18"]

{ Although the Sicilian, as a whole, is still the best fighting defense at Black's disposal, much of the steam has been taken out of the time-honored Dragon Variation. This is one of the key games which helped to batter its reputation.
In a laudable attempt to create complications, Larsen deviates from the book on move 15. That proves to be disastrous, since his counterattack never gets started. Mechanically, routinely, Fischer pries open the h-file, sacrificing first a Pawn and then the exchange. There is an aura of the inevitable about the outcome. Here the notes are as instructive and lucid as the text, which is an object lesson in how to mount an assault against the fianchettoed King. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6
{ Larsen was one of the diehards who refused to abandon the Dragon until recently. White's attack almost plays itself ... weak players even beat Grandmasters with it. I once thumbed through several issues of Shakhmatny Bulletin, when the Yugoslav Attack was making its debut, and found the ratio was something like nine wins out of ten in White's favor. Will Black succeed in reinforcing the variation? Time will tell. }
6. Be3 Bg7 { 6...Ng4? still loses to 7 Bb5+. } 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4
{ This refinement supersedes the old 0-0-0. The idea is to prevent ...d5. }
9... Nxd4
{ Just how Black can attempt to thread his way to equality is not clear. Interesting is Donald Byrne's 9...a5. The strongest reply is 10 g4 and if 10...Ne5 11 Be2 d5? 12 g5! wins a Pawn. }
10. Bxd4 Be6 11. Bb3 Qa5 12. O-O-O b5
{ After 12...Bxb3 13 cxb3! Black cannot make any attacking headway against this particular Pawn configuration. White is lost in the King and Pawn ending, it's true, but Black usually gets mated long before then. As Tarrasch put it: "Before the endgame the gods have placed the middle game.' 99 }
13. Kb1 b4 14. Nd5 { Weaker is 14 Ne2 Bxb3 15 cxb3 Rfd8. } 14... Bxd5
{ Bad judgment is 14...Nxd5? 15 Bxg7 Kxg7 16 exd5 Bd7 17 Rde1 with a crushing bind. (Suetin-Korchnoi, USSR Championship prelims 1953.) }
15. Bxd5
{ Stronger is 15 exd5! Qb5 16 Rhe1 a5 17 Qe2! (Tal-Larsen, Zurich 1959) where White abandons the attack and plays for pressure along the e-file instead. }
15... Rac8 $2
{ The losing move. After the game Larsen explained he was playing for a win, and therefore rejected the forced draw with 15...Nxd5 16 Bxg7 Nc3+ 17 bxc3 (17 Bxc3 bxc3 18 Qxc3 Qxc3 19 bxc3 Rfc8 renders White's extra Pawn useless.) 17...Rab8! 18 cxb4 Qxb4+! 19 Qxb4 Rxb4+ 20 Bb2 Rfb8, etc. After 15...Nxd5, however, I intended simply 16 exd5 Qxd5 17 Qxb4, keeping the game alive. }
16. Bb3 $1
{ He won't get a second chance to snap off the Bishop! Now I felt the game was in the bag if I didn't botch it. I'd won dozens of skittles games in analogous positions and had it down to a science: pry open the h-file, sac, sac ... mate! }
16... Rc7
{ This loss of time is unfortunately necessary if Black is ever to advance his a-Pawn. 16...Qb5? is refuted by 17 Bxa7. }
17. h4 Qb5
{ There's no satisfactory way to impede White's attack. If 17...h5 18 g4! hxg4 (18...Rfc8 19 Rdg1 hxg4 20 h5! gxh5 21 fxg4 Nxe4 22 Qf4 e5 23 Qxe4 exd4 24 gxh5 Kh8 25 h6 Bf6 26 Rg7! wins) 19 h5! gxh5 (on 19...Nxh5 20 Bxg7 Kxg7 21 fxg4 Nf6 22 Qh6+ mates) 20 fxg4 Nxe4 (on 20...hxg4 21 Rdg1 e5 22 Be3 Rd8 23 Bh6; or 20...Nxg4 21 Rdg1 Bxd4 22 Rxg4+! hxg4 23 Qh6 leads to mate) 21 Qe3 Nf6 (21...Bxd4 22 Qxe4 Bg7 23 Rxh5) 22 gxh5 e5 23 h6 wins.
Now Black is threatening to get some counterplay with ..a5-a4. }
18. h5 $1 { There's no need to lose a tempo with the old-fashioned g4. } 18...
Rfc8
{ On 18...gxh5 19 g4! hxg4 20 fxg4! Nxe4 21 h2 Ng5 22 Bxg7 Kxg7 23 d5 Rc5 24 Qh6+ Kg8 25 Rxg5+ Rxg5 26 Qxh7 mate. }
19. hxg6 hxg6 20. g4
{ Not the impatient 20 Bxf6? Bxf6 21 Qh6 e6! (threatening ... Qe5) and Black holds everything. }
20... a5
{ Now Black needs just one more move to get his counterattack moving. But for the want of a nail the battle was lost... }
21. g5 Nh5
{ Vasiukov suggests 21...Ne8 as a possible defense (not 21...a4? 22 gxf6 axb3 23 fxg7! bxc2+ 24 Qxc2! e5 25 Qh2 wins); but White crashes through with 22 Bxg7 Nxg7 (22...Kxg7? 23 Qh2) 23 h6! e6 (if 23...a4 24 Qh2 Nh5 25 xg6+) 24 Qh2 Nh5 25 Bxe6! fxe6 (25...Qxg5 26 Rxg6+! Qxg6 27 Bxc8, threatening Rg1) 26 Rxg6+ Ng7 27 Rh1, etc. }
22. Rxh5 $1
{ Fine wrote: "In such positions, combinations are as natural as a baby's smile." }
22... gxh5
{ No better is 22...Bxd4 23 Qxd4 gxh5 24 g6 Qe5 (if 24...e6 25 Qxd6) 25 gxf7+ Kh7 (if 25...Kf8 26 Qxe5 dxe5 27 Rg1 e6 28 Bxe6 Ke7 29 Bxc8 Rxc8 30 Rg5 wins) 26 Qd3! (intending f4) should be decisive. }
23. g6 e5
{ On 23...e6 24 gxf7+ Kxf7 (if 24...Rxf7 25 Bxe6) 25 Bxg7 Kxg7 26 Rg1+ Kh7 27 Qg2 Qe5 28 Qg6+ Kh829 Rg5 Rg7 30 Rxh5+ Kg8 31 Bxe6+ Kf8 32 Rf5+ Ke7 33 Rf7+ wins. }
24. gxf7+ Kf8 25. Be3 d5 $1
{ A desperate bid for freedom. On 25...a4 (if 25...Rd8 26 Bh6) 26 Qxd6+ Re7 27 Qd8+! Rxd8 28 Rxd8+ Re8 29 Bc5+ mates. }
26. exd5 $1 { Not 26 Bxd5 Rxc2! } 26... Rxf7
{ On 26...a4 27 d6! axb3 28 dxc7 wins. } 27. d6 Rf6
{ On 27...Rd7 White can either regain the exchange with 28 Be6 or try for more with 28 Bh6. And on 27...Rxf3 28 d7, threatening Qd6 mate. }
28. Bg5 Qb7
{ On 28...Qd7 29 Qd5! Qf7 (if 29...Rf7 30 Be7+!) 30 Bxf6 wins material. } 29.
Bxf6 Bxf6 30. d7 Rd8 31. Qd6+ { A mistake! 31 Qh6+! forces mate in three. } 1-0

[Event "Portoroz 1958"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1958.??.??"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Petrosian"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "Bear hug"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "23"]

{ In what appear to be perfectly equal positions, Petrosian consistently finds seemingly innocuous moves which gradually overwhelmhis opponent. He accomplishes his objective simply by exchanging pieces and maneuvering for victory without taking unnecessary risks. This essentially defensive technique has the virtue, when it doesn't utterly succeed, of producing a draw. Fischer, by contrast, generally chooses the sharpest course, however precipitous it may become. Occasionally he overreaches himself, but it makes for interesting chess.
In this game, replete with errors on both sides, Petrosian succeeds in pinning his opponent for a time to a static endgame. But Fischer manages to burst his bonds, only to blunder on the very next move (51...Kd6). Petrosian, however, by blundering in his turn, restores the balance.The ensuing Rook and Pawn ending produces a thrilling draw. }
1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O Nc6 7. d3
{ On 7 d4 I intended 7...e5. Petrosian is striving for an English Opening formation, a slow system for which he is temperamentally suited. }
7... Nh5 8. d4
{ Reckoning he can afford this loss of time in view of Black's misplaced KN. On 8 Rb1 f5! 9 Qc2 a510 a3 f4 (Petrosian-Vasiukov, Moscow 1956) Black obtains an excellent aggressive setup. I was as mpressed by that game as Petrosian must have been, since he got erushed. }
8... e5 9. d5
{ 9 dxe5 dxe5 10 Qxd8 Rxd8 11 Nd5 Rd7 12 Bh3 f5 13 g4 Nf6! If 14 gxf5 (14 Nxf6+ Bxf6 15 gxf5 gxf5 16 Bxf5?? Rg7+ wins) 14...Nxd515 cxd5 Rxd5. }
9... Ne7 10. e4
{ This is the right time to get in 10...c5! Petrosian-Boleslavsky, USSR Championship prelims 1957, continued: 11 Nel Kh8 12 Nd3 f5 13 Rb1 Nf6=. }
10... f5 11. exf5 gxf5
{ Tempting but unsound is 11...Nxf5 12 g4 Nd4 13 gxh5 Bg4 14 Nxd4! Bxd1 15 Ne6, etc. And on 11...Bxf5 12 Ng5 Qd7 13 Ne6! Bxe6 14 dxe6 Qxe6 15 Bxb7 Rab8 16 Bg2 Qxc4 17 Nd5! White comes out on top. }
12. Nxe5 $1 Nxg3
{ A "desperado" combination: this Knight (which is doomed anyway) sells its life as dearly as possible. }
13. hxg3
{ Simple and good. I had expected 13 fxg3, but Petrosian eschews the K-side attack and plays for control of the center squares instead. His judgment turns out to be right. }
13... Bxe5 $2
{ 13...dxe5, keeping a fluid Pawn center, offers more play. I was unduly worried about White's passed d-Pawn after 14 c5. }
14. f4 $1 Bg7 15. Be3 Bd7 16. Bd4
{ Forcing the trade of Black's most active piece. White soon obtains a firm grip on the position. }
16... Ng6 17. Re1 $2
{ A careless transposition. Now by 17...Bxd4+ 18 Qxd4 h5! followed by ..h4, Black could exchange his isolated h-Pawn for White's g-Pawn and the game would be dead equal. Correct was 17 Bf3. }
17... Rf7 $2 18. Bf3 $1 { Black doesn't get a second chance. } 18... Qf8 19.
Kf2 Re8 20. Rxe8 Qxe8 21. Bxg7 Rxg7 22. Qd4 b6 23. Rh1
{ White has effortlessly achieved a plus and now he wants to improve his position before embarking on a committal course. 23 b4!, threatening c5, is much sharper, and poses more immediate problems. }
23... a5 { My first free breath! } 24. Nd1 Qf8 25. Ne3
{ Petrosian keeps building without getting sidetracked – even by good moves. I was more afraid of 25 Bh5! tying me up completely. Then the Rook can't move because of Bxg6 followed by a check on h8. }
25... Rf7 $1 26. b3 Qg7
{ The exchange of Queens eases the cramp. White can't afford to retreat and cede this important diagonal. }
27. Qxg7+ Kxg7 28. a3 Rf8 29. Be2
{ White constantly finds ways to improve his position. Not 29 b4 axb4 30 axb4 Ra8 and Black seizes the open file. }
29... Ne7 30. Bd3 h6 31. Rh5 Be8 32. Rh2
{ Avoiding a little trap: 32 Nxf5+? (or 32 Rxf5? Rh8!) 32...Nxf5 33 Rxf5 Rh8! followed by ... Bg6 winning the exchange. }
32... Bd7 33. Rh1 Rh8 34. Nc2 $1
{ Headed for an even stronger post on d4. I was amazed during the game. Each time Petrosian achieved a good position, he managed to maneuver into a better one. }
34... Kf6 35. Nd4 Kg7 36. Be2
{ Feigning an invasion with Bh5 and Rel and Ne6. White has two wings to operate on: Black must be flexed to react appropriately, and this requires alertness. }
36... Ng8 $2
{ Panicking and giving him the opportunity he's been waiting for to sneak b4 in at a moment when Black can't counter with ..axb4 and ...Ra8. Petrosian likes to play cat-and-mouse, hoping that his opponents will go wrong in the absence of a direct threat. The amazing thing is – they usually do! Witness a case in point. I should just have ignored his "threat" with, say, 36...Ra8 37 Bh5 Rc8 38 Re1 Kf6 and if 39 Ne6 c6. }
37. b4 $1 Nf6 38. Bd3 $1 { 38 bxa5 Ne4+ 39 Kg2 bxa5 40 Rb1 Nc5 holds. } 38...
axb4
{ 38...Ne4+? 39 Bxe4 fxe4 40 bxa5 bxa5 (if 40...Ra8 41 axb6 cxb6 42 Rb1) 41 Rb1 followed by Rb7 wins easily. White also invades after 38...Kg6 39 bxa5 bxa5 40 Rb1. }
39. axb4 Kg6 40. Ra1 $1
{ White has finally achieved his ideal setup, but Black's game is still tenable. }
40... Ng4+ 41. Ke2 Re8+ 42. Kd2 Nf6 43. Ra6
{ 43 Ra7 Rc8 transposes to the game. } 43... Rb8 44. Ra7 Rc8 45. c5 $1
{ This Pawn sac caught me completely by surprise. It's the only line that gives Black any trouble. }
45... bxc5 { Not 45...Nxd5? 46 c6. } 46. bxc5 dxc5 47. Nf3 $1 Kf7 $1
{ 47...Nxd5 loses to 48 Ne5+. } 48. Ne5+ Ke7 49. Nxd7 Nxd7 50. Bxf5 Rf8 $1 51.
g4
{ 51 Bxd7 Kxd7 52 Ke3 (if 52 Ra6 Rg8) 52...Kd6 53 Ra6+ Kxd5 54 Rxh6 Re8+ 55 Kf3 c4 should draw. }
51... Kd6 $2
{ Should be the losing move! Correct is 51...Nf6! 52 Be6 Nxd5! 53 Bxd5 Rxf4 (53...Rd8 also draws) winning the last Pawn and forcing a draw. }
52. Bxd7 $1 Kxd7 53. Ke3 Re8+
{ On 53...c4 54 Ra6 wins. The idea is to force his King to the K-side, away from the passed c-Pawn. }
54. Kf3 { Not 54 Kd3 Rg8. } 54... Kd6 55. Ra6+ Kxd5 56. Rxh6 c4 57. Rh1 $2
{ As Petrosian points out in the Russian bulletins of the tournaiment, White can win with the following line: "57 Rh7! c6 58 Rd7+ Kc5 59 Rd1 c3 60 g5 Kc4 61 g6 c2 62 Rc1 Kc3 63 f5 Rg8 64 Kf4 Kd2 65 Rxc2+ Kxc2 66 Kg5 c5 67 f6 c4 68 f7 Rxg6+ 69 Kxg6 c3 70 f8=Q." White is a tempo ahead of the game, where Black's Pawn succeeds in reaching c2, instead of c3 (as here).
What if Black tries to improve? For example, after 57 Rh7 c5 58 Rd7+ Ke6 59 Rd1 Rb8. Now there are two main lines:
A] 60 g5? c3 61 Kg4 (if 61 Rc1 Kf5 62 Rxc3 c4! 63 Rxc4 Rb3+ with a draw by blockade although two Pawns down) 61...Rb4! 62 Re1+ Kf7 63 Kf5 c2 64 Rc1 Rc465 g6+ Kg7 66 Kg5 Rc3 draws.
B] 60 f5+! Ke5 61 Re1+ Kd4 (after 61...Kf6 62 Kf4 c3 63 g5+ Kg7 64 g6 c2 65 Kg5 Rb1 66 f6+ wins) 62 g5 c3 63 f6 c2 64 Rc1! (64 f7? Rb1! draws) 64...Ke5 65 Kg4! snuffs out Black's resistance. }
57... c3 58. g5 c5 59. Rd1+
{ It's tough right down the line. After 59 g6 Rg8 60 f5 Ke5! 61 Kg4 Kf6 62 Rc1 c4! 63 Rxc3 Rc8! White can make no headway. Or on 59 Kg4 Re2! 60 g6 Ke4! 61 Kg5 Rg2+ 62 Kf6 Kxf4, etc., as the Russian bulletins also point out. }
59... Kc4 60. g6 c2 61. Rc1
{ On 61 Rg1 Rd8! the threat of ...Rd1 forces 62 Rc1 (not 62 g7? Rg8! followed by ...Rxg7 and wins). }
61... Kd3 62. f5 Rg8 $1 63. Kf4 Kd2 64. Rxc2+ Kxc2 65. Kg5 c4 66. f6 c3 67. f7
{ I offered the draw, not realizing it was bad etiquette. It was Petrosian's place to extend the offer after 67...Rxg6+ (if Black wants to get melodramatic 67...Rc8 68 g7 Kb1 69 f8=Q Rxf8 70 gxf8=Q c2 reaches the same position); 68 Kxg6 Kb1 69 f8=Q c2 with a book draw. }
1/2-1/2

[Event "Mar del Plata 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Pilnik"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Tact and tactics"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "32"]

{ The presence in Argentina of Pilnik, Najdorf, and Eliskases, who chose to remain there after participating in the Buenos Aires chess Olympic of 1939, created a chess renaissance, as attested by the annual event at Mar del Plata which, though not lavish with prizes, offers an exotic vacation and attracts the world's best. Fischer tied for 3-4 with Ivkov, a mere half point behind Pachman and Najdorf in a strong field of fifteen.
After a lackluster opening by both sides, and a middle game that, with the exception of 26...bxa3 can scarcely be described as more than routine, Fischer pilots the game into an even ending. Both he and Pilnik then proceed to complicate; but the latter is drawn into making a false lead, which Fischer exploits by obtaining a passed center Pawn. From this point on, although Pilnik does all that can be done to stave off the inevitable, Fischer is not gulled into making a single wrong step. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2
{ For 6 h3 see games 35, 40, 43. For 6 Bc4 see games 17, 55, 58. } 6... e5 7.
Nb3 Be7 { For 7...Be6 see game 42. } 8. O-O
{ Another try is 8 Bg5 0-0! (8...Nbd7? 9 a4! gives a powerful bind) 9 Nd2 Nxe4! 10 Bxe7 Nxc3 11 Bxd8 Nxd1 12 Be7 e8 13 Nc4 Nxb2! 14 Nb6 Rxe7 15 Nxa8 Na4 16 0-0-0 (Fischer-Ghitescu, Leipzig 1960) 16...Rd7! with the bettergame. }
8... O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. f3
{ A sharper alternative is 10 a4 c7 11 a5 Nbd7 12 Nd5 Nxd5 13 exd5 Bf5 14 c4 Bg6 15 Kh1 ac8 16 Qd2d8 17 ac1 h6 18 f4 exf4 19 Bxf4 Bg5=. (Smyslov-Gligorich, Havana 1962.) }
10... Qc7
{ Premature is 10...d5 11 exd5 Nxd5 12 Nxd5 xd5 13 Qxd5 Bxd5 14 Rfd1 with a slight edge in the ending. }
11. Qe1
{ Once popular, this whole system is now known to give White nothing. It hinders neither Black's development nor his Q-side expansion. }
11... Nbd7 12. Rd1 { On 12 a4, ...d5 is strong. } 12... b5 13. Rd2
{ 13 a3 is met by 13...Nb6 14 Bxb6! Qxb6+ =. } 13... Nb6
{ More direct is 13...b4 14 d5 xd5 15 exd5 Bf5 16 f2 a5 with good play against White's backward c-Pawn. }
14. Qf2 $2 { Careless. 14 Bxb6 is necessary. } 14... Rab8 $2
{ I figured if he didn't take it off last move he wouldn't take it now; so I wanted to build a little more and keep the option of moving the Knight to a4 as well as c4. But Black should pounce on the chance to play 14...Nc4! 15 Bxc4 bxc4 16 Bb6 (if 16 Na1 ab8 17 b1 Rb7 is strong; or 16 Nc1 Rab8 17 Na4 c3! 18 bxc3? Qc6 19 Nb6 Bd8) 16...Qc8 17 Na5 Nd7! and White's in trouble. E.g., 18 Nd5 (or if 18 Be3 Bd8 19 Nd5 Bxd5 20 xd5 Nf6 winning at least the exchange) 18...Bxd5 19 xd5 (if 19 exd5 Nxb6 20 Qxb6 Bd8 21 Qb4 Rb8 22 Qa3 Bxa5 23 Qxa5 Rxb2) 19...Rb8 winning at least a Pawn. }
15. Bxb6 $1
{ Pilnik hastens to make amends for his omission. Not 15 Na5? d5! wins material. (STAHLBERG) }
15... Rxb6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Bd7 18. f4 Bf6
{ I didn't want to weaken my e6 square with 18...f5 19 c3 Bf6 20 fxe5 dxe5 (if 20...Bxe5 21 Nd4) 21 Nc5=. }
19. c3 Rbb8 20. fxe5 Bxe5
{ On 20...dxe5 21 d6 gives White active play. Black's advantage of the Bishop pair is neutralized by the weakness of his c6, which White can later occupy with his Knight. }
21. Nd4 g6 22. a3 { More to the point was 22 Bf3 b4 23 cxb4 Rxb4 24 Nc6=. }
22... a5 23. Kh1 { 23 Bf3 should again be played. White starts drifting. }
23... b4 24. cxb4 { On 24 axb4 axb4 25 c4 b3! holds the initiative. } 24...
axb4 25. Rc2 { On 25 Bf3 bxa3 26 bxa3 Ra8 presents its problems. } 25... Qb6
26. Nc6
{ This is the position White was playing for. A draw now looks secure. } 26...
bxa3 $1 27. Qxb6
{ 27 bxa3 is also adequate. But not 27 Nxb8? Qxf2 28 xf2 a2 29 Rf1 (if 29 Rc1 Bxb2) 29...Bf5 30 Rcc1 Rxb8 wins. }
27... Rxb6 28. bxa3 Ra8 29. Nxe5 $2
{ This gives Black a strong passed e-Pawn. Correct is 29 Ra2 Rb2 30 Rxb2 Bxb2 31 Bb5! Rxa3 (otherwise a4) 32 Ne7+ Kf8 33 Nxg6+ with a draw in view. On 33...hxg6 34 Bxd7 produces opposite colored Bishops. Or on 33...Ke8 34 Re1+! (34 Bxd7+ xd7 35 Rxf7+? d8 wins a piece) 34...Kd8 35 Bxd7 fxg6 (if 35...Kxd7 36 Nf8+ any 37 Nxh7) =. }
29... dxe5 30. Rc3
{ 30 Rc7 gets nowhere after 30...Rd6; and the sacrifice 31 Bb5?! is refuted by 31...Bxb5 32 Rfxf7 Rc8! Or on 30 Ra2 (30 Ra1? Rxa3) 30...Ra5 31 Bc4 (if 31 d1 Ba4) 31...Rc5 and the d-Pawn falls. }
30... Rb2 $1 31. Rc7
{ Desperately striving for counterplay. On 31 Bc4 (to prevent ...Ra2) 31...Kg7 32 d6 f5 33 Bd5 a6 again wins the d-Pawn. Or 31 Bf3 f5 32 Rc7 Bb5 33 Re1 e4 squelches White's play. }
31... Bf5 32. g4
{ A wild hope. Hopeless is 32 Bc4 Rc2! 33 d6 Rxc4! 34 Rxc4 d3 35 Rfc1 Bxc4 36 Rxc4 xa3 37 Kg1 Kg7! Black soon picks off the d-Pawn and wins easily. }
32... Be4+ 33. Bf3 Bd3 34. d6
{ Or 34 Rel e4! 35 Bg2 (if 35 Bxe4 Re8 wins) 35...d8 36 Rc5 Kg7! 37 g1 (if 37 g5 h6 38 h4 hxg5 39 hxg5 h8+ 40 g1 Rh4! wins) 37...f5 38 gxf5 gxf5 and the two passed center Pawns should win (if 39 d6 Kf6!). }
34... Rd8 35. Re1 Rxd6
{ Najdorf chided me after the game for "missing" 35...e4 36 Bxe4! Rb1! 37 Rxb1 xe4+ 38 g1 xb1 and wins. }
36. Rxe5
{ Falling into the trap. 36 e7 holds out longer, but 36...f6 37 Bd5 e4 38 Bxe4 Bxe4+ 39 7xe4 Rff2 wins. }
36... Rf6 $1 37. Re3 { Forced. On 37 Bg2 b1+ is decisive. } 37... Rxf3 $1 38.
Rxf3 Be4 39. Rxf7 Rf2 40. Rf8+ Kg7
{ After 41 R8f7+ Kh6 wins. Or 41 8 f4 Bd5 (41...Bxf3+ 42 Kg1 g2+ 43 f1 Bc6 44 Rc4 b5! also wins) cooks White's goose. }
0-1

[Event "Mar del Plata 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Rossetto"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "The unpleasant obligation"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "38"]

{ This game exemplifies most dramatically the German expression zugzwang.
Unable to achieve any workable advantage from the opening or mid-game, Fischer embarks on an equally unpromising ending. He manages, however, after 19 Nd5, to acquire a Bishop against a Knight. Subsequently he employs an unusual Rook maneuver along the third rank (24 Ra3) in order to make spatial inroads. Rossetto unwittingly co-operates and soon is faced by a rare predicament: although material is equal, any move he makes must disturb the precarious balance and hasten his own disaster. That is zugzwang - and, appropriately, Rossetto resigns. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. c4 Qc7
{ Stronger is 5...Nf6 6 Nc3 Bb4 which theory gives as equal for Black. } 6. Nc3
Nf6 7. Bd3 $2
{ Misplacing the Bishop. Right is 7 a3! and if 7...Nc6 8 Be3, etc. (if 8...Ne5 9 Be2! Nxc4? 10 Bxc4 Qxc4 11 Rc1 wins).
7 a3!, by preventing .. Bb4 once and for all, forces Black to adopt a kind of Scheveningen formation that keeps him desperately cramped. A Spielmann-Tartakover game proceeded: 7...Be7 8 Be2 0-0 9 0-0 d6 10 Be3 Nbd7 11 c1 b6 12 b4! Bb7 13 f3 Rac8 14 Qe1 b8 15 f2 "with a beautiful position for White." (From More Chess Questions Answered.) }
7... Nc6 8. Be3 Nxd4 $2
{ Instead of trying to simplify, Black should select the aggressive 8...Ne5! 9 Rc1 (not 9 0-0 eg4! or if 9 Be2 Nxc4 10 Bxc4 Qxc4 11 Rc1 Qb4 12 0-0 d6; and if 13 Nb3? Nxe4! 14 a3 Nxc3, etc.) 9...Nfg4! with advantage. }
9. Bxd4 Bc5 10. Bc2 d6 11. O-O Bd7 12. Na4
{ Forcing a series of exchanges which give White a microscopic edge, at best. }
12... Bxd4 13. Qxd4 Rd8 14. Rfd1 O-O 15. Rac1
{ On 15xd6xd6 16 Rxd6 Bxa4 17 Rxd8 Rxd818 xa4 Nxe4=. } 15... Qa5
{ 15...b5? 16 cxb5 axb5 17 Nc3 (not 17 e5? dxe5 18 Bxh7+ Nxh7) and Black's b-Pawn is weak. 15...Bc6 is solid but cramped. }
16. Qb6 Qxb6 { Unsound is 16...e5 17 xd6 g4 18 g3 Qh5 19 h4. } 17. Nxb6 Bc6 18.
f3 Nd7 $1
{ Black had relied on this move to get him out of trouble. Now 19 Nxd7 (or 19 Na4 Ne5 20 Bb3 g5! holds the balance) 19...Rxd7 20 Rd2 Rfd8 21 Rcd1 Kf8 is dead equal. }
19. Nd5 $1
{ An unexpected reply which throws Black, unjustifiably, into a state of confusion. }
19... Bxd5
{ Caught by surprise. Rossetto fails to find the most accurate reply: 19...Ne5! (also tenable is 19...exd5 20 exd5 Ne5 21 dxc6 bxc6 22 b3 c5 23 Be4 Rfe8) 20 Ne7+ (if 20 Ne3 g5!) 20...Kh8 21 Nxc6 bxc6 22 Ba4 g5! followed by ... Kg7-f6-e7=. }
20. exd5 e5 { Safer is 20...Ne5 21 Be4 b6 (not 21...f5? 22 dxe6!). } 21. b4
{ Playing for the big breakthrough on c5. } 21... g6
{ On 21...a5 22 a3 axb4 23 axb4 Ra8 24 Bf5! holds the advantage. } 22. Ba4 b6
{ On 22...Nb6 23 Bb3 followed by c5. } 23. Rd3 f5 $2
{ Oblivious to the danger! The best defense is 23...a5 24 a3 (Black should hold after 24 Bxd7 Rxd7 25 bxa5 bxa5 26 c5 dxc5 27 Rxc5 a4) 24...f5 and it's hard for White to make progress. Black should never allow c5 without first forcing White to make the concession of exchanging the Bishop for the Knight. }
24. Ra3 $1
{ The threat is simply 25 Bxd7. The veiled and seemingly insignificant attack on Black's a-Pawn is the means of forcing him to drop the protection of his c5 square. }
24... Nb8
{ Forced. On 24...Nf6 25 Bc6 wins a Pawn. The only other try is 24...a5 25 bxa5 bxa5 26 Bc6! Nc5 (on 26...Nb8 27 Bb5! or 26...b6 27 Rb1! Nxc4? 28 c3 Nd2 29 b2 traps the Knight) 27 Rxa5 and White should win. }
25. c5 $1 bxc5
{ On 25...b5 26 Bb3 f7 27 c6 Rc7 28 Ra5! Kf7 29 a4 bxa4 30 Rxa4 Kə7 31 Bc4 picks off the a-Pawn. }
26. bxc5 dxc5 27. Rxc5 Kg7
{ On 27...Nd7 (if 27...Rc8 28 ac3 keeps the bind) 28 c7 Nf6 29 Bb3 Kh8 30 Rxa6 Nxd5 31 Bxd5 Rxd5 32 Raa7 wins. Black's game collapses once the heavy guns penetrate. }
28. Rb3 Rf7 29. d6 $1 Nd7
{ The Pawn is obviously immune. So Black attempts to revive his Knight. } 30.
Rc7 Nf8
{ Also hopeless is 30...Nf6 31 Rbb7 Rxc7 32 dxc7 c8 33 Bb3 Ne8 34 Rb8 Nd6 35 xc8 Nxc8 36 Be6, etc. }
31. Rbb7 Rxc7 32. dxc7 Rc8 33. Bb3 $1
{ Completely immobilizing Black. He is reduced to Pawn moves. } 33... a5 34. a4
h6 35. h3 g5 36. g4 fxg4 37. hxg4
{ Zugzwang! Black has run out of satisfactory Pawn moves. On 37...Kf6 38 Rb8 wins a piece. On 37...Ng6 38 Be6 wins. Or on any Rook move, say 37...Re8, 38 c8= Q+. }
1-0

[Event "Mar del Plata 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Shocron"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "A small oversight"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "43"]

{ Fischer's opening repertoire has been less extensive than most practicing Grandmasters', yet his contributions to theory have been numerous. 20 g5!? is one of his innovations. However, it is not responsible for Shocron's defeat. Neither is Shocron's response; in retrospect, his system of defense seems surprisingly adequate. Nevertheless, after defending sensibly, Shocron outfoxes himself. Thinking he has seen one move further than his adversary, he provokes a combination. But his vision is one move short. In consequence, though otherwise it had withstood all of Fischer's assaults, his game crumbles. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 { For an immediate 9 d4!? see game 36. } 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4
Qc7 { For Keres' 11...Nd7 see game 38. } 12. Nbd2 Bd7
{ Some alternatives are 12...Kh8, ... e8, ... d8, ... Nc6, ... Bb7, .. Nd7, ...cxd4. I don't think there's any easy way for Black to achieve complete equality – but who knows? }
13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3 g6
{ The Yugoslav System, popularized by Gligorich and Matanovich. The idea is to strengthen the position, and transfer the Bishop to f8 while awaiting further developments. Black will undertake specific counteraction only after White commits himself. }
15. dxe5
{ This positional approach bares the hole on d5, so that White may gain access to it with his Knight. Alternate plans ensue after 15 Bd2 Bf8 and now:
A] The quiet 16 Rc1 Bg7? (after 16...Nc6 17 d5! Na5 [on 17...Nd8. 18 c4! robs Black of his normal counterplay with ...c4, Olafsson-Ivkov, Buenos Aires 1960, and now White can leisurely build up a strong attack with Kh2, g4, Rg1 and later the stock sac Nf5] 18 b4 Nb7 19 a4 with a slight advantage) 17 b4 Nc6 18 Nd5! Nxd5 19 exd5 Ne7 20 dxc5 Nxd5 (if 20...dxc5 21 Be3 Qd6 22 Bxc5 Qxd5 23 Bb3!) 21 Bb3 Nf6 (if 21...Bc6 22 Bg5!) 22 cxd6 Qxd6 23 Ng5! Rf8 24 Be3 Qxd1 25 Rexd1 h6 26 Ne4! Nxe4 27 Rxd7 ac8 28 c4 Ng5 29 Bxg5 hxg5 30 c5 Black resigns. (Fischer-Rinaldo, US Open 1957.)
B] The sharp 16 b4 cxb4 17 cxb4 Nc4 18 Nxc4 bxc4 19 c1 exd4 20 Nxd4 d5 (not 20...c3 21 Bb3 Nxe4 22 Rxe4! Rxe4 23 Qf3 Bc6 24 xc3 wins) 21 exd5 Rxe1+? (better is 21...Qd6) 22 Bxe1 Qd6. (Fischer-Matanovich, Bled 1961.) And now, as Keres pointed out, White can retain his extra Pawn with 23 Nf3! Not 23...xd5? 24 Bc3! wins a piece. Or 23...Nxd5 24 Be4. }
15... dxe5 16. Nh2
{ When examined microscopically, Black has his problems. The hole on d5 might be described as "gaping". }
16... Rad8 17. Qf3 Be6
{ On 17...h5? 18 Nd5! Nxd5 19 exd5 Black's K-side is weakened. } 18. Nhg4 Nxg4
19. hxg4
{ Black has a new burden: neutralizing the potential attack along the open h-file. }
19... Qc6 20. g5 $5
{ The old line 20 Wg3 f6 (better is 20...Bf6) 21 g5! is good for White. (Boleslavsky-Tal, USSR 1957.) I'd always thought my line was an improvement (the idea is to clear g4 for the Knight before Black can force an exchange with .. Nc4) but a closer look at this game shows that Black may have resources. }
20... Nc4
{ 20...Bxg5 puts White's concept to the crucial test. After 21 Nd5! Bxc1 (21...Bxd5 22 Bxg5 wins the exchange) 22 Nf6+ Kh8! (if 22...Kf8 23 Raxc1 Re7 24 Nxh7+ Kg8 25 Rcd1! Ra8 [25...Rxd1 26 Nf6+ Kg7 27 Rxd1 Nb7 28 g4! followed by g5 with a strong attack] 26 Nf6+ Kg7 27 Nd5 Bxd5 28 Rxd5 White's better) 23 Raxc1 (23 Nxe8 Bg5 24 Nf6 Nc4! Black has good play for the exchange) 23...Rf8 (not 23...Re7 24 Qg3 threatening either Qxe5 or Qh4) 24 Qg3 Qc7! 25 Qg5 Kg7 and now White can force a draw with 26 Nh5+, etc., or try for more with either 26 f4 or Re3. }
21. Ng4 Bxg4
{ Black can't allow Nf6+. As a consequence, White obtains the Bishop pair and attacking prospects along the open h-file. }
22. Qxg4 Nb6 $1
{ To prevent a4 and, as will be seen, to swing the Knight to f8 in order to defend the vulnerable h7 Pawn. For Unzicker's 22...f6 see game 10. }
23. g3 c4 $1
{ The right timing. He's careful to see that a4 is restrained. Wrong, for example, is 23...Nd7 24 a4 b4 25 cxb4 cxb4 26 Bb3! and this Bishop comes crashing back into the game via the open diagonal. }
24. Kg2 Nd7 25. Rh1 Nf8
{ Phase one is over. Having seen his K-side threats neatly parried, White is compelled to start some action on the opposite wing. }
26. b4
{ 26 b3 leads to trouble after 26...b4! 27 cxb4 c3 28 a3? Ne6 29 Qh3 h5 30 gxh6 e.p. Nd4! and wins. On 26 a4 b4! 27 cxb4 Bxb4 28 a5 threatening Ba4) 28...Qc7 is satisfactory. }
26... Qe6
{ Sharper is 26...a5! 27 a3 (if 27 bxa5 Qa6 28 a4 xa5) 27...Ra8=. Bad, however, is 26...cxb3 e.p. 27 Bxb3 Qxc3 28 Be3 (28 Bb2!? xb2 29 Qf3 Ne6 30 Bxe6 Rf8! holds. But not 30...fxe6? 31 Rxh7! ≡f8 32 Rah1! Qxf2+ 33 xf2 Rxf2+ 34 Kxf2 Rd2+ 35 Kf3 Bxg5 36 Rh8+ mates) 28...Rd7 (not 28...Qc8? 29 Qf3 Ne6 30 Rxh7! wins) 29 Rxh7! should win. }
27. Qe2 a5 28. bxa5 Qa6 29. Be3 Qxa5 30. a4 Ra8
{ 30...Qxc3! 31 axb5 leads to equal play. } 31. axb5 Qxb5
{ More active is 31...xc3! (if 32 Rac1 Ba3!). But not 31...xa1? 32 Rxa1 xa1 33 Qxc4, resigns. }
32. Rhb1 Qc6 33. Rb6 $1 Qc7 34. Rba6 { Obtaining control of the a-file. } 34...
Rxa6 35. Rxa6 Rc8 36. Qg4 Ne6
{ Bringing the Knight back into the game. Wrong would be an attempt to simplify with 36...Bc5 37 Bxc5 Qxc5 38 Ba4! and Black has trouble finding a move. If 38...Ne6 39 Bd7. Or 38...Rd8 39 Rc6 Qa7 40 Rxc4 Rd2 41 Wf3 staying a Pawn ahead. }
37. Ba4 Rb8 38. Rc6 Qd8 $2
{ Up to here Shocron has defended coolly, but now he makes a fata1 miscalculation. Correct is 38...d7! and it's not clear how White can improve his position any further. If 39 h2 (not 39 Rxc4 Qd3! 40 Rc6 b1 with a strong attack) 39...Rb1! 40 Rb6 (if 40 Rxc4 Qd3 41 Rc8+ g7 42 h4 Bxg5! 43 Bxg5 Rh1+! 44 Kxh1 f1+ with a perpetual) 40...Qd3 41 Rxb1 (not 41 Rxe6? f1!) 41...xb1 42 Bd7 Nc7! (on 42...Nc5 43 Bc6 followed by Bd5 should win) 43 Bc6 Qd3! with drawing chances. }
39. Rxe6 $1 Qc8 $1
{ Blow for blow! Apparently Shocron was prepared for this trick, having seen that 39...fxe6? 40 Qxe6+ f8 41 xe5 is crushing.
Now how does White avoid losing materia1? }
40. Bd7 $1
{ This is the shot he overlooked. On 40...Qxd7 41 xg6+ wins his Queen. } 1-0

[Event "Zurich 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "7"]
[White "Olafsson"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "Pride goeth"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "49"]

{ Miscalculating, as he explains in his notes, Fischer rapidly gets into trouble and is thrown on the defensive. Olafsson quietly strengthens his attacking prospects and seems well on the road to victory. But he tries, prematurely, to force the issue (21 Nb1) and, as the game opens up, loses the initiative, although he seems to be blissfully unaware of it. He fails to foresee the power of the riposte to his 24 Qd2. Still blind to the danger, while seeking a forced win, he misses several opportunities to equalize. In extreme time pressure, he is compelled to exchange Queens under particularly unfavorable circumstances. The resulting endgame holds no further surprises. }
1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 Nbd7 8. Bg5
{ Steinitz automatically gave this pin a question mark. Since there is no real threat involved (because the Bishop is worth more than the Knight) Black can now gain time by kicking it around.
Petrosian has had some success with this treatment. The idea is to restrain ... Ne8 and the subsequent ...f5. }
8... h6 9. Bh4 a6
{ This slow system has never been refuted. But better is 9...g5! 10 Bg3 (Black's break with ...f5 has now been blunted, but on the other hand White's Bishop on g3 is deadwood), 10...Nh5! 11 h4, the latest wrinkle (11 Nd2 Nf4 12 0-0 Nxe2+ 13 Qxe2 f5 14 exf5 Nf6 15 c5 Bxf5 16 Rac1 Rf7! 17 Nc4 Bf8 is better for Black. Wexler-Reshevsky, Buenos Aires 1960), 11...Nxg3! 12 fxg3 gxh4! 13 Rxh4 (if 13 Nxh4 Qg5 14 Nf5 Nf6; or 13 gxh4 f5 14 exf5 Nf6 15 0-0 Bxf5) 13...f5 14 Qc2 Nc5 15 Bd3 Qf6 with advantage. (Damyanovich-Hort, Sarajevo 1964.) }
10. Nd2 Qe8
{ The idea is to free the Knight and thus make possible the break with ...f5. In some variations, the Queen may help to support ...b5 also, particularly if White elects to castle long. }
11. g4 $5
{ In the Candidates' Tournament, 1959, Tal essayed against me the quieter 11 0-0 Nh7 12 b4 Ng5 (later I tried 12...Bf6!? against him) 13 f3 f5 with chances for both sides. The text is risky and commits White to Q-side castling. }
11... Nh7 12. Qc2 Ng5 $2
{ Intending ... Nh3-f4, but White's simple reply brands it as a mistaken plan. Correct is 12...Nc5 13 0-0-0 (13 b4 Nd7 leaves White with a shaky game) 13...f5=. }
13. h3 $1 Nc5 14. O-O-O Bd7
{ Weak is 14...f5 15 Bxg5! hxg5 16 gxf5 gxf5 17 Rdg1. } 15. f3 Na4
{ 15...b5!? 16 b4 Nb7 may not be too bad. } 16. Nxa4 Bxa4 17. b3 Bd7 18. Bf2 c5
$1 19. h4
{ Black's game springs to life after 19 dxc6 e.p.? bxc6 20 Nb1 d5! 21 exd5 (or 21 Bc5 d4 22 Bxf8 Bxf8 with a juicy position) 21...cxd5 22 Rxd5 Bc6, etc. }
19... Nh7 20. Be3 b5 21. Nb1 $2
{ Intending Qd2. But correct is 21 Bd3! (if 21 h5 Ng5), maintaining the bind by restraining ...f5. }
21... f5 $1
{ Ready or not – here we come! Olafsson was sure that this break was impossible, or he wouldn't have allowed it. }
22. gxf5 gxf5 23. exf5
{ To prevent ...f4 which would seal the K-side and neutralize White's attack. }
23... Bxf5 24. Qd2 { This is the position White played for. } 24... e4 $1
{ The game turns on this shot. 24...Rf6 or 24...h5 cedes the initiative. } 25.
Rdg1
{ 25 Bxh6 loses to 25...e3! 26 Bxe3 (if 26 Qxe3 Bxb1! 27 Kxb1 Qg6+ wins a piece) 26...Bxb1 27 Kxb1 Qe5. }
25... exf3 $2
{ Correct is 25...Ra7! and if 26 Bxh6 exf3 transposing to the game. } 26. Bxh6
$2
{ 26 Rxg7+! (sSTAHLBERG) 26...Kxg7 27 Bxh6+ Kh8 28 Bxf8 Qxf8 29 Bxf3 wins a Pawn (if 29...Bxb1 30 Qc3+). }
26... Ra7 $1
{ Olafsson later told me he had underestimated the strength of this defensive move. }
27. Bxg7 Rxg7 28. Rxg7+ Kxg7 29. Bd3
{ An admission of defeat since Black's f-Pawn now becomes dangerously potent. But not 29 Bxf3? (or 29 Qc3+ Qe5 30 Qxf3 Bxb1 31 Qg4+ Bg6) 29...Bxb1 30 Kxb1 三xf3 31 g2+ Qg6+ (the saving resource) and Black hangs on to his extra piece. My game hinges on this defense, on the fact that the f-Pawn has such divine protection. }
29... bxc4 30. Rg1+ Kh8 31. Qc3+
{ Now Black gets another passed center Pawn, but White's defense is difficult anyway. 31 bxc4 f2 32 Rf1 Bxd3 33 Qxd3 Qe1+ 34 Kc2 Nf6 35 Nd2 Ng4 wins. }
31... Qe5 32. Qxe5+
{ After 32 Bxf5 Rxf5 33 bxc4 Nf6 White has nothing better than to transpose into the actual game with 34 Qxe5. }
32... dxe5 33. Bxf5 Rxf5 34. bxc4
{ 34 d6 Nf6 35 Nd2! offers no better: e.g., 35...cxb3 36 axb3 e4 37 Nc4! f2 38 Rf1 Ng4! (threatening ...e3) 39 Kd1 Nh2! 40 d7 Rd5+ is decisive. }
34... Nf6 35. Nd2 f2 36. Rh1
{ White must keep his f1 square open for the Knight. } 36... e4 37. Kd1
{ A better chance is offered by 37 Nf1 Ng4! 38 Kd2 (if 38 d6 Rf6 39 d7 Rd6) but 38...Rf3! squelches all counterplay. }
37... e3 38. Nf1 Re5 39. Ke2 Nh5 $1 40. Kf3 e2
{ One of the Black Pawns must reincarnate. } 0-1

[Event "Zurich 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Keres"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Meat and potatoes"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "54"]

{ Alekhine said, in his prime, that to wrest a point from him it was necessary to win the same game three times: once at the beginning. once in the middle, once at the end. No less a tribute may be paid to Keres.
Each phase of this game is fascinating and hard-fought. Even the errors, and there are more than a few, contribute to making it an unusually complete exhibition of two master craftsmen using all the tools of their trade. While perhaps unglamorous, there is meat here, and potatoes too. And it is likely that as a result of this victory Fischer came to be regarded as a serious contender by the leading Soviet Grandmasters – this was the first time he had defeated one. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3
d6 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 cxd4
{ Keres has abandoned 12...Rd8 13 Nf1 d5? 14 dxe5! dxe4 (if 14...Nxe4 15 Qe2 Bb7 16 Ne3 threatening Nxd5) 15 N1d2! exf3 16 exf6 Bxf6 17 Qxf3 Be6 18 Ne4 with a winning attack. }
13. cxd4 Bb7 14. Nf1 Rac8 15. Bd3 Nc6
{ Keres later played 15...Nd7 against Smyslov at the Candidates' 1959. The game continued 16 Ne3 (better is 16 d5 f5 17 Ne3! f4 18 Nf5 Bd8 19 Bd2!) 16...exd4 17 Nxd4 Bf6 18 Ndf5 g6=. }
16. Ne3 Rfe8
{ Black already has difficulties. On 16...Nxd4 17 Nxd4 exd4 18 Nf5 fe8 19 Bg5 is strong. Not 16...Nb4? 17 Bb1 Bxe4?? 18 Bxe4 Nxe4 19 a3 Nc6 20 Nd5, etc. }
17. Nf5 $2
{ A superficial plan. Correct is 17 d5! Nb4 (if 17...Nb8 18 a4!) 18 Bb1 35 19 Qe2! Nd7 (if 19...Qb6 20 Nf5!) 20 Bd2 Qb6 21 a3 Na6 22 b4 followed byBd3 with a bind. }
17... Bf8 18. Bg5 Nd7 19. Rc1 Qb8 { To get out of the pin. } 20. Bb1 Nxd4 21.
N3xd4 Rxc1 $2
{ Overlooking White's follow-up. After 21...exd4 22 Rxc8 Qxc8 23 Qxd4 Nc5! White has precisely nothing. }
22. Bxc1 exd4 23. Nh6+ $1 { Keres probably expected 23 Qxd4 Nc5=. } 23... gxh6
24. Qg4+ Kh8 25. Qxd7
{ Regaining the piece. Black's extra Pawn is meaningless in view of his mangled Pawn formation. }
25... Bd5 $1
{ An energetic maneuver! This Bishop is headed, if circumstances permit, toward the defense of Black's weakened K-side. }
26. Qf5 Re5 $1 27. Qf3 f5 $1 { Aggressive defense! } 28. Bf4 $1 Re8
{ On 28...Re7 29 Qd1 Bxe4 30 Bxe4 fxe4 31 Qxd4+ Bg7 32 Qd5! maintains pressure against the weak Pawns. }
29. Qh5 $1 Bxe4 30. f3 Bc6 31. Rc1 $1
{ Not 31 Rxe8? Bxe8 32 Qxf5? Bg6! and Black wins! } 31... Bd7 32. Bxh6
{ A difficult choice. The alternative was 32 Bxf5 Bxf5 33 Qxf5 with possibilities of probing his sick Pawns. }
32... Re6 $1 33. Bxf8 { On 33 Bf4 Qe8! looks tenable. } 33... Qxf8 34. Qh4 $1
{ On 34 Qxf5?? Re1+ wins! Or 34 Bxf5?? Rh6 wins! The text forces Black into an ending where his weak Pawns can't be concealed by
tactical tricks. On 34 Rc7 Qe7 holds; e.g., 35 Bxf5? Re1+ 36 Kh2 (or 36 Kf2 Qe3+ 37 Kg3 Qe5+) 36...Qe5+ wins. }
34... Qf6 { Not 34...Qg7 35 Rc7 Re2? 36 Qd8+ e8 37 Rxd7! Rxd8 38 Rxd8+ wins. }
35. Qxf6+ Rxf6 36. Kf2 $2
{ Losing a vital tempo, which gives Black time to rush his King toward the center. At the time I rejected 36 Rc7 because I couldn't see a winning continuation after 36...Rf7 (if 36...Be6 37 b3! squelches all counterplay). But I underestimated the strength of 37 Ra7! (37 Bxf5 ≡xf5 38 Rxd7 Rd5 39 Kf2 d3 40 Ke1 Re5+ 41 Kd1 Re2 should draw) 37...Be6 38 Rxa6 Rc7 39 Kf2 Rc1 40 Bd3 and Black's Pawns are hopelessly weak. }
36... Kg7 $1 37. Rc7 Rf7 38. Ke2
{ Now 38 Ra7 loses all of its effectiveness after the reply 38...Bc8. } 38...
f4 $5
{ A risky selection in time-pressure, aimed against 39 Kd3?? Bf5+. Best is simply 38...Kf6 (on 38...Be6 39 Rc6 is strong) 39 Ra7 (not 39 f4 Be6 40 Rc6? Rg7 41 Kf2 xg2+!) 39...Bc8 40 Rxf7+ Kxf7=. }
39. Ra7 Kf6 { Not 39...Bc8 40 Rxf7+ Kxf7 41 Bxh7. } 40. Rxa6 Re7+ 41. Kf2
{ 41 Kd2 is refuted by Rg7. } 41... Be6 $1
{ Sacrificing a second Pawn for counterplay on the open c-file. On 41...Ke5 42 Ra7 keeps Black tied up. }
42. Rxd6 Ke5 43. Rc6
{ On 43 Rb6 Bc4 44 b3 d3! 45 bxc4 bxc4 and Black's passed Pawns should be sufficient to draw. }
43... Bd5 44. Rh6
{ On 44 Rc5 Kd6 45 Rxb5? Bc4 is menacing. 44 Rc1 is too passive tc yield any real winning chances. }
44... Rc7 45. Rh5+ Kd6 46. Rh6+ Ke5 47. Rh5+ { Not 47 Rb6 Rc1 48 Bxh7? Bc4. }
47... Kd6 48. Rf5 $2
{ No better is 48 Rxh7 (if 48 Bxh7 Rc1 threatening 49...Bc4) 48...Rc1 49 Bd3 Bc4. The winning line is 48 b3! (to shut him out from c4). If 48...Rc1 49 Be4! Bxe4 50 fxe4, etc. }
48... Rc1 49. Bd3 Rd1 { Not 49...Bc4? 50 Rxf4! Bxd3 51 Rxd4+, etc. } 50. Ke2
Rg1 51. Kf2 Rd1 52. Ke2 Rg1 53. Rg5 Bxa2 $2
{ Keres later claimed that 53...Ra1 would have drawn easily: e.g., 54 Bxh7 Bc4+ 55 Kd2 Rxa2 56 Kc2 Ra1. }
54. Bxb5 Rb1 55. Kd3 h6 $2 { 55...Rxb2 56 Kxd4 Bb1! should hold the draw. } 56.
Rh5 Rxb2 57. Kxd4 Rxg2 58. Rxh6+
{ The second adjournment. There are two technical obstacles facing White:
1) He cannot exchange Bishops; the ending where he is two Pawns ahead remains a theoretical draw with Rooks on the board.
2) He cannot exchange Rooks so long as Black's King has access to f6; the ending (even with two extra Pawns) is still a draw with Bishops on the board. }
58... Ke7 59. Ke4 Rg5 60. Ba6 $2
{ Keres thought 60 Bf1! was the winning chance. If 60...Rg1 61 Ba6! and Black no longer has the defense mentioned in the next note. Wrong is the immediate 60 Bd3 owing to 60...Bf7! 61 Kxf4 Rh5 62 Rxh5 Bxh5 followed by ... Bxf3. }
60... Bf7 $2
{ This slip is fatal. Keres told me he had reached this position in adjournment analysis, but had forgotten his drawing line over-theboard. Right is 60...Bb1+! 61 Kxf4 Rf5+ 62 Kg4 Rf6 63 Rxf6 Kxf6 with the aforementioned blockade. }
61. Bc8 $1 { Headed for g4. Not 61 Kxf4? Rh5 draws. } 61... Rg6
{ After 61...Rc5 62 Bg4 Rc4+ 63 Ke5 White's penetration is decisive. even though he doesn't win a second Pawn immediately. }
62. Rh7 Kf8 63. Bg4 Rg7 { Not 63...Rxg4? 64 Rxf7+! } 64. Rh6
{ White still can't afford to trade. } 64... Rg6 65. Rxg6 $1 Bxg6+ 66. Kxf4 Kg7
67. Kg5 $1
{ This makes all the difference. Black's King can no longer set up : blockade on f6. }
67... Bd3 68. f4 Be4 69. h4
{ Of course not 69 f5? Bxf5! and White is left with what Hans Kmoch :alls "the impotent pair." }
69... Bd3 70. h5 Be4 71. h6+ Kh8
{ On 71...Kf7 72 Bh5+ Kg8 73 Bg6 makes progress. } 72. Bf5 Bd5 73. Bg6 Be6 74.
Kf6 Bc4 75. Kg5 Be6 76. Bh5 { Back on the right track. } 76... Kh7 77. Bg4 $1
Bc4 { On 77...Bxg4 78 Kxg4 Kxh6 79 Kf5 wins. } 78. f5
{ Finally the f-Pawn is free to advance. } 78... Bf7 79. Bh5 Bc4 80. Bg6+ Kg8
81. f6
{ On 81...Bb3 82 Kf4 Kh8 83 Ke5 Bc4 84 Kd6 Bb3 85 Ke7 Bc4 86 Bf7 Bd3 87 Be8! Bc4 88 Bd7 Bg8 89 Be6 Bh7 90 f7 and queens. }
1-0

[Event "Zurich 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "9"]
[White "Walther"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Betwixt the cup and the lip"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "62"]

{ Here, against a minor European master, Fischer appears to be busted after seventeen moves, and admits he was ready to resign on move 36. Nevertheless, he extracts a miraculous draw from a hopeless ending, two Pawns down. Time and again Walther fumbles, allowing Fischer to prolong the struggle until he gets his break on move 54.
What makes this game memorable is the demonstration it affords of the way inwhich a Grandmaster redeems himself after having started like a duffer; and how a weaker opponent, after masterfully building a winning position, often lacks the technique required to administer the coup de grâce. As Capablanca remarked, "The good player is always lucky." }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Be7
{ Sharper is 7...Qb6 8 Qd2 Qxb2 9 Rb1 Qa3 which I tried with success later in my career. }
8. Qf3 Nbd7 { More exact is 8...Qc7to prevent9Bc4. } 9. O-O-O
{ Sharper is 9 Bc4! 0-0 (9...Qc7? 10 Bxe6! fxe6 11 Nxe6 Qbć 12 Nxg7+ Kf7 13 Nf5 with a crushing attack) 10 0-0-0. The text gives Black a chance to repair his earlier inaccuracy. }
9... Qc7 10. Bd3 { For 10 Be2 see game 14. For 10 g4 see games 12 and 15. }
10... b5 11. Bxf6 { Pointless. Better is 11 a3. } 11... Nxf6
{ Only not 11...Bxf6? 12 Bxb5! axb5 13 Ndxb5 wins. } 12. Rhe1
{ 12 a3 is necessary. Black comes out all right after 12 e5 Bb7 13 Qg3 dxe5 14 fxe5 Nd7 15 Rhe1 0-0-0! (Paoli-Tolush, Balatonfüred 1958.) }
12... Bb7
{ As I learned (see game 15), Black should avail himself of ...b4! (followed by ... Bb7 and ...d5) the instant he has the opportunity. }
13. Kb1 { At the risk of repeating myself, 13 a3 is mandatory. } 13... Rc8 $2
{ The disadvantage of this move is that Black loses his option of Q-side castling. Right is 13...b4 14 Nce2 g6! 15 g4 e5 16 Nb3 d5 17 Nd2 0-0 with initiative. }
14. g4
{ 14 a3 is still correct. We both suffered from the idée fixe that ...b4 was unplayable. }
14... Nd7 15. g5 Nb6 $2 { 15...b4! 16 Nce2 Nc5 still makes a fight of it. } 16.
f5 $1 e5
{ Now on 16...b4? 17 fxe6! splatters Black. Not 16...Bxg5? 17 Bxb5+! Ke7 18 fxe6 fxe6 19 Nxe6!, etc. But I still thought Black was all right. Walther's next move quickly disabused me of that notion. }
17. f6 $1 gxf6 { I'd overlooked that on 17...exd4 18 Nd5! is deadly. } 18. gxf6
Bf8 19. Nd5 $1 { Black's busted. } 19... Nxd5 20. exd5 Kd8 21. Nc6+ $1
{ A wise investment. The Pawn sac is insignificant compared to the opening of the center files against Black's King. }
21... Bxc6 22. dxc6 Qxc6 23. Be4 Qb6 24. Qh5 Kc7 25. Bf5 $1 Rd8 26. Qxf7+ Kb8
27. Qe6 Qc7 28. Re3 $1 Bh6 29. Rc3 Qb7 30. f7
{ Very business-like. Not 30 Rxd6??h1+. } 30... Bg7 31. Rcd3 Bf8 32. Qxe5 $1
{ Should be decisive. "Any resemblance to chess is purely :ncidental. } 32...
dxe5 33. Rxd8+ Ka7 34. R1d7 h5 35. Rxb7+ Kxb7 36. c3 Kc7
{ dinarily the curtain would be drawn here, but I just wanted to see - at he'd do next. }
37. Ra8 { Tne wrong track. On 37 Re8! Black resigns. } 37... Kd6 38. Rxa6+
{ I was still prepared to resign after 38 Re8! Black has absolutely no moves. White simply strolls his King to e4, creating zugzwang. }
38... Ke7 39. Re6+ { Even simpler is39Ra7+ Kf6 40 Bd3. } 39... Kxf7 40. Rxe5 b4
{ The game was adjourned. Strangely enough, I began to feel the position contained some swindling prospects. }
41. cxb4 Bxb4 42. h3 Kf6 43. Rb5 Bd6 44. Be4
{ On 44 a4 b8! forces the exchange of Rooks (if 45 d5? Be5). The pure opposite-colored-Bishop ending is Black's best chance to draw. }
44... Re8 { My first threat in the entire game! } 45. Rf5+ Kg7 46. Bf3 Re1+ 47.
Kc2 Rf1 $1 48. Rd5
{ The threat was 48...Rf2+ 49 Kb1 (if 49 Kb3? Kg6 wins a piece) 49...Rf1+ with a draw. }
48... Rf2+ 49. Rd2
{ 49 Kb1 Ba3! 50 bxa3 Rxf3 51 Rxh5 Rxa3 is also hopeless for Black. } 49...
Rxd2+ { Or 49...Rxf3 50 Rxd6 Rxh3 51 a4,etc. } 50. Kxd2 h4 51. Kd3 Kf6 52. Kc4
Ke7 53. Kb5 Kd7 54. a4 $2
{ This natural push throws away the win! The Swiss endgame composer, Fontana, pointed out the proper method: 54 b4! Kc7 55 Ka5! Kb8 56 b5 Ba3 57 b6 Kc8 58 Ka6 Kb8 59 Bg2! and Black is in zugzwang. If 59...Kc8 (or 59...c560 a4) 60 Ka7 Bc5 61 a4, etc.
The theme underlying Black's defense is this: once he succeeds in sacrificing his Bishop for both the Q-side Pawns, then White wili be left with the"wrong Bishop" for his h-Pawn. }
54... Kc7 55. b4 Kb8 56. a5 Ka7 57. Kc4 Bg3 58. b5 Bf2 59. Be2
{ 59 b6+ is met by59...Bxb6! } 59... Be3 60. Kb3 Bd2 { 60...Bf2 also draws. }
61. b6+ Kb7 62. Ka4 Kc6 63. Bb5+ Kc5
{ White's Pawns are stymied. On 64 b7 Bf4 65 a6 Kb6= } 1/2-1/2

[Event "Zurich 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Unzicker"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Milking the Cow"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "68"]

{ The Ruy Lopez has been so extensively analyzed that often both players reel off their first twenty moves in two minutes flat. Nevertheless, it gives rise to situations that call for tact and patience. As Bronstein remarked, "When you play the Ruy,it's like milking a cow." Fischer, here, milks the cow to a fare-thee-well.
The first twenty-two moves are identical with game number 6. Unzicker then varies with the dubious ...f6,which undermines his e-Pawn and leaves him with weak squares. Retribution, though not swift, is sure. Creating simultaneous threats on both wings, Fischer finally infiltrates on the a-file. Black is so tied up that he cannot defend one of his Pawns when attacked – making defeat imminent. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5
{ 10...c6 11 d4 Qc7 is an interesting alternative. (RossOLIMO) – Black avoidsweakeninghis d5. }
11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 Bd7 13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3
{ Tal and Geller recommend 14 b3 and if 14...g6 15 Bg5. } 14... g6 15. dxe5
dxe5 16. Nh2 Rad8 17. Qf3 Be6 18. Nhg4 Nxg4 19. hxg4
{ Against Matanovich, at Portoroz 1958, I tried the inferior 19 Nxg4 Bxg4 20 hxg4 c4 21 g3 Nb7 22 Kg2 Nc5 23 h1 f6=. Black's Knight is already prepared to parachute to d3. }
19... Qc6 { White gets an edge after 19...Nc4 20 Nd5 Bxd5 21 exd5. } 20. g5 $5
Nc4 21. Ng4 Bxg4 22. Qxg4 f6 $2
{ For analysis of this position see game 6. Unzicker prefers active defense. He eliminates the potential pressure along the h-file, it is true, but at the cost of weakening his e-Pawn. }
23. gxf6 Bxf6 24. a4 $1 Nb6 25. axb5 axb5 26. Be3
{ Because both flanks are fluid, White, with his two Bishops, is better able to penetrate and exploit the "loose" Pawns. 70 }
26... Ra8 { 26...Nc4 is met by 27 Bb3 instituting an awkward pin. } 27. Red1
{ If he swaps, White gains the a-file; if he doesn't, White keeps the d-file. }
27... Kh8 28. b3 Bg7
{ Black should ease the pressure by 28...Rxa1 29 Rxa1 Ra8 30 Rxa8+ Nxa8, though 31 Qd1! followed by Qal! forces an invasion on the Q-side. }
29. Qh4 Bf6 30. Bg5 $1 Bxg5 31. Qxg5
{ Now White must penetrate on either the a- or d-file. Black's e-Pawn has clearly been exposed as a weakling. }
31... Rxa1 { The threat was 32 Rxa8 Nxa8 33 Rd5. } 32. Rxa1 Nd7
{ Apparently defending everything. 33 Ra7 can be met by 33...Qd6. } 33. Bd1 $1
{ Bringing the Bishop into the game puts additional pressure on Black's overburdened pieces. }
33... Nf6
{ Not 33...Qxe4 34 Bf3 Qf4 (if 34...Qc2 35 Ra7 with a winning attack) 35 Qxf4 exf4 36 Bc6 Re7(or 36...Rd8 37 Rd1) 37 Ra8+! Kg7 38 Ra7 wins a piece. }
34. Ra7 { Infiltration! } 34... Qd6 { On 34...Nxe4? 35 Qh6 forcesmate. } 35.
Be2 $1
{ Simply attacking a Pawn. Curiously, Black is in too much of a straitjacket to do much aboutit. }
35... Re7
{ How can Black defend the Pawn? A]35...b4? 36 Ra6 Nxe4 37 Qh4 Qd5 38 Bf3 Qd3 39 Ra7 wins. B] 35...Nxe4? 36 Qh6 Re7 37 Qf8 mate. c]35...Qb6 36 Rf7 Ng8 37 Qh4 h6 38 Qg4 Rd8 39 Bxb5! wins. D] 35...Rb8 36 Rf7 Ng8 37 Rd7! Qf6 (if 37...Qxd7 38 Qxe5+ Qg7 39 Qxb8 Qxc3 40 Qxb5) 38 Qe3 Qc6 39 Rd5 when one of Black's hanging Pawns must fall. }
36. Rxe7 Qxe7 37. Bxb5 Kg7 38. Be2
{ The win is still far from clear. White's major technical problem is creating a passed Pawn on the Q-side while sheltering his King from a perpetual check. }
38... Qc7 { Threatening...Nxe4. } 39. Qe3 Qa5 40. g3 Qa3
{ Black should wait aroundwith 40...Qc7. } 41. Kg2
{ Sharper and possibly immediately decisive is 41 b4! cxb4 42 Qc5! } 41... Qa5
{ On 41...Qxb3 42 Qxc5 Nxe4 43 Qxe5+ Nf6 44 c4 should win. } 42. Qd3 Qb6 43.
Qc4 Qc6 44. Bd3
{ It's better to refrain from 44 f3 which weakens the K-field. } 44... Qb6 45.
b4 cxb4 46. cxb4 { Step one is completed: White has a passed b-Pawn. } 46...
Ng4 47. Qc5 Qxc5 { Forced. 47...Qd8? loses a second Pawn after 48 Be2. } 48.
bxc5 Kf7 49. f4 { The idea is to create another passed Pawn in the center. }
49... Ke7 50. Kf3 Nf6
{ On 50...h5 51 Bc4 maintains the bind (if 51...Kd7 52 Bf7). } 51. Bb5
{ Not51 fxe5? Nd7 draws. } 51... Ke6
{ Striving to keep the blockade as long as possible. } 52. Bc4+ Ke7 53. c6 $1
Ne8
{ Makes it easy. The best defense was 53...exf4 (if 53...Kd6? 54 fxe5+ Kxe5 55 c7) 54 gxf4 Ne8 55 e5 Nc7 56 Ke4 Ne8 (if 56...h5 57 Kf3 wins) 57 Bg8 Kf8 58 Bxh7 Kg7 59 Bxg6 Kxg660 f5+ Kg5 61 f6 Kg6 62 Kd5 Kf7 63 Kc5 Ke6 64 Kb6 wins. }
54. fxe5 h6 55. Ke3 Nc7 56. Kd4 h5 57. Ke3 $1 g5 58. Be2 h4 59. gxh4 gxh4 60.
Bc4 Ne8 61. Kf4 Kd8 62. Kg4 Kc7 63. Bf7 Ng7 64. Kxh4 Kxc6 65. Kg5
{ After 65...Kd7 66 Kf6 Ne8+ 67 Bxe8+ leads to an elementary win. } 1-0

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "11"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Benko"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Unheard melodies"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "74"]

{ Paul Morphy is the idol of all the romantics who pine for the swashbuckling chess of yesteryear;but it is rarely possible to succeed with that kind of flamboyance any longer since players now are more evenly matched. A rise in the standard of defense has necessitated a corresponding adjustment in the character of attack.In modern chess most of the beauty resides in the annotations. Brilliancies often exist only as grace notes – because the opposition anticipates and thwarts them with appropriate rejoinders. To the uninitiated, some of the most hard-fought struggles seem devoid of all bravura.That is the situation in this game.
Confronted with a dozen beautiful outlandish losing variations, Benko chooses what appears to be a prosaic one.Is this "ugliness" not a by-product of skill? Though the reader may feel cheated, and the winner frustrated, does it not argue for the perspicacity of the loser who sidestepped those seductive invitations? }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bc4 Qb6
{ For 6...Bd7 see game 13. By putting immediate pressure on the center, Black forces the Knight to a passive post. }
7. Nde2
{ For 7 Nb3 see game 58, note to Black's sixth move. No good is 7 Ndb5a6 8 Be3 Qa59 Nd4 Nxe4, etc. And 7 Nxc6!? bxc6 only helps Black strengthen his center, but after 8 0-0 White has promising tactical chances. }
7... e6 8. O-O Be7 9. Bb3 O-O 10. Kh1 { If 10 Be3 Qc7 11 f4 Ng4! } 10... Na5
11. Bg5 Qc5 $1
{ A finesse aimed at provoking 12 Be3 Qc7 after which White's initiative is blunted. }
12. f4 b5 { Another virtue of Black's last move was that it freed this Pawn. }
13. Ng3 b4 $2
{ Gligorich suggests simply 13...Bb7. Also satisfactory is 13...Nxb3! 14 axb3 Bb7(or 14...b4) 15 Nh5 Kh8=.
The text exposes Black to a vicious attack. }
14. e5 $1 dxe5
{ A] It's too late now for 14...Nxb3 15 exf6 gxf6 (if 15...Bxf6 16 Nce4) 16 Bh6, etc.
B] 14...bxc315 exf6 Bxf6 (if15...gxf6 16 Bh6 f5 17 Nh5 threatening to bring the Queen to g3) 16 Bxf6 gxf6 17 Ne4! Qf5 18 Nxd6 Qg6 19 Rf3 with a decisive attack (YUDOVICH). If now 19...Kh8 20 Rg3 Qh6 21 Qg4 (threateningQg8+!) 21...Qg6 22 Qh4. }
15. Bxf6 gxf6
{ A]On 15...Bxf6 16 Nce4 Qe7 (if 16...Qd4 17 Nxf6+ gxf6 18 Qg4+ Kh8 19 Rad1 Qxb2 20 Nh5 Rg8 21 Qxg8+! Kxg8 22 Rd8 mate) 17 Nh5! Kh8 (if 17...Bh4 18 fxe5 followed by Qg4 GLIGORICH) 18 Nexf6 gxf6 19 fxe5 fxe5 20 Nf6 threateningQh5 and wins.
B] The best chance is 15...bxc3! 16 Ne4 Qb4 17 Qg4 Bxf6 18 Nxf6+ Kh8 19 Qh4 h6 20 Ng4 threatening Nxh6 with a strong attack. }
16. Nce4 Qd4
{ Benko gave this move a lot of thought. Onthe alternative 16...Qc7 17 Nh5! (Black holds after 17 Qg4+ Kh8 18 Qh4 Rg8 19 Nxf6 Rg7 20 Qh6 Bb7!) 17...f5 18 Nhf6+! Kg7 19 Qh5! Bxf6(not 19...h6 20 Rf3 Rh8 [20...exf4 21 Rh3 Rh8 22 Ne8+!] 21 Rg3+ Kf8 22 Qxh6+!) 20 Nxf6 h6 (if 20...Rh8 21 Qg5+ Kf8 22 Qh6+ Ke7 23 Qh4 Kf8 24 Nxh7+ wins the exchange) 21 Rf3! Rh8 (not 21...Kxf6 22 Qh4+ Kg7 23 Rg3+ Kh7 24 Rh3 wins) 22 Ne8+! Rxe8 23 Rg3+ Kf8 24 Qxh6+ Ke7 25 Qh4+ Kd6 (if 25...Kf8 26 Rh3! Qd8 27 Qh6+ Ke7 28 Qg5+ wins the Queen) 26 Rd3+ Kc6 (if 26...Kc5 27 Ba4! threatens Qf2+) 27 Ba4+ Kb7 28 Bxe8 wins material. }
17. Qh5 $1 { Already Black is without a satisfactory defense. } 17... Nxb3
{ A] On 17...Kh818 Qh6 Rg8 (if 18...exf4 19 Nh5 wins) 19 Nxf6, etc. B] 17...exf4 18 Nf5! exf5 19 Rxf4 Qxe4 (otherwise Rh4) 20 Rxe4 fxe4 21 Qxa5 wins. (This key line wouldn't work had Black interpolated13...Nxb3! earlier!)
c]17...Kg7 (LOMBARDY) 18 Rad1 Qxb2 19 Qh4 Bb7 20 Nxf6! }
18. Qh6 $1 exf4
{ On 18...f5 19 c3! is devastating: e.g., 19...bxc3 20 bxc3 Q-any 21 Nh5 forces mate. }
19. Nh5 f5 20. Rad1 $1 Qe5 21. Nef6+ Bxf6 22. Nxf6+ Qxf6 23. Qxf6
{ Now it's skin and bones. } 23... Nc5 24. Qg5+ Kh8 25. Qe7 $1 Ba6 26. Qxc5
Bxf1 27. Rxf1 1-0

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "12"]
[White "Gligorich"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Castling into it"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "78"]

{ Although not perfect, this is perhaps the most bitterly contested game in this book. Fischer chooses a difficult variation which requires Olympian judgment. He submits his King to an attack which, on the face of it, can only be described as irresistible.Why did he do it? Because, we are told, it was strategically justified.
Gligorich, too, must be given equal credit for his courage and restraint. His continuation, despite intensive post-mortem analysis, has yet to be improved upon. The complexity of each phase of this tumultuous struggle must be studied to be believed. Curiously, in the "barren" Rook and Pawn ending, Gligorich somehow missed a win — a fact which he is probably unaware of to this day. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3
Qc7 9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. g4 b5
{ Gligorich and I have a standing feud with this position, which we've reached no less than three times. I've lost twice and drawn once (this one). }
11. Bxf6
{ Interesting is 11 Bg2 Bb7 12 Rhe1 b4 13 Nd5!? exd5 14 exd5 Kf8 15 Nf5 Re8 and Black won. (Bernstein-Fischer, US Championship 1957-8) }
11... gxf6 $5 { For 11...Nxf6 see game9. } 12. f5
{ Giving up e5 in order to exert pressure on e6. Our game at Zurich 1959 continued: 12 Bg2 Bb7 13 Rhe10-0-014 a3 Nb6=.
In the US Championship 1959-60, Mednis played12 a3 against me. The game went 12...Bb7 (12...Rb8is sharper) 13 f5 e5 14 Nde2 Nb6 15 Nd5 Bxd5 16 exd5 Rc8 17 Nc3 Nc418 Bxc4 bxc4 19 Kb1 Rb8 20 Ka2 with a better game for White.
12 Bd3 Bb7 13 Kb1 Nc5 14 f5 b4 15 Nce2 d5 16 fxe6 dxe4 17 exf7+ Kf8 18 Bxe4 Bxe4 19 Qxe4 Nxe4 20 Ne6+ Kxf7 21 Nxc7 Ra7 is exciting but equal. (Padevsky-Evans, Havana Olympic 1966.) }
12... Ne5
{ Simagin gave this a "?" and proceeded to analyze the alternative 12...b4 13 fxe6 bxc3(if 13...Ne5 14 Nd5!) 14 exd7+ Bxd715 e5, etc. By this logic, the whole variation is unsound for Black. We shall see. }
13. Qh3 O-O $1
{ Not13...Bd714 g5! fxg5 (if 14...exf5 15 Nd5) 15 fxe6 fxe6 16 Nxe6 andBlack's game collapses.
Petrosian and Tal both happened to stroll by the board at this instant. Petrosian made a wry face which looked to me like "Can Black do this and live?"
Black's "ugly" defense is based on sound positional considerations: once he can consolidate, there is a strong potential in the two Bishops coupled with his beautifully posted Knight and compact Pawn mass. These assets, in the long run, hopefully, should outweigh the temporary weakness of his King and the immobile target on e6. }
14. Nce2 $1
{ The consistent strategical plan. Gligorich intends Nf4, bringing additional pressure to bear on e6. Simagin gave the offhand 14 Qh6 Kh8 15 g5! and "wins". It is incredibly naïve to imagine that a player of Gligorich's caliber could overlook such a simple refutation. In this line simply 15...Rg8! refutes White's strategy. 16 g6(if 16 gxf6 Ng4 regains the Pawn with advantage) 16...fxg6 17 Nxe6 (weaker is 17 fxe6 Bb7) 17...Bxc618 fxe6 Rac8 19 Qd2 (if 19 a3d5! 20 exd5 Bxa3) 19...Nc4 20 Bxc4(if 20 Qd4 Qa5) 20...Qxc4 21 Qd5 Rge8 threatening .. Bf8 (or ...b4).
In a sense myjudgment was vindicated when Bronstein (as Black) reached the diagram against Kholmov in the 1964 USSR Championship. The continuation was 14 g5!? b4? (Kholmov gives the best defense: "14...fxg5! 15 fxe6 fxe6 16 Nxe6 Qd7 17 Nd5 Qxe6 18 Qxe6+ Bxe6 19 Nxe7+ Kf7 20 Nf5=") 15 gxf6 Bxf6 16 Rg1+ Kh8 17 Qh6 Qe7 18 Nc6!! Nxc619 e5!! and White won brilliantly. }
14... Kh8
{ 14...Qb7 also comes intoserious consideration. Unsound is 14...Bd7 15 Nf4 Qc8 16 Qh6 Kh8 17 Nh5 Rg8 18 Nxf6 Rg7 19 Be2 Qd820 g5, etc. }
15. Nf4 Rg8 16. Rg1
{ Gligorich gives 16 Be2as correct. After 16...Qb7 17 fxe6 fxe6 it's anybody's game. On 16 fxe6 fxe6 17 Ndxe6 Bxe6 (or ...Qa5 or ...Qb7) 18 Nxe6 Qc8=. }
16... d5 $1
{ Suddenly the game opens up! But more prudent is 16...Qb7 and if 17 Re1 Qb6! }
17. fxe6
{ Weak is 17 exd5? exf5! and White can't recapture because of the pin on the g-file. }
17... dxe4 18. Nd5 Qc5
{ Black's game hangs by a hair. 19 exf7 Nxf7 20 Nxe7 Qxe7 is roughly even. }
19. Nxe7
{ Weird complications result from the key line 19 Nf5!:
A) 19...Bd8 20 h6! (not 20 Rg3 fxe6 or 20 e7 Bxf5 21 exd8= Raxd8) 20...Rg6 21 Nf4! Nd3+ 22 xd3! wins.
B) 19...xg1 20 Nfxe7 Bxe6 (if 20...xg4 21xg4 xg4 22 Nc7 b8 23 Rd8+ Kg7 24 Nf5+ Kg6 25 e7 wins) 21 Nxg8 (if 21 Nxf6 Rg7) 21...Bxg4 22 Wh6 Bxd1 23 Ngxf6 Qg6 24 Qxg6 hxg6 25 Kxd1 with winning chances. }
19... Qxe7 20. Nf5 { If 20 e3b721 exf7 Qxf7 Black wins a Pawn. } 20... Qxe6 21.
Qh6
{ Possibly Gligorich had originally intended 21 Rd6? Qxa2 22 Qh6 overlooking that Black can win with 22...Qa1+ (if 22...Bxf5 23 Qxf6+ Rg7 24 Rd8+ leads to a draw by perpetual) 23 Kd2 Nf3+ 24 Ke3 (if 24 Ke2?? Qe1 mate; or 24 Kc3? Qa5+ 25 Kb3 Qa4+ 26 Kc3 b4+ 27 Kc4 Be6+ leads to mate) 24...Qxb2! wins. }
21... Bd7 $1
{ Defending against the powerful threat of Rd8! Not 21...Nxg4? 22 Rxg4! Rxg4 23 Rd8+ Rg8 24 Qg7 mate. }
22. Rd6
{ White has no choice. Black threatened ...Rg6 followed by ...Qxa2. Or on 22 KblNxg4. }
22... Nxg4 $1 23. Rxg4 { Forced. 23 Rxe6? Nxh6 wins a piece. } 23... Qxf5 24.
Rxg8+ $2
{ Handing Black the game on a silver platter. Correct is 24 Rf4 Qg5 25 Qxf6+ Qxf6 26 Rdxf6e3! 27 Rxf7 Rg1! 28 Rxd7 e229 Rd1! exd1=Q+ 30 Kxd1 Kg7 31 Ke1 Rf8 32 Rxf8 Kxf8 33 Kf2 with good drawing prospects.
Black has two ways to recapture: which one is correct? }
24... Rxg8 $2
{ Returning the compliment! The winning line is 24...Kxg8! 25 Rxf6 Qe5 with an extra Pawn and a dominating position (if 26 Rxa6? Rxa6 27 Qxa6 Qf4+ wins a piece). }
25. Rxf6 Qd5 26. Rd6 $1 Qf5
{ On26...Qb7? 27 Qf6+ Rg7 28 Qd8+ Rg829 Qxd7 wins. } 27. Rf6 Qg5+
{ I should have contented myself with the draw now by 27...Qd5 28 Rd6, etc. }
28. Qxg5 Rxg5 29. Rxf7 Bg4 30. Kd2 Bf3 31. Ke3 Rg1 32. Bh3 { Not 32 Kf2? Rh1. }
32... Re1+ 33. Kf4 Bd1
{ Playing, as Dr. Tarrasch wryly put it, "for the loss." Nowadays I would know better than to try to squeeze a win out of such a simplified ending. }
34. Ke5 $1
{ Gligorich is also playing to win – by cashing in on my inexperience. Simply 34 Re7 Bxc2 35 Bf5 holds the draw. But he is purposely inviting me tooverextend myself. }
34... e3 35. Bf5 Rg1 36. Rxh7+ Kg8 37. Rc7 Bg4 $2
{ Still chasing the chimera of the missed win. Black should simply force a draw with 37...e2 38 Kf6 Kh8 39 Rh7+ Kg8 40 Rc7 Kh8, etc. }
38. Bxg4 Rxg4 39. Rc3 e2 40. Re3 Rg2
{ In my excitement I had originally intended 40...Rg7? overlooking the simple reply 41 Kd4 (among others). Fortunately, Black can still hold the draw. }
41. Kd4 e1=Q $1 { After 41...Rxh2? 42 Kd3 Black is really lost! } 42. Rxe1 Rxc2
43. Rb1 Kf7 $1
{ Not 43...Rxh2? 44 Kc5 with a fatal penetration on the Q-side. } 44. a3 Ke6
45. b3 { On 45 h4 Kd6 holds the balance. The threat now is Rh1. } 45... Rxh2
46. Kc5 Kd7 47. Kb6 Ra2 48. Kxa6 Rxa3+ 49. Kb7
{ Trying to finagle. 49 Kxb5 Kc7 leads to an easy book draw. } 49... Kd6
{ Crisper is 49...b4 50 Rd1+ (on 50 Kb6 Kc8 the Black King gets in front of the Pawn) 50...Ke6 51 Rd3 Ke5 52 Kb6 Ke4 53 Rh3 Kd4 54 Kb5 Ra8 55 Kxb4 Rb8+ 56 Ka5 Ra8+ 57 Kb6 Rb8+ 58 Kc6 (if 58 Ka6 Kc5=) 58...Rb4 59 Rg3 Rb8 with an impenetrable blockade. }
50. Kb6 Kd7 51. b4 Rh3 52. Rc1 Rh8 $2
{ After the game Olafsson scolded me: "How can you play an ending like this so fast?"(I'd only been taking a few seconds a move for the last dozen moves or so.) "Because there's no danger. It's a dead draw, I replied. Had I known then what I know now, I would have selected 52...Rh5 and if 53 Rc5 Rxc5 54 Kxc5 Kc7 55 Kxb5 Kb7 holding the opposition, with a book draw. }
53. Kxb5 $2
{ Now it's Gligorich's turn to let me out. As Olafsson showed me, White can win with 53 Rc7+! It's hard to believe. I stayed up all night analyzing, finally convincing myself and, incidentally, learning a lot about Rook and Pawn endings in the process.Gligorich failed to point it out in his notes to the Bled tournament book. The main point is Black cannot get his King in front of the Pawn.
A sample line is 53 Rc7+! Kd6 (if 53...Kd8 54 Rc5 Kd7 55 Kb7! Kd6 56 xb5) 54 Rc6+ Kd7 (if 54...Kd5 55 Kxb5 b8+ 56 Rb6) 55 Kxb5 Rb8+ (if 55...Rh4 56 Rc1 Rh8 57 Ka6) 56 Rb6 Rh8 57 Rb7+ κc8 58 Ka6 Rh6+ 59 Ka7 with a book win. }
53... Rb8+ 54. Ka4 Ra8+
{ The game was adjourned again. But the crisis has passed. } 55. Kb3 Rc8 56.
Rxc8 Kxc8 57. Kc4 Kb8 $1
{ Black holds the "distant opposition." For example, 58 Kc5 (or 58 Kd5 Kb7) 58...Kc7 59 Kb5κb7, etc. }
1/2-1/2

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "13"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Gligorich"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Something new"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "87"]

{ During the mid-fifties, Gligorich, Reshevsky, and Najdorf were considered the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters. Within a few years Fischer managed to surpass them. However, in so doing, he succeeded in beating Gligorich only once – up to 1966 (see game 56).
On the occasion of this first win, Fischer employs a novel attacking system (13 Qe2) against the Dragon Variation. Gligorich fails to react vigorously enough and makes the mistake of castling too soon, thereby exposing himself to the same type of sacrificial combination that demolished Larsen in game 2. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bc4 Bd7
{ For Benko's 6...Qb6 see game 11. Recently in a skittles game someone tried 6...g6!? against me. The game continued: 7 Nxc6 bxc6 8 e5 Nh5? (correct is 8...Ng4. Not 8...dxe5?? 9 Bxf7+ winning the Queen - that was another skittles game!) 9 Qf3! e6 (if 9...d5 10 Nxd5!) 10 g4 Ng7 11 Ne4 Qa5+ (if 11...d5 12 Nf6+ Ke7 13 Qa3+) 12 Bd2 Wxe5 13 Bc3 Black resigns. }
7. Bb3
{ 7 Be3 is met by 7...Ng4! On 7 Bg5 e6 8 Bxf6? Qxf6 9 Ndb5 0-0-0 10 Nxd6+ Kb8 with a winning attack (GLIGORICH). Also strong is 7 0-0 g6 8 Nxc6! Bxc6 (or 8...bxc6 9 f4) 9 Bg5 Bg7 10 Nd5! }
7... g6 8. f3
{ The only other try for any advantage is 8 Be3 Ng4 9 Nxc6 bxc6 (9...Nxe3? 10 Bxf7+!) 10 Qf3 (not10 Bxa7?c5) 10...Ne5 11 Qg3. }
8... Na5
{ Releasing the central tension this way is wrong. Correct is 8...Nxd4 9 Qxd4 Bg7 but after 10 Bg5! White still keeps control. }
9. Bg5 Bg7 10. Qd2 h6
{ A concession. But on 10...0-0 11 Bh6 followed by h4-h5 produces a strong and almost mechanical attack. }
11. Be3 Rc8 12. O-O-O Nc4 13. Qe2 $5
{ A totally new idea at the time. 13 Bxc4 Rxc4 14 g4 was the usual, and good, procedure. The text permits Black to capture what was considered, then, to be the more important of the White Bishops.
Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he enthusiastically gave my thirteenth move "!!" claiming it was virtually the winning line. Alexander Kotov, the commissar of chess criticism in the Soviet Union, wrote,with more sober restraint: "It is difficult to agree with this." }
13... Nxe3 { Not13...Qc7? 14 Ndb5. } 14. Qxe3 O-O
{ Reminiscent of game 12, it is now Gligorich who castles into it! At Mar del Plata 1960, Marini played against me the stronger 14...Qb6 (threatening ...e5) 15 Qd2 Qc5 16 f4 h5? (better is 16...b5 or ...0-0) 17 Nf3 Bh618 e5! with a powerful attack.
Kotov recommends 14...Qa5 15 Kb1 (he gives only 15 g4? Qg5!) 15...Qc5 16 Qd3 a6 and Black's all right. So best is probably 15 f4 (after 14...Qa5) 15...0-016 h3 e6 but Black's two Bishops may offset the weakness of the d-Pawn. }
15. g4 { Timing is important. On 15 h4 h5 locks it up. } 15... Qa5 16. h4 e6
{ On 16...h5 17 g5 Ne8 18 f4-f5 gives White a strong game. } 17. Nde2 $1
{ Black holds out after 17 g5 hxg5 18 hxg5 Nh5 19 f4 Qc5 (threatening..e5). }
17... Rc6 18. g5 hxg5
{ On18...Nh5 19 gxh6 Bf620 f4 continues the Pawn stampede. } 19. hxg5 Nh5 20.
f4 Rfc8 21. Kb1
{ An important preparatory move. On the immediate 21 f5!? exf5 22 Nd5 Qxa2! gives Black good play. }
21... Qb6 22. Qf3 Rc5 23. Qd3 $1
{ Several Yugoslav chess journalists scurried forward toward the analysis room, where Matanovich was explaining the game on a demonstration board. Apparently the feeling was that I had just blundered.
The more obvious 23 f5 looks good, but Black still has defensive resources with 23...exf5 24 Rxh5 (if 24 Nd5 Qd8 25 exf5 Bxf5 26 Rxh5? Rxc2! wins for Black) 24...gxh5 25 Nf4 Rxc3 26 bxc3 Rxc3 27xh5 Rxb3+28 cxb3e3, etc. }
23... Bxc3
{ The threat against the d-Pawn is awkward to meet. On 23...R5c6 24 f5! exf5 25 Rxh5! gxh5 26 exf5 is crushing. Or if 23...R8c6? 24 Na4 wins the exchange. Finally on 23...Bf8 24 f5! exf5 25 Nd5 Qd8 (if 25...fxe4 26 Qxe4 Bf5 27 Qxf5 wins a piece) 26 Rxh5! gxh5 (26...Rxd5 27 Bxd5 gxh5 28 exf5) 27 Nf6+ Kg7 28 h3 releases an avalanche. }
24. Nxc3 { Not 24 bxc3? Bb5. } 24... Nxf4
{ This is what the crowd thought I had overlooked. } 25. Qf3 Nh5
{ On 25...e526 Ne2!is decisive. } 26. Rxh5 $1
{ I've made this sacrifice so often, I feel like applying for a patent! } 26...
gxh5 27. Qxh5 Be8
{ The best defensive try On 27...Kf8 28 Qh8+ Ke7 29 Qf6+ Ke8 30 Rh1 Bb5 31 Bxe6! fxe6 32 Qxe6+ Kd8 (or 32...Kf833 Rh8+ Kg7 34 Qf6 mate) 33 Rh8+ Kc7 34 Rxc8 mate. }
28. Qh6 $1 Rxc3 29. bxc3 { On 29 Rh1,Qd4 holds out for a while. } 29... Rxc3
{ White still retains a winning attack after 29...Qe3 30 Rh1 Qxc3 31 g6 Qg7 32 Qh2!(BRONSTEIN). }
30. g6 $1 fxg6 31. Rh1 Qd4 32. Qh7+
{ Again a mistake! 32 Bxe6+ mates more quickly. } 1-0

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "14"]
[White "Keres"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Too many cooks"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "92"]

{ Professionals spend much of their spare time hunting for "cooks" with which they hope to surprise future opponents. It was rumored, for example, that Marshall waited for over ten years before springing his famous gambit on Capablanca, at New York, in 1918. But, as it happened, the wily Cuban refuted it over-the-board!
Keres, in like manner, confronts Fischer with an innovation which the latter, in all innocence, proceeds to destroy. Rather than admit that his surprise Queen sacrifice is good for only a draw at best, Keres presses for more, offering material in order to sustain his inítiative. Fischer continues to accept everything, but — at the very moment when victory is within his grasp (on move 31) — stumbles. Now he must win the game all over again; and he manages to do so, with an assist from Keres, in another twenty-two moves. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Be7
{ Sharp is 7...b6 which I've tried on several occasions. } 8. Qf3 Qc7
{ 8...h6 9 Bh4 g5!? 10 fxg5 Nfd7 11 Nxe6!? fxe6 12 h5+ Kf8 13 Bb5! h7! (Gligorich-Fischer, Portoroz 1958) is now considered a drawish variation! }
9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. Be2
{ An innovation whose dubious merit appears on move 13. For 10 Bd3 see game 9. For 10 g4 see games 12 and 15. }
10... b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6
{ Not 11...Bxf6? 12 Bxb5! Or on 11...gxf6 12 Qh5 Nb6 (if 12...0-0? 13 Rd3) 13 a3 followed by f5 is strong. }
12. e5 $5 { On 12 a3 Rb8! followed by ..b4 gives good counterplay. } 12... Bb7
13. exf6 $5
{ The crux of Keres' prepared line. After 13 Qg3 dxe5 14 fxe5 Nd7 15 Qxg7 Qxe5 Black stands better. }
13... Bxf3 { So I chopped it off! } 14. Bxf3 Bxf6
{ Not 14...Rc8? 15 fxe7 Qxe7 16 Nf5!(BONDAREVSKY). } 15. Bxa8 d5
{ So far, so forced. Now ..0-0is threatened. } 16. Bxd5
{ On 16 Bc6+ Kf8 17 Nce2 Ke7 followed by ...Rc8. } 16... Bxd4
{ Not 16...Qxf4+ 17 Kb1 Bxd4 18 Bc6+! Ke719 Ne2, etc. Larsen suggested 16...b4 17 Bc6+ Ke7 18 Nce2 Rd8 but 19 Rd2! (19 g3? Qb6!) 19...Bxd4 20 Nxd4 Qxf4 21 Nb3 holds for the time being. }
17. Rxd4 exd5 18. Nxd5 Qc5 19. Re1+ Kf8 20. c3
{ White seems to have fair prospects. But a Queen is a Queen! } 20... h5 $1
{ A hard move to find - even somewhat risky – over-the-board. Probably Keres had expected 20...g6 21 g4 Kg722 g5 h6 (if 22...Rd8 23 Nf6 Rxd4 24 Re8 d8!25xd8 Qe3+ draws) 23 h4 hxg5 24 fxg5 Rd8 25 Nf6 Rxd4 26 Re8 Rd8! 27 Rxd8 Qe3+ with a draw by perpetual. }
21. f5
{ To hinder ...g6. Kotov gives 21 Nb4 Qc8? (simply 21...g6 is more than sufficient; if then 22 Nxa6 Qc6) 22 Nc6! and wins.
Zagoryansky also mistakenly thinks White has all the chances. He gives "21 Re5!g6 (more passive is 21...Qc8 22 Ne7 Qa823 Nc6 f6 24 Re6 Kf7 25 f5) 22 f5!"but 22...Kg7 23 f6+ Kh6 (if 24 g4 b4!) is quite satisfactory for Black. }
21... Rh6 $1
{ The key to Black's defense; now the Rook sneaks into play via the side exit. }
22. f6 $2
{ Throwing away a Pawn in an attempt to keep Black bottled up. Keres should just try to maintain the status quo with 22 Red1, although Black retains some slight winning chances. But he seems to labor under the delusion that White has the initiative.
22 Rde4 Kg8 23 Re8+ Kh7 24 Rd8 Rd6 neutralizes all White's threats. }
22... gxf6 23. Nf4 h4 24. Rd8+ $2
{ Continuing the "attack". The defensive 24 Re2 was in order. } 24... Kg7 25.
Ree8 Qg1+ 26. Kd2 Qf2+ 27. Ne2 Rg6 28. g3
{ 28 Rg8+ Kh6 29 Rh8+ Kg5 gets White nowhere. } 28... f5 29. Rg8+ Kf6 30. Rxg6+
{ Or 30 Rd6+ Ke7 31 Rdxg6 fxg6 32 Rxg6 h3 wins (ZAGORYANSKY). } 30... fxg6 31.
gxh4 Qxh2 $2
{ The winning method is 31...Qxh4! 32 Rd6+ Kf7 33 h3 Qh6+! (if 33...Qxh3 34 Rxg6! Kxg6?? 35 Nf4+) 34 Kd1 (if 34 Ke1 Qxh3 35 Rxg6 Qh4+! 36 Rg3 f4 wins; or on 34 Kc2 Qxh3 35 Rxg6 Qh2 wins) 34...Qxh3 35 Rxg6 Qf1+ and ...Kxg6. }
32. Rd4 $1 { Now Black has to win the game over again. } 32... Qh1 33. Kc2
{ 33 Nf4? allows penetration with ... Qb1. } 33... Ke5 34. a4
{ An even tighter defense is 34 Nc1! followed by Nd3+ with a probable draw. But not 34 Nf4? Qh2+! 35 Kd1 Qxf4! 36 Rxf4 Kxf4 with a won King and Pawn ending: e.g., 37 Ke2 (if 37 b3 Ke3!) 37...Kg338 b3 Kxh4 39 c4 bxc4 40 bxc4 Kg5 and the King is "in the square". }
34... Qf1
{ Trying to capitalize on the disjointed state of White's minor pieces. } 35.
Nc1 { Forced. Not 35 Kd2? Qa1! 36 Kc2 bxa4, etc. } 35... Qg2+ 36. Kb3 $2
{ In time pressure, Keres creates new losing chances for himself. Also bad is 36 Kb1 bxa4 37 Rxa4 Qf1 38 Kc2 Kf6! followed by .f4 (if 39 Nd3? Qe2+).
The right defense is 36 Kd1! bxa4 (not 36...Qxb2?? 37 Nd3+) 37 Nd3+ Kf6 38 Rxa4 a539 Rd4 (not 39 Rxa5? Qg4+ and ... Qxh4) and Black can't make any headway. }
36... bxa4+ 37. Ka3
{ On 37 Rxa4 (if 37KXa4 Qc2+ 38 Nb3 Qxb2) 37...Qd2! 38 Na2 f4 is deadly. }
37... Qc2 38. Nd3+ Kf6 39. Nc5 Qc1 $1 { Threatening ...Qa1+. } 40. Rxa4
{ 40 Nxa4 f4 41 Nc5 f3 42 Nd3 Qe3 transposes into the note after White's 41st move. }
40... Qe3 { The game was adjourned and Keres sealed his move. } 41. Nxa6 $2
{ This makesit easy. I had expected 41 Rd4 f4 42 Nd3 f3. The win is hard, but eventually Black breaks through on a3. For example, 43 Kb3 (43 b4? f2 wins) 43...Kg7 44 Ka3 Qe2 45 Kb3 Qd1+ 46 Kc4 (if 46 Ka3 a5 47 Ka2 a4 48 Nf2 Qb3+ 49 Ka1 a3, etc.) 46...a5 47 Kb5 a4 48 Ka5 Qb3 49 Ka6 a3 50 bxa3 Qxa3+51 Kb5 Qxc3, etc. Maybe White can improve, but Black should win because the blockade is not airtight. }
41... f4 42. Rd4 Kf5 $1
{ The move Keres missed when he sealed his forty-first. He had probably anticipated 42...f3 43 Nc5 f2 44 Ne4+ Qxe4 45 Rxe4 f1=Q with a dead draw since Black can't create another passed Pawn. }
43. Nb4
{ More resistance could have been offered by 43 Nc5 but it's still lost after 43...e7! 44 b4 xh4 45 b5 Qf6, etc. }
43... Qe7 $1
{ This temporary pin is decisive. Now Black wins the h-Pawn and his two passed Pawns become irresistible. }
44. Kb3 Qxh4 45. Nd3 g5 46. c4 Qg3 47. c5 f3 48. Kc4 f2 49. Nxf2 Qxf2 50. c6
Qxb2 51. Kc5 Qc3+ 52. Kd5 { On 52 Rc4 Qa5+ 53 Kd4 Wc7 wins. } 52... g4 53. Rc4
Qe5# 0-1

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "15"]
[White "Smyslov"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "A whopper"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "100"]

{ Here is Fischer's first win against VassilySmyslov; and it is hard to recall when the former world champion, conductingWhite, has been so badly outplayed.
On move 13 of a crucial opening variation, Smyslov makes what appears to be a "lapsus manus."Rather than fight a prolonged uphill positional battle, he sacrifices a Pawn to try to regainthe initiative. This proves to be a piece of bad judgment, since, basically, the loss of this Pawn alone brings about his demise. Defending with deadly precision, Fischer gradually consolidates – the shadow of his Pawn looming larger with each approach to the endgame. Smyslov thrashes about, striving desperatelyforcomplications,avoiding exchanges like the plague. But he isunable to get off the hook. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5
{ For 6 Be2 see games 4 and 42. For 6 Bc4 see games 17, 55, 58. } 6... e6 7. f4
Be7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O Nbd7
{ Weak is 9...h6 10 Bh4 Nbd7 11 Bd3 b512 e5! Bb7 13 Nxe6! fxe6 14 Bg6+ Kf8 15 exf6 Bxf3 (better is 15...Nxf6) 16 fxe7+ Kg8 17 gxf3 Nf6 18 Bxf6 gxf6 19 e8=Q+ Rxe8 20 Bxe8 d5 (Gligorich-Bobotsov, Hastings 1959-60); 21 f5!(MCO)wins. }
10. g4 b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 { For 11...gxf6 see game 12. } 12. g5 Nd7 13. Bh3 $2
{ Innovation or omission? In either case, after this move White throws away his theoretical advantage and even loses the initiative. Necessary is 13 a3 Bb7 (13...Rb8! is in vogue) and now there are two main lines:
A] 14 h4 d5 15 exd5 Nb616 f5 Nxd5 17 fxe6 0-0-0 18 Bg2 Nxc3 19 Qxb7+ Qxb7 20 Bxb7+ Kxb7 21 bxc3 Bxa3+ 22 Kb1 fxe6 23 Nxe6 Rc8 24 Rh3 g6 25 c4 Rhe8 and shortly drawn. (Sherwin-Fischer, US Championship1959-60.)
B]14 Bh3 0-0-0 15 f5!? (interesting is 15 Bxe6!? fxe6 16 Nxe6 Qc4 [Keres recommends 16...Qb6] 17 Nd5 with unclear complications, though White won, Tal-Gligorich, Moscow 1963) 15...Bxg5+ 16 Kb1 e5 17 Ndxb5 axb5 18 Nxb5 Qb6! (better than 18...Qc5 which I played against Gligorich at the Candidates'1959) 19 Nxd6+ Kc7 20 Nxf7 Bf6 is roughly equal. }
13... b4 $1 { My game with Walther (game 9) had taught me this lesson well. }
14. Nce2 Bb7 15. Kb1 $2
{ In this sharp variation, White has no time for such amenities. 15 Ng3 avoids incurring any severe disadvantage, while the speculative 15 Bxe6!? fxe6 16 Nxe6 Qc4 17 Nxg7+ (better is17N2d4) 17...Kf8! favors Black. }
15... Nc5 16. Ng3 d5 $1
{ I could see from the expression on Smyslov's face that he already thought he was busted. }
17. f5 $5
{ On 17 e5g6! 18 Rc1 Qb6! and if 19 c3 a5 followed by ...0-0 with a powerful attackin the works. Smyslov's keen positional judgment tells him that such a course for White is lifeless. So he sacs a Pawn instead. }
17... dxe4 18. Qg4 exf5 19. Ndxf5 g6 $1
{ Perhaps this simple retort escaped him. Was he hoping for 19...0-0? 20 Nh5! g6 21 Qg3! winning material (if 21...Bd8 22 Rxd8! Qxd8 23 Nf6+, etc.)? The rest of the game is, if one may use that hackneyed phrase, "a matter of technique." Black's a Pawn ahead with the better game to boot. }
20. Nxe7 { Not 20 Nh6? Bc8 21 Qh4 Bxh3 22 Qxh3 Bxg5. } 20... Qxe7 21. Qf4 O-O
22. Rd6 Rad8 23. Rf6 { NaturallySmyslov avoids swapping. } 23... Rd5 24. Bg4
Nd7 25. Rf1
{ A desperate attempt to complicate. On 25 Nxe4? Rd4 26 Bxd7 Qxd7 wins a piece.Or 25 Qxe4 Rd1+! 26 Rxd1 Bxe4 27 Rxd7 Qe5. }
25... e3 { Not 25...Nxf6? 26 gxf6 Qe5 27 Qh6 wins. } 26. b3 Rd2
{ Threatening 27...Nxf6 28 gxf6 Qc5! 29 Rc1 Qd4. To avert further material loss, Smyslov is forced to indulge in the simplifications he has been trying so hard toforestall. }
27. Bxd7
{ Forced, but now White's game rapidly deteriorates and the extra Pawn makes its presence felt. }
27... Rxd7 28. Re1 Re8 29. h4 Qc5 $1 30. Qc4
{ Horrible, but necessary, to meet the main threat of ... Qc3. } 30... Qxc4 31.
bxc4 Rd4 { Now Black hacks away unmercifully. } 32. c5 Rxh4 33. c6 Bc8 34. Rd6
Rc4 35. Kb2 Kg7 36. Kb3 Rg4 37. Ne2 Re6 $1 38. Red1
{ Or 38 Rxe6 Bxe6+ 39 Kb2 Rc4, etc. } 38... Rg2 39. Nf4 Rxd6 40. Rxd6 Rd2 41.
Rd3
{ The only move. Smyslov might have resigned had we adjourned here. But we were both playing fast and, as a consequence, were still well in the first session, carried by sheer momentum. }
41... Rf2 42. Rd4 { Again forced. On 42 Nd5 e2 43 Re3 Rf3 wins. } 42... e2 43.
Nd3 Bf5 44. c7 Rf3
{ Quicker is 44...Bxd3 45 c8=Q e1=Q 46 Rxd3 Qb1+ 47 Ka4 Rxc2, etc. } 45. c8=Q
Bxc8 { Again 45...e1=Q was quicker, but I wanted to avoid "complications.' }
46. Re4 Bf5 47. Rxe2 Bxd3 48. cxd3 Rxd3+ 49. Kxb4 Rd5 50. Rg2 h6 51. gxh6+ Kxh6
52. a4 g5 53. Rc2 Rd6 54. Kc5 Re6 0-1

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "16"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Petrosian"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[Title "Four Queens"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "106"]

{ Fischer extracted a slight minus score from the seven games in which he was called upon to face the Caro-Kann, prompting Botvinnik to note: "Fischer's both strong and weak point lies in that he is always true to himself and plays the same way regardless of his partners or any external factor. 31
This variation is not only complex and critical – but perilous. After a single slip the edge passes to Black. But Petrosian overestimates his position and, somewhat recklessly, dissipates his advantage. In timepressure he misses a forced draw and Fischer regains the upper hand. From this point on, with four marauding Queens roaming the board, the play becomes "rich and strange" – resulting in a tortuous draw. }
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3
{ The purpose of this line is to exclude the possibility of .. Bf5. For example, 3...dxe4 4 Nxe4 Bf5? 5 Ng3 Bg6 (if 5...Bg4 6 h3) 6 h4 h6 7 Ne5 Bh7 8 Qh5 g6 9 Bc4! e6 10 Qe2 (threatening Nxf7) and Black has a terrible game. }
3... Bg4
{ 3...Nf6 4 e5 Ne4 5 Ne2! Qb6 6 d4 c5 7 dxc5 Qxc5 8 Ned4 Nc6 9 Bb5 a6 10 Bxc6+ bxc6 11 0-0 b6 12 e6! fxe6 13 Bf4 is good for White. (Fischer-Olafsson, Candidates' 1959.) }
4. h3 Bxf3
{ In our first round game here, Smyslov played 4...Bh5 5 exd5 cxd5 6 Bb5+ Nc6 7 g4 Bg6 8 Ne5 Rc8 9 d4 e6 10 h4 (correct is 10 Qe2! to prevent ...f6) 10...f6 11 Nxg6 hxg6 12 Qd3 Kf7 13 h5 gxh5 14 gxh5 Nge7=. }
5. Qxf3 Nf6
{ The old 5...e6 6 d4 dxe47 Nxe4 Qxd4 8 Bd3 gives White a good attack for the Pawn. Andon 5...dxe4 6 Nxe4 Nd77 Ng5!? (better is simply 7 d4) 7...Ngf6 8 Qb3 e6 9 Qxb7 Nd5! Black gets good play. (Fischer-Cardoso, Portoroz 1958.)
Inferior is 6 d4 dxe4 7 Nxe4!? (7 Qe3 Nbd7 8 Nxe4 Nxe4 9 Qxe4 Nf6 10 Qd3 Qd5! is equal, Fischer-Keres, Bled 1961) 7...Qxd4 8 Bd3 Nbd7 threatening ...Ne5.
On 6 e5 Nfd77 e6?(playable is 7 Qg3 e6 8 Be2 Spassky-Reshko, Leningrad1961) 7...fxe68 d4 e5!
Finally on 6 g3 dxe4 7 Nxe4 Nxe4 8 Qxe4 Qd5! 9 Qxd5 cxd5 10 Bg2 e6 (if 11 c4 Nc612 cxd5 Nb4!) gives Black an even ending (SUETIN). }
6. d3 e6 7. g3
{ A recent try is 7 Bd2 followed by 0-0-0. Against Larsen, at Zurich 1959, I tried 7 a3 Bc5 8 Be20-0 9 0-0 Nbd7 and Black got a satisfactory game. }
7... Bb4 8. Bd2 { Not 8 Bg2? d4 9 a3 Qa5. } 8... d4
{ Inferior is 8...Qb69 0-0-0 d4 10 Ne2. } 9. Nb1 Bxd2+
{ In this tournament Keres and Benko both tried9...Qb6 forcing White to weaken the Q-side with 10 b3. But Black's Queen is slightly misplaced after 10...Nbd7 11 Bg2 a5 12 a3 Bxd2+ (the retreat 12...Be7 seems illogical –- even though Keres beat me with it) 13 Nxd2 Qc5 14 Qd1 h5 15 h4! with an edge. (Fischer-Benko.) Petrosian apparently didn't want to get involved with this line despite the fact that his countryman, Tal, accused me of "bad judgment" for preferring White here. }
10. Nxd2 e5 11. Bg2 c5 12. O-O Nc6 13. Qe2
{ The critical juncture. In our earlier game (round two) Petrosian continued with 13...g5 14 Nf3? (Simagin gives14 f4 gxf415 gxf4 Qe7 16 Nc4 Nd7 17 Qg4 "with advantage"but after 17...0-0-0 18 fxe5 Kb8 Black succeeds in planting his Knight on e5 where it cannot be dislodged) 14...h615 h4 Rg816 a3 Qe7 17 hxg5 hxg5 18 Qd2 Nd7 19 c30-0-0 20 cxd4 exd4 with advantage for Black. }
13... Qe7
{ Fearing a prepared line, Petrosian deviates. On 13...g5I had intended 14 c3! Qe7 15 Nf3 h6 16 cxd4! exd4 (if 16...Nxd4 17 Nxd4 cxd4 18 Rac1.Or 16...cxd4 17 h4 Rg818 hxg5 hxg5 19 Rfc1 0-0-0 20 b4! Kb8 21 b5 Na5 22 Qd2 wins a Pawn) 17 e5! 0-0-0 18 Rfe1. Now the Bishop diagonal is unblocked and Black can't set up a blockade on his e5, as in our first game.
After 13...0-014 f4 Kh815 f5 Ng816 g4 f6 White has a tough nut to crack, but his initiative is permanent. The text indicates Petrosian's intention to castle long without trying to prevent f4. }
14. f4 O-O-O 15. a3 Ne8 { Also playable is 15...Nd716 b4 f6 and if 17 Nc4 b5. }
16. b4 cxb4
{ Wide open! Safer is 16...f617 b5 (if 17 bxc5 Qxc518 fxe5 Nxe5) 17...Na5 18 Nb3 Nxb3 19 cxb3 Kb8 20 a4=. }
17. Nc4 $2
{ Now Black has time to consolidate. Correct is 17 fxe5! with advantage in all variations:
A] 17...Qxe518 Rxf7 Qxg319 e5! Qe3+ 20 Qxe3 dxe321 Nc4. B]17...bxa3 18 Nc4 Rf8 19 Rxa3 (if 19...b5 20 Qg4+!). c]17...Nxe518 axb4 Kb8 19 Nf3f6 20 Qf2! Nxf3+ (if 20...Qxb4 21 Nxe5 fxe5 22 Qf7 penetrates) 21 Qxf3 Nd6(if 21...Qxb4 22 e5!) 22 Ra5a6 23 Qf4 Rhe8 24 Rd5.
D] 17...Kb8 18 axb4 Nxb4 (18...Nxe5 19 Nf3 transposes to "C") 19 Nc4 Nc6(if 19...Nc7 20 Nd6! Rhf821 Rfb1 Nca6 22 Qd2 Qxe5 23 Nxb7! Kxb7 24 Rxa6! wins) 20 Qf3 Rf821 e6! Qxe6 22 e5! followed byRfb1 and White has a winning attack. }
17... f6 $1 { I had expected 17...bxa3 18 fxe5 transposing to "B" above. } 18.
fxe5 fxe5 19. axb4 Nc7
{ Black wants to secure a Q-side blockade. The b-Pawn won't run away. } 20. Na5
Nb5 $1
{ I already knew I'd been outplayed. Petrosian didn't even consider 20...Nxb4, opening up the lines. }
21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. Rf2 g6
{ On 22...Qxb4? 23 Qg4+ Rd7 24 Rf7 Rhd8 25 Qxg7 regains the Pawn. } 23. h4 Kb7
{ Ordinarily one would expect Petrosian to simplify and simplify in order to reach a winning ending. 23...Rhf8!is strong. }
24. h5 Qxb4
{ Really risky! I was amazed he was allowing so much counterplay. 24...Rhf8is still right. On24...gxh5 25 Qxh5 Rhf8 26 Rf5! }
25. Rf7+ Kb6
{ On 25...Kb8 26 Qf2 Rhf827 c4! Nc7 (if 27...dxc3 e.p.? 28 Raxa7! Rxf7 29 Qb6+) 28 Qf6 with good play. }
26. Qf2 $1 a5 { Not 26...Rhf8? 27 c4! Nc3 28 Raxa7 wins. } 27. c4 Nc3 $2
{ Continuing to underestimate the danger. Safer is 27...Nd6. } 28. Rf1 $2
{ Why didn't I play 28 Qf6! immediately? If then28...Rdf8(28...Rhf8 29 Rf1! Rxf7 30 Qxd8+! Rc731 Rf7 wins) 29 Qxe5 Rxf7 30 Qxh8 Qc5 31 e5 – it's difficult for Black in view of his exposed King and White's passed e-Pawn.
Finally, after 28 Qf6! Qc5 29 Qg7! Ka6 (if 29...a4 30 Ra7! or 29...Ra8 30 Rb7+ Ka6 31 Qc7! Rhc832 Rb5! wins. Or 29...Rdg8 30 Rb7+ Ka6 31 Qc7 Rc8 32 Rxa5+!) 30 Ra7+! Qxa7 31 Rxa5+ Kxa5 32 Qxa7+ Kb4 33 Qb6+ Ka3 34 c5! and White's c-Pawn is dangerous. }
28... a4
{ Still playing with reckless abandon! Safer is 28...Qd6 to prevent Qf6. } 29.
Qf6 Qc5 30. Rxh7 $1
{ Now I decided to start playing for the win. On 30 Qg7 Rdg8! 31 Rb7+ Ka6 32 Qc7 Rc8! 33 Qg7 Rcg8! draws by a "perpetua1 check"on the Queen. }
30... Rdf8 $1 { Forcing what looks like a favorable ending. } 31. Qxg6 Rxf1+
32. Bxf1 Rxh7 33. Qxh7 a3
{ Petrosian had been banking on the speed of this Pawn. } 34. h6 a2 35. Qg8
a1=Q 36. h7 Qd6 $2
{ In time-pressure, Petrosian overlooks 36...Ne2+ 37 Kf2 Nxg3! and White has nothing better than to take a perpetual with 38 Qb8+. }
37. h8=Q Qa7 38. g4 Kc5 $1
{ A good last-ditch try. Curiously, the King will be safer in White's territory where it obtains shelter from the cluster of Pawns. }
39. Qf8 $2
{ Right is 39 Qh2! immediately, preventing Black's King from reaching safety behind the lines. If then 39...Qf6 40 g5. Or 39...Qa1 (39...Qa2? 40 Qxa2 Nxa2 41 Qa8! wins a piece) 40 Qg7 wins the e-Pawn. Finally on 39...Qae7 40 Qa8 renews the attack. }
39... Qae7
{ Forced,to defend against the threat ofQxe5+. Not 39...Qxf8 40 Qxf8+ Kb641 Qb4+ Ka6 (if 41...Kc7 42 Qe7+) 42 Qa3+ Kb7 43 Qxa7+ Kxa7 44 g5 and queens. }
40. Qa8 { I thought this was it! The two Queens are closing in for the kill. }
40... Kb4 $1 41. Qh2 Kb3 $1
{ Slippery as an ee1!
Now White sealed. It's fantastically complicated! The tournament bulletins suggest 42 c5 Qxc5 (if 42...Qg6 43 Be2! Qgg5 44 Bd1+!) 43 Qg8+ Ka3 44 Qc2 Qb4 45 Qa8+ Qa4 (not 45...Na4? 46 Qc1+ Ka2 47 Qg8+ Qb3 48 Qc2+! wins) 46 Qcxa4+ Nxa4 47 Qxc6 "with good winning chances," but after 47...Nc3 it's likely Black can draw. }
42. Qa1
{ After the game a kibitzer asked Petrosian if he thought 42 c5 would have won for White. Petrosian, who must have analyzed it for many hours (not knowing, of course, what my sealed move was), simply replied: "I don't know." }
42... Qa3 { The only move to stop mate on b2. } 43. Qxa3+ Kxa3 44. Qh6
{ Now White has to try to make do with the g-Pawn. } 44... Qf7 $1 45. Kg2
{ On 45 Qxc6 Nd1! } 45... Kb3
{ Not 45...Nd1 46 Qc1+ Nb2 and the Knight is stranded out-of-play. The text renews the threat of ... Nd1. }
46. Qd2 Qh7 $1 47. Kg3
{ A gross oversight, but probably best anyway! White can't win anymore. If 47 g5 Qh4, etc. }
47... Qxe4 $1 48. Qf2 $2
{ Having overlooked Petrosian's last move, I was somewhat shaken! Not 48 dxe4? Nxe4+ 49 Kh4 Nxd2 50 g5 Nxf1 51 g6 d3 wins. Also on 48 Qd1+? (48 Qxc3+? dxc3 49 dxe4 c2 wins) 48...Nxd1 49 dxe4 Ne3 50 Be2 Nxc4 51 g5 Nd6 52 g6 Ne8 and Black again wins.
The right retort, however, is 48 g5! and it's still a hard fight. }
48... Qh1 $1
{ I offered a draw, afraid that he wouldn't accept. Black certainly has the edge now. If 49 g5 e4! Or if 49 Bg2 Wh6.
After having fought so hard for the draw, however, Petrosian was obviously unprepared to readjust his frame of mind and start playing for a win. So... }
1/2-1/2

[Event "Candidates' 1959"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Round "17"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "A very near miss"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "116"]

{ This is one of the four games that Fischer lost toTal who, in winning this tournament, earned the right to meet and trounce Botvinnik for theworld championship.
In jest the whimsical Tal signed Fischer's name, in addition to his own, when asked for an autograph. "Whynot?" he quipped; "I've beaten Bobby so often ...that gives me the right to sign for him!"
A careful reading of Fischer's notes will reveal a clear echo of the strong emotions that engulfed him during this tense encounter. He misses a win in the opening and several draws along the way, demonstrating dramatically how a continuously advantageous position can abruptly be turned into defeat by seemingly insignificant miscalculations. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4
{ We had some excellent results with this. See also games 55 and 58. } 6... e6
7. Bb3
{ I had no better luck against Blackstone, in an exhibition game at Davis, California, 1964, with 7 0-0 Be7 8 Bb3 Qc7 9 f4 b5 10 f5b4 11 fxe6!? (11 Nce2 e5 12 Nf3 Bb7 is bad for White) 11...bxc3 12 exf7+ Kf8 13 Bg5 Ng4! and Black should win. }
7... b5 $1
{ This reaction must be prompt! In our first lap game here Tal played the weaker 7...Be7? 8 f4 0-0 (for 8...b5 see the note to Black's 8th move) 9 Qf3 Qc7 and now 10 f5! (instead of 10 0-0? b5 11 f5 b4! 12 Na4 e5 13 Ne2 Bb7 and Black stands better) 10...e5 (not 10...Nc6 11 Be3 with a bind) 11 Nde2 b5 12 a3 Bb7 13 g4 with a strong attack. }
8. f4 $5
{ Against Olafsson, at Buenos Aires 1960, I continued 8 0-0 Be7 (if 8...b4 9 Na4 Nxe4 10 Re1 Nf6 11 Bg5 with attack) 9 Qf3!? Qc7 (not 9...Bb7? 10 Bxe6!) 10 g3 b4 11 Nce2 g6 12 c3? (12 Bh6! is very strong) 12...Nxe4 13 Qe3 f6 14 cxb4 0-0= with a double-edged position.
R.Byrne-Evans, US Championship 1967 went 8 Wf3, but White got nothing after 8...Bb7 9 Bg5 b4 10 Na4 Nbd7 11 0-0 a5 12 Bxf6 Nxf6 13 Rfe1 Be7. }
8... b4 $1 { Indirectly undermining White's center. } 9. Na4 Nxe4
{ 9...Bb7 is also playable. } 10. O-O g6 $2 { Correct is 10...Bb7. } 11. f5 $1
{ This riposte caught Tal completely unaware. Black's King, trapped in the center, will soon be subject to mayhem. }
11... gxf5 { Not 11...exf5 12 Bd5 Ra713xf5! gxf5 14 Qd4. } 12. Nxf5 $1
{ 118 17/CANDIDATES' TOURNAMENT 1959
Panov, with typical iron curtain "objectivity" commented in the Soviet tournament bulletins: "Almost allgame Fischer played in Ta1 style. But all his trouble was in vain because Tal did not defend in Fischer style - instead he found the one and only saving counterchance!" }
12... Rg8
{ Woozy, Tal stumbles into a dubious defense. Better is 12...d5 (not 12...exf5? 13 Qd5 Ra7 14 Qd4 spearing a Rook) 13 Nh6 Bxh6 14 Bxh6. }
13. Bd5 $1 { A shot! } 13... Ra7
{ "13...exd5 14 Qxd5 Bxf5 15 Rxf5 Ra7 16 Qxe4+ Re7 17 Qxb4 Re2 18 Bg5! Rxg5 19 Rxg5 Qxg5 20 Qxb8+ wins."(PANOv.) }
14. Bxe4 $2
{ Correct is 14 Be3! Nc5 15 Qh5! Rg6 (if15...Nxa4 16 Bxa7 exd5 17 Rael+) 16 Rael! and White's every piece is bearing down on Black's King (KEVITZ). }
14... exf5 15. Bxf5
{ Probably it's better to avoid exchanges with 15 Bd5 or 15 Bf3. } 15... Re7 $1
{ A unique way of shielding the e-file. } 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 17. Bf4 $2
{ The right move is simply 17 c3! (not 17 Qxd6? Rxg2+ 18 Kxg2 Re2+ 19 Kf3 Bxd6 20 Kxe2 Qxc2+ wins) and if 17...Qc6 18 Rf2. }
17... Qc6 $1 18. Qf3 Qxa4 $1
{ Such a surprise that I didn't dare believe my eyes! I had expected 18...Qxf3 19 Rxf3 Re2 20 Rf2 Rxf2 21 Kxf2 and White has a slight edge after a3 because of Black's disconnected Pawns. }
19. Bxd6 Qc6 $1 { Tal finds an inspired defense. } 20. Bxb8 Qb6+
{ White remains a clear Pawn ahead after 20...Qxf3 21 Rxf3 Bg7 22 c3. } 21. Kh1
Qxb8
{ The crowd was shouting and whistling with each move. Later I was informed that many sport fans were in the audience. Maybe some soccer match had been canceled. As a consequence chess was the main attraction that day in Belgrade. }
22. Qc6+
{ Many annotators believed that 22 Rael was the winning move. Ta1 himself confessed he thought Black was lost after that. But 22...Kd8! holds in all lines (not 22...Rg6? 23 Qxf7+ Kd7 24 Rd1+! Rd6 25 Rxd6+ Kxd6 26 Rf6+! wins). I've studied this position for ages, it 120 17/CANDIDATES'TOURNAMENT 1959
seems, and the best I can find is 23 Rd1+ Kc7! (23...Kc8? 24 Qc6+ wins) 24 Qf4+ (if 24 Rd4 Qb7!) 24...Kb7 25 Rd6 Qc7 26 Qxb4+ Kc8 27 Rxa6 Qb7! 28 Qxb7+ Kxb7 29 Raf6 Rg7=. }
22... Rd7 23. Rae1+
{ Black holds after23Rad1 Bd6 24 Rxf7 (if 24 Rf6 Rg6 25 Rdxd6? Qxd6!) 24...Qc7, etc. Andon 23 Rxf7 Qd6. }
23... Be7
{ Finally Tal "develops" his Bishop. Not 23...Kd8 24 Rxf7! Be7 25 Rfxe7 Rxe7 26 Rd1+ wins. }
24. Rxf7 Kxf7 25. Qe6+ Kf8 $1
{ I thought he had to go to g7, whereupon 26 Qxd7 wins easily. } 26. Qxd7
{ Not26Rf1+ Kg7 27 Rf7+ Kh8 and if 28 Qxd7 Rd8 29 Qg4 Qe5 wins. } 26... Qd6 27.
Qb7 Rg6
{ Within a handful of moves the game has changed its complexion. Nowit isWhite who must fight for the draw! }
28. c3 $1 { Black's extra piece means less with each Pawn that's exchanged. }
28... a5 { On 28...bxc3 29 Qc8+ Bd8 30 Qxc3=. } 29. Qc8+
{ On the wrong track. Right is 29 cxb4! Qxb4 (if 29...axb4 30 a3! bxa3 31 bxa3 Qxa3 draws) 30 Qf3+ Kg7 31 Qe2 draws, since Black can't possibly build upa winning K-side attack and his own King is too exposed. }
29... Kg7 30. Qc4 Bd8 31. cxb4 axb4
{ On 31...Qxb4 32 Qe2 White should draw with best play. } 32. g3 $2
{ Creating losing chances. I don't see how Black can make any progress after 32 Qe4. If 32...Bc7 33 Qe7+ Kg8 34 Qe8+ Qf8 35 Qe4, etc. }
32... Qc6+ 33. Re4 Qxc4 34. Rxc4 Rb6 $1
{ I overlooked this. Now Black has winning chances. I had planned on a draw after 34...Be7? 35 a3! dissolving Black's b-Pawn (35...b3 is answered by 36 Rc7 followed byRb7). }
35. Kg2 Kf6 36. Kf3 Ke5 37. Ke3
{ 37 a3 is met, as always, by ..b3. Once White can eliminate Black's b-Pawn it's a theoretical draw. }
37... Bg5+ 38. Ke2 Kd5 39. Kd3 Bf6
{ White might be able to draw this ending, but it's an ugly defensive chore. }
40. Rc2 $2
{ 122 17/CANDIDATES'TOURNAMENT1959
Too passive. I wanted to avoid immobilizing my Q-side Pawns with 40 b3, but it's the best hope now. On 40...Be7 41 Rd4+ preserves drawing chances. }
40... Be5 41. Re2 Rf6 42. Rc2 Rf3+ 43. Ke2 Rf7 44. Kd3 Bd4 $1
{ Little by little Tal inches hisway in. } 45. a3
{ On 45 b3 Rf3+ 46 Ke2 Rf2+ 47 Kd3 Rxc2 48 Kxc2 Ke4 wins. } 45... b3 46. Rc8
{ Equally hopeless is 46 Re2 (or 46 Rd2 Rf3+ 47 Ke2 Rf2+) 46...Rf3+ 47 Kd2 Bxb2, etc. }
46... Bxb2 47. Rd8+ Kc6 48. Rb8 Rf3+ 49. Kc4 Rc3+ 50. Kb4 Kc7 51. Rb5 Ba1 52.
a4 b2 $1
{ If 53 Kxc3 b1=Q+! The discovered-check theme is strangely reminiscent of the finale of game 31. }
0-1

[Event "Mar del Plata 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "18"]
[White "Spassky"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "King's Gambit"]
[Title "Old wine in a new bottle"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "123"]

{ Here is the second of the three losses contained in this volume. As in the previous example, Fischer misses a win by inches. Deviating from his cherished Sicilian, he enables Spassky to employ the King's Gambit – not quite believing he would. Spassky is one of the few Grandmasters who still does so in competition. Fischer promptly wins a Pawn and hangs on to it, but neglects to steer for a highly favorable ending (23...Qg3). Just four moves later, 27 e5! effects his undoing.
Undaunted by this early setback, Fischer scored 12'/ out of his last 13, pulling neck and neck with Spassky for first. }
1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5
{ This loss spurred me to look for a "refutation" of the King's Gambit, which I published in the American Chess Quarterly, Vol. 1 (1961), No. 1. The right move is 3...d6! }
4. h4
{ The only realistic try for any advantage. There is no longer anything "romantic" about the Muzio Gambit, which has been analyzed to a draw after 4 Bc4 g4 5 0-0 (if 5 Ne5 h4+ 6 Kf1 Nc6!) 5...gxf3 6 Qxf3 Qf6, etc. }
4... g4 5. Ne5 Nf6
{ On 5...h5 6 Bc4 Rh7 7 d4 d6 8 Nd3 f3 9 gxf3 Be7 10 Be3 Bxh4+ 11 Kd2 Bg5 12 f4 Bh6 13 Nc3 White has more than enough compensation for the Pawn. This is vintage analysis. 124 18 / MAR DEL PLATA 1960 }
6. d4
{ On 6 Bc4 d5 7 exd5 Bg7 (the old 7...Bd6 is also adequate) is the modern panacea. And on 6 Nxg4 Nxe4 7 d3 Ng3 8 Bxf4 Nxh1 9 Qe2+ (9 Bg5 Be7 10 Qe2 h5 11 Qe5 f6! 12 Nxf6+ Kf7 wins − Steinitz) 9...Qe7 10 Nf6+ Kd8 11 Bxc7+ Kxc7 12 Nd5+ Kd8 13 Nxe7 Bxe7 and Black should win. Morphy-Anderssen, Paris 1858. }
6... d6 7. Nd3 Nxe4 8. Bxf4 Bg7 9. Nc3 $2
{ After this White has no compensation for the Pawn. Better is 9 c3 Qe7 10 Qe2 Bf5. At least White keeps a grip on his f4 – for what that's worth. }
9... Nxc3 10. bxc3 c5
{ Immediately nibbling at White's center. Keres gives 10...0-0 first. } 11. Be2
{ On 11 Qe2+ Be6 (12 d5? Bxc3+). } 11... cxd4 12. O-O Nc6
{ It doesn't pay to be greedy with 12...h5. After 13 Bg5 f6 14 Bc1 followed by Nf4 Black's K-side is all messed up. }
13. Bxg4 O-O 14. Bxc8 Rxc8 15. Qg4 f5
{ Winning a second Pawn, but creating a K-side weakness. Simply 15...Kh8 is stronger. }
16. Qg3 dxc3 17. Rae1
{ Black snatches the initiative after 17 Bxd6 Rf6 18 Bf4 Rg6. } 17... Kh8
{ Also good is 17...Qd7 18 Bxd6 Rfe8 and if 19 Nc5 Qf7 (KMOCH). } 18. Kh1 $2
{ More accurate is 18 Bxd6 Rf6 (if 18...Rg8 19 Ne5!) 19 Be5 Nxe5 20 Nxe5 with a little play left for White. }
18... Rg8 { On 18...d5 19 Nc5 creates problems. } 19. Bxd6 Bf8 $1
{ The key! On 19...Bd4 20 Qh2 Rg4 21 Be5+! (to prevent Black from doubling Rooks on the g-file), 21...Kg8 (if 21...Bxe5 22 Nxe5 Rxh4? 23 Nf7+) 22 Bg3 holds. }
20. Be5+ Nxe5 21. Qxe5+ Rg7 $1 { Now White's h-Pawn must fall. } 22. Rxf5
{ What else? On 22 Qxf5 (not 22 Rf4? Bd6 or 22 Qf4? Rg4) 22...Qxh4+ 23 Kg1 Qg4 forces a favorable exchange of Queens (if 24 Qf2 Bd6 produces a strong attack). }
22... Qxh4+ 23. Kg1 Qg4 $2
{ Drifting. Not realizing the danger, I thought Black could whip up an attack along the g-file. But correct is 23...Qg3! 24 Qxg3 (if 24 Qe2 Bd6) 24...Rxg3 (threatening 25...Rxd3 followed by ...c2) and White, a Pawn down, has a tough ending to hold – as Spassky pointed out in our post-mortem analysis. }
24. Rf2 Be7 { Threatening ... Bh4. } 25. Re4 Qg5
{ I started to feel uncomfortable, but little did I imagine that Black's game would collapse in four short moves! I should have taken a draw by repetition with 25...Qd1+ 26 Rel Qg4 27 Re4 Qd1+, etc. And if 28 Kh2 Rc6 29 Qb8+ Rg8 30 Qe5+ Rg7. }
26. Qd4 $1 { This powerful centralization completely paralyzes Black. } 26...
Rf8 $2
{ Overlooking White's real threat. I was worried about Ne5, not realizing it could be met successfully with ... Bc5. The right defense is 26...Bf8! 27 Qxa7 (if 27 Ne5 Bc5 28 Nf7+ Kg8 29 Nxg5 Bxd4 30 Rxd4 Rxg5) 27...Bd6=. }
27. Re5 $1
{ I had reckoned on 27 Ne5? Rxf2 28 Qxf2 Bc5! 29 Qxc5 Qxg2 mate.
Incredibly, Black must lose a piece. While trying to figure out what was going on in Spassky's head, I blundered and lost the game! }
27... Rd8
{ Trying to squirm out! The Queen has no shelter. On 27...Qg6 28 Rxe7 wins. Or 27...Qh4 28 Rxf8+. Or 27...Bf6 28 Qd6! }
28. Qe4 Qh4
{ I knew I was losing a piece, but just couldn't believe it. I had to play one more move to see if it was really true! }
29. Rf4 { On 29...Qg3 30 Rxe7 is most efficient. } 1-0

[Event "Reykjavik 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "19"]
[White "Gudmundsson"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Gruenfeld Defense"]
[Title "A long voyage home"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "128"]

{ Illustrating, rather subtly, how a weaker player may be lured to his own destruction, Fischer entices his opponent to abandon his passive though solid attempts to settle for a draw. Wrongly convinced that he holds an advantage, Gudmundsson, with 16 e4, gives Fischer the opportunity to launch a long, unclear sacrificia1 combination. Gudmundsson makes matters unexpectedly easy with 24 Rb1, but the analysis accompanying the text shows the sacrifice to be sound in all variations. Fischer's performance from here on is typical in its clarity and forcefulness. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 { Solid but passive. } 2... d5 3. e3
{ Voluntarily locking in the Bishop lacks energy and reduces White's options. }
3... g6 4. c4 { 4 c3 would lead to the Colle System. } 4... Bg7 5. Nc3 O-O 6.
Qb3
{ After 6 Be2 c5!it's difficult for White to equalize: A]7 0-0?cxd4 8 Nxd4 Nc6 9 cxd5 Nxd5 10 Nxd5 Qxd5 11 Bf3 Qc4 12 Nxc6 bxc6 and Black stands better. Aaron-Gligorich, Stockholm1962.
B]7 cxd5 Nxd5 8 Qb3 Nxc3 9 bxc3 Qc710 0-0 b611 a4 Nc6 again Black's better.Goglidze-Botvinnik, Moscow1935.
c] 7 dxc5 Qa5 8 cxd5 (if80-0 dxc4 9 Bxc4 Qxc5) 8...Nxd5 9 Qxd5 Bxc3+ 10 Bd2 (after 10 Kfl Bg7 11 Bd2 Qc7 Black regains his Pawn at will, with a strong attack) 10...Rd8! 11 Bxc3 Qxc3+ 12 bxc3 Rxd5 with the superior ending (if 13 Rd1 Rxc5 14 Rd8+ Kg7 15 0-0Nc616 Re8b6). }
6... e6
{ Another good build-up is 6...c6 followed by ...e6, ...b6, ... Bb7, ...Nbd7, ...c5, etc. }
7. Be2 Nc6 8. Qc2
{ Probably best (Black threatened ..Na5 winning the two Bishops). As Evans pointed out in Trophy Chess (in an analogous position): "8 cxd5 exd5 permits Black to solve the problem of his QB. He has the semiopen e-file and good squares for his pieces. It is now White who must fight for equality!" }
8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 e5 $1 10. dxe5
{ Also good is 10 Nxe5 (if 10 d5? Na5) 10...Nxe511 dxe5 Ng412 e6! (not 12 f4? Nxe5! 13 fxe5? Qh4+ 14 g3 Qxc4, etc.) 12...Bxe6 13 Bxe6 fxe6140-0=. }
10... Ng4 11. O-O
{ Stronger is 11 e6! Bxe6 12 Bxe6 fxe6 13 0-0 (13 Qe4? Nce5! 14 Nxe5? Nxf2! 15 Qc4 Bxe5 16 Qxe6+ Rf7 17 Qxe5 Nd3+ wins), and Black seems to have nothing better than a draw by perpetual after 13...Rxf3 14 gxf3 Qh4 15 fxg4 Qxg4+16 Kh1 Qf3+, etc. }
11... Ncxe5 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. Be2 c6 { The chances are now equal. } 14. f4
{ Apparently stronger is 14 e4 but after 14...Qh4! 15 h3 (if 15 f4 Ng4) 15...g5! 16 f4 (or 16 Nd1 f517 f4 Ng6 18 fxg5f4!) 16...gxf4 17 Bxf4 (on 17 Rxf4 Qg3) 17...Kh8 gives Black good prospects along the ventilatedg-file. }
14... Ng4 $1 15. h3 Bf5 $1
{ White was doubtlessly expecting15...Nf6 16 e4 with an ideal center. } 16. e4
$2
{ Provoking a powerful combination. White should abandon his hopes in the center and settle for 16 Qb3 Nf6 17 Qxb7 Ne4! 18 Qxc6 Rc8 19 Qa6 Nxc3 20 bxc3 Bxc3 21 Ba3 Bxa1 22 Bxf8 Bd4! 23 exd4 Qxd4+ 24 Kh1 Kxf8. Black is better, but White has excellent drawing chances. }
16... Qd4+ 17. Kh1 Nf2+ 18. Rxf2 { All forced. Not 18 Kh2 Nxe4. } 18... Qxf2
19. exf5 Bxc3 $1
{ This clean-cut line reduces White's options. Inexact would be 19...Rfe8 20 Ne4! Qe1+ 21 Kh2 gxf5 (if 21...Bd4 22 Be3! Qxa1 23 Bxd4) 22 Ng3 and if 22...Bd4? 23 Be3! }
20. bxc3 Rae8 21. Bd3 Re1+ 22. Kh2 Qg1+ 23. Kg3 Rfe8
{ Just as complicated is 23...gxf5 24 Bxf5 Rfe8 25 Bxh7+ Kg7 26 Qf5, etc. } 24.
Rb1 $2
{ Also bad is 24 Qf2 R8e3+! 25 Bxe3 Rxe3+ 26 Qxe3 Qxe3+. The toughest defense is 24 fxg6! hxg6 and now: A] 25 Bxg6? R1e2! (not 25...R8e2 26 Bxf7+ Kh82715xgZ+ 28 Kh4 Qf2+ 29 Kh5 Qf3+ 30 Kh6! and White wins!) 26 Bxf7+ Kh8 27 Qf5 Rxg2+ 28 Kh4 Qe1+ 29 Kh5 Kg7! (threatening 30...Rh8+) 30 Bxe8 Qxe8+ 31 Kh4 Qd8+ 32 Qg5+ Rxg5 33 fxg5 Qd1 with an easy win.
B]25 Rb1 R8e3+! 26 Bxe3 (if 26 Kg4 Qh2 27 Bxe3 leads to the same) 26...Rxe3+ 27 Kg4 (not 27 Kh4? Qh2 28 Qf2 Rxh3+!) 27...Qh2 28 Qf2(if 28 Bxg6 Qg3+ 29 Kh5 Qxf4! 30 Bf5 Rg3! 31 Bg4 Kg7 32 Qc1 [if 32 Kh4 Rxg4+ 33 hxg4 Qh2+ mates] 32...Re3 wins), 28...Rxd3 (if 28...Rxh3!? 29 Bxg6! fxg6 30 Rh1! holds) 29 Rb2! (29 Rxb7 loses to 29...Rxh3! 30 Rb8+ Kh7 31 f5 Rh6!) 29...Rxc3 30 Rd2 and White has drawing chances even though a Pawn behind. }
24... gxf5
{ Threatening either .... Kh8 or .. R8e6 with a devastating check to follow on the g-file. }
25. Bd2
{ No better is 25 Bxf5 R1e2 26 Bxh7+ Kh8 27 Qf5 Rxg2+ 28 Kh4 Rg7 (among others) wins. }
25... Rxb1 26. Qxb1 Qxb1 27. Bxb1 Re2
{ This is what Gudmundsson overlooked. If now 28 Bc1 Re1 picks off one of the Bishops. So... }
0-1

[Event "Leipzig Olympic 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "20"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Euwe"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[Title "Theoretical scuffle"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "133"]

{ Former world champion, Dr. Max Euwe had for decades been considered one of the world's leading authorities on opening theory. His Chess Archives ranks with Modern Chess Openings as an indispensable source of reference. It is no small wonder, then, when he selects a risky but playable variation. Fischer, however, just a little better versed in its intricacies, introduces a nuance on move 15 which ruffles his opponent no end.
Fischer's method of dispatching his veteran adversary – on home grounds, as it were – is deceptively simple. After a mere eighteen moves the opening has become an ending and the duel is over. Euwe fights on, but to no avail. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4
{ At that time I was convinced the Panov-Botvinnik attack was the sharpest. }
4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6
{ In our game at Buenos Aires 1960, Ivkov played 5...e6 6 Nf3 Be7 7 c5 0-0 8 Bd3 b69 b4 bxc5 (better is 9...a5 10 Na4 Nfd7!) 10 bxc5 Nc611 0-0 Bd7 12 h3 Ne8 13 Bf4 with a bind. }
6. Nf3
{ On Botvinnik's old 6 Bg5 e6! (6...dxc4? 7 d5 Ne5 8 Qd4 is strong) 7 cxd5 exd5 8 Bxf6 xf69 Nxd5 Qd8 10 Nc3 (if 10 Bc4 Be6 11 e2? b5!) 10...xd4 11 Qxd4 Nxd4 12 0-0-0c513 a4 c6. }
6... Bg4 $5 { Risky but playable. Safer is 6...e6. } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Qb3 Bxf3
9. gxf3 e6
{ On 9...Ndb4!? 10 Be3 Nxd4 11 Bxd4 Qxd4 12 Bb5+ Nc6 13 0-0 White gets a strong attack. (Evans-Henin, Las Vegas Open 1965.) }
10. Qxb7 Nxd4 11. Bb5+ Nxb5 12. Qc6+ Ke7 13. Qxb5 Nxc3
{ An alternative is 13...Qd7 14 Nxd5+ exd5 (14...Qxd5 15 Qxd5 exd5 16 0-0 gives White good play against Black's isolated d-Pawn and a-Pawn) 15 Qb4+ (15 Qe2+ Kf6 16 h4 wins, according to Evans) 15...Ke8 16 Qd4 with a clear advantage. }
14. bxc3 Qd7 { After 14...Qd5 15 Qxd5 exd5 16 Rb1 gives White a slight edge. }
15. Rb1 $1
{ The innovation. Months before the game I had shown this line to Benko and he suggested this innocent-looking move. Upon looking deeper I found that, horrible as White's Pawn structure may be, Black can't exploit it because he'll be unable to develop his K-side normally. It's the little quirks like this that could make life difficult for a chess machine. }
15... Rd8 $2
{ Also difficult is 15...Qxb5 16 Rxb5 Kd6! 17 Rb7 f6 18 Ke2 Kc6 19 Rf7 a5 20 Be3 with an enduring pull. }
16. Be3 Qxb5 17. Rxb5 Rd7 18. Ke2
{ 18 Ra5 is unnecessary. White can win the a-Pawn at his leisure. } 18... f6
19. Rd1 $1 { To swap Black's only active piece. } 19... Rxd1 20. Kxd1 Kd7 21.
Rb8 $1
{ 21 Bxa7 Bd622 Rb7+ Kc6 23 Rxg7 Bxh2 would be hard to win. Now the threat is22Bc5. }
21... Kc6 22. Bxa7 g5 { Striving to untangle the K-side. } 23. a4 Bg7 24. Rb6+
Kd5 25. Rb7 Bf8 26. Rb8
{ Still trying to decide how to squeeze the most out of it. } 26... Bg7 27.
Rb5+ Kc6 28. Rb6+ Kd5 29. a5 f5 30. Bb8 $1 Rc8 31. a6 Rxc3 32. Rb5+ Kc4
{ After the game Euwe showed me a cute trap he might have played for - and almost fainted when I fell into it! The line arises after 32...Kc6 33 Ra5 Bd4 and he asked, "What do you do now?"I looked a few seconds and played 34 Be5? whereupon he uncorked 34...Rc5! which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. }
33. Rb7 Bd4 34. Rc7+ Kd3 35. Rxc3+ Kxc3 36. Be5 { He can't stop the a-Pawn. }
1-0

[Event "Leipzig Olympic 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "21"]
[White "Letelier"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "A Queen for the King"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "137"]

{ Letelier transgresses opening principles by neglecting his development in order to win material. Pugnaciously, he mixes it up with the unorthodox 5 e5 and proceeds to snatch Pawns. But his judgment proves to be unwise.
Striking from behind the lines, Fischer causes the overextended White center to crumble. Letelier, busily engaged on a material hunt, neglects to safeguard his rear, leaving his King marooned in the center. Fischer rapidly encircles the hapless monarch and, with a startling Queen sacrifice, induces abdication. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. e5
{ Weak. Letelier snapped at the chance to take me "out of the book," but this premature advance leaves White with all the responsibility of holding his overextended center Pawns. }
5... Ne8 6. f4 d6
{ Weaker is 6...c5 7 dxc5 Qa5 8 Be3 f6? 9 Nf3 fxe5 10 fxe5 Nc6 11 Be2 Nc7 12 0-0 Ne6 13 Nd5 Qd8 14 Qd2 etc. (Koralev-Roshal, USSR 1962.) }
7. Be3
{ 7 Nf3 is safer, though White can no longer lay claim to any kind of initiative. My game with Schoene in the US Junior Championship 1957 continued: 7...dxe5 8 fxe5 (better is 8 dxe5) 8...Bg4 9 Be2 c5 10 Bf4 cxd4 11 Qxd4 Nc6 12 Wxd8 Rxd8 soon winning a Pawn. }
7... c5 $1
{ "Now the artificially constructed White center begins to crumble." (LOMBARDY.) }
8. dxc5 Nc6
{ "Black rapidly develops his pieces while White nurtures his own dreams with ill-gottengains."(LOMBARDY.) }
9. cxd6
{ White tries to compensate for his lack of development by continuing to snatch material. Instead he should be seeking to return the Pawn in the least damaging way (by keeping the lines closed). Better is 9 Nf3 Bg4 10 Be2. }
9... exd6 10. Ne4
{ "More realistic would have been 10 Nf3."(LOMBARDY.)I intended 10...Bg4. After the text White no longer has time to castle. }
10... Bf5 $1 11. Ng3 $2
{ A better chance is 11 Nxd6 Nxd6 12 Qxd6 Qxd6 13 exd6 Bxb2 14 Rd1 Nb4! (threatening . Bc2) 15 Kf2 (if 15 Ne2 Bc2 16 Rd2 Nd3+) 15...Nxa2 16 Ne2 (if 16 Rd2? Nc3!) 16...a5. Black is better but White may have drawing resources. }
11... Be6
{ I also considered 11...Qc7 12 Nxf5 gxf5. White's center must collapse. } 12.
Nf3
{ By now White is more than willing to return the Pawn in order to catch up in development. }
12... Qc7
{ Also playable is 12...dxe5 13 Qxd8 Rxd8 14 Bc5 exf4. But I wanted to fracture him in the middle game. }
13. Qb1
{ Continuing his "attack". On 13 Be2 dxe514 Bc5 Qa5+ 15 b4 Nxb4 16 Bxf8 Kxf8!17 0-0 exf4 18 Ne4 Bf5 is overwhelming. And on 13 Qc2 (in order to prepare 0-0-0) 13...dxe514 f5 gxf5 15 Nxf5 Nb4 16 Qb3 (if 16 Qb1 Bxf5 17 Qxf5 Nd6 18 Qb1 Nxc4 19 Ng5 f5 20 Ne6 Qc6 21 Bxc4 Qxc422 Nxf8 Rxf8 with a winning attack) 16...Bxf5 17 Qxb4 Nf6! is strong. If 18 Qc5 Qb8 (threatening ... Ng4). }
13... dxe5 14. f5 e4 $1
{ "An unexpected shot that sends White spinning."(LOMBARDY.) } 15. fxe6
{ On 15 Qxe4 gxf5! 16 Nxf5?(if 16 Qh4 Bxb2) 16...Qa5+ wins a piece. } 15...
exf3 16. gxf3 f5 $1
{ "The Pawn on e6 can be ignored in favor of the attack." (LOMBARDY.) The threat is ...f4. }
17. f4 Nf6 18. Be2 Rfe8 19. Kf2 Rxe6
{ Finally Black regains the Pawn with interest. } 20. Re1 Rae8 21. Bf3
{ "Anyone interested in sui-mate (helpmate) problems?" (LOMBARDY.) } 21... Rxe3
$1 22. Rxe3 Rxe3 23. Kxe3 Qxf4+ $1
{ On 24 Kxf4 Bh6 mate! Or 24 Kf2 Ng4+ 25 Kg2 Ne3+ 26 Kf2 Nd4 27 Qh1 Ng4+28 Kfl Nxf3 with a winning attack. }
0-1

[Event "Leipzig Olympic 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "22"]
[White "Szabo"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "Bad judgment"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "141"]

{ Once a contender for the title, Szabo's performances nowadays are spotty and unpredictable. Here, with breathtaking bluntness, he attempts to wipe Fischer from the board. In the process he leaves himself wide open on the dark squares. Fischer promptly invades on the Q-side, by means of a curious Queen maneuver, while Szabo, preoccupied with his own K-side attack, fails to realize the danger in time. 21...Re3! is the blow that ends all effective resistance. Rather than fight on against hopeless odds, Szabo resigns three moves later. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. Bg5
{ For 5 e5 see game 21. For 5 Nf3 see games 7, 28 and 30. } 5... d6
{ After 5...h6 6 Be3! allows White to set up a Saemisch formation (6...d6 7 f3) where the inevitable Qd2 will be more effective than usual. }
6. Qd2
{ Better is simply 6 Be2 c5 7 d5 e6 8 Nf3 h6 9 Bh4 exd510 cxd5 g5 11 Bg3 Nh5 (not 11...b5? 12 Nd2!) with a double-edged struggle. Larsen-Fischer, Santa Monica 1966. }
6... c5 $1 7. d5
{ On 7 Nf3(7 dxc5 dxc5 8 Qxd8 Rxd89 e5 Ng4 10 f4 f6 is better for Black) 7...cxd4 8 Nxd4 Nc6=. }
7... e6 8. Bd3
{ 8 dxe6 Bxe6 9 Nf3 Nc610 Be2 Bg4! 11 0-0 Re8 12 Qf4 Bxf3 13 Bxf3 Nd4 14 Rad1 Re5 leads to equality. (Evans-Gligorich, Dallas 1957.) }
8... exd5 { White has no advantageous way to recapture. } 9. Nxd5
{ A]9 exd5 Nbd710 f4 (to stop ...Ne5) 10...h6 11 Bh4 Re8+ 12 Nge2 Ne4! 13 Bxd8 Nxd2 14 Bc7 Bxc3 15 bxc3 Ne4 16 Bxe4 Rxe4 17 Bxd6 Nb6 18 Kf2 Bg4! with a promising ending.
B]9 cxd5a6! 10 f4 (if 10 a4 Qa5 threatening ...b5) 10...h6 11 Bh4 Nxe4! 12 Bxd8 Nxd2 13 Bc7 Bxc3 14 bxc3 Ne4! 15 Bxe4 Re8 etc.
In this opening variation Black must play sharply. White has a space advantage, but he temporarily lags in development. }
9... Be6 10. Ne2 Bxd5
{ Releasing the tension and, by forcing White to recapture with a Pawn, eliminating the backward d-Pawn on an open file. }
11. exd5 { On 11 cxd5 c4! 12 Bc2 Nbd7130-0Nc5 14 Nc3 b5! } 11... Nbd7 12. O-O
{ Not 12 f4 Qe8! 13 Qc2? (to stop .. Ne4) 13...Ng4! invading on the weak dark squares (notably e3). }
12... Ne5 13. f4 $2
{ After 13 Nc3 the game is even. This lemon, weakening White's e3 and e4, came as a pleasant surprise. Szabo misjudges White's attacking prospects. }
13... Nxd3 14. Qxd3 h6 15. Bh4 Re8 16. Rae1
{ 16 Nc3 is the best of a bad choice, though 16...Qb6! (threatening ... Ng4) creates problems. If 17 h3 Nh5! increases the pressure on White's game. }
16... Qb6 $1 17. Bxf6 { On 17 b3 Ne4! gives Black a nice bind. } 17... Bxf6 18.
f5 g5 19. b3 Qa5 $1 { A nettlesome maneuver! } 20. Rc1 $2
{ 20 a4 loses to 20...Bd4+ 21 Kh1 (not 21 Nxd4? xe1) 21...Re3 22 d1ae8 and the pin on the e-file is decisive.
The best defense is 20 Qb1! }
20... Qxa2 21. Rc2 Re3 $1 22. Qxe3 Qxc2 23. Kh1 a5 24. h4 a4
{ White's Pawns fall like ripe apples. I'll never forget the disgusted look on Szabo's face as he took his King and just sort of shoved it gently to the center of the board, indicating his intention to resign. }
0-1

[Event "Leipzig Olympic 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "23"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Title "No holds barred"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "145"]

{ Their first encounter after Tal became world champion proves to be an old-fashioned slugfest. Typically aggressive, Fischer rapidly achieves a winning bind, but unwisely permits Tal to touch off "a dazzling array of fireworks" with 14...Nxe5! The struggle seesaws for seven moves before settling in a perpetual check.
The quality of this confrontation left little doubt that, in time, Fischer would yet take Tal's measure. He did just that less than a year later, after Tal had lost his return match with Botvinnik (Game 32). Reading Tal's palm, Fischer predicted: "The next world champion will be …. Bobby Fischer!" }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Ba5
{ A dubious alternative to 5...Bxc3+ (see game 24). } 6. b4 $1
{ Alekhine's recommendation. } 6... cxd4
{ 6...cxb4 7 Nb5 yields a potent attack. } 7. Qg4 Ne7
{ Or 7...Kf8 8 bxa5 dxc3 9 a4! followed by 10 Ba3+ is strong. (LILIENTHAL and ZAGORYANSKY) }
8. bxa5 { Also good is 8 Nb5. } 8... dxc3 9. Qxg7 Rg8 10. Qxh7 Nbc6
{ On 10...Nd7 11 Nf3 Qc7 12 Bb5 a613 Bxd7+ Bxd7 14 0-0 d4!? (Archives) 15 Nxd4 Qxe5 16 Qd3 is better for White. }
11. Nf3
{ 11 f4 bolsters the center but shuts in the QB and weakens the dark squares. }
11... Qc7
{ On 11...Qxa5 12 Ng5! Rf8 13 f4 (followed by the advance of the h-Pawn) ties Black up. }
12. Bb5 $1
{ Harmoniously pursuing development without losing time. Also playable is 12 Bf4 Bd7 13 Be2 0-0-0 14 Qd3 Qxa5 15 0-0 Rg4 16 Bg3. (Unzicker-Duckstein,Zurich 1959.) }
12... Bd7
{ Not 12...Rxg2 13 Kfl! Rg8 14 Rg1! Rxg1+ 15 Kxg1 and Black's King remains hemmed in the center while White merely marches his h-Pawn to victory. }
13. O-O
{ Unsound is 13 Bxc6? Bxc6 14 0-0 d4! 15 Ng5 Qxe5 16 Qxf7+ Kd7 with advantage. }
13... O-O-O
{ After the game Petrosian suggested 13...Nxe5 but 14 Nxe5 Qxe5 15 Bxd7+ Kxd7 16 Qd3! keeps White on top (if 16...Qe4? 17 Qxe4 dxe4 18 f3! wins a Pawn). }
14. Bg5 $2
{ I simply underestimated the force of Tal's reply. Correct is 14 Bxc6! Bxc6 (if 14...Qxc6 15 Bg5 d4 16 h4! or 14...Nxc6 15 Rel followed by Bg5 and h4 with a decisive bind) 15 Qxf7 d4 (unsound is 15...Rxg2+!? 16 Kxg2 d4 17 Kg1 Rg8+ 18 Ng5) 16 Qxe6+ Bd7 (16...Kb8 17 Ng5 is hopeless) 17 Qxe7 Rxg2+ 18 Kxg2 Bh3+ 19 Kxh3 Qxe7 20 Bg5 and White soon consolidates to victory. }
14... Nxe5 $1
{ Setting off a dazzling array of fireworks! I thought Tal was merely trying to confuse the issue. }
15. Nxe5
{ Originally I'd intended 15 Bxd7+ but saw that after 15...Rxd7 16 Nxe5 (if 16 Bxe7 Nxf3+ 17 Kh1 Qxh2+!) 16...Qxe5 17 Bxe7 Rh8! Black regains his piece with greater activity: e.g., 18 RaelRxh7 19 Rxe5 Rxe7 and the compact center Pawns far outweigh White's passed h-Pawn.
Not playable is 15 Bxe7? Nxf3+ 16 Kh1 Rh8! }
15... Bxb5
{ Playing for the win. After 15...Qxe5 16 Bxe7 Rh8 17 Rfe1 (17 Rae1? loses to 17...Qb8!) 17...Qxe1+ 18 Rxe1 Rxh7 19 Bxd8 Kxd8(weak is 19...Bxb5 20 Bf6!) 20 Bxd7 Kxd7 21 Re3! bails White out. }
16. Nxf7
{ White could still have kept some tension with 16 Bxe7 Qxe7 (if 16...Qxe5? 17 Rfe1) 17 Rfe1, etc. }
16... Bxf1 $1 { 16...Rdf8 17 Rfb1 Bc6 18 Nd6+! Qxd6 19 Qxe7 is about equal. }
17. Nxd8 Rxg5 18. Nxe6 Rxg2+ $1 19. Kh1 $1
{ The saving move. Not 19 Kxf1? Rxh2! 20 Qf7 (if2 0 Nxc7 Rxh7 wins a piece) 20...Rh1+! produces a winning attack from nowhere! }
19... Qe5
{ On 19...Qc4 20 Qxe7 Rg8 21 Nf4! holds nicely (if 21...Qxf4? 22 Qe6+ Kc7 23 Qxg8). }
20. Rxf1 Qxe6
{ On 20...Rg6 21 Qxe7 Rxe6 22 f8+ Re8 23 Qf3 is in White's favor. } 21. Kxg2
Qg4+ { Black has a perpetual check. } 1/2-1/2

[Event "West Berlin 1960"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "24"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Darga"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Title "Asking for trouble"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "150"]

{ The Winawer Variation has given Fischer consistent trouble. He has had the utmost difficulty cracking Black's tortoise-like shell; even his successes are unconvincing. Maintainingthe same line of attack year after year has provided his opponents with ample opportunity to sharpen their defenses.
Darga's 12...f6 obliges Fischer, in order to get something out of the opening, to speculate on a Pawn sacrifice (13 Ba3!?). Though Darga's reaction may not be ideal, he proceeds sensibly and equalizes. Underestimating Fischer's chances, however, he falls prey to a scintillating mid-game attack.And so, once again, by virtue of his native ability, Fischer avoids the retribution that is the usual price for failing to secure an advantage in the opening. }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ { For 5...Ba5 see game 23. }
6. bxc3 Ne7 7. a4
{ Smyslov's favorite, largely responsible for Botvinnik's giving up the Winawer Variation. Sharper is 7 Qg4. I felt that Black's carapace could be cracked only by positional means, but my results have been somewhat disheartening. }
7... Qc7
{ More usual is 7...bc6 8 f3 Qa5 9 d2 (on 9 Bd2 Bd7 10 Be2 c4 11 h4!? f6 12 h5 fxe5 13 h6 gxh6 14 Nxe5 Nxe5 15 dxe5 0-0-0 16 Rxh6 Ng6 Black's better, Fischer-Padevsky, Varna 1962) 9...Bd7 10 Bd3 and now Black has two main continuations:
A] 10...c4 11 Be2 f6 12 Ba3 0-0-0 (if 12...Ng6 13 0-0 0-0-0 14 d6 White keeps the edge. Fischer-Uhlmann, Buenos Aires 1960) 13 0-0 Nf5 14 Rfe1 Be8 15 g4!? Nfe7 16 Bfl Bd7= (Fischer-Weinstein, US Championship1960-1.)
B] 10...f6! 11 0-0 fxe5 12 Nxe5 (no better is 12 dxe5 as Smyslov tried against Uhlmann at Havana 1964) 12...Nxe5 13 dxe5 0-0 14 c4 Qxd2 15 Bxd2 Bc6= (Fischer-Uhlmann, Stockholm 1962.)
I may yet be forced to admit that the Winawer is sound. But I doubt it! The defense is anti-positional and weakens the K-side. }
8. Nf3 b6
{ The idea is to eliminate the bad Bishop with ... Ba6. An alternative is 8...Bd7 9 Bd3 Nbc6. }
9. Bb5+ $1 Bd7
{ More radical is 9...Kf8!? 10 Bd3 Ba6. On 9...Nec6 (after 9...Nbc6 Black can no longer enforce ... Ba6) 10 0-0 Ba6 11 Ng5 h6 12 Nh3 is in White's favor. }
10. Bd3
{ After 10 0-0 Bxb5 11 axb5 a5 12 Ng5h6 13 Nh3 Nd7 14 Nf4 0-0! (Ivkov-R.Byrne, Sousse1967) Black has no problems. }
10... Nbc6
{ Black has gained a tempo, but whether his Pawn belongs on b6 is moot. } 11.
O-O c4 { Not 11...0-0? 12 Bxh7+! Kxh7 13 Ng5+, etc. } 12. Be2 f6 13. Ba3 $5
{ Keeping tension in the center at the cost of a Pawn. 13 Re1 is solid but less aggressive. I tried this same Pawn sac against Mednis in the 1962-3 US Championship with the slight but significant difference that Black's b-pawn was still on b7. The sac may well have been unsound in thatgame. }
13... fxe5
{ Mednis declined and castled, but after 14 Re1! got a cramped game (14...fxe5 is answered by 15 Nxe5! keeping the e-file open). }
14. dxe5
{ White doesn't have anything to show after 14 Nxe5 Nxe5 15 dxe5 Qxe5 16 Re1 Qxc3 17 Bh5+ g618 Bg4(if 18 Bxe7 Kxe7 19 Qxd5? Qxa1! wins) 18...Qf6, etc. }
14... Nxe5
{ After 14...0-0 15 Nd4!is followed by f4 and White has not been inveigled into misplacinghis Rook on e1. }
15. Re1 { The threat is 16 Nxe5 Qxe5 17 Bh5+. } 15... N7c6
{ Black has a seeming multiplicity of defenses: A] 15...N5c6 16 Ng5! 0-0! (if 16...h6 17 Bh5+ g6 18 Nxe6 Bxe6 19 xe6 gxh5 20 xd5!d8 21 Qxh5+ Kd7 22 Rae1 regains the piece) 17 Bg4 Qf4! (if 17...e5 18 Be6+ Kh8 19 Bxd5!) 18 Bxe6+! (if 18 619118...19220 Rf5! 21 Re2! Qh4 22 Nd4! Rh5 (not 22...Nxd4? 23 Bxe7) 23 Nf3 Qf6 24 e1e8 25 Re6 Qf7 26 Qe2! Rh627 Re3 followed by Re1 and Black's crushed.
B] 15...N5g6 16 h4! (on 16 Ng5 0-0! 17 Bg4 Qf4 holds) 16...Nc6 17 Ng5 and it's difficult for Black's King to escape the crisscross; if 17...0-0-0? 18 Nf7. Or 17...h6? 18 Nxe6! Bxe6 19 Bg4. Or on 17...Nf4 18 Bg4 continues the pressure.
C] 15...N7g6 16 Nxe5 Nxe5 transposes to the game. }
16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. f4 Nc6
{ On 17...Nf7 (17...Ng6? 18 f5) 18 Bh5 g6 19 f5! 0-0-0 20 fxe6 Bxe6 21xe6 gxh5 22xh5 White stands much better. }
18. Bg4
{ Better is the finesse 18 Bh5+! g6 (18...Kd8 19 f5 is unhealthy) 19 Bg4 0-0-0 20 Bxe6 Bxe6 21 Rxe6 Rd7 22 Qf3 Nd8 23 Rf6! Re8 24 Rd1 etc. }
18... O-O-O 19. Bxe6 Bxe6 { 154 } 20. Rxe6 Rd7 21. f5
{ To continue with f6 which gets a grip on the f-file. On 21 Qf3 Nd8 22 Re5 Qc6=. }
21... Nd8 $1 { Driving the Rook from its command outpost on the sixth rank. }
22. Re3 Qf4 { Darga is defending with vigor! } 23. Rf3 Qe4 24. a5 $1
{ Commencing operations against the castled King while Black's Queen is temporarily cut off from the Q-flank. }
24... Nc6 $2 { Correct was 24...b5 with an even game. } 25. axb6 axb6 26. Qb1
$1 Kc7
{ The opening of the a-file is already decisive.On 26...Kb7 27 Bc5 wins. Or 26...Rb7 27 f6 gxf628 Rxf6d4 29 Qb5, etc
Problem: White to play and win. }
27. Bc1 $1 Qe1+
{ There's no satisfactory defense to the threat of Bf4+. On 27...Ne5 28 Bf4 e8 29 Qb5 penetrates decisively. }
28. Rf1 Qxc3 29. Bf4+ Kb7 30. Qb5 $1 1-0

[Event "USA Championship 1960-1"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Round "25"]
[White "Lombardy"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "When the Maroczy didn't bind"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "156"]

{ Geza Maroczy left a strange legacy: the discovery that a certain type of Pawn formation imposes a near-decisive cramp on the opponent. In this game, after Lombardy's sixth move, he obtains, with Fischer's consent, the dread "Maroczy bind."From here on, given a few developing moves, White's game almost plays itself – unless Black takes early and energetic counter-measures. This is easier said than done.
The method that Fischer chooses to free himself (9...d5)involves the sacrifice ofaPawn. Lombardy reacts sluggishly, overlooking a neat tactical point(17...Bh4 +) at the tail-end of a combination. Even so, he still has excellent drawing chances. But somewhat discouraged by the rapid turn of events, he indulges in a unique form of self-immolation. In short,Lombardy,not Maroczy,lost. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. f3
{ A passive, non-developing move which leads to nothing. White wants to gain control of d5, establishing a Maroczy bind with c4,Nc3 etc. But after going to all that trouble he can't prevent ...d5 after all. Correct is that tired old move – 5 Nc3. }
5... Nc6
{ Sharper is 5...e5! 6 Bb5+ (6 Nb5 a6 7 N5c3 Be6 8 Nd5 Nxd5 9 exd5 Bf5=) 6 ....Nbd7 7 Nf5 d5! 8 exd5 a6 9 Bxd7+ Qxd7 10 Ne3 Bc5 11 c4 b5=. (Cardoso-Fischer, 5th match game1957.) }
6. c4 e6 { 6...Nxd4 7 Qxd4 g6 is a sound alternative. } 7. Nc3 Be7
{ Premature is 7...d5? 8 cxd5 exd5 9 Bb5 winning a Pawn. } 8. Be3
{ 8 Nc2 0-0 9 Ne3 d5!? 10 cxd5 exd5 11 exd5? (better is 11 Nexd5) 11...Ne5 12 Qb3 Bc5 13 Bd2 Re8 14 Be2 Ng6 15 Nc2 Nh4 16 0-0-0 Nxg2 with advantage (Foguelman-Fischer, Mar del Plata 1960.) }
8... O-O 9. Nc2 d5 $5
{ Reckoning that the loss of a Pawn is compensated for by superior development. 9...Re8 is sound but passive. }
10. cxd5 exd5 11. Nxd5
{ Better is 11 exd5 Nb4 (11...Ne5 doesn't work well now because of 12 Qd4 followed by 0-0-0) 12 Bc4 Bf5 13 Nxb4 Bxb4 14 0-0 Rc8. Black regains the Pawn, but with an inferior position. }
11... Nxd5 12. Qxd5
{ On 12 exd5 Nb413 Bc4 Bf5 14 Nxb4 Bxb4+ 15 Kf2 Re8 with zood play for the Pawn. (If 16 Qb3 Ba5 threatening ...Rxe3.) }
12... Qc7 $1 13. Qb5 $2
{ Too intent on holding on to the Pawn. Correct is 13 Be2 Bh4+! 14 g3 Bf615 0-0 Bxb2 16 Rab1 =. }
13... Bd7 14. Rc1
{ Again too optimistic. After 14 Qe2 Bf6 15 0-0-0White can survive – temporarily anyway (if 15...Nb4 16 Rxd7!). }
14... Nb4 $1 { This unexpected "discovery" jolts White back to reality. } 15.
Nxb4
{ Loses the exchange, but avoids the worst. On 15 Qe2 Nxa2 regains the Pawn with continuing pressure. And on 15 Qc4 Qa5 16 Nxb4 Bxb4+ 17 Kf2 Rac818 Qd5 Rxc1 19 Bxc1 Be1+! White meets a devastating attack wherever he turns: i.e., 20 Ke3 (if 20 Kg1? Qb6+) 20...Qb6+ 21 Kf4 (not 21 Qd4? Bf2+ or 21 Ke2 Qf2+22 Kd1 Be6) 21...Qc7+ 22 e5 Qxc1+, etc. }
15... Qxc1+ 16. Bxc1 Bxb5 17. Nd5 Bh4+ $1
{ The scorpion's sting at the tail-end of the combination. } 18. g3 Bxf1 19.
Rxf1 Bd8
{ The smoke clears. Black is an exchange ahead for a Pawn. But there are still great technical difficulties. White's Knight is on a dominating outpost and his Pawn structure is solid. }
20. Bd2 Rc8 21. Bc3 f5 $1
{ Weaker would be 21...Re8 because of 22 g4 blocking the K-side. } 22. e5
{ This advance is necessary, but it undermines the support of the Knight (which can now be driven away). }
22... Rc5 23. Nb4 { 23 Nf4 (or 23 Ne3) would cost a pawn after 23...Ba5. }
23... Ba5 24. a3 Bxb4 25. axb4 Rd5 26. Ke2 Kf7 27. h4 Ke6 28. Ke3 Rc8 29. Rg1
Rc4
{ Black has steadily improved his grip, but his winning chances are still problematic, hinging mainly on sacrificing on b4 or e5 at the right moment. }
30. Re1 $2 { A gross blunder. Correct is 30 Ra1 a6 31 Rg1. } 30... Rxc3+ $1
{ Swapping everything leads to a won King and Pawn ending. } 31. bxc3 Rxe5+ 32.
Kd2 Rxe1 33. Kxe1 Kd5 34. Kd2 Kc4 35. h5 b6 36. Kc2 g5 37. h6 f4 38. g4 a5 39.
bxa5 bxa5 40. Kb2 a4 41. Ka3 Kxc3 42. Kxa4 Kd4 43. Kb4 Ke3 0-1

[Event "2nd match game 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "26"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Reshevsky"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Title "Time will tell"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "161"]

{ NEWYORK1961:2ndMatch Game
SICILIANDEFENSE
Time will tell
The opening has always been regarded as the oldwarrior's weak point, and were it not for this handicap who knows how far Reshevsky might have gone toward the summit?
Whatever the case, being familiar with the latest wrinkles does have the merit of saving time on the clock and, hopefully, of catching an opponent off guard. Although Reshevsky is bested in the theoretica1 duel (after 13 Bf3) his practical cunning enables him to extricate himself – at a great cost of time. In the end it is the clock, as much asFischer's persistence, thatcauses his downfall. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6
{ Allowing White the chance to get a Maroczy bind (with 5 c4). Apparently Reshevsky had booked up on this for the match. Black's idea is to dispense with an early ..d6 and possibly strive for a later ...d5 (thereby saving a tempo). }
5. Nc3
{ In match game8I got an edge with the more traditional 5 c4 Nf6 6 Nc3 Nxd4 7 Qxd4 d6 8 Be2 Bg79 Be3 0-010Qd2, etc. }
5... Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Be2
{ In the 4th and6th games of the match I continued with7 Bc4 0-0 8 Bb3 Ng4 (8...Na5? brought Reshevsky to grief againstme in the US Championship1958-9 after 9 e5 Ne810 Bxf7+! Kxf7 11 Ne6!! winning Black's Queen) 9 Qxg4 Nxd4 and White got a clear advantage both with 10 Qh4 and 10 Qd1 respectively. }
7... O-O { On7...d5? 8 Bb5 wins a Pawn. } 8. f4
{ Despite his familiarity with the Dragon Variation, I felt Reshevsky really didn't know the latest wrinkles in Alekhine's Attack. The point of Black's "accelerated fianchetto" becomes apparent after the indifferent 8 0-0?d5! 9 exd5 Nb4=. }
8... d6
{ Now on 8...d5? 9 e5 Ne4 10 Nxe4 dxe4 11 Nxc6 bxc612 Qxd8 Rxd8 13 Bc4 gives White a winning ending. (Olafsson-Larsen, Wageningen1957.) }
9. Nb3 Be6
{ I was right. This is the old (and second-rate) move. Correct is 9...a5! 10 a4 Be6 11 Nd4? (after 11 g4 Nb4! Black's Knight can no longer be dislodged by a3; the best White has is 11 0-0 Rc8=) 11...Qb6! 12 Nxe6 Qxe3 13 Nxf8 Ng4 with a strong attack. (Makievsky-Veresov, USSR 1954.) }
10. g4 d5 11. f5 Bc8 { Lipnitzky recommends 11...gxf5!? It's interesting. } 12.
exd5 Nb4 13. Bf3 $1
{ The modern way - White maintains his center Pawn and sacs two Pawns on the K-side where Black must expose his King to get them.
13 d6 has been known to be only a draw since the famous Alekhine-Botvinnik encounter, Nottingham 1936, which continued: 13...Qxd6! (if 13...exd6? 14 g5) 14 Bc5 Qf4 15 Rf1 Qxh2 16 Bxb4 Nxg4! 17 Bxg4 Qg3+ 18 Rf2 Qg1+ with a perpetual.
Another weak line is 13 fxg6 hxg6 14 Bf3 Bxg4! 15 Bxg4 Nxg4 16 Qxg4 Nxc2+ 17 Kf2 Nxa1 18 Rxa1 Rc8! with a good game. If 19 Bd4? Rc4. (PANOV) }
13... gxf5 14. a3 fxg4 15. Bg2 $1
{ On 15 axb4 gxf3 16 Qxf3 Bg4 followed by ... Bh5-g6. White's whole idea is to keep Black's QB restricted to the Q-side. }
15... Na6 16. Qd3 $1
{ Ney's improvement over 16 Qe2 Bf5! and the Bishop retreats to g6, when necessary, defending the K-side. }
16... e6
{ The best choice in a difficult position. Up to here we had both played briskly, but now Reshevsky began to consume time on the clock. After 16...Nd7 17 0-0-0Ne5 18 Qe2 Black's game is lifeless. White has h3 and Bd4 in the offing. }
17. O-O-O
{ Black gets the initiative after 17 d6? Nd5! 18 Bxd5 exd5 19 Qxd5 Re8, etc. }
17... Nxd5 { The lesser evil. On 17...exd5 18 h3 g3 19 Bd4 is strong. } 18. h3
$1 g3 19. Rhg1 Qd6 $1 { Reshevsky is putting up a first-rate defense. } 20.
Bxd5 exd5
{ Despite his material deficit, it is obvious White has a strong attack. His problem is how to land a haymaker. }
21. Nxd5 $2
{ This gives Black a little breathing space. Nowadays I would have played 21 Bd4! without giving it a second thought. After 21...Bxd4 22 Rxg3+ Bg7 (22...Kh8 23 Qxd4+ f6 24 Rf3 leads to a bind) 23 Rdg1 Qh6+ 24 Kb1 Be6 25 Rxg7+ Qxg7 26 Rxg7+ Kxg727 Qg3+ Kh8(if 27...Kf628 Qd6 Kg7 29 Nxd5 wins) 28 Qe5+ Kg8 29 Qg5+ Kh8 30 Qf6+ Kg8 31 Ne2 with a winning bind. }
21... Kh8 22. Bf4
{ 22 Bd4 is less convincing now after 22...Bxd4 (if 22...f6? 23 Rxg3 Qxd5 24 Bxf6!) 23 xd4+ f6 and White has no forced win. }
22... Qg6 23. Qd2
{ Overlooking his reply. Vukovich suggests 23 f3 but 23...Bf5! 24 Rxg3 Qc6 holds. }
23... Bxh3 $1
{ Reshevsky chopped it off fast – he doesn't wait to be asked twice. } 24. Rxg3
Bg4 { Rlack hae euooaded.intranferringhaiahapahida m sealing the g-file. } 25.
Rh1 { So White begins operations on the h-file! } 25... Rfe8 26. Ne3 Qe4 $2
{ Anxious to simplify and ease the tension (in time-pressure), Peshevsky finally goes astray. 166 26 / NEW YORK 1961
The tempting 26...f6 is refuted by 27 Nxg4 Qxb2+ 28 Kd1 Rad8 29 Rd3, etc.
But simply 26...f5! holds (if 27 Qh2 Kg8). }
27. Qh2 $1 { Now the roof caves in. } 27... Be6
{ The late Abe Turner suggested 27...Bf5 but after 28 xg7! Kxg7 29 Nxf5+ Qxf5 30 Nd4 wins. }
28. Rxg7 $1
{ That does it. Once this Bishop is gone, White has a field day. "Why didn't White play 28 Nd2...? So far as I can see Black can then resign. Or am I missing something?" (A.R.B.Thomas in a letter to Chess) Right, Mr. Thomas! }
28... Kxg7 29. Qh6+ Kg8 { Or 29...Kh8 30 Be5+ mates in two. } 30. Rg1+ Qg6 31.
Rxg6+ fxg6 32. Nd4 Rad8 33. Be5 Rd7 34. Nxe6 Rxe6 35. Ng4 Rf7 36. Qg5 Rf1+ 37.
Kd2 h5 38. Qd8+
{ After 38...Rf8 39 Nh6+ taxes even Reshevsky's defensive ability. } 1-0

[Event "5th match game 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "27"]
[White "Reshevsky"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Title "Sheer pyrotechnics"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "167"]

{ LOS ANGELES 1961: 5th Match Game
SEMI-TARRASCHDEFENSE
Sheer pyrotechnics
Here, in probably the most exciting game of the match, Fischer, trying to win a Pawn, unwittingly triggers a series of "desperado combinations which are brilliant and unorthodox. Pure tactics predominate for a period of ten moves (19 to 29). It is almost impossible to determine who is winning until Reshevsky emerges a clear exchange ahead. In the tricky ending that ensues Fischer obviously is fighting for a draw. But, once again, he has the clock as an ally.
In time-pressure, trying to preserve his slim advantage, Reshevsky piuys un uggressive nire m wricn riscner finas a nole – enabling him to reverse their roles. Conscious of his newly acquired advantage, Fischer storms down the board with his K-side Pawns and overwhelms his opponent. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5
{ This exchange variation, though insipid, has always been to Reshevsky's taste. }
4... Nxd5 { 4...exd5 leads to the kind of wood pushing that always bored me. }
5. Nf3 { Prematurely forceful is 5 e4 Nxc3 6 bxc3 c5 7 Nf3 cxd4 8 cxd4 Bb4+=. }
5... c5 6. e3 Nc6 7. Bd3
{ Botvinnik and Robert Byrne prefer 7 Bc4. A possible continuation might be 7...cxd4 8 exd4 Be790-0 0-0 10 Re1 a6= (weaker is 10...b6 11 Nxd5 exd5 12 Bb5 Botvinnik-Alekhine, AVRO1938). }
7... Be7
{ An alternative is 7...cxd4 8 exd4 g6 9 h4!? (9 0-0Bg7 10 Be4 is the positional approach) 9...Bg7 (better is 9...h6) 10 h5 Ndb4 11 Bg5 Nxd3+12 Qxd3 Qa5 13 Kf1 h6? 14 hxg6! hxg5 15 Rxh8+ Bxh8 16 gxf7+ Kxf7 17 Qh7+ Bg718 d5! White won shortly.(Balcerovsky-Dunkelblum, Varna1962.) }
8. O-O O-O 9. a3 cxd4 10. exd4
{ Fairly typical of the semi-Tarrasch formation: White has the freer game and attacking prospects, but the drawback of his isolated d-Pawn should not be minimized. Chances are even. }
10... Nf6
{ Also playable is 10...Bd7 11 Qc2 g6 12 Bh6 Re8 13 Ne4 Rc8 14 Qe2 f5 15 Nc3 Bf6 (R.Byrne-Bisguier, US Championship 1963-4).
Another possibility is 10...b6!? 11 Nxd5 Qxd5 12 Qc2 Bb7! 13 Bxh7+ Kh8 14 Be4 Nxd4 15 Bxd5 Nxc2 16 Bxb7 Nxa1 17 Bxa8 Rxa8 18 Bg5 f6=. }
11. Bc2 { A more flexible plan is11 Be3 followed byQe2 and Rad1. } 11... b6 12.
Qd3 Bb7 13. Bg5 g6 14. Rfe1 Re8 15. h4
{ Evans criticized this "aggressive pass" and Barden extolled it. I don't see how else White can make headway. He has to create some K-side threats before Black consolidates and piles up on his d-Pawn. }
15... Rc8 16. Rac1 Nd5 17. Ne4 f5 $5
{ I knew this was an "ugly positional blunder."But I actually thought Black would get the better of it after 18 Nc3 Bxg5 19 hxg5 Nxc3 20 bxc3 (not 20 Qxc3? Ne5) 20...Na5! (threatening ... Bxf3 and ...Qxg5). }
18. Nc3 Bxg5 19. Nxg5 $1 { Crossing me up! } 19... Nf4 20. Qe3
{ Not 20 Qg3? Nh5 21 Qe3 Nxd4. } 20... Qxd4 21. Nb5 $1
{ Marvelously alert! After the practically forced trade of Queens, White wins the exchange because of the imminent fork on d6. }
21... Qxe3
{ Best. After the game we analysed 21...Qd5 22 Qxf4 Qxb5 (if 22...Nd4? 23 Be4! Rxc1 24 Qxc1! fxe4 25 Qc7) 23 Nxe6 Qxb2 (if 23...Qd5 24 Nc7 Rxel+ 25 Rxe1 Qf7 26 Ne6 keeps the advantage); 24 Qh6! (RESHEVsKY) with an irresistible attack. On 24...Na5 (to stop Bb3; if 24...Rxe6 25 Rxe6 Nd4 26 Re7 wins) 25 Bxf5! gxf5 (if 25...Qf626 Ng5! or 25...Rxc1 26 Rxc1 gxf5 27 Rc7 wins) 26 Rb1 Qh8 (if 26...Qc3 27 Re3) 27 Qg5+ Kf7 28 Qxf5+ Kg8 (not 28...Qf6? 29 Qxh7+) 29 Re3 Rc3 30 Ng5! Rf8 31 Re8! forces mate. }
22. fxe3 Nxg2 $1 23. Kxg2 Nd4+ 24. Be4 $1
{ This game was played at the Beverly Hilton Hotel in Los Angeles, and I can still hear the audience gasping with each blow, thinking each of us had overlooked it in turn. "Fischer is winning!" "Reshevsky is winning!" The true state of affairs will crystallize in a matter of moves. }
24... Bxe4+ 25. Nxe4 Nxb5 26. Nf6+
{ So the fork, after all, takes place here instead of d6! } 26... Kf7 27. Nxe8
Rxe8 28. a4 $1
{ Inaccurate is 28 Red1 Re7! and the Knight can climb back into the game via c7-d5. }
28... Nd6 29. Rc7+ Kf6 $1
{ Black can't afford 29...Re7 30 Rec1. The Rook is needed to mobilize theK-side Pawns. }
30. Rec1 $1
{ Keeping control of the open file. On either 30 Rxa7 orRxh7, ...Rc8. } 30...
h6 31. Rxa7 Ne4 32. Ra6 Rd8 $1
{ 32...Rb8 33 Rc6 is hopeless.
Now it's clear that Black's fighting for a draw. }
33. Rc2
{ The only way to preserve winning chances. After 33 Rxb6 Rd2+ 34 Kg1g5 35 hxg5+ (on 35 Rcc6 gxh4 36 Rxe6+ Kg5 37 Rg6+ Kh5 38 Rxh6+ Kg4 Black has enough play on the K-side to hold the draw; but not 35 a5? gxh4 36 a6 h3 37 a7h2+ 38 Kh1 Ng3 mate) 35...hxg5 36 Rcc6 (not 36 a5? g4 37 a6 Ng538 a7 Nf3+ 39 Kf1 g3 40 a8=Q g2 mate!) 36...g4 37 Rxe6+ Kg5 38 Rh6 f4 keeps the balance. }
33... Rd3 34. Rxb6 { After 34 Kf3 Rb3 Black is in great shape. } 34... Rxe3 35.
a5 f4 36. Rf2 $2
{ Short of time, Reshevsky probably didn't see how Black's Rook could get back in time to stop the a-Pawn. But now it is doubtful that White can even draw!
White should settle for 36 a6 f3+ 37 Kf1 (not 37 Kh2? Re2+) 37...Rd3 38 Ke1 Re3+ 39 Kfl Rd3 with a draw. If 40 Kgl Rd1+ 41 Kh2 f2 42 Rxf2+ Nxf2 43 Rb3 (43 a7 Ra1 wins) 43...Rd744 Rf3+ Kg7 45 Rxf2 Ra7=. }
36... Nxf2 37. Kxf2 Re5 $1 38. b4 Re3 $1
{ This maneuver permits the Rook to get behind the passed Pawn. } 39. a6 Ra3
{ Now White is stymied. In order to mobilize his Q-side Pawns, he must inch forward with b5, Rb7, a7, b6, etc. But a half-dozen moves, in chess, can be a lifetime. }
40. Rc6
{ The last move of the time-control, and it definitely loses. The best chance is 40 b5 with the possibility of Rb8 and b6(giving up the a-Pawn)followed by b7, in some key variations. }
40... g5 41. hxg5+ hxg5 42. b5 g4
{ The sealed move. Black's Pawns suddenly proliferate from nowhere! } 43. Rc8
{ The line I had expected was 43 Rcl (intending to bolster the Pawns from behind with Rb1) 43...g3+ 44 Kg1 (on 44 Kg2 Ra2+ 45 Kf3 Kf5 wins) 44...Ra2! 45 Rb1 f3 46 b6 Rg2+ 47 Kfl Rh2! 48 Ke1 Rh1+ 49 Kd2 Rxb1 50 a7 f2 51 a8=Q f1=Q and Black wins, since White has no perpetual check. }
43... Kf5 44. b6 g3+ 45. Ke1
{ He decides to let the Pawns through rather than get mated after 45 Kg2 Ra2+ 46 Kg1 f3, etc. }
45... Ra1+ 46. Ke2 g2 47. Rf8+
{ On 47 Rg8 Rxa6 48 b7 (if 48 Rxg2 Rxb6 wins) 48...Rb6 is decisive. } 47... Ke4
48. Rxf4+ Kxf4 49. b7 g1=Q
{ A hasty slip which, fortunately, still wins. As Isaac Kashdan pointed out after the game 49...Ke4! wins outright: e.g., 50 b8=Q Ra2+ 1 K-any g1=Q mate. "What will the Russians say when they see this atch?"he inquired, with gentle irony. }
50. b8=Q+ Kf5 51. Qf8+ Ke4 52. Qa8+
{ No better is 52 Qf3+ Ke5 53 Qc3+ (if 53 Qh5+ Kd6) 53...Qd4 54 Qg3+ Kd5 55 Qf3+ Qe4+, etc. }
52... Kd4 { Delicate footwork is required to escape the perpetual. } 53. Qd8+
{ Better than 53 Qh8+ Kc4 54 Qc8+ Qc5 55 Qxe6+ Kb4 56 Qe4+ Qc4+. } 53... Kc4
54. Qd3+ Kc5 55. Qc3+ Kd6 56. Qd2+ Ke5 57. Qb2+ Kf5
{ He runs out of checks after 58 Qb5+ Kf6 59 Qb2+ e5. } 0-1

[Event "11th match game 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "28"]
[White "Reshevsky"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Title "A peccable draw"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "175"]

{ LOS ANGELES 1961:11th Match Game
KING'SINDIANDEFENSE
A peccable draw
What proved to be the last game of this ill-starred match is a good example of how Reshevsky, by virtue of pluck, stamina, and alertness, salvages a draw from a lost position. It exemplifies, too, the demoralizing effect that continuously strong resistance can have on even the most robust opponent.
Fischer rapidly wrests the initiative and wins the exchange asthe result of a pretty combination (28...Qxe4). However, he has difficulty gaining the offensive because Reshevsky throws obstacle after obstacle in his path. Nevertheless, Fischer's material advantage begins to meke itoelf felt. He misses dear virs (on mores 38 end 42), whereupon his game deteriorates sufficiently to permit his stubborn opponent to set up an adequate defense. Still, there are severa1 surprises in store just at the very end. }
1. c4 Nf6 2. d4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. Be2
{ Inferior is 5 e5. See game 21. } 5... d6 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O
{ For7 d5 see game 7. } 7... Nc6 8. d5
{ Match game 9 (Reshevsky as White) had continued: 8 Be3 Re8 9 dxe5 (9 d5 Nd4! equalizes fully) 9...dxe5 10 Qxd8 Nxd8 11 Nb5 Ne6 12 Ng5("fullof sound and fury, signifying nothing" – Evans) 12...Re7=. See game 57 note to Black's move13. }
8... Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. exf5
{ 11 f3 f4 followed by ..g5 etc gives Black a strong K-side attack. } 11...
Nxf5 { 11...gxf5 keeping Black's Pawn front mobile is very strong. } 12. f3 Nd4
{ For 12...Nf6 see game 30. } 13. Ne4 b6
{ HinderingWhite's thematic break with c5. } 14. Bg5 $2
{ Apparently gaining a tempo, but only driving the Queen to a better square. 14 Bd2 or Rel appear to be more accurate. }
14... Qe8 15. Bd2
{ This Bishop must retreat eventually after ...h6.The idea is to prepare b4 without having to fear the reply.a5. }
15... a5
{ Gaining more time. White must now stop for b3 (to enforce b4). On the immediate 16 a3 a4! fixes the Q-side. }
16. Re1 Nxe2+ { Otherwise the Bishop retreats to f1. } 17. Qxe2 h6 18. b3 g5
19. a3 Qg6
{ Now it's obvious that it was a mistake to force Black's Queen to e8 – its presence on g6 lends momentum to the K-side initiative. }
20. b4 Nf6 21. bxa5 $2
{ Correct is 21 Ndf2, but after ... Nh5 Black stands better.
Now Reshevsky is hoping to get some counterplay after 21...bxa5 22 Ndf2 Nh5 23 c5, etc. But – }
21... g4 $1 { – doesn't give him time to get it in. } 22. Ndf2
{ Not 22 axb6? gxf3 23 Qxf3 Nxe4 24 Qxe4 Bf5 wins a piece. On 22 Nxf6+ Bxf6 23 f4 Bf5 White's in trouble. }
22... gxf3 23. Qxf3 Nh5 { Increasing the pressure. On 23...Nxd5 24 Qg3 holds. }
24. Qe3 bxa5 { Finally! } 25. Rac1
{ Typically, Reshevsky wants to mobilize his Q-side without making any concessions or creating any K-side weaknesses. After25 g3 Nf4 26 Kh1 Nh3! it's just a matter of time before Black invades on the light squares. }
25... Bf5 26. c5
{ Loses material, but probably the best chance. On 26 g3 Rf7 followed by ..Raf8, White is not long for this world. }
26... Nf4 27. Qg3
{ Blunders the exchange. But no longer possible is 27 g3 Nxd5, etc. } 27...
Bxe4 $1 28. Rxe4 $2
{ Flustered, White makes the task somewhat easier. Better is 28 Nxe4 (not 28 Bxf4? exf4) 28...Qxg3 29 hxg3 Nd3 30 cxd6 cxd6 31 Rc6 Nxel 32 Bxel with many more chances of holding the ending than in the actual game. }
28... Qxe4 $1 29. Nxe4 Ne2+ 30. Kh1 Nxg3+ 31. hxg3 Ra6 $1 32. cxd6 cxd6 33. a4
{ "Black has won the exchange, but the technical difficulties confronting him are enormous.His Pawns are discombobulated, his Bishop is hemmed in and his Rooks are virtually immobilized. Still, one has the feeling Fischer should win this game." (EVANs.) }
33... Rf7 34. g4 Bf8 35. Kh2 Kh7 36. Rc8 Rb6 37. Ra8 Rb3 38. Bxa5 Rf4 $2
{ The right concept is to destroy the blockade on e4 with 38...Re3! 39 Nc3 (if 39 Ng3 Rd3) 39...e4 40 Re8 Bg7 41 Nxe4 Be5+, etc. }
39. Bc7 $1
{ With his usual tenacity, Reshevsky finds the only move to keep the game alive. White is still quite lost, however. }
39... Rxe4 40. Rxf8 Rd3 41. Rf6 Rxg4 42. Rxd6 Rg7 $2
{ Now the "technical difficulties" become more real thanapparent. Correct is 42...Rd2! 43 Rd7+ Kg6 44 Bxe5 Rgxg2+ 45 Kh3 Rg5 wins easily. }
43. Rc6 $1 { Forced. 43 Bb6 loses to43...Rd2. And 43 Ba5 loses to 43...Rdg3. }
43... Rxd5
{ If 43...Rd2 44 Bxe5 Rgxg2+ 45 Kh3 Rg5 46 Bf4 Rd3+ 47 Kh2 (if 47 Kh4? Rd4 48 Rf6 Kg7) 47...Rh5+ 48 Kg2 Rdxd5 49 Rc7+! Kg6 50 Rc6+ draws. }
44. Rc2 $1 { Again I had overlooked White's reply. } 44... e4
{ Discouraged, I gave it one last try. } 45. a5 Rd3
{ On 45...e3 46 Bf4 Re7 47 Re2 draws. } 46. Bf4 Rf7 47. g3 e3 48. Rc1
{ Reshevsky, once more in severe time-pressure, overlooked that 48 Kg2 draws easily. For on 48...Rxf4 49 Rc7+!is the saving clause. }
48... Re7 49. Re1 Ra3 50. Re2 Kg6 51. Kg2
{ Not 51 Bd6? Rd7! 52 Bxa3 Rd2 wins. } 51... Rxa5 52. Rxe3
{ 52 Bxe3 draws easily. Black can't make anything out of the pin on the e-file. }
52... Ra2+ 53. Kf3 $2
{ A comedy of errors. Correct is 53 Kh3! in order to keep Black's King out of g4 after the exchange of Rooks: e.g., 53...Rxe3 54 Bxe3 h5 55 Bf4 Ra1 56 Bc7 Kf5 57 Bf4 Rb1 58 Bc7! Rh1+ 59 Kg2 Rc1 60 Bf4! (gaining a vital tempo by hitting the Rook), 60...R-any 61 Kh3! maintaining the blockade. RESHEVSKY / KING'S INDIAN DEFENSE 181 }
53... Rb7 $2
{ Returning the favor. As Evans originally pointed out in Chess Life, "The best winning chance is 53...Rxe3+ 54 Bxe3 h5 followed by ... Kf5."
Disgusted, I no longer thought there was a win. However, later I worked out a problem-like variation (after 54...h5):
A] 55 g4? h4 wins. B] 55 Ke4 Kf6! 56 Bd4+ Ke6 57 Kf4 Ra4 58 Ke3 Kf5 leads to variations similar to "D".
c] 55 Kf4 Ra5! 56 Bd2 Rf5+ 57 Ke4 Kf6 and Black's King will eventually penetrate to g4. For example, 58 Bf4 (58 Ke3? Kg5) 58...Ra5 followed by ...Ra4+ and ... Kf5.
D]55 Bf4 Kf5 56 Bd6 Rb2 57 Bf4 Rb3+ 58 Kg2 Kg4 59 Bd6 Rb2+ 60 Kg1 Kh3 61 Be5 Rb4! 62 Bc7 (not 62 Bf4? h4) 62...Rg4! 63 Kf2 Kh2 64 Be5 Kh1 65 Kf3 Rg8 66 Bf4 Rf8 67 Kf2 (if 67 Ke3 Kg2) 67...h4 68 Kf3 h3 69 Kf2 h2 70 Kf1 Ra8 71 Kf2 Ra2+ 72 Kf1 Ra3! 73 Kf2 Rf3+!! 74 Kxf3 Kg1 75 Be3+ Kf1 and the Pawn queens. }
54. Re6+ Kf5 55. Re5+ Kf6 56. Rd5 Rb3+ 57. Kg4 1/2-1/2

[Event "Bled 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "29"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Geller"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Hoist with his own petard"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "182"]

{ As was his wont, Geller gambles with 7...Qf6 in an attempt to assume an early offense. To thwart this maneuver, part of a patently prepared variation, Fischer sacrifices a Pawn (9 d4). Undaunted, Geller tries to continue his attack. But it backfires. With a series of rapier-like thrusts, Fischer demolishes Black in a mere twenty-two moves.
Subsequent attempts to improve on Geller's play have likewise failed. Thus, this fruitful encounter offers what has come to be accepted as the refutation of Black's ultra-aggressive system. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. O-O
{ At that time this was considered inferior because it allows the pin which Black can initiate with his next move. 5 Bxc6+ or 5 c3 were more standard. The text is more non-committal. White can deploy his forces to greater effect after he gets a look at Black's reply. }
5... Bg4 { This aggressive sally weakens Black's Q-side. } 6. h3 $1
{ It's important to kick immediately, otherwise after ..Qf6 followed by ... Bxf3 White's Pawn formation could be smashed. }
6... Bh5
{ As a result of this game 6...h5 became fashionable. I had intended 7 d4 b5 8 Bb3 Nxd4? (8...Qf6 is better) 9 hxg4 hxg4 10 Ng5. Unclear is 7 c4!? b5 (if 7...Qf6 8 Qb3! 0-0-0 9 Bxc6 bxc6 10 hxg4 hxg4 11 Nh2 Qh6 12 Qg3 – but 11...d5! is dangerous, Zhuravlev; 7...Bd7 avoids the piece sac, but after 8 d4 White has a superior variation of the Duras Attack) 8 cxb5 Nd4 9 bxa6+ c6 10 Nxd4! Bxd1 11 Bxc6+ Ke7 12 Nf5+ Kf6 13 Bxa8 xa8 14 Rxd1 Qxe4 15 Nc3 Qa8 16 Ne3 Qxa6 17 d4 κg6 18 b4 and White's passed Q-side Pawns should win. (Grabczewski-Brzuska, Warsaw1961.) }
7. c3 Qf6 $2
{ Geller looked quite happy after his novelty, but sounder is 7...Nf6 8 d4 Nd7 bolstering the center. }
8. g4 $1
{ I realized the danger inherent in weakening my K-side, but felt that I could capitalize on Black's lack of development (the traffic jam on his K-side) before he could get to my King. }
8... Bg6 9. d4 $1 { It's worth a Pawn to open up the game. } 9... Bxe4
{ What else? The threat was 10 Bg5 followed by 11 d5 winning a piece. He still looked happy. }
10. Nbd2 Bg6
{ No better is 10...Bxf3 11 Nxf3 e4 12 Rel d5 13 Bg5 Qd6 (on 13...Qe6 14 c4! is the bone-crusher; or 13...Qg6 14 Qb3! b5 15 Qxd5 bxa4 16 Ne5 Qe6 17 Qxe6+ fxe6 18 Nxc6 wins) 14 c4! dxc4 (if 14...f6 15 cxd5 Qxd5 16 Bb3) 15 d5! b5 16 dxc6 bxa4 17 Rxe4+ Ne7 18 Bxe7 Bxe7 19 Qe2 wins.
An attempt to rehabilitate Geller's line was made in Smyslov-Medina, Tel Aviv 1964, which continued10...Bd3 11 Bxc6+ bxc6 12 Re1 0-0-0 but 13 Re3! proved to be very strong. }
11. Bxc6+
{ Trading old advantages for new. Now Black's Q-side Pawns are a shambles and his King can expect no shelter there. }
11... bxc6 12. dxe5
{ A few weeks after the game it dawned on me that 12 Qa4 would have been a tremendous shot. After 12...Ne7(apparently forced) 13 dxe5 dxe5 14 Re1 e4 15 Nxe4 Qxf3 16 Qxc6+! Nxc6 17 Nf6++ Kd8 18 Re8 mate. I was kicking myself for not having taken this course, but then I found that after 12 Qa4 Kd7! 13 dxe5 dxe5 14 Nc4 Bd6 White has no immediate way to exploit the exposed King. }
12... dxe5 13. Nxe5 $1 Bd6
{ On 13...0-0-0 14 e2 Kb7 15 Nb3 (intending Na5+) is murderous. } 14. Nxg6 $1
{ A little surprise, permitting him to open his h-file. Of course not 14 Nxc6 h5. }
14... Qxg6
{ Geller took a half hour on this recapture and stopped looking happy. He rejected 14...hxg6 15 Ne4 Qh4 16 Nxd6+ cxd6 17 Qxd6 Qxh3? 18 Re1+ and mates. }
15. Re1+ Kf8
{ Another difficult decision. On 15...e/16 c4 0-0-017a4 White's attack comes first. }
16. Nc4 h5 { Still hoping to rise from the ashes and fan his attack. } 17. Nxd6
cxd6 { The best chance is 17...Qxd6. } 18. Bf4 d5 $2
{ Loses outright. In the post-mortem Tal tried to hold the game with 18...Rd8 19 Qe2 hxg4 but after 20 hxg4 Black is in virtual zugzwang. If 20...Qh7? 21 Bxd6+ wins. }
19. Qb3 hxg4
{ Geller spent about forty minutes on this move. If 19...Ne7 20 Rxe7! xe7 21 Qb7+ wins. Or 19...Nf6 20Wb7 Re8 21 Rxe8+ Nxe8 22 Re1 f624 Qc8, etc. 186 29 /BLED 1961 }
20. Qb7 $1 { Stronger than 20 Qb4+ Ne7 21 Qxe7+ Kg8 22 h4, etc. } 20... gxh3+
21. Bg3 Rd8 22. Qb4+ { He must now lose both a Knight and a Rook. } 1-0

[Event "Bled 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "30"]
[White "Gligorich"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "A lyrical performance"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "187"]

{ This draw has the charm of perfection. Each move is interesting and, to this day, appears flawless.
With 17...c5 Fischer launches an intricate double-Pawn sacrifice which involves exact timing. Gligorich rises to the occasion, returning material in an attempt to wrest the advantage. The economy and ingenuity displayed by both players produces a harmonious flow of movement, remarkable in its esthetic appeal. The effect is of a pas de deux in which each partner contributes equally to the total symmetry. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5
Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3
{ The older 10 f3 f5 11 Be3 f4 12 Bf2 g5 has been abandoned. Black's K-side attack has practically been worked out to a forced mate! }
10... f5 11. exf5
{ Petrosian-Tal, in this same tournament, continued (with Black's N on e8): 11 f4 exf4 12 Bxf4 fxe4 13 Nxe4 Nf5 14 Bg5 Nf6 15 g4 Nd4 16 Ndf2 Qe7=. }
11... Nxf5
{ In this line White gets a grip on e4, Black on d4. 11...gxf5 is more energetic. }
12. f3 Nf6
{ For 12...Nd4 see game number 28. Both moves give Black a nice game. } 13. Nf2
Nd4 14. Nfe4 Nh5
{ White has the c5 lever; Black has the dynamic break with ...g5-g4. Chances are roughly even. }
15. Bg5 Qd7 { Keeping an eye on the d-Pawn so that ..c5 becomes possible. } 16.
g3 h6
{ In a later round Gligorich (as Black) played against Tal 16...c5? but after 17 Nb5! Nxb5 18 cxb5 White obtained a bind. }
17. Be3 c5 $1
{ I was informed that Gligorich thought I had blundered a Pawn, but it is a deliberate sac. On 17...Nxe2+ 18 Qxe2 g5 19 c5 White has it all his own way. }
18. Bxd4 { Not 18 Nb5 Nf5 19 Bd2 a6, etc. } 18... exd4 19. Nb5 a6
{ Not 19...Be5? 20 f4. } 20. Nbxd6
{ Apparently Black has lost a Pawn without any visible compensation. His pieces, which are now so awkwardly placed, soon spring to life, however. }
20... d3 $1 21. Qxd3
{ A double-edged game would result from 21 Bxd3 Bd4+ 22 Kh1 Nxg3+ 23 Nxg3xd6 24 c2 Bh3. }
21... Bd4+
{ The combination requires intricate footwork. A mistake would be 21...Bxb2 22 Nxc8 Bxal 23 Nb6 and it's all over (23...Bd4+? 24 Qxd4). }
22. Kg2
{ After 22 Kh1 Nxg3+ 23 Nxg3 xd6 White is weak on all the squares and his K-side looks like Swiss cheese. Chances would be even. }
22... Nxg3 $1
{ This is the resource it was necessary to visualize as far back as move 17. }
23. Nxc8 $1
{ Best. Not 23 hxg3? (or 23 Kxg3) 23...Qh3 mate. On 23 Nxg3 xd6 again is good. }
23... Nxf1 24. Nb6 $1 Qc7 $1
{ Blow for blow! The threat of mate on h2 keeps the exchange. } 25. Rxf1 Qxb6
26. b4 $1 { The saving clause. } 26... Qxb4
{ I saw the draw coming but felt the position was too precarious to play for a win. On 26...cxb4 27 c5! Bxc528 Nxc5 Qxc529 Qxg6+ Kh8 30 Qxh6+ Kg8 31 Kh1 wins. The only other try is 26...Rf7 27 bxc5 Bxc5 28 Rb1 followed by d6 with tons of play. }
27. Rb1 Qa5 28. Nxc5 { On 28 Rxb7 Rf7. } 28... Qxc5 29. Qxg6+ Bg7 30. Rxb7 Qd4
{ The only move. Gligorich was so sure I'd "find" it that he wrote it down on his scoresheet while I was taking a minute to look for something better. }
31. Bd3 Rf4 32. Qe6+ Kh8 33. Qg6 1/2-1/2

[Event "Bled 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "31"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Petrosian"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[Title "The sincerest form of flattery"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "191"]

{ This is Fischer's only win against Petrosian and it is achieved through an unconscious mimicry of the latter's style. Right up to the endgame Fischer seems content to return the ball without trying to force the issue. Each attempt to seize the initiative is meticulously rebuffed. Move by move, they seem to be drifting toward a draw. Petrosian offers one at move 27, but Fischer declines. Perhaps out of irritation, Petrosian immediately commits his first and only error. And Fischer, reverting to his normal style of play, takes full advantage of it. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 { For 2 Nc3 see game 16. } 2... d5 3. Nc3
{ For 3 exd5 see game 20. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
{ For 4...Bf5 see game 49.
5 23
5 Qe2 Ndf6 (weaker is 5...Ngf6 6 Nd6 mate) gives White nothing. I tried 5 Bc4 against Portisch at Stockholm 1962, which continued: 5...Ngf6 6 Ng5 Nd5 (the idea is to omit the usual ...e6 until after the QB has been developed) 7 N1f3 h6 8 Ne4 N7b6 9 Bb3 (better is 9 Bd3 Nb4 10 0-0 Nxd3 11 xd3 e6 12 Ne5! with pressure) 9...Bf5 10 Ng3 Bh7 11 0-0 e6=. }
5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nxf6+
{ The knight is not particularly well-placed after 6 Ng3. } 6... Nxf6 7. Bc4
Bf5 8. Qe2 e6 9. Bg5 Bg4 $1
{ This super-refinement reduces all of White's attacking prospects. Petrosian has a knack of snuffing out such dreams twenty moves before they even enter his opponent's head! After 9...Be7 10 0-0-0 h6 11 Bh4 Ne4!? 12 g4 Bh7 13 Bg3 Nxg3 14 fxg3 Qc7 15 Ne5 Bd6 16 h4 keeps the initiative. (Tal-Fuster, Portoroz 1958.) }
10. O-O-O Be7 11. h3
{ It might have been better to prevent further simplifications with 11 Kb1 Nd5 12 Bc1! 0-0 (not 12...Bxf3 13 Qxf3 Bg5 14 Qg3!) 13 Bd3, etc. }
11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nd5 $1
{ Forces an exchange of bishops. If 13 Bd2 Bg5, etc. } 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. Kb1
Rd8 { I had expected 14...0-0-0. } 15. Qe4 b5 $1
{ Now it's apparent why Black didn't castle long. He wants to drum up counterplay on the Q-side, which he couldn't do if his King lived there. }
16. Bd3 a5 17. c3
{ The threat was ..a4-a3. Weaker is 17 a3 b4. Already White has been thrown on a milddefensive. }
17... Qd6 { 17...a4 would be met by18 a3. } 18. g3
{ I thought he wanted to exchange Queens. } 18... b4 $1 19. c4
{ Practically forced – but now the d-Pawn is weak. } 19... Nf6 20. Qe5
{ After 20 Qe2 0-0 (20...Qxd4 21 Bxh7) 21 Bc2 c5 White could easily end up with the bad Bishop. }
20... c5
{ After 20...Qxe5 21 dxe5 Nd7 22 f4 Nc5 produces a drawn ending. And not20...Qxd4 21 Qxa5. }
21. Qg5 { This looked like a shot – } 21... h6 $1
{ – but instead it's a shock. } 22. Qxc5
{ Now I saw that 22 xg7 Ke7! 23 dxc5 Wc6! (not 23...xc5 24 he1 Rdg8 25 xe6+! Kxe6 26 el+ should win) 24 Bg6 (forced) 24...df8 25 Bxf7We4+! 26 Ka1 Rh7! and Black wins. }
22... Qxc5 23. dxc5 Ke7 { 23...c8 immediately is also good. } 24. c6 Rd6 25.
Rhe1 Rxc6 26. Re5 Ra8 27. Be4
{ After 27 b5 Ra7 followed by ... Nd7-c5 Black's solid as a rock. Right after I made this move, Petrosian offered a draw. I was ready to accept, but Tal happened to be standing there at that instant, hovering anxiously, since a drawn result would practically clinch first place for him. So I refused – not because I thought White has anything in the position, but because I didn't want to give Tal the satisfaction! }
27... Rd6 $2 { Serendipity. Simply 27...Nxe4 leads to a dead draw. } 28. Bxa8
{ This obvious capture shattered Petrosian, who apparently had been engrossed in analyzing the intricacies of 28 xd6 Kxd6 29 Rxe6+ fxe6 30 Bxa8 Kc5 31 b3 Nd7 32 Kc2 Kd4 with an absolute bind on the dark squares. }
28... Rxd1+ 29. Kc2 Rf1 { There's no turning back. If 29...d8 30xa5 wins. } 30.
Rxa5 Rxf2+ 31. Kb3 Rh2 32. c5 Kd8
{ On 32...xh3 33 Ra7+ Kd8 34 xf7 xg3+ 35 Kxb4 the Q-side Pawns hurtle toward a touchdown. The Bishop, since it can control both wings at once, is vastly superior to the Knight. }
33. Rb5 $1 { Not 33 a7 Nd7! 34 c6 Nb6 holds. } 33... Rxh3
{ Now 33...Nd7 is refuted by 34 c6. } 34. Rb8+ Kc7
{ Or 34...Ke7 35 Kxb4 (not 35 c6? Nd5) 35... xg3 36 a4 wins. } 35. Rb7+ Kc6
{ I suspect Petrosian saw White's reply, but wanted to be put out of his misery. 35...Ke8 36xf7 Rxg3+ 37 Kxb4 is futile. }
36. Kc4 $1 { There's no defense to the discovered checkmate. } 1-0

[Event "Bled 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "32"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "The moral victor"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "196"]

{ After an early lapse by Tal on move 6, Fischer relentlessly presses home his advantage. He misses several opportunities to shorten Tal's resistance, but the outcome is never really in doubt.
"Finally, he has not escaped me!" exulted Fischer. "It is difficult to play against Einstein's theory," sighed Tal, who went on to capture first prize.
But it was Fischer, finishing a strong second, who had the consolation of scoring 3½ out of 4 against the Russian contingent, and of being the only player (in a field of twenty) to emerge undefeated. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3
{ No doubt Tal expected 5 Nb5 which I had played exclusively at Buenos Aires 1960. I still think that might be best (see game 54). }
5... Qc7 6. g3
{ A perfectly legitimate treatment which Botvinnik labeled a "very cunning and well-masked idea." Actually no trap is intended. It becomes one only by virtue of Tal's reply. }
6... Nf6 $2
{ Probably the losing move! Tal looked worried immediately after having made it, but I'm not sure he was convinced he had really been careless. Correct is 6...a6 7 g2 Nf6 8 0-0, etc. }
7. Ndb5 $1
{ Curiously enough, Bisguier, who was present at Bled and witnessed the game, forgot this move when he reached the identical position against Benko at San Antonio 1962! }
7... Qb8 { On 7...Qa58 Bd2 Qd89 Bf4 e5 10 Bg5 is strong. } 8. Bf4 Ne5
{ Tal took a long time on this risky reply. The alternative 8...e5 9 Bg5 a6 10 Bxf6 (not 10 Na3 b5 11 Bxf6 b4!) 10...axb5 (not10...gxf6 11 Na3 b5 12 Nd5) 11 Bg5 gives a clear advantage. }
9. Be2 $1
{ Perhaps Tal underestimated this simple move. It prepares d4 and keeps an eye on the b5 square.
On 9...a6 10 Qd4d6 11 Rd1 axb5 12 Bxe5 wins at least a Pawn. Or 9...d6 10 Qd4 Nc6 11 Nxd6+ (Tal pointed out 11 Qxd6! Bxd6 12 Bxd6) 1...Kd7 12 Bb5 Bxd6130-0-0, etc.
In the tournament book Tal suggested the rather startling 9...Ng8 to avoid material loss. After 10 Qd4 f6 11 0-0-0 (if 11 Bxe5 fxe5 12 Qc4 Kd8! holds) 11...a6 12 Nd6+ Bxd6 13 Qxd6 Qxd6 14 Rxd6 leads to a promising endgame. }
9... Bc5 10. Bxe5 $1 Qxe5 11. f4 Qb8 12. e5 a6
{ Tal didn't give this a second thought. On 12...Ng8 13 Ne4 Be7 14 Qd2 followed byNbd6+ and 0-0-0 is crushing. }
13. exf6 axb5 14. fxg7
{ Keres thought 14 Ne4 Bf8 15 Qd4 was stronger. But I wanted the Pawn. With only two draws against Tal, out of six times at bat, I was in no mood to speculate! }
14... Rg8 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Qd4 Ra4
{ A desperate attempt to complicate. 16...Qc7 (BOTVINNIK) held out more chance for survival. }
17. Nf6+ Bxf6 { 17...Kd8? loses to 18 Qb6+. } 18. Qxf6 Qc7 19. O-O-O $1
{ 19 Bh5 is answered by 19...d5. And 19 Bxb5? Qa5+ wins a piece. } 19... Rxa2
20. Kb1 Ra6
{ Not 20...a5 since 21 Bh5 d5 (if 21...d6 22 xd6!) 22 xd5! exd5 23 Rel+ wins outright.
Also bad is 20...Qa5 21 b3! and the threat of Bh5 is decisive. }
21. Bxb5
{ I was so intent on snatching material and not botching this one that I missed 21 Bh5 d6 (or 21...d5 22 xd5!) 22 he1 e7 23Wh6 Kd7 24Wxh7 with a quick win in view. }
21... Rb6 22. Bd3 e5
{ The best chance. On 22...Wd8 23Wh6 f5 24 h5+K≥7 25 g4 cracks Black open. }
23. fxe5 $1
{ Black was hoping for 23 xe5+xe5 24 fxe5 xg7 with some drawing prospects, even though a Pawn behind. In top-flight chess, you have to drive your advantage home unmercifully. }
23... Rxf6 24. exf6 { The threat is simply Bxh7. } 24... Qc5
{ The only move. 24...b6 25 Rhf1 wins easily. } 25. Bxh7 Qg5 26. Bxg8 Qxf6 27.
Rhf1 Qxg7 28. Bxf7+ Kd8
{ Black has succeeded in staving off immediate defeat, but the endgame is hopeless. }
29. Be6 Qh6 { On 29...Kc730 Bf5 maintains the pressure. } 30. Bxd7 Bxd7 31. Rf7
Qxh2 32. Rdxd7+ Ke8 33. Rde7+ Kd8 34. Rd7+ Kc8 35. Rc7+ Kd8 36. Rfd7+ Ke8 37.
Rd1 { White has been gaining time on the clock. } 37... b5 38. Rb7 Qh5
{ 38...Qxg3 39 Rxb5 is equally convincing. } 39. g4 Qh3
{ Or 39...Qxg4 40 Rh1 Qd4 41 Rh8+! Qxh8 42 Rb8+ wins. } 40. g5 Qf3 41. Re1+ Kf8
42. Rxb5 Kg7 43. Rb6 Qg3 44. Rd1 Qc7 45. Rdd6
{ Threatening 46 Rg6+ Kh7 47 Rh6+ Kg7 48 Rbg6+ Kf8 49 Rh8+ Kf7 50 Rh7+ winning the Queen. }
45... Qc8 { 45...Qc546 Rb7+K-any 47 Rd8 mate. } 46. b3 Kh7 47. Ra6
{ Black must submit to the loss of his Queen or get mated. There is no defense against 48 Ra7+ Kg8 49 Rdd7, etc. }
1-0

[Event "Bled 1961"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Round "33"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Trifunovich"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "The drawing master"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "201"]

{ Trifunovich has earned the reputation of being a very hard man to beat, and the other Grandmasters have acquired a healthy respect for his technical skill. At Bled, for example, he lost only this one game.
Trifunovich's experiment with a dubious line in the opening meets with an abrupt and effective antidote (13 Nxd4), saddling him with an isolated e-Pawn for the duration of the mid-game. He decides, perhaps unwisely, to sacrifice it at an appropriate moment to gain some counterplay. Although he succeeds in outplaying Fischer in the endgame, he cannot overcome his material deficit. What ultimately defeats him is force majeure. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 exd4 $2
{ Considered to be weak - and it is. But Trifunovich must have had some equalizing idea in mind, since he rarely chooses a genuinely risky line. The tried and tested 7...d5 must be played. }
8. Re1
{ A reader of Tal's Latvian chess magazine (Shakhmaty) suggested 8 xd4 but 8...e7! seems to hold: e.g., 9 e1 (if 9 Bxf7+ Kxf7 10 Wf3+ Kg8 11 xe4 d5 is adequate) 9...d5 10 c6! xc6 11 Bxd5 Bb7! 12 Bxe4 Be7 13 xc6+ xc6 14 Qe2 Kf8, etc. }
8... d5 9. Nc3 $1
{ 9 Nxd4? Nxd4 10 Qxd4 Be6 (threatening ..c5) is better for Black. } 9... Be6
{ On 9...dxc3 10 Bxd5 Bb7 11 Bxe4 (not 11 Rxe4+? Ne7!) 11...Be7 (11...Qxd1? loses to 12 Bxc6++) 12 Qe2 prevents Black from castling. }
10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Rxe4 Be7 12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. Nxd4 $1
{ An improvement over the "book" line. Trifunovich probably expected the usual 13 Rxe6 but after 13...Qd5! 14 Qe2 0-0 15 Rxe7 Nxe7 16 Qxe7 Rae8 17 Qxc7 Rf7 18 Qg3 Rxf3! 19 gxf3 Re1+ 20 Kg2 Qc4 21 Kh3 Qe6+ 22 Qg4 Qc6 and shortly drawn. (Dolodonov-Kicin, corres., USSR 1965.) }
13... O-O
{ He thought quite a while on this. Weak is 13...e5? 14 Qh5+ g6 15 Nxc6, etc. On 13...Qd5 14 Qg4 0-0-0 15 Be3 Black's e-Pawn is untenable. Finally the "simplifying combination"13...Nxd4 14 Rxd4 Qxd4? 15 Qxd4 Rd8 falls short after 16 Qg4. }
14. Qg4 Nxd4 15. Rxd4 Qc8 16. Re4 Rf6
{ White has a strategically won game, but the technical problems are considerable. Moreover a tempting trap now stared me in the face. }
17. Be3
{ Keres suggests 17 Bf4 in the tournament book, but 17...Bd6 is an adequate reply.
I was considering the blunder 17 Bg5? Rg6 18 h4 h6 19 h5 but Trifunovich seemed too quiet all of a sudden, and I suspected he had tuned in on my brain waves. At the last minute I saw 19...We8! wins; for if 20 Bxe7 xg2+! 21 Kxg2 Qxh5. }
17... Qd7 18. Rd1 Qc6 19. Bd4 Rg6 20. Qe2 Rd8 21. g3 Qd5 { Threatening ...c5. }
22. Re1 $1 c5
{ Black's welcome to 22...Qxa2 23 b3 Qa5 (otherwise a1) 24 Rxe6 with a crushing attack. }
23. Bc3 Rd6 24. Be5 Rd8 25. Bf4 $1 { Preventing ...d2. } 25... c4
{ Again 25...Wxa2 is met by 26 b3 followed by Rxe6. Black decides to sacrifice his e-Pawn in order to get some activity. After 25...Kf7 26 b3 (threatening a4 at the right moment) leaves Black with little to do but sit back and wonder where White will penetrate next. }
26. Rxe6 Rxe6 27. Qxe6+ Qxe6 28. Rxe6 Bf6 29. Rxa6 Rd1+ 30. Kg2 Bxb2
{ After the game Gligorich suggested that 30...Rb1! offered drawing chances. It makes things harder, but White should win after 31 a4! (not 31 b3 b2) 31...xb2 (if 31...b4 32 c6 Rxb2 33 xc4 Bc3 34 Bd6!) 32 axb5 xb5 33 c6 c3 34 Re6 Kf7 35 e2 and eventually White's King marches to d3 and, after trading Bishops, captures the weak c-Pawn. }
31. Rb6 Ra1 32. Rxb5 Rxa2 33. Rc5 Ra4
{ On 33...c3 34 Be5 Ba1 35 c7 Rxc2 36 xg7+ Kf8 37 Rc7 leads to an easy win. Black's c-Pawn isn't going anywhere. }
34. Be5 Bxe5 35. Rxe5 Ra2 { On 35...a3 36 e3! Ra2 37 Rc3 wins. } 36. Re2 Kf7
37. Kf3 Kf6 38. Ke4 g5 39. Kd4 Kf5 40. f3
{ While not bad in itself, the text indicates a wrong frame of mind. White should be looking for the quickest win, not ways to prolong Black's agony.
Simply 40 Kxc4 κg4 41 Kb3 is easy. }
40... c3 $1 41. Rf2 $2
{ The simplest path is 41 xc3 a3+ 42 Kd4 xf3 43 c4, etc. (KEREs) Curiously now, I never do win his c-Pawn! }
41... Ra3 42. Kc4 h5 43. Kb4 Ra8 44. f4
{ On 44 κxc3 h4 offers a few little problems. } 44... Ke4 $1
{ I hadn't seen this defense. Now Black saves his c-Pawn and the win takes twenty moves longer than it should have. }
45. fxg5 Ke3 46. Rg2 { If the Rook leaves the second rank, then ...d2. } 46...
Kd4
{ On 46...c8 47 h4 Kf3 48 g1 Kf2 49 d1 Kxg3 50 Rd4 followed by Rc4 does the trick. }
47. Re2 Rb8+ 48. Ka4 Rg8 49. h4 Rf8 50. Re7 Rf3 51. Rd7+ Kc4 52. Rc7+ Kd4 53.
Rd7+ { Repeating moves to gain time on the clock. } 53... Kc4 54. Rc7+ Kd4 55.
Kb3 Rxg3 56. Rd7+ Ke4 57. Rh7 Kd4 58. Rxh5 Rg1 59. Rh8 Rb1+ 60. Ka4 Ra1+
{ There is no time for 60...Rb2 because of 61 g6. } 61. Kb5 Rb1+ 62. Kc6 Rg1
63. Rd8+ Kc4 64. Re8 { Threatening Re4 mate! } 64... Kb4 65. Kd5 Rd1+
{ On 65...Ka3 66 Rb8 wins. } 66. Ke6 Re1+ 67. Kf7 Rf1+ 68. Kg6 Rf2 69. h5
{ Now the h-Pawn becomes the dangerous candidate. } 69... Rxc2 70. h6 Rh2 71.
h7 c2 72. Rc8 Kb3 73. Kg7 1-0

[Event "Stockholm 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "34"]
[White "Bertok"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Title "Hanging pawns unhung"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "207"]

{ Classical theory expounds the danger of "hanging Pawns," but Fischer demonstrates here, in a revolutionary manner, that they are just as often an asset as a liability.
Bertok's errors seem insignificant, yet he drifts into a passive position. On the verge of exploiting Black's loose center, he always lacks just the one tempo needed to do so. Meanwhile, using the open b-file as a base of operations, Fischer manages to force White into a defensive posture. In the midst of this Q-side tension, the winning move (21...g5) comes unexpectedly on the opposite wing. }
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Be7
{ A refinement attributed to Petrosian, but actually played by Charousek in the 'nineties – and probably dating back even farther. }
4. Nf3
{ White, having no other good waiting move, is obliged to develop, thus restricting his option of playing this Knight to e2.
4 cxd5 exd5 5 Bf4 c6 6 e3 Bf5 7 g4 (R.Byrne's 7 Nge2! is best) 7...Be6 (7...Bg6! is better) 8 h3 was played frequently in the 1963 title match between Botvinnik and Petrosian. White is slightly better. }
4... Nf6 5. Bg5 { Back to the main line. The shadow boxing is over. } 5... O-O
6. e3 h6 { Petrosian usually omits this move (see note to Black's 8th). } 7.
Bh4 b6 { Tartakover's Defense. } 8. cxd5
{ The best procedure, opening the c-file and preparing Rcl with pressure on Black's c-pawn. An alternative is 8 Bd3 Bb7 9 0-0 Nbd7 10 Rcl c5 11 Qe2 dxc4 12 Bxc4 Ne4=. (Petrosian-Fischer, Candidates'1959.) }
8... Nxd5
{ Inferior here is 8...exd59 Bd3 and1 0 Ne5! followed by f4 with a Pillsbury attacking formation: White has g4-g5 in the air – this line is playable for Black only with his pawn on h7 (instead of h6). }
9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5
{ The text is drawish, but I had already clinched first prize. } 11. Be2
{ Sharper is 11 Rc1 Be6! 12 Qa4 c5 13 Qa3 Rc8 14 Be2 and now 14...Kf8 levels while 14...Qb7! is the prescription for maintaining tension. If 15 dxc5 bxc516 0-0 (16 Rxc5? Rxc5 17 Qxc5 Qxb2 is bad forWhite) 16...Qb6is double-edged. }
11... Be6 $1
{ The right post. At b7 this Bishop would block the b-file and obstruct later operations there. }
12. O-O c5 13. dxc5 $2
{ Producing hanging center Pawns which, in this case, exert a tremendously cramping influence on White's future development. Better is 13 Ne5 Nd7 (not 13...c4? 14 b3 b5 15 a4) with equality. }
13... bxc5 14. Qa4 Qb7 $1 15. Qa3 Nd7 16. Ne1
{ What else is there? Black's center is well-protected, and he is ready to assume the Q-side initiative with ..a5 and ..Qb4. }
16... a5 17. Nd3 c4 18. Nf4 Rfb8 19. Rab1 $2
{ White's game is already difficult, e.g., 19 Bf3 Nf6 20 Rfd1 Qxb2 21 Qxb2 xb2 22 Nxd5 Nxd523 Bxd5 Bxd524 Rxd5 c3! 25 Rdd1 (if 25 Rc5 c2 26 Rcl Rd8 wins) 25...c2 26 Rdc1 Rab8 27 KflRb1 28 Ke2 Rxa1 29 Rxa1 Rb1 wins.
Best is 19 Nxe6 fxe6 20 Bg4 Ra6! 21 b3! (if 21 Qe7? Nf8 or 21 Rab1 Qb4 22 Qc3 Qxc3 23 bxc3 Rab6) 21...xb3 22 axb3 Qxb3 23 Qe7 Nf8 24 Ra3 with good drawing chances (if 24...Qb4 25 Qxb4 Rxb4 26 Be2 Ra7 27 Rfa1 a4 28 Bd1, etc.). }
19... Bf5 $1 20. Rbd1 Nf6 21. Rd2
{ The following variation gives some insight into the nature of White's problem: 21 Bf3 Qxb2 22 Qxb2 Rxb2 23 Nxd5 Nxd5 24 Bxd5 (if 24 Rxd5 Be6 25 Rc5 Rc8! 26 Rxa5 c327 Rc1 c2 28 Be4 Rb1! 29 Rxb1 cxb1=Q+ 30 Bxb1 Rc1 mate) 24...Rc8 25 e4 Be6! 26 Bxe6 fxe6 27 a4 c3 28 Rcl c2 and White, completely tied up, must lose material. }
21... g5 $1 { Practically forcing the win of a piece. } 22. Nxd5
{ To break the hammer-lock. On 22 Nh5 Ne4 23 Rc2 Qb4 is crushing. } 22... Nxd5
23. Bxc4 { Not23Bf3? Bd3. } 23... Be6
{ Black has some temporary discomfort but it's only a matter of time before he consolidates and wins with his extra piece. }
24. Rfd1
{ Blundering a Pawn. The lesser evil is 24 Bxd5 Bxd5 25 f3 but White is still lost if Black exercises a modicum of caution. }
24... Nxe3 $1 { Threatening mate. } 25. Qxe3 Bxc4 26. h4 Re8 27. Qg3 Qe7 28. b3
Be6 29. f4 g4 { Sealing the g-file and neutralizing all threats. } 30. h5 Qc5+
31. Rf2 Bf5 0-1

[Event "Stockholm 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "35"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Bolbochan"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "A brilliant cadenza"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "212"]

{ Called upon to face his favorite defense, Fischer quickly obtains the advantage against Black's rather passive opening strategy. Bolbochan, burdened with a bad Bishop against a good Knight, defends with extreme care but is gradually forced to retreat behind his lines. Disdaining several opportunities to enter a favorable ending, Fischer presses for a quick decision in the mid-game. His judgment is rewarded when the pressure which he painstakingly has accumulated erupts in a violent attack, beginning with 34 hxg6. Fischer's invasion on the weakened squares is a model of accuracy. It culminates in a keen combination which, appropriately, earned a tie for the first brilliancy prize. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3
{ Black's loss of time with ..a6 may possibly justify this loss of time. The variation is specifically directed against the characteristic ...e5 of the Najdorf System. Thus if 6...e5 7 Nde2 Be7 (or 7...Be6 8 g4 d5 9 exd5 Nxd5 10 Bg2 with a comfortable edge) 8 g4 0-0 9 Ng3! g6 10 g5 Ne8 11 h4 with a powerful attack: e.g., 11...f6? 12 Bc4+ Kg7 13 h5 fxg5 14 hxg6 hxg6 15 Nh5+!. }
6... Nc6 { For 6...g6 see game 43. For 6...b5 see game 41. } 7. g4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4
e5 9. Qd3 Be7 { More accurate is 9...Be6 immediately. } 10. g5 $1
{ Weak is 10 b3 as played in Gereben-Geller, Budapest 1952. } 10... Nd7
{ Now the Knight interferes with the normal development of the QB. But on 10...Nh5 11 h4 followed by an eventual Be2 will cause trouble. }
11. Be3 { Sharper is 11 h4 Nc5 12 f3. } 11... Nc5 $2
{ The best chance is 11...Bxg5 12 Bxg5 xg5 13 xd6e7 14 Qxe7+ Kxe7 15 Nd5+ f8 16 0-0-0 g6 (κOTov) holding White to a minimal endgame edge. }
12. Qd2 Be6 13. O-O-O O-O 14. f3 Rc8 15. Kb1
{ Amateurs are often puzzled by this apparent loss of time. Actually it is a handy defensive move, getting out of the pin on the c-file which could become annoying after ...b5-b4. One never knows when lightning will strike! }
15... Nd7
{ The knight has no future on c5, so Bolbochan tries to bring it into play via b6. }
16. h4 b5 17. Bh3 Bxh3
{ After 17...Nb6 18 Bxb6 xb6 19 Nd5 d8 (not 19...Bxd5? 20 Bxc8) 20 Nxe7+xe7 21xd6, etc.
On 17...Re8 18 Nd5 Bf8 19 h5 with a tremendous bind. Black has to reckon with the possible breakthrough on g6. }
18. Rxh3 Nb6 19. Bxb6 Qxb6 20. Nd5
{ White has a strategically won game; his Knight cannot be dislodged. } 20...
Qd8 21. f4
{ Threatening f5. An example of some of the nonsense that has been written about my games, both by admirers and detractors, is the following (by Lublinsky) in the 1962 Russian Yearbook: "Brilliant intuition! Fischer refuses to enter into the Rook and Pawn endgame and plays to continue his attack." But White can't! Not 21 Nxe7+? Qxe7 22Wxd6?? Rfd8 and Black wins. }
21... exf4 22. Qxf4 Qd7 23. Qf5 Rcd8
{ Insufficient is 23...Rfd8? (or 23...Wxf5? 24 Nxe7+) 24 Qxd7 xd7 25 Nb6. } 24.
Ra3 $1
{ Shows how ideal the position is – White can afford the luxury of probing weaknesses on both wings. }
24... Qa7 25. Rc3
{ Tempting is 25 Nf6+!? Bxf6 (if 25...gxf6? 26 gxf6 Kh8 27 Qg5 Rg8 28 fxe7!) 26 gxf6 g6 27 Qg5 Kh8 and White has no forced win in sight.
Objectively best is 25 Nxe7+ Qxe7 26 Rxa6 Rfe8 27 a4! But I was zoping to win in the middle game. Ironically, I wouldn't have been ¿warded the brilliancy prize had I chosen the best line here. They don't ve medals for endgame technique! }
25... g6 $1
{ On 25...Qd7? 26 Rc7 wins. On 25...Rd7? 26 Nf6+! Bxf6 (26...gxf6
gxf6 Kh8 28 fxe7 wins) 27 gxf6 g6 28 Qg5 Kh8 29 Qh6 g8 c8! forces mate. }
26. Qg4 Qd7 27. Qf3 Qe6 { Not 27...Rc8? 28 Rxc8 Rxc8 29 Nb6. } 28. Rc7 Rde8
{ 28...Rd7 29 Nf4 wins. And after 28...Rfe8 29 Rf Black has -ly any moves. 29...Rc8 is answered by 30 Ra7 Ra8 31 Rxa8 Rxa8 c7. }
29. Nf4 Qe5 30. Rd5 Qh8 31. a3 h6
{ A bid for freedom – otherwise Ra7 mops up the Q-side Pawns. On 31...f6 32 Wb3! Rf7 33 xd6 fxg5 34 hxg5e5 35 Rf6! Ref8 36xf7 Rxf7 37 Rc8+ Bf8 38 Ne6 wins. }
32. gxh6 Qxh6
{ On 32...Bxh4? 33 Nxg6! fxg6 34 b3 is decisive (34...f7 35 f5). } 33. h5 Bg5
{ After 33...g5 34 Ne2 followed by Nd4(or g3)-f5 maintains a winning bind. Black also has to contend with the threat of Ra7. }
34. hxg6 $1 fxg6
{ On 34...Bxf4 35 gxf7+ Rxf7 36 xf7 Kxf7 37 Rh5! wins. BOLBOCHAN / SICILIAN DEFENSE 217 }
35. Qb3 $1 { The coup de grâce. } 35... Rxf4
{ On 35...Kh8 (or 35...Bxf4 36 Rh5+) 36 Nxg6+ Qxg6 37 xg5 f1+ (37...Qxg5 38 Qh3+ forces mate) 38 Ka2 xg5 39 h3+ Kg8 40 Qxfl leads to a win. }
36. Re5+ Kf8 37. Rxe8+ { After 37...Kxe8 38 Qe6+ Kf8 39 Qc8+ mates. } 1-0

[Event "Stockholm 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "36"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Korchnoi"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Gaston and Alphonse"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "218"]

{ "I like to coax my opponents into attacking, to let them taste the joy of the initiative, so that they may get carried away, become careless, and sacrifice material,"wrote Korchnoi, whose comments are interwoven in the notes.
Fischer needs no coaxing. He improves on a well-known Capablanca line (with 15 d5). Still, the advantage he derives, if any, is microscopic. Korchnoi seems to labor under the delusion that he has the worst of it, though Fischer keeps asserting that White has nothing. Nevertheless, he overreaches himself, giving Korchnoi a chance to assume the initiative. But Black falters and then cracks under the pressure of the clock. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3
d6 9. d4
{ An old try, championed by Yates and Alekhine, but discarded in the course of progress. It is still theoretically crucial – on its success (or failure) hinges the necessity of White's losing a tempo here with the customary 9 h3. }
9... Bg4 10. Be3 exd4
{ Gligorich's 10...d5 is probably best. On the old 10...Nxe4!? 11 Bd5 d7 12 Bxe4 d5 13 Bxh7+! Kxh7 14 dxe5, White, according to Robert Byrne, can keep his extra Pawn and weather the attack. }
11. cxd4 Na5
{ 11...d5 12 e5 Ne4 13 Nbd2 Nxd2 14 xd2 Bxf3 15 gxf3 Bb4 16 c2 Bxe1 17 Qxc6 Bb4 18 Bxd5 is known to favor White. }
12. Bc2 Nc4
{ 12...c5 may be better; 13 Nbd2 cxd4 14 Bxd4 Nc6 15 Be3 d5 16 exd5 Nb4= (Yates-Bogolyubow, New York 1924.) Hence 13 dxc5 dxc5 14 Nbd2 seems the only try for an advantage. }
13. Bc1 c5 14. b3
{ Interesting is 14 Nbd2 Nxd2 15 Wxd2 Bxf3 16 gxf3. (Geller-Panno, Amsterdam 1956.) }
14... Na5
{ Korchnoi is of a mind that the retreat 14...Nb6 completely equalizes. But White can keep a pull after 15 Nbd2 cxd4 (maybe better is 15...fd7 16 h3 Bh5 17 g4 Bg6 18 d5 Bf6 19 b1 h5! Pietzsch-Szabo, 1962) 16 h3 h5 17 g4! Bg6 18 Nxd4. (Pietzsch-Matanovich, Havana 1962.) }
15. d5 $1
{ "A strong continuation which improves on 15 Bb2 Nc6! 16 d5 Nb4 (Capablanca-Bogolyubow, London 1922) when Black gains the advantage of the pair of Bishops."(KORCHNOI.) }
15... Nd7
{ On 15...Nxe4 16 xe4 Bxf3 17 xf3 Bf6 18 Nc3 b4 19 Bb2 bxc3 20 Bxc3 Bxc3 21 Qxc3 White has a big advantage. }
16. Nbd2 Bf6
{ Aiming to strike on the dark squares before White can mobilize a K-side initiative. }
17. Rb1 c4
{ Korchnoi considers this overambitious, believing that it creates too many Pawn weaknesses. He thinks Black ought to play 17...Ne5 18 h3 Nxf3+ 19 Nxf3 Bxf3 20 Qxf3 b4; but after 21 Bf4 e8 (too passive is 21...Nb7 22 Bd3, etc.) 22 g3 Be5 23 Bxe5 xe5 (if 23...dxe5 24 Bd3) 24 f4 e7 25 Rbd1 (threatening e5) maintains the pressure. }
18. h3
{ "White does not fall for 18 b4? c3! 19 bxa5 cxd2 20 Bxd2 Ne5 when the ensuing break-up of the Pawn protection of White's King more than compensates for his extra doubled Pawn."(KoRCHNOI.) }
18... Bxf3
{ "Giving White the two Bishops, but if 18...Bh5 19 b4! is now strong: 19...c3 20 bxa5 cxd2 21 xd2 Ne5 22 g4." (KORCHNOI.)
In this line simply 19...Nb7 followed by ...a5 yields good counterplay. On 18...Bh5 I intended 19 g4! (deadening Black's Bishop is worth this weakness). 19...Bg6 20 Nf1-g3, etc. }
19. Nxf3 cxb3
{ 19...e8 20 b4 Nb7 21 Nd4 is strong. And 19...c3? 20 a3! leaves the c-Pawn artificially isolated: White can encircle it by Be3-d4, Re3, etc. Black's Knight on a5 is stranded; should it retreat to b7, then b4 smothers its future. }
20. axb3 Qc7 21. Be3
{ "Up to here, White has played in excellent style, but this inaccurate move considerably improves the Black position. White ought to play 21 Bd2 or, still better, 21 Re2! protecting the KB and preparing the powerful maneuver Nd4."(KORCHNOI.)
The truth is, White just doesn't have that much. After 21 Bd2 or Re2 Black can still reply with .. Bc3. }
21... Bc3 $1 22. Re2 b4
{ "Now Black has sufficient play on the black squares."(KORCHNOI.) } 23. Nd4
Rfe8
{ "Worried by his loose Pawn front and his scattered minor pieces, Black decides that he ought to get another piece into play rather than spend a move protecting his King's side. However, Fischer soon demonstrates that the White Knight obtains splendid prospects on the King's side, hence 23...g6 is much better." (KORCHNOI.)
On 23...g6 24 Bd3 c5 25 c2 Nab7 26 Ne2 Bg7 the Bishop dances away and White has nothing. }
24. Nf5 Nb7 25. Bd4 g6 { The threat was Nxg7. } 26. Nh6+ Kf8 27. Rc1 $1
{ "This powerful move is a reminder to Black that he has problems on the c-file as well as in the neighborhood of his King." (κORCHNOI.) }
27... Rac8
{ "In the event of White's exchanging Bishops, Black wants to be ready to recapture with a piece (Queen or Rook) rather than be left with a Pawn on c3 which will most likely be fatally weak." (KoRCHNOI.)
Not 27...Bxd428xd4f629xb4. }
28. Bd3
{ "This inaccuracy grants Black a fresh chance of recovery. A very strong continuation here was 28 Re3! with the threat of 29 Bxc3 bxc3 30 Wd4! f6 31 Bb1 when the c-Pawn would fall."(KORCHNOI.) }
28... Qa5 { On 28...d8 29 ec2 maintains the pin. } 29. Rec2 Ne5 30. Bf1 Nc5
{ "Sacrificing a Pawn for the moment; but the Black pieces obtain excellent activity."(KORCHNOI.) }
31. Bxc3 bxc3 32. Rxc3 Kg7 33. Ng4 Nxg4 34. Qxg4 Rb8
{ Breaking the pin and threatening the e-Pawn and/or the b-Pawn. Not 34...Rxe4?? 35 Qxc8.
35 f
"White's best chance is to revive his attack on the King's wing. (KORCHNOI.) }
35. Rf3 Nxe4 36. Qf4 f5
{ "36...Rb7 may be safer here."(KORCHNOI.) The text weakens the K-side, but White can't exploit it. }
37. Re3 Re5 38. Rc6 Rbe8 $5
{ "Short of time, I overlooked White's next move completely. Even so, the text is not bad, but for practical purposes Black ought to choose the simple 38...g5 maintaining a good position without risk."(KoRCHNOI.)
After 38...g5 39 Qf3 Rbe840 Rxa6 Qxd5 41 b4 the chances are approximately equal. }
39. Rxd6 $1 Qa1 $2
{ "A bad error, after which Black is two Pawns behind with no compensation. Correct and necessary is 39...g5! 40 Rd7+ Kg6 41 Qf3 Qb6! with the threat 42...Nxf2!.For instance:
A] 42 Bd3? Nxf2 43 Rxe5 Nxd3 dis. ch. B]42 g4? Nxf2 43 Rxe5 Nxg4 dis. ch. c]42 Rxe4! (best) 42...Rxe4 43 g4 Rf4 44 gxf5+ Kh6 45 Qg3 Re5 with at least a draw.
"Instead, Black panics at the unexpected turn of events, and Fischer efficiently finishes the game in a few moves."(KORCHNOI.)
Incidentally, Korchnoi neglects to add that after 41...Qb6! Black has the additional threat of ... Nf6 (as well as ...Nxf2) trapping the Rook. He also neglects to analyze the right defense: 42 Qe2! Nxf2 (What else? If 42...f4 43 Rxe4 Rxe4 44 Qc2 Kh6 45 Rc7! wins); 43 Rxe5! 224 36 / STOCKHOLM 1962
Ne4+ (it's fascinating that Black has no better discovery; if 43...Ng4+ 44 e3! Rxe3 45 Qxa6 xa6 46 Bxa6 Nf6! 47 Rd8 Rxb3=. But not 43...Nxh3++? 44 Kh2 g1+ 45 Kg3! f4+ 46 Kf3! xe5 47 Qxa6+! wins) 44 Kh2 Rxe5 45 Qxa6 xa6 46 Bxa6 Nf6 regaining the d-Pawn with a draw in view. }
40. Rxa6 Qd4 41. Rd3 Qb2 42. d6 g5 43. Qe3 f4 44. Qa7+
{ Black must lose a Rook after 44...Kf8 45 d7 Rd8 46 Qb6 κe7 47 Qxd8+ κxd8 48 Ra8+ followed by 49 d8= Q+. }
1-0

[Event "Curaçao 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "37"]
[White "Keres"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Only a draw"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "226"]

{ This contest brings to mind Emanuel Lasker's axiom: "When evenly matched opponents play correctly' the games seldom have any content and frequently end in draws." Here a little incorrect play provokes a series of brilliant moves leading to a most unusual draw.
Employing a slow, closed system against the Sicilian, Keres is strategically outplayed. By adjournment, however, he manages to achieve equality. Fischer refuses a draw, and the struggle flares anew. Working with a Rook against two minor pieces, he makes steady inroads. Each serpentine twist in the endgame, including the double error on move 56, is a joy, a revelation, and a study in itself. Keres' saving resources smack of sheer wizardry. }
1. e4 c5 2. Ne2 d6 3. g3 g6
{ Sharper is 3...d5! 4 Bg2 dxe4 5 Bxe4 (on Lombardy's 5 Nbc3 Nf6 equalizes – but not 5...f5 6 d3 exd3 7 cxd3 Nf6 8 0-0) and White's attack is worth more than a Pawn) 5...Nf6 6 Bg2 Nc6 7 0-0 e6=. }
4. Bg2 Bg7 5. O-O
{ Harmless. Correct is 5 c3 Nc6 6 d4 with a powerful center. If Black goes Pawn hunting now he gets shattered: 6...cxd4 7 cxd4 Qb6? 8 Nbc3 Nxd4? 9 Nd5c5 10xd4 Bxd4 11 Be3! Bxe3 12 fxe3 a5+ 13 b4 d8 14 Rcl b8 15 0-0 d7 16 d4 f6 17 Nc7+ Kf7 18 e5! with a strong attack. (Samarian-Wesen, corres. 1958.) The sober 6...e5 7 dxc5 dxc5 8 Wxd8+ Nxd8 9 Na3 allows White only a slightly better ending. }
5... Nc6 6. c3 e5 $1 7. d3 { Now White has to regroup in order to get in d4. }
7... Nge7 8. a3
{ A lemon, but already White must fight for equality. On 8 Be3 0-0 9 d4 exd4 10 cxd4(Pachman-Tal, Amsterdam 1964) 10...cxd4(also good is 10...d5 11 Nbc3 Bg4!) 11 Nxd4 Ne5 Black captures the initiative. }
8... O-O
{ Deciding to ignore the Q-side. Keres hoped for 8...a5 9 a4! and White has tricked Black into weakening his b5 square. }
9. b4
{ Probably played against Keres' better judgment, but I guess he wanted to justify his last move. One lemon leads to another. }
9... b6 10. f4 exf4 $1
{ Abandoning the center to play against White's shaky Pawn structure. } 11.
gxf4 { Not 11 Nxf4?(or 11 Bxf4 d5) 11...cxb4 12 axb4 Nxb4! } 11... d5 $1
{ Wrong is 11...cxb4 12 axb4 Nxb4? 13 f5! Nec6 14 d4 Na6 15 e5, etc. } 12. e5
{ Poker-faced, as always, Keres made this move as though it were the most natural one on the board. But it was the last thing he wanted to do, since it exposes the poverty of White's strategy. }
12... Bg4
{ Not bad, but 12...Nf5 à la Nimzovich is even better. After 13 Ng3 Nce7 maintains a solid blockade, and Black can break with ..f6 at his leisure. }
13. h3 Bxe2
{ g i 13.6 14 g3d715 h2 f6. The absence of Black's QB makes it difficult to exploit the white square weaknesses. }
14. Qxe2 f6 15. b5
{ The only way to keep the center from crumbling. After 15 e6 f5 the advanced e-pawn becomes a target. }
15... Na5 16. Nd2
{ Better is 16 Ra2. If then 16...fxe5 17 fxe5 Rxf1+ 18 Qxf1 Bxe5 19 Bg5! is strong. }
16... fxe5 17. fxe5 Rxf1+ 18. Nxf1
{ Any recapture proves to be awkward. Also 18 Bxflc7! 19 Nf3 (not 19 d4 cxd4 20 cxd4 c3) 19...Nb3 is similar to the game. }
18... Nb3 19. Rb1 Nxc1 20. Rxc1 Qc7 $1 21. Re1
{ Still impossible is 21 d4? cxd4 and White can't recapture because of the pin. Despite the drawing tendency of the opposite colored Bishops, White has a difficult game: he's weak on all the squares and his King is somewhat exposed. }
21... Rd8 22. Nh2
{ Black gets an iron grip after 22 d4 cxd4 23 cxd4 Nf5 followed by ... Bh6, etc. }
22... d4 23. cxd4 cxd4 24. Nf3 $2
{ A terrible boner, just when White could equalize with 24 Ng4! Rf8 25 Rf1. }
24... Bh6 $1 { Keres probably underestimated the strength of this reply. } 25.
Qa2+ Kh8 26. Qe6 Nd5 $2
{ Tempting but wrong.Correct is 26...Nf5! 27 Qf6+(if 27 Nh2? Be3+ wins) 27...Bg7 28 Qe6 Rf8 followed by ... Bh6 again, and it's just a matter of time before Black invades on the weak dark squares. For example, 29 Ng5 (not 29 Nh2 Qc3!) 29.., Bh6 30 Ne4 Be3+ 31 Kh1 Bf4 32 Nf6 Qc333 Rd1 Qc2, etc. }
27. Nh2 $1 { The saving clause. Not 27 Nxd4? Qc5. } 27... Ne3
{ After 27...Nf4? 28 Qf6+ Kg8 29 Ng4 White wins! } 28. Bc6 $1
{ Now the Queen is hemmed in and Black has nothing. The Knight on e3 must coordinate with the heavy artillery to be really meaningful. }
28... Rf8 29. Nf3 Bf4 { On 29...Qd8 30 Qd6 holds. The chances are now even. }
30. Nxd4 Bxe5 31. Nf3 Bd4 $1 32. Rxe3 { Not 32 Nxd4?? Qg3+. } 32... Bxe3+
{ On 32...Qf4 33 Kf2! holds. } 33. Qxe3 Qg3+ 34. Kf1 Qxh3+ 35. Ke1 Qf5 36. d4
Kg7 { 36...h5? 37 Qh6+ Kg8 38 Bd5+ Qxd5 39 Qxg6+ draws. } 37. Kf2 $1
{ The right plan – the King must stay on the K-side to blockade Black's Pawns. Eventually Black,to make progress, must advance; but in so doing he will expose his King to perpetual check.
Worse is 37 Qe5+ Qxe5+ 38 dxe5 Rf4 (intending Ra4). White's e-Pawn can always be stopped by the King. }
37... h5 38. Kg3 Qg4+ 39. Kh2 Rf4
{ On 39...Qf4+ 40 Qxf4 Rxf4 41 Kg3 holds. And not 39...h4? 40 Qe7+ Rf7 41 Qxf7+! }
40. Qe7+ Kh6
{ The game was adjourned and Keres sealed his move. Upon resuming the next day, he offered me a draw, which I rejected. I knew Black no longer had a winning advantage, but felt no harm could come from continuing since there was little danger of losing. Besides, winning this game would still have put me in contention for first place even as late as round 14, the halfwaymark. }
41. Qe2 Qf5 42. Qe3 g5 43. Kg2 Rg4+ 44. Kf2 Rf4 45. Kg2 Qc2+
{ Beginning a series of exploratory checks to see if White goes to the wrong square. For instance, 46 Kg3? Rg4+ 47 Kh3 Qg2 mate. Hope springs eterna1! }
46. Kh1 Qb1+ 47. Kh2 Qa2+ 48. Kh3 Qf7 49. Kh2 Qf6 50. Kg2 Kg7
{ Getting out of the potential pin. Not 50...g4? 51 Kg3. Black must strive to advance the Pawns so that they retain maximum mobility. }
51. Kg3 h4+ 52. Kg2 { 52 Kh2? loses to 52...g4. } 52... Rg4+
{ 52...g4 is refuted by 53 Nxh4! } 53. Kh1 Rg3 54. Qe4 g4 55. Nh2 Qg5 56. Nf1
$2
{ A blunder on the last move of the second time-control. Perhaps Keres has allowed me to get a little too much out of the position, but he can still hold a draw with 56 Qe5+! Qxe5 57 dxe5(threatening Bd7) 57...Rxa3 58 Nxg4, etc. }
56... Rh3+ $2
{ I had a feeling this might be a mistake, but time was short and I had to make a move – any move. "Patzer sees a check, gives a check." But now the game can no longer be won.
Correct is 56...Rxa3! 57 d5g3 58 d6 (if 58 Bd7 Ra1 59 Kg2 Ra2+ 60 Kg1 Qf6 61 Bf5 Rf2) 58...Ra1 59 Qe7+ (if 59 Kg1 Qc5+ wins) 59...Qxe760 dxe7 h3! 61 e8=N+ Kf8 wins. }
57. Kg1 Rxa3 58. d5 g3 59. Bd7 $1 Ra1
{ No longer gains a tempo, as in the last note. } 60. Bf5 $1
{ The idea is to advance the Pawn to d6 without allowing...Qc5+. I must confess that I still expected to win. But now Keres really starts to find moves! }
60... Qf6 61. Qf4 Re1 62. d6 Re5 63. Qg4+ $1
{ Not 63 d7? Rxf5 64 Qxf5 Qxf5 65 d8=Q Qf2+ and mate next. } 63... Kf8 64. d7
Rd5 { Now 64...Rxf5 65 d8=Q+! Qxd8 66 Qxf5+ draws. } 65. Kg2 $1 Rxd7 $1
{ On 65...Qb2+ 66 Kh3 Qf2 67 Be4! Qxf1+ 68 Bg2 Qf2 69 Qb4+! holds. } 66. Bxd7
$1
{ I thought this was a mistake at the time, but that he was lost anyway. Keres, however, has seen just one move further – }
66... Qf2+ 67. Kh3 Qxf1+ 68. Kxh4 g2 69. Qb4+ Kf7 $1 70. Qb3+ Kg7 71. Qg3+ Kh7
$1
{ Haven – at last. Now I was sure I had him. Surely he would go in for 72 Bf5+ Qxf5 73 Qxg2 Qf4+! 74 Qg4 (on 74 Kh3 Qh6+! wins) 74...Qxg4+ 75 Kxg4 Kg6! gaining the opposition and winning White's last Pawn by force. }
72. Qe5 $3
{ What's this? He makes no attempt to stop me from queening!? Gradually my excitement subsided. The more I studied the situation, the more I realized Black had no win. }
72... Qh1+
{ The main line also draws – by a miracle: 72...f2+ 73 Kh3 g1= (making a Knight with check also doesn't win) 74 Bf5+ Kh6 (74...Qxf5+ 75 xf5+ g6 76 Qxg6+ κxg6 77 κg4! is similar to the final note) 75 f6+ Kh5 76 Bg6+! xg6 77 g5+!! Kxg5 Stalemate! }
73. Bh3 Qxh3+ { 73...g1=74 Qh5+ g7 75 Qg6+! forces stalemate or a perpetual. }
74. Kxh3 g1=Q 75. Qe7+ Kh8 76. Qf8+ Kh7 77. Qf7+
{ A last try might have been 77...g7 78 xg7+! Kxg7 79 κg3! 2olding the "distant opposition": e.g., 79...Kf6 80 Kf4 κe6 81 Kə4 d6 82 d4c7 83 d5 b7 84 K4c7 85 Kd5 Kd7 865 and Black can't penetrate. }
1/2-1/2

[Event "Curaçao 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "38"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Keres"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Detective story"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "235"]

{ Occasionally one comes across a miraculous victory in which, despite intensive post-mortems, there seems to be no losing move or pattern, no blunder on the part of the vanquished. But how can that be possible? A loser must make a mistake somewhere, however infinitesimal, however it may evade detection. Is it Keres' opening novelty which leads him to disaster? Could his defense have been improved afterward? If so: where? Whatever the answer, the reader is invited to share the magnifying glass with Fischer and hunt for that elusive error. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 { For 9 d4 see game 36. } 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Nd7
{ "Now it is not easy to find a satisfactory continuation for White. (KERES.)
Keres' novelty, introduced on this occasion, has since become quite fashionable. I was – and still am – unimpressed. Black loses time redeveloping his knight to b6, but the K-side is weakened by its absence and it's questionable whether the Knight is not better where it stands originally. }
12. dxc5 $1
{ 12 Nbd2 was all the rage, but 12...cxd4 13 cxd4 Nc6 may equalize. But not 12 dxe5 Nxe5! with complete freedom.
"In spite of having won this game, it is probable that Fischer is not very convinced of the correctness of this continuation, because in a later game he closed the center with 12 d5."(KERES.)
According to that logic, Keres must not be convinced of the correctness of 11...Nd7 since he later varied with the old 11...Qc7 (against Gligorich at Hastings 1965). }
12... dxc5 13. Nbd2 Qc7 $2
{ This is supposed to lead to trouble. If such a natural developing move is bad, then what kind of a position is this for Black? At the time Boleslavsky in his notes gave "13...f6!=" and this cryptic evaluation apparently cowed the chess world into abandoning 12 dxc5 -- until very recently. After 13...f6 14 Nh4 ¿b6 15 Nf5 f7 (Fischer-Ivkov, Havana 1965) 16 Qg4! (instead of my 16 Nxe7+?) 16...Kh8 17 h4! threatening h5 followed by Nf3-h4 is in White's favor: e.g., 17...g6 18 Nh6 g7 19 f3, etc. }
14. Nf1 Nb6 15. Ne3 Rd8 16. Qe2 Be6 17. Nd5 $1
{ The idea is to open lines and take advantage of Black's weakened K-side. }
17... Nxd5
{ On 17...Bxd5 18 exd5 f6 19 h4! is strong. Now if 19...Nxd5 (19...Nac4 20 h5 Nd6 21 Qd3! e4 22 Rxe4! Nxe4 23 Qxe4 wins) 20 h5 (threatening 21 Qe4) is powerful. }
18. exd5 Bxd5 19. Nxe5
{ Since his early attacking days, Keres has switched to positionaldefensive chess. But this type of position is too much even for him. }
19... Ra7
{ To defend the second rank. What else can Black do? White threatens to build up with Bf4 and Rad1, and already sacrificial themes are in the air.
On 19...Bd6 20 Qd3! Bxe5 21 Qxh7+ Kf8 22 f4 wins. Or 19...Be6 20 Nxf7! Or 19...Bf8 20 Qh5 g6 (if 20...h6 21 Ng4) 21 Qh4 Bg7 (if 21...Be7 22 g3 threatening 23 Nxg6) 22 Ng4. Finally 19...f6 (19...Re8? 20 Qd3) 20 Qh5! fxe5 21 Bxh7+ Kf8 22 Rxe5 Bf7 23 Rf5 Bf6 24 Rxf6! gxf6 25 Bh6+ Ke7 26 Re1+ Be6 (if 26...Kd6 27 Bf4+ K26 28 Be4+ wins) 27 Bf5 Rd6 28 Bf4 wins. }
20. Bf4 Qb6
{ The threat was 21 Ng6. On 20...Qc8 21 ad1 quietly continues the build-up. }
21. Rad1 $1
{ Threatening 22 Rxd5 Rxd5 23 Qe4. Instead of seeking a "violent solution," my instinct told me to strengthen the position. }
21... g6
{ Eliminating stock combinations against h7, but creating new weaknesses on the dark squares. What's better? On 21...Bxa2 22 Rxd8+xd8 (if 22...Bxd8 23 Nc4!e6 24 d1 d7 25 Nd2) 23 b4! cxb4 24 cxb4 Bxb4 (the Knight can't move because of Nc6) 25 Qe4! Bxe1 26 Qxh7+ Kf8 27 Qh8+ Ke7 28 Bg5+ f6 29 Ng6+ Kd7 30 Bf5+ Kc7 31 Bf4+ wins the Queen. }
22. Ng4 Nc4
{ An attempt to bring this Knight toward the embattled sector. After 22...xa2 23 xd8+ Qxd8 24 Bh6! White has just too many threats. For example, 24...f5 (not 24...f6 25 b3! or 24...Bf8 25 Qe8 Ra8 26 Nf6+ Kh8 27 Bxf8 wins) 25 Qe5! Bd6 (if 25...Bf8 26 Qe8 Qxe8 27xe8f7 28 e5 Rf6 29 Nd7) 26 Rd1! Nc4 (if 26...fxg4 27 xd6 d7 28 Qg7+!! wins) 27 Qe6+ Kh8 (if 27...f7 28 b3 fxg4 29 bxc4 h4 30 Rxd6 Qxh6 31 Rd8+ Kg7 32 Qe5+) 28 b3! fxg4 29 bxc4 Rd7 30 Bg5! wins. }
23. Bh6
{ Some recommended the more direct 23 Nh6+ Kg724 Rxd5 Rxd5 25 Nxf7. I thought this might win at the time, but it looked speculative – and 25...Qf6! refutes. Since Black is tied up in knots, I felt sure of a patient strategicalvictory. }
23... Be6
{ 23...Nxb2 loses to 24 Rxd5! Rxd525 Be4 Rd8 26 Qxb2 f5 27 c4! (threateningQg7 mate). }
24. Bb3 $1 { Pinning the Knight and piling on the pressure. } 24... Qb8
{ To prevent White's Queen, in some variations, from penetrating to e5. } 25.
Rxd8+ Bxd8
{ Not 25...Qxd8? 26 Bxc4 Bxc4 (if 26...bxc4 27 Qe5) 27 Nf6+! Kh8 28 Qe5, etc. }
26. Bxc4 bxc4 27. Qxc4 $1
{ Cashing in! "Converting a spatial advantage into a material one." (See Evans'New Ideas in Chess.) }
27... Qd6 { Not27...Qxb2 28 Rxe6! Or 27...Bxc4?? 28 Re8 mate. } 28. Qa4 Qe7 29.
Nf6+ Kh8 30. Nd5 Qd7 31. Qe4 $1
{ Back to the old stand, angling for e5 again. } 31... Qd6
{ 31...Bxd5? 32 e8+ mates. The weakness of Black's first rank has consistently proved to be his undoing throughout the mid-game. }
32. Nf4 { 32 c4 is also good. } 32... Re7 { Token resistance. } 33. Bg5
{ 33 Bf8! wins outright. } 33... Re8 34. Bxd8 Rxd8
{ On 34...xd8 35 e5+ f6 (not 35...Kg8 36 Nd5!) 36 xc5 Bxa2 37 xe8+ Qxe8 38 c4 is the easiest path to victory. }
35. Nxe6 Qxe6 { On 35...e8 36 e5+ isdecisive. } 36. Qxe6 fxe6 37. Rxe6 Rd1+ 38.
Kh2 Rd2 39. Rb6 Rxf2 40. Rb7 $1 Rf6 41. Kg3
{ Not only is Black a Pawn behind, but his King on the first rank is cut off as well. A likely winning line is 41...Kg8 42 b4 cxb4 43 cxb4 Rd6 44 a4 Rd3+ 45 Kh2 Ra3 46 a5 Ra4 (if 46...Kh8 47 Kg1 Ra2 48 Kf1 g8 49 Ke1 xg2 50 b5 axb5 51 a6a2 52 a7) 47 Kg3 and the King invades! }
1-0

[Event "Varna Olympic 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "39"]
[White "Botvinnik"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Opening "Gruenfeld Defense"]
[Title "The confrontation"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "241"]

{ This dramatic meeting between the generations took place on board 1 after it was rumored that Botvinnik would be given a "rest day against the American team. But it was fated that Fischer, at last, albeit with Black, would have a crack at the world champion.
Walking into a prepared variation, Fischer promptly refutes it. "The reader can guess that my equanimity was wrecked," confesses Botvinnik, whose notes are incorporated here. Nervously, he proceeds to run his still tenable position downhill. But Fischer, instead of nursing his winning advantage, simplifies too quickly and reaches an adjournment where victory is problematical. After a sleepless night of analysis, Botvinnik finds a stunning defense. Fischer engages in a seemingly harmless transposition of moves (51...b5), and falls into a pit – throwing away the win he maintains was still there. }
1. c4 g6 2. d4
{ If White so desires, he can prevent the Gruenfeld by 2 Nc3 Nf6 3 e4. } 2...
Nf6 3. Nc3 d5
{ The spur of the moment. I could see by the glint in his eye that he had come well armed for my King's Indian. }
4. Nf3 { The sharpest try is 4 cxd5 Nxd5 5 e4. } 4... Bg7 5. Qb3
{ The main line, but I don't believe this early development of the Queen can give White anything. }
5... dxc4 { A solid alternative is 5...c6. } 6. Qxc4 O-O 7. e4 Bg4
{ Also interesting is Donald Byrne's provocative 7...Nc6. } 8. Be3
{ On 8 e5 Be69 d5 Bc8 followed by ...e6 equalizes. } 8... Nfd7
{ Smyslov's Variation.
So far theory has found no way to derive any clear advantage for White.
A] 9 0-0-0 Nc6 10 Be2 Nb6 11 c5 Qd6 12 h3 Bxf3 13 gxf3 f5! (13...fd8? 14 e5! [Reshevsky-Evans, Las Vegas 1965], practically forces a won endgame for White, since if 14...Qd7? 15 d5! Nxe5 16 f4) 14 d5 (if 14 e5 Qxc5 15 dxc5 f4! is adequate) 14...Ne5 15 f4 (if 15 Nb5? Qf6 16 Bd4 fxe4 17 fxe4 Qf4+, etc.) 15...Ned7 with a nice game.
B] 9 d1 Nc6 10 Qb3 e5! 11 dxe5 Bxf3 12 gxf3 Ncxe5 13 Bh3 Nxf3+ 14 Ke2! Nfe5 15 Bxd7 Nxd7 16 b5 c6 17 Qxb7 Rb8 18xd7 Rxb2+ 19 Kf1 Qxd7 (Simagin's 19...f6 has also been analyzed to a draw) 20 Rxd7 Bxc3= Evans-Fischer, US Championship 1962-3. }
9. Be2 Nc6 { Botvinnik thinks 9...Nb6 first is more accurate. } 10. Rd1 Nb6
{ 10...Bxf3 followed by 11...e5 also gives Black active play. } 11. Qc5 Qd6 $1
12. h3
{ 12 Qxd6 is answered by ...cxd6! improving Black's Pawn structure and neutralizing White's center. }
12... Bxf3 13. gxf3 Rfd8
{ Wrong is 13...Qxc5 14 dxc5 Bxc3+ 15 bxc3 Na4 16 Kd2! Botvinnik is of the opinion that 13...e6 (FURMAN) gives Black an equal game. But I feel it is not in the hypermodern spirit, which is precisely to tempt White into advancing his center Pawns in the hope they will become overextended. }
14. d5
{ Opening the diagonal for Black's KB can't be right, but White is still striving for an opening advantage. On 14 e5 Qxc5 15 dxc5 Rxd1+ 16 Kxd1 Nd7 17 f4 g5! 18 fxg5 Bxe5=. Or 14 Qxd6 (if 14 Nb5 Qxc5 15 dxc5 Na4!) 14...cxd6=. }
14... Ne5 15. Nb5
{ "At once 15 f4 is not good because of 15...Nec4 16 Bxc4 Qxc5 17 Bxc5 Nxc4 18 e5 Nxb2 19 Rd4 f6! and White's central position breaks up." (BOTVINNIK.)
On 15 f4 also playable is simply 15...Ned7 16 Qb5 (16 Qxd6 cxd6 gives Black a comfortable ending) 16...e5! 17 f5 (17 dxe6 e.p. Bxc3+! 18 bxc3 Qxe6) with equal chances. }
15... Qf6 $1
{ Weak is 15...Qxc5 16 Bxc5 c6 17 Nc7 (not 17 Nxa7? Na4) 17...Rab8 18 Bxe7 Rd7 19 d6 Nc8 20 Ne8! (not 20 f4 Nxe7 21 fxe5 Bxe5 22 dxe7 Rxe7) 20...Nxe7 21 Nxg7 Kxg7 22 f4! }
16. f4 Ned7 17. e5
{ On 17 Qxc7? Qxb2 White can't castle and 18...Qb4+ is threatened.
"When I was preparing to meet Smyslov, I, of course, made a thorough analysis of the Smyslov System in general and of the position on the diagram in particular! Here I reckoned that whether the Black Queen went to h4 or f5, it would be in danger; for example, 17...Qf5 18 Qb4 a5 19 Qd4 threatening 20 Bg4 or 17...Qh4 18 Qc2 g5 19 Rd4!
Alas, my opponent found a third continuation!"(BOTVINNIK.) }
17... Qxf4 $1
{ "A very unpleasant surprise – now White really had to start playing. ¯p to here I had only had to remember my analysis, though that was zot so easy. I had a recollection of the Black Queen being trapped somewhere on the K-side; and following this track I managed to recall he whole variation. At last everything was in order – on the board was -he familiar position; then suddenly it was obvious that in my analysis I had missed what Fischer had found with the greatest of ease at the oard. The reader can guess that my equanimity was wrecked.
However, if you assess 17...Qxf4 from an objective point of view, then although it is the best way out for Black, as you will see from what comes later, his position is still difficult."(BOTVINNIK.)
When I made this move, I felt sure he had overlooked it. }
18. Bxf4
{ Black's last is tactically justified after 18 Qxb6 Qe4! 19 f3 Qh4+! 20 Bf2 Qb4+ followed by ..axb6! (toward the center). }
18... Nxc5 19. Nxc7 Rac8 20. d6 exd6 21. exd6 { Not 21 Rxd6? Ncd7. } 21... Bxb2
{ "So Black has won a Pawn; but the Knight on c7 and the Pawn on d6 confine his Rooks – and also, White has two Bishops. The first thing White must do is complete his development." (BOTVINNIK.) }
22. O-O Nbd7
{ "A bad mistake; evidently, Black overestimated his possibilities. Of course, he had to prepare the move ... Be5; the only way this could be done was by 22...Ncd7 and after 23 Bf3 Be5 24 Bxe5 Nxe5 25 Bxb7 Rb8 White has no more than a minimal advantage.
Now White has the two important squares d5 and c5 at his disposa1 and his spatial advantage becomes crushing."(BOTVINNIK.)
Needless to add, I couldn't disagree more. Why should Black return the Pawn? }
23. Rd5
{ Archives recommends 23 Nd5 but after 23...Kg7 the burden of proof rests with White – he's a Pawn down. }
23... b6 24. Bf3 $2
{ "Feeble play. Actually, White had played quite consistently so far and here he could have deployed his force with maximum efficiency by 24 Bc4! with the threat of e1-e7.
The bishop is out of it on f3 and merely becomes an object of attack. Black now frees himself. and a Pawn down White is in a critica1 position." (BOTVINNIK.)
After 24 Bc4! it is true that White has a bind, but with 24...Ne6 Black can practically force a draw, if he wants it, after 25 Bh2 Nd4 threatening ... Nf6) 26 b1 Bc3 27 c1 Bb2, etc. }
24... Ne6 $1
{ "Apparently, this forces the exchange of the Knight on c7, for 25 Bh2 d4 26 Bg2 Nf6 is very bad for White. In fact, even here 26 Rxd4! pointed out by Geller) 26...Bxd4 27 e1 gave White a real chance to zet out of all his troubles. A second error running makes his position 2opeless."(BOTVINNIK.)
The reader is invited to judge for himself whether, in Geller's line, White has any real compensation for the exchange and a Pawn. Here 7...Bc5 followed by ... Nf6 or ... Nf8 should extricate Black. }
25. Nxe6 $2
{ This really took me aback. After 25 Be3 at least White's still in the game. }
25... fxe6 26. Rd3
{ On 26 Rdd1? (or 26 Rd2? Bc1 27 d4 e5) 26...Rf8! 27 Bg4 Rxf4 28 Bxe6+ Rf7 wins. }
26... Nc5 27. Re3 { Bad is 27 Rd2 Rf8 28 d7 Rcd8. } 27... e5
{ "The simplest. If 27...Bd4 28 Ra3 e5 29 Bg5 xd6 30 Be7 Rd7 31 Bg4 Black lost the exchange." (BOTVINNIK.) }
28. Bxe5
{ Just leads to a dead lost ending. I expected 28 Rxe5!? (hopeless though it is) to try and keep a little "dynamic imbalance.' 99 }
28... Bxe5 29. Rxe5 Rxd6 30. Re7 Rd7 31. Rxd7
{ On 31 Rfel Rcc7! flushes White off the 7th rank. } 31... Nxd7 32. Bg4
{ "A pointless move, since White cannot go into the lost Rook and Pawn ending; he should have played at once 32 Re1 Kf8 33 Re3 (or 33 Bd5) – Black would still have had technical difficulties. (BOTVINNIK.) }
32... Rc7 33. Re1 Kf7 34. Kg2
{ "And now 34 Be6+ was preferable, as the Bishop is poorly posted ε: g4."(BOTVINNIK.) }
34... Nc5 35. Re3 Re7 36. Rf3+
{ "White's best practical chance consisted in an exchange of Rooks and a position with his King on d4 (or e3), his Bishop on c2 and his f-Pawn at f4. But all that is impossible – 36 Kf3 h5! and White loses his Bishop."(BOTVINNIK.) }
36... Kg7 37. Rc3 Re4 38. Bd1 Rd4
{ "Before this I considered the game completely hopeless for me, but the text move gave me new heart; why had my opponent allowed my Bishop to get to a good post (and the only good one!) at c2? Surely, by 38...Re1! (39 Bc2 Rc1) White's defenses could have been completely disorganized." (BOTVINNIK.)
Anel Jo1 smpry 1 1s mor lugia. }
39. Bc2 Kf6 40. Kf3 Kg5
{ "In general terms, d6 is the best square for the King; for then the Knight would not need to defend the b-Pawn and Black would win by advancing his Q-side Pawns. But this maneuver is also not bad. (BOTVINNIK.) }
41. Kg3 Ne4+
{ "Could have led to an immediate draw -- and just at the very moment when Black was nearing his goal. You see, White was already in zugzwang: against a King move Black plays ... Kh4 and ... Ne6-f4(xh3); if Bb1, the reply ...d1 wins; and if the White Rook moves from its place, then ...Rc4 is decisive. So after, for instance, 41...Rb4 42 a3 Rd4 43 f3 a5 White would have had no satisfactory reply.
It is psychologically understandable why Black decided on the Rook and Pawn ending – earlier (see White's 23rd move) White had avoided it. But there is a difference between these two endings – the Black King is in a bad position on g5." (BOTVINNIK.)
Although I agree that Black can win by keeping the minor pieces and gradually improving his position, the text should also produce the same result, if only by a hair's breadth. }
42. Bxe4 Rxe4 43. Ra3
{ "Natural and bad. White is now in danger of defeat again. 43 Rc7!! was essential, and White gets a draw as in the game. The fact that his Pawn is on a2 and not a4 has no significance." (BOTVINNIK.)
After 43 Rc7 Ra4 44 Rxh7 Ra3+! 45 f3 (if 45 Kg2 Rxa2 46 Rb7 Ra1 47 Kf3 Kf5 48 Rf7+ Ke5 49 Rg7 b5 gains a tempo over the game because White's pawn is on a3 instead of a4) 45...xa2 46 h4+ leads to the same ending as the game, except White has already played f3 which Botvinnik, for some reason, carefully avoided. So the difference may be significant. }
43... Re7
{ "Perhaps 43...a5! 44 b3 b4 was better; Black obtained either a won Rook and Pawn ending or – after 45 xb4 axb4 46 f4+ Kf5 47 Kf3 6 48 K4 (48 Kg4 h6) 48.…. Kd6 49 d4 b5 50 Kd3 d5 – a probably won Pawn ending."(BOTVINNIK.) }
44. Rf3 Rc7
{ "His last chance consisted in improving the position of his King by the maneuver ... Kh6-g7." (BOTVINNIK.) }
45. a4
{ "Here Black sealed a move. White's threat is to exchange a pair of Pawns on the Q-side by a5 (for example, 45...:c4 46 a5 bxa5 [or 46...b5 47 f7] 47 f7 a6 48 h4+ Kh6 49 d7) after which the weakness of Black's h-Pawn together with the unfortunate position of his King would guarantee the draw.
The most subtle move was 45...Kh6, although even then Black gets nowhere after 46 d3! Rc547 h4a5 48 d4.
What happened in the game is also most probably a draw.' (BOTVINNIK.)
The game was officially "drawn" at breakfast. The Russian team had a table near the American team. Someone inquired of Botvinnik what he thought of the adjourned position. Hardly looking up from his plate, the world champion shrugged, "Nichia"(draw). The word quickly spread, and I overheard someone at the English table saying: "The Russians said Fischer could have won before adjournment... }
45... Rc5 46. Rf7 Ra5 47. Rxh7 $1
{ A very fine idea, found during overnight analysis by Geller. Because his bad King position Black finds it difficult to mobilize his :onnected passed Pawns."(BOTVINNIK.)
This was the first defense I had considered! Passive play is hopeless: g., 47 f4f548 c4 f7 followed by .. f5 and Black brings his King to the Q-side. }
47... Rxa4 48. h4+ $1
{ I had analyzed mainly 48 f4+ but Botvinnik's line is best and also contains a sly trap. }
48... Kf5
{ "Or 48...Kf6 49 Rb7! Ra5 50 Kg4 b5 51 f4 a6 52 Rb6+ Kf7 53 Rb7+ and White is quite safe." (BOTVINNIK.) }
49. Rf7+ Ke5 50. Rg7
{ "The weakness of the g-Pawn and the a-Pawn gives White sufficient counterplay."(BOTVINNIK.) }
50... Ra1 51. Kf3 b5 $2
{ Originally Botvinnik wrote in Chess Life: "This is a mistake in analysis. But even after 51...Kd5! 52 Rxg6 b5 53 Kə2 κc4 54 h5 b4 55 g4+ Kb5 (if 55...Kc3 or 55...Kb3 then 56 Rh4 followed by h6-h7 is very strong) 56 Kd3 the White King reaches the Q-side and it is easy to credit the draw. 99
Later in the 1962 Russian Yearbook, he analyzed the entire ending more exhaustively and came to the conclusion, after considerable soulsearching, that it was drawn even against the best line: 51. Kd4! The bracketed comments are mine. "52 Rxg6 b5 53 h5 b4 54 Kg2 b3 55 h6 b2 56 h7 Rh1! 57 Kxh1 b1=Q+ 58 Kh2 Qb8+ 59 Kg1 Qh8 [59...e5! seems to do the trick: if 60 Rg8 Qe1+ 61 Kh2 Qxf2+ 62 Kh3 Qf3+! forces the win of the h-pawn or if 60 Kf1 Qh2 61 Rg7 a5 62 Ra7 Kd3!] 60 Rg4+ Kc3 61 h4 a5 62 Kg2 Kb3 63 h3+ Kc2 64 Rh4 a4 65 Rxa4 Qg7+! (if 65...Qxh7 66 Rg4-g3 and the Rook holds the third rank, shuttling to e3, if necessary, keeping Black's King out – with a draw);66 Kf1 Qxh7 wins, since White can't get his Rook back to the third rank: e.g., 67 Ra2+ Kb3 [68 Ra5 seems to hold here] or 67 Ra3 Qh1+ 68 Ke2 Qd1+ 69 Ke3 Qc1+ wins the Rook. Or 67 Rg4 Qh1+ 68 Rg1 (if 68 Ke2 Qd1+ wins the Rook) 68...Qh3+ 69 Rg2 (if 69 Kel Qf3 forces mate) 69...Kd2 70 Kg1 Kel wins (71 Rg3 Qf1+).
Was it really true that the adjourned position was lost? Was I mistaken?"(BOTVINNIK.)
Botvinnik then went on to give a corrected analysis which, as we shall see, also falls short. 51...Kd4! 52 Rxg6 b5 53 h5 b4 54 h6! (instead of his previous 54 Kg2) 54...b3 (if 54...Rh1 55 Kg2! Rh5 56 Ra6 b3 57 Rxa7 Rxh6 58 Rb7 Kc4 59 Kf3 leads to a theoretica1 draw) 55 Rg4+ (if 55 h7 Rh156 Rg7 a5 wins) 55...Kc5! (not 55...Kc3? 56 Rh4 and White queens with check; or 55...Kd3? 56 Rb4 Kc2 57 Rc4+ draws) 56 Rg5+ Kc6! [Here I break camp with Botvinnik, only to meet at the next diagram. He gives 56...Kb4 overlooking that White can obtain animmediate draw with 57 Rg7! b2 (57...a5? loses to 58 Kg2!) 58 h7 Rh1! 59 Rxa7 Kb3 60 Rb7+ Kc2 61 Rc7+ Kd2 62 Rb7, etc.] 57 Rg6+ Kb7! 58 Rg7+ (if 58 Rg4 a5 wins) 58...Ka6! (the idea is to keep the King off the b-file so that White's Rook can't check from behind) 59 Rg6+ (if 59 Kg2 b2 60 h7 b1=Q 61 h8=Q Qe4+! and White is bombardedwith checks which lead to probable mate, certainly win of material) 59...Ka5!(not 59...Kb5? 60 Rg7 a5? 61 Kg2! wins) 60 Rg5+ (if 60 Rg7?b2 61 Rxa7+ Kb6 wins) 60...Ka4! (finally Black has crawled up along the a-file) 61 Rg4+ (61 Rg7 a5 62 Rb7 Rh1is easy; or61Rh5 b2 62 h7 b1=Q63 h8=Q Qd3+ 64 Kf4 Re1! is the pause that refreshes – White is checkless – if 65 Re5 Qd4+ 66 Kf5 Qxf2+ 67 Ke6 Qb6+ is decisive) 61...Ka3 62 Rh4 b2 63 h7 b1=Q64h8=Q.
FISCHER
Possible position after 64 h8= Q
(analysis)
BOTVINNIK
Botvinnik also reached this position in his analysis independently, and concluded that it was a draw. However, it is precisely here, in this barren wilderness, that Black can wend his way to a win.
Correct is 64...Qb3+! 65 Ke2 (if 65 Kf4 Qf7+ or 65 Kg2 Qd5+ 66 f3 Qd2+) 65...Qd1+ 66 Ke3 Rb1!! 67 Qf8+ (not 67 Qc3+? Rb3 or 67 Rh3 Ka2! 68 Qg8+ Qb3+ wins) 67...Ka2 and White's King will be without shelter from the coming avalanche of checks.
Now to return to the dreary (for me) game. }
52. h5 $1
{ "Now Black is left with two RP's and the draw becomes a question of theory."(BOTVINNIK.)
The move I overlooked. 52 Rxg6 Kd4 transposes into the note to Black's51st. }
52... Ra3+ 53. Kg2 gxh5
{ Botvinnik visibly relaxed. I had played right into his hands. } 54. Rg5+ Kd6
55. Rxb5 h4 56. f4 Kc6 57. Rb8 $1 h3+ 58. Kh2 a5 59. f5 Kc7 60. Rb5 Kd6
{ "Generally speaking, this ending would be drawn even without the f-Pawn – any textbook on the endgame will tell you this."(BOTVINNIK. }
61. f6 Ke6 62. Rb6+ Kf7 63. Ra6 Kg6 64. Rc6 a4 65. Ra6 Kf7 66. Rc6 Rd3 67. Ra6
a3 68. Kg1
{ "Too many mistakes?' the reader may justly ask. Yes, there were rather a lot!" (BOTVINNIK.) }
1/2-1/2

[Event "Varna Olympic 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "40"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Najdorf"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "The Najdorf Variation"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "255"]

{ The durable Najdorf Variation remains Fischer's favorite, and he constantly experiments when confronted with it. Here, against the originator of the defense, he employs an unorthodox continuation. Najdorf counters sharply, launching an early struggle.
In order to preserve the initiative, Fischer gambits a Pawn with 7 Nd5. Najdorf unwisely declines, only to accept three moves later under more unfavorable circumstances. He loses his way in the complications, allowing a devastating sacrifice which pins his King in the center. Although Najdorf defends with precision, it is too late to compensate for his earlier dilatory tactics. He finds himself ensnarled in a mating net after twenty-four moves. Rather than prolong his agony, the grand old master tenders his resignation. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3
{ For 6 Bg5 see games 9 and 15. For 6 Be2 see games 4 and 42. For 6 Bc4 see games 17, 55, 58. }
6... b5 $5
{ The sharpest reply. For 6...g6 see game 43. For 6...Nc6 see game 35. } 7. Nd5
$5
{ The idea is to exploit the hole on c6. Najdorf apparently underestimated the strength of this "eccentric" move which appears to violate principle by moving the same piece twice.
A good alternative was 7 a4. }
7... Bb7 $2
{ Black's subsequent troubles can be traced to this. No better is 7...Nbd7?? 8 Nc6 winning the Queen. And on 7...Nfd7 8 Bg5! h6? 9 Ne6!
On 7...e6 8 Nxf6+ xf6 9 c4 b4 Black's Queen is misplaced and his Q-side has been weakened.
Unclear are the complications stemming from 7...Nxe4! 8Wf3 Nc5 and White is confronted with 2 main lines:
A] 9 Nf6+? gxf6 10 xa8 Bb7 11 a7 Qc7 (or 11...e5 12 b4 exd4 13 bxc5 Qe7+ 14 Be2 Nc6 15 b6 dxc5 16 0-0! is good for White) 12 b4 Ncd7 and Black has excellent play for the exchange.
B] 9 b4! e6 (not 9...Nb7? 10 Qc3! but interesting is 9...Ncd7 10 c3! a7 11 Bg5!? or even 11 Bxb5) 10 bxc5 (if 10 Nf6+ Qxf6 11 xa8 xd4 12 xb8 xa1 13 xc8+ Ke7) 10...exd5 11 xd5 a7=. }
8. Nxf6+ gxf6 9. c4 $1
{ White must play sharply, else his advantage evaporates. After the me 9 Bd3, Nd7 is tenable. }
9... bxc4
{ Had Najdorf correctly evaluated the results of this decision, he would zave chosen 9...b4. The 1962 Russian Yearbook gives 9...Bxe4 10 cxb5 Bg7 11 g4 Bg6 12 Nf5 with advantage. But 12...0-0 is unclear. }
10. Bxc4 Bxe4
{ On 10...Qa5+ 11 Bd2 We5 12 Wb3! xe4+ 13 Kd1 White has a very strong attack. }
11. O-O d5 12. Re1 $1 e5
{ A] 12...Rg8 13 xe4! dxe4 14 Qh5 Rg7 (if 14...Rg6 15 xh7 Rg7 16 xe4 a7 17 Nf5 or Bf4) 15 Nf5, etc.
B] 12...e6 13 Qh5 Bg6 14 Qxd5 xd5 15 Bxd5 Ra7 16 Bf4 d7 17 Nxe6 fxe6 18 Bxe6 Nc6 19 ac1, etc.
c] 12...h5 13 xe4! dxe4 14 b3!xd4 15 Be3 with a winning attack.
D] 12...Nd7 13 Nc6 Qc7 14 xd5, etc. B] 12...Bxg2 13 Kxg2 dxc4 14 f3 Nd7 15 f5 g8+ (if 15...e6 16 xe6+! fxe6 17 h5 mate) 16 Kh1 e5 (if 16...e6 17 c6 threatening 18 Rxe6+) 17 Be3 with a winning bind despite the two-Pawn deficit.
F] Relatively best is 12...dxc4 13 Rxe4 Qd5 14 f3 e6. }
13. Qa4+ $1
{ Inferior is 13 Rxe4 dxe4 14 Qa4+ d7 15 Bb5 axb5 16 Qxa8 exd4 17 Qxb8+ κe7, etc. }
13... Nd7
{ On 13...Qd7 14 Bb5! axb5 15 Qxa8 Bd6 16 Rxe4! dxe4 17 Qxe4 followed byNf5 with a powerful bind. }
14. Rxe4 $1 dxe4
{ 14...dxc4 15 Nf5 leads to the same type of position as the game, except Black is without any material compensation. }
15. Nf5 $1
{ Perhaps Black had hoped for 15 Qb3 Qb6 16 Bxf7+ Kd8 with some chances for survival. }
15... Bc5 16. Ng7+ $1 Ke7 { On 16...Kf8 17 Bh6 Kg8 18 Qb3 is murder. } 17. Nf5+
Ke8 { Back where we started – but Black has lost the right to castle. } 18. Be3
{ Tal suggested 18 Bh6 but after 18...Ra7 19 Rd1 Qb6 Black is still alive. The text robs Black of any possible counterplay. }
18... Bxe3 19. fxe3
{ The exchange of Bishops has failed to ease Black's defensive task. The threat of Nd6+ is now in the offing. }
19... Qb6 20. Rd1 $1
{ Again after 20 Bxf7+ Kd8! 21 Rd1 Qb5 White has no immediate forced win. }
20... Ra7 21. Rd6 $1
{ The crusher! Either 21 Bxf7+ Kd8 or 21 Nd6+ Ke7 allow resistance. } 21... Qd8
{ Best under the circumstances. On 21...Qc7 22 Rxf6 wins. And on 21...Qxb2 22 Bxf7+ (finally!) 22...Kxf7 (if 22...Kd8 23 Qa5+ Kc8 [23...Rc7 24 Be6 wins] 24 Ne7+ Kb8 25 Nc6+ Ka8 26 Nxa7) 23 Rxd7+ Rxd7 24 Qxd7+ Kg6 25 Qg7+ Kxf5 26 Qg4 mate. }
22. Qb3 Qc7 { On 22...Rf8 23 Ng7+ Ke7 24 Qa3! is very decisive. } 23. Bxf7+ Kd8
{ On 23...Kf8 24 Bh5 and mates. } 24. Be6
{ Najdorf has no taste for prolonging the torture. If, for example, 24...b7 25 Qa4 Qc8 26 Qa5+ κe8 27 Qxa6 Kd8 28 Bxd7 Rxd7 29 xd7+ Qxd7 (29... xd7 30 Qd6+ Ke8 31 e7 mate) 30xf6+ κc7 31 Qxe5+ κb6 32 xh8 with a winning endgame. }
1-0

[Event "Varna Olympic 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "41"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Robatsch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Center Counter Defense"]
[Title "A bright cameo"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "261"]

{ Facing one of Robatsch's pet lines, Fischer proceeds to institute such a crisp attack that one is reminded of Morphy in his heyday. Noteworthy are White's 5th and 6th, practically refuting the whole variation.
Seeking safety for his King, Robatsch makes the mistake of castling too early. Fischer, already castled on the opposite wing, incurs no risk advancing his K-side Pawns, using them as battering rams to pry open the g-file. Robatsch is unable to effect a closure and Fischer rushes into the breach – compelling resignation in only twenty moves. }
1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5
{ This old move is considered weak but Black has a new twist in mind. The modern way is 2...Nf6. Then White has the choice of either 3 c4 or 3 Bb5+ to hold the Pawn, or simply 3 d4. Against Bergrasser at Monaco 1967, I chose 3 Bb5+ Bd7 4 Bc4 Bg4 5 f3 Bf5 (safer is 5...Bc8) 6 g4! Bc8 7 Nc3 Nbd7 8 g5 Nb6 9 Bb5+ Nfd7 10 f4 Nxd5 11 Nxd5 c6 12 Bc4 cxd5 13 Bxd5 and White's extra Pawn should prevail. }
3. Nc3 Qd8
{ A hypermodern approach, championed by Bronstein. The idea is to give up the center and then play against it. Seidman, as Black, played the more traditional 3...a5 against me in the 1958-9 US Championship, which continued: 4 d4 Nf6 5 Nf3 Nc6 6 d5!? (possibly an improvement over the usual 6 b5) 6...Nb4 7 b5+ c6 (more crucial is 7...d7 8 Bxd7+ Nxd7 9 a3 Nf6 10 axb4 xa1 11 0-0 a6 12 e1 with a terrific attack. Not 12...0-0-0? 13 Ne5) 8 dxc6 bxc6 9 Ba4 Ba6? (9...Bd7 is necessary) 10 a3! Rd8 11 Bd2 f5 12 axb4 Rd6 13 Bb3 Ne4 14 xa6 xd2 15cl xc316 bxc3 Rd617 0-0 Black resigns. }
4. d4 g6 $5
{ The idea is to reserve the option of developing the KN to h6 followed by ... Nf5 with pressure on the d-Pawn. After the game Robatsch told me he'd enjoyed excellent results with this system. }
5. Bf4 $1
{ Against the pedestrian 5 Nf3 (or 5 Bc4 Bg7 6 Nf3 Nh6) 5...Bg7 6 h3 Nf6 (not 6...Nh6 7 g4! Sokolsky) White holds no more than a minimal edge. }
5... Bg7 { On 5...Nh6 6 Be5! f6 7 Bf4 messes up Black's Pawns. } 6. Qd2 $1
{ Ignoring the "threat." Weak is 6 Nb5 Na6 followed by ...c6, etc. (Bronstein-Kholmov, USSR 1959). And 6 Nf3 Nh6 would permit Black the setup he is striving for. }
6... Nf6
{ Stymied, Black can no longer play ... Nh6. The main line is 6...Qxd4 7 Qxd4 Bxd4 8 Nb5 Bb6 (forced) 9 Nxc7+ Bxc7 10 Bxc7 with the two Bishops and all the chances. Another possibility is 6...Bxd4? 7 0-0-0 Nc6 8 Bb5 Bd7 9 Nd5! (not 9 Bxc6? Bxc6 10 Qxd4? Qxd4 11 Rxd4 Bxg2) 9...e5 10 Nf3 and Black will never get out of the opening alive. }
7. O-O-O c6
{ Better is 7...Nd58 Be5 (8 Bh6!? Bxh6 9xh6 Dxc3 ruptures White's Pawns) 8...0-0 9 h4 h5 10 Nge2 with a clear advantage but no forced win. }
8. Bh6 O-O $2
{ Castling into it – with a vengeance. Black should strive to castle long with 8...Bxh6 9xh6 Bf5. }
9. h4 Qa5 10. h5 $1
{ The attack plays itself. My experience with this line dates back to the Dragon-slaying days (see game 2). }
10... gxh5
{ Horrible, but Black must keep the h-file closed one way or another. On 10...Rd8 11 hxg6 fxg6 12 Bxg7 Kxg7 13 h6+ Kg8 14 Nf3-g5 is slaughter.
Or 10...Nxh5 11 Be2 Nf6 12 Bxg7 Kxg7 13 h6+ Kg8 14 g4! Rd8 15 g5 Nh5 16 Bxh5 gxh5 17 xh5 Bf5 (or 17...f5 18 g6! xg6 19 Rg5) 18 g6! wins.
On 10...Bf5 11 f3 (threatening g4) 11...Bxh6 (11...gxh5? 12 g5 wins) 12 Qxh6 gxh5? 13 g5+ h8 14 Bd3 wins a piece. }
11. Bd3 { It's important to exclude Black's Bishop from f5. } 11... Nbd7
{ Not 11...Bf5? 12 Qg5. } 12. Nge2
{ Taking advantage of the lull to bring out the reserves. } 12... Rd8 13. g4 $1
Nf8
{ On13...Nxg4 14 Rdg1!(threatening both f3 and/or Rxg4+) wins at least a piece. Black hopes to hang on by clustering minor pieces around his King. }
14. gxh5 { Now the open g-file becomes the new base of operations. } 14... Ne6
15. Rdg1 Kh8 { Also hopeless is 15...Kf816 Bxg7+ Nxg7 17 Qh6 Ng4 18 Qxh7. } 16.
Bxg7+ Nxg7 17. Qh6 Rg8 { Or 17...Ne6 18 Nf4! forces mate. } 18. Rg5 Qd8
{ Or 18...Nf5 19 Rxg8+ Nxg8 20 Qf8 followed by Rg1 is tasty. } 19. Rhg1 Nf5
{ Blundering a piece. But Black is completely tied up, and it's a pity he didn't allow the prettier finish after 19...Qf8 20 d5! Bd7 (if 20...cxd5 21 Nxd5 Nxd5 22 xh7 mate) 21 d6! Nf5 22 xf8 Raxf8 (or 22...Rgxf8 23 Bxf5 h6 24 dxe7 Rfb8 25 Rg7 Bxf5 26 Rxf7, etc.) 23 Bxf5 Rxg5 24 Rxg5 h6 25 dxe7 Rb8 26 Rg3! Bxf5 27 Rf3 winning a piece. }
20. Bxf5 1-0

[Event "Varna Olympic 1962"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "42"]
[White "Unzicker"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Playing by ear"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "266"]

{ This game illustrates the hazard of trying to rely solely on natura1 talent, without detailed knowledge of the latest opening innovations. Seldom is a chess master so drastically punished, as is Unzicker here, for failing to do his homework.
Disastrously pursuing a line with which Tal had just barely survived against Fischer, Unzicker blunders further through apparent unfamiliarity with Geller's improvement (15 Kh1!). That had previously defeated Fischer at Curaçao. Unzicker simply puts his trust in "natural moves" and drifts into a constrained position, allowing Fischer to penetrate neatly on the weakened squares. The early decision, on move 26, comes as a surprise only to Unzicker. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2
{ On and off, White resorts to this solid and still respected system (championed by Smyslov) whenever the sharper tries fail. }
6... e5
{ Black's expectation in this Najdorf Variation is that his control of important squares, with possibilities of Q-side expansion, will more than compensate for the slight weakness of his backward d-Pawn. }
7. Nb3 Be6
{ To provoke f4-f5, weakening White's e-Pawn. For the non-committa1 7...Be7 see game 4. }
8. O-O Nbd7 9. f4 Qc7 10. f5 Bc4 11. a4 { To hinder ...b5. } 11... Be7
{ Better than 11...c8? 12 a5 Be7 13 Bxc4 xc4 14 Ra4!c7 15 Be3 h6 16 Rf2 with a bind. (Schmid-Evans, Varna 1962.) }
12. Be3 O-O 13. a5
{ A critical alternative is 13 g4 d5! 14 exd5 (if 14 g5 d4! or 14 Nxd5 Nxd5 15 exd5 Nf616 d6? Bxd6 17 Bxc4xc4 18xd6?xg4+ 19 Kh1 e4+) 14...Bb4 15 g5 Bxc3 16 gxf6 Bxb2 17 fxg7 Rfd8 18 b1 Bc3. White's Pawns are overextended and his King is exposed. }
13... b5 { Too passive is 13...h6 14 g4 Nh7 15 Bf2 followed by h4. } 14. axb6
Nxb6 15. Bxb6 $2
{ At Curaçao 1962, Geller had found the right line: 15 Kh1! Rfc8 16 Bxb6 Qxb6 17 Bxc4 Rxc4 18 Qe2 b419 Ra2! and Black is hardpressed to defend his a-Pawn, but 19...h6! 20 Rfa1 Bf8 21 xa6 xa6 22 Rxa6b7 23 Na5 Qc7 24 Nb3 Qb7 (ZUCKERMAN) =. }
15... Qxb6+ 16. Kh1 Bb5 $1 { Intending ... Bc6 followed by ..a5. } 17. Bxb5
{ White has already dissipated his theoretical advantage. He should settle for 17 Nxb5 axb5 18 Wd3 with opposite colored Bishops. }
17... axb5 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Ra4 $1
{ Avoids conceding the a-file and puts pressure on the e-Pawn. } 20. c3 Qa6 21.
h3
{ It's hard for White to hit upon a constructive plan. At Curaçao 1962, Tal played against me 21 Rad1 Rc822 Nc1 b4 23 Nd3!? (White's in a bad way anyhow) 23...bxc3 24 bxc3 and now 24...Rxc3 (instead of my 24...a5 lemon) wins outright. If 25 Nxe5 dxe5 26 Qxe5 (26 Qd8+ Bf8!) 26...Bb4!27xc3xf1+!(KMOCH). }
21... Rc8 22. Rfe1 h6 $1 { A handy luft, as becomes apparent later. } 23. Kh2
Bg5 24. g3 $2 { Creating more K-side weaknesses. Better is 24 Rad1. } 24... Qa7
$1 25. Kg2 Ra2 26. Kf1
{ What else? On 26 Rxa2xa2 27 e2xc3!
Now Black has a decisive shot. } 26...
Rxc3 $1
{ 27 Rxa2 (27 bxc3?f2 mate) 27...Rf3+ 28 Ke2 Rf2+ 29 Kd3xa2 30 Ra1 Qxb2 wins. Black's first rank is no longer vulnerable since the King can escape to h7 on the check. }
0-1

[Event "USA Championship 1962-3"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Round "43"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Reshevsky"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "The missing link"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "270"]

{ Many critics have dubbed this the "I2th game" of the unfinished match, which had ended in a 5′/-5½ tie. It is as adventurous and as bitterly contested as their earlier ones.
This time Reshevsky is well prepared for Fischer's opening, countering forcefully and equalizing without difficulty. However, instead of maintaining tension, he strives inconsistently for simplifications, forcing an exchange of Queens which leaves him with a strategically weak ending. Working with simultaneous threats on both wings, Fischer, despite the reduction in material, succeeds in exploiting several of his opponent's targets (backward Pawns on open files). Reshevsky defends this passive position with his usual tenacity, but is unable to prevent an eventual breakthrough. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3 g6
{ A good reaction. So is 6...b5 (see game 40). By transposing into a Dragon Variation, Black hopes to render h3 useless, since in the normal Yugoslav Attack White will be forced to advance this Pawn again, thereby losing a tempo. }
7. g4 Bg7 8. g5 $5
{ Consistent, but perhaps premature. However, no other method offers more: e.g., 8 Be3 0-0 9 g5 Ne8! Or 8 Bg2 0-0 9 0-0 Nc6=. }
8... Nh5 $1 { On 8...Nfd7 9 Be3 Nc6 10 Qd2 Black is slightly bottled up. } 9.
Be2 e5
{ I had intended to answer 9...Nc6!? with 10 Nb3 (and not 10 Nxc6 bxc6 11 Bxh5 gxh5 12 Qxh5 Rb8 with good compensation for the Pawn). }
10. Nb3 { Bad is 10 Nf5 gxf5 11 Bxh5 f4 shutting out White's QB. } 10... Nf4
11. Nd5
{ 11 Bg4 Nc612 Nd5 might transpose to the game.
Objectively speaking, White has no opening advantage. }
11... Nxd5
{ Instead of simplifying so readily, Black could try to exploit the weakened K-side. Tal says more "logical"is 11...0-0 12 h4 f5 (or the interesting Pawn sac 12...Nd7!? 13 Nxf4 exf4 14 Qxd6 Be5).
Another possibility is 11...Nxe2 (not 11...Nxh3? 12 Be3 and the threat of1 3 Bb6 wins material) 12 Qxe2 Be6=. }
12. Qxd5 Nc6
{ Tal gives the dubious sac 12...Be6!? 13 Qxb7 Nd7 but 14 Be3 keeps the upper hand. But there's no need for Black to gamble. His positior. is basically sound. }
13. Bg4 Bxg4
{ Tal gives 13...f5 14 gxf6 e.p. (if 14 exf5 Ne7! 15 Qd3 gxf5 16 Bh5+ Ng6, etc.) 14...xf6 15 Bxc8 Rxc8 16 c3 Rc7, but after 17 Be3 followed by 0-0-0 White stands better. }
14. hxg4 Qc8 $1 { With the double threat of ...xg4 and/or ...Nb4. } 15. Qd1
{ On 15 Qxd6 Qxg4 16 Qd3 Rd817We2 Qg2 18 Rf1 h6 Black wrests the initiative. }
15... Nd4 $1
{ Apparently intent on simplifying at all cost. Reshevsky steers for an inferior ending. Tal gives 15...Qe6 16 Be3 0-0-0=.
A Bulgarian magazine gives the sharp 15...d5!? as best, since it dissolves the backward d-Pawn immediately. The tactical justification shows up after 16 exd5 (if 16 Qe2 or 16 c3, ...d4) 16...Nb4 17 c3 (if 17 0-0 Nxc2 18 Rb1 0-0 foils White's strategy) 17...Qc4! and now there are two main lines:
A] 18 cxb4? Qe4+ 19 Kd2 Rd8 20 Kc3 Bf8! 21 a3 (if 21 Nc5 Bxc5 22 bxc5 Rxd5 wins) 21...Rc8+ 22 Kd2 (not 22 Nc5? Bxc5 23 bxc5 Rxc5+ 24 Kb3 Qc4 mate) 22...Be7! with a continuing attack.
B] 18 Rh3 Nxd5 (if 18...e4+ 19 Kf1 Nc2? 20 Nd2 wins) 19 Qe2 Qc7=. }
16. c3 Nxb3 17. axb3 Qe6 18. Ra5 $1 f6 $2
{ Leads to a lifeless ending. Better chances are offered by 18...0-0 (or 18...b5 19 Qd5 Qxd5 20 exd5 Kd7) 19 Rd5 ad8 followed by ...f5. }
19. Qd5 $1 Qxd5 { Not 19...xg4? 20 Qxb7 0-0 21 gxf6. } 20. Rxd5 Kd7 21. gxf6
Bxf6 22. g5 Be7 23. Ke2
{ Now it's clear that Black's game is, at best, barely tenable. Both his d-Pawn and h-Pawn are ugly weaknesses exposed on open files. }
23... Raf8 24. Be3 Rc8 25. b4
{ More accurate possibly is 25 c4 c7 26 b4 and Black has constantly to worry about breaks with c5, b5, or even f4. }
25... b5 $5
{ Many annotators criticized this because it creates a fresh weakness (the a-Pawn). But if Black just waits he ultimately should get squeezeċ to death after Kd3 followed by c4, etc. Reshevsky apparently feels more comfortable living with his new weaknesses, rather than with the uncertainties which would be created after an eventual c4. }
26. Rdd1
{ White cannot keep d5 under control indefinitely. In order to make progress, the Rooks must strike at the backward RPs. }
26... Ke6 27. Ra1 Rc6 28. Rh3 { On 28 Rh4? h5! eliminates the weakness. } 28...
Bf8
{ 28...d5!? loses a Pawn but offers a slight ray of hope: e.g., 29 exd5+ Kxd5 30 Rah1 Kc4 31 Rxh7 Rxh7 32 Rxh7 Re6 33 Kd2 (33 Kf3? e4+! 34 Kf4 Kb3) 33...Kb3 34 Kd3 Kxb2 35 Ke4! Bf8 (if 35...Kxc3 36 Kd5 d6+ 37 Kxe5 Bf8 38 Rc7+! Kb3 39 Rc8 Be7 40 Re8 Rd7 41 Ke6 wins a piece) 36 Rh8 Bg7 37 Rg8 Re7 38 Rc8! followed by Rc6 should win. }
29. Rah1 Rc7
{ Now on 29...d5? 30 exd5+ Kxd5 31 Rxh7 Rxh7 32 Rxh7 K24 33 Rf7 Rc8 34 Rf6 Kb3 35 xa6 Kxb2 36 Bd2, etc. }
30. Rh4 $1
{ The critical position. Black is virtually in zugzwang. The Rook on h4 serves a valuable function, as will become apparent. }
30... d5
{ On 30...Rc4(if 30...Rf7 31 Ra1) 31 f3 Rc7 32 Kf2!d533 Ra1c-34 exd5+ Kxd5 35 Rd1+ Ke6 36 Rd8 should win. }
31. Ra1 $1
{ Reshevsky doubtlessly underestimated this interpolation. H probably expected 31 exd5+ Kxd5 32 Rd1+ Ke633 Rd8 Bg7! }
31... Rc6 { Or 31...dxe4 32 Rxa6+ Kd5 33 Rb6 wins. } 32. exd5+ Kxd5 33. Rd1+
Ke6
{ The merit of the Rook on h4 is that it prevents the King from entering at c4. }
34. Rd8 Kf5
{ With Black's Rook on c6 (instead of c7, as before) he no longer has the reply ... Bg7. And on34...Rc735 Ra8 is decisive. }
35. Ra8 Re6 36. Rh3 $1 Bg7
{ Equally useless is 36...Kg4 37 Rg3+ Kh5 38 Rf3 Bg7 39 Rxh8 Bxh8 40 Rf8 Bg7 41 Rf7. Or36...Ke4 37 Rf3 Bg7 38 Rxh8 Bxh8 39 Rf8 Bg7 40 Rf7 Bh841 f3+ Kd5 42 Rxh7 Re8 43 Kd3. }
37. Rxh8 Bxh8 38. Rxh7 Re8 39. Rf7+ Kg4 { Or 39...Ke4 40 f3+ Kd5 41 Kd3 wins. }
40. f3+ Kg3 41. Kd3 $2
{ A buzz began to circulate in the playing hall and I wondered what it was all about. Later they told me 41 Kf1! (threatening 42 Bf2+) forces at least the win of a piece. Of course the text move also wins, but it -akes ten moves longer. }
41... e4+
{ Throwing another Pawn to the winds in order to get the Bishop into lay. On 41...c8 42 Bc5 also is easy. }
42. fxe4 Rd8+ 43. Bd4 Kg4 44. Rf1 Be5 { On 44...Kxg5 45 Rg1+ Kf4 46 xg6, etc. }
45. Ke3 Bc7
{ After 45...Bxd4+ 46 cxd4 4 Kxg5 47 e5 the center Pawns are 二esistible. } 46.
Rg1+ Kh4 47. Kf3 Rd7
{ 47...Rf8+ 48 Bf6 h3 49 Rh1+ Bh2 50 e5 e8 51 Ke4, etc. The : is silence. } 48.
e5 Rf7+ 49. Ke4 Rf5 50. e6 Bd8 51. Bf6 $1 Bxf6 52. gxf6 Rxf6 53. Kd5 Rf2 54.
Re1 1-0

[Event "Skittles Game 1963"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "44"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Fine"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Title "Shock treatment"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "277"]

{ NEW YORK 1963: Skittles Game
EVANS GAMBIT
Shock treatment
Having become one of the leading players in the world, Fine quit chess at the height of his career (1945) to become a practising psychoanalyst; but he has lost none of his love for the game and little of his brilliance. The following is one of seven or eight offhand games played at his home in New York. As far as can be ascertained, Dr. Fine very nearly held his own.
Here, departing for the first time from his beloved Ruy Lopez, Fischer employs the daring gambit introduced by Captain Evans a century ago. This ploy has all but disappeared from the arena. Fine, although the author of several opening manuals, is understandably rusty, and he gets caught in a vise from which he never escapes. Fischer uncorks a sparkling finish in seventeen moves. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 $5 Bxb4
{ Safer is 4...Bb6, but that is hardly the way to refute the gambit. } 5. c3
Ba5 { For 5...Be7 see game 50. } 6. d4 exd4
{ 6...d6 7 0-0 (better is 7 Wb3) 7...Bb6 is the famous Lasker's Defense. which put the Evans out of commission last century. }
7. O-O dxc3
{ "A little too greedy." (MCO, 10th Ed.) 7...Bb68 cxd4 d6 leads to the so-called "normal variation"which is tenable. After 7...d6 8 Qb3 (Waller's Attack) someone played 8...Qd7 against me at an exhibition (Davis College 1964) 9 cxd4 Bb6 10 Bb5 Kf8! 11 d5 Na5 and Black saves the piece. }
8. Qb3 Qe7
{ More usual is 8...Qf6 9 e5 Qg6 10 Nxc3 Nge7 and now either 11 Ne2 or Ba3 leads to complicated positions which Tchigorin, for example, thought were playable for Black. }
9. Nxc3 Nf6 $2
{ On 9...xc3 10 Qxc3 f6 (if 10...Nf6 11 Ba3 d6 12 e5 Ne4 13 Wb2 and against 13...Nxe5 14 Nxe5xe5 15 Rfe1! wins a piece) 11 Ba3 d6 12 Bd5! Bd7 13 Rab1 0-0-0 14 Nd4 is crushing.
The best defense follows an old analysis from Freeborough and Ranken (1893); 9...Qb4! 10 Bxf7+ Kd8 11 Bg5+ (if 11 Bxg8? Qxb3! holds) 11...Nge7 12 Nd5 Qxb3 13 axb3 Bb6 (13...Bb4! looks better 14 fc1 h6 15 Rxc6 hxg5 16 Nxb6 cxb6 17 Rxb6, etc. }
10. Nd5 $1 Nxd5
{ Necessary is 10...Qxe4; but 11 Ng5 produces a violent attack. } 11. exd5 Ne5
{ On 11...Nd8 12 Ba3 is decisive (12...d6 13 b5+). } 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 13. Bb2 Qg5
14. h4 $1 { Deflecting the "overloaded" Queen. } 14... Qxh4
{ On 14...Qh6 15 Qa3 (threatening Rfe1+) wins. Or 14...g-15 fe1+ Bxe1 (if 15...Kd8 16 Qe3 Bb4 17 Qh6!! gxh6 18 Bf6-Be7 19 Bxe7+ Ke8 20 Bg5+! Kf8 21 Bxh6+ Qg7 22 Re8+!! Kxe 23 Bxg7 wins) 16 Rxe1+ Kd8 17 e3 Qxh4 18 g3! and Black: Queen must relinquish its guard of e7. }
15. Bxg7 Rg8 16. Rfe1+ Kd8 { 16...Bxe1 17 xe1+ leads to the same finale. } 17.
Qg3 { FINE
17...xg3 18 Bf6 mate. } 1-0

[Event "New York State Open 1963"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "45"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Bisguier"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Two knights ' Defense"]
[Title "Ghosts"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "281"]

{ Steinitz, nicknamed "the Austrian Morphy" (although two styles could hardly be more dissimilar), apparently exercises a great influence on Fischer, who has restored several of his pet lines to prominence. One of these is the bizarre 9 Nh3!?, found wanting at the turn of the century, and perhaps best left there.
Bisguier appears unimpressed, regaining his gambit Pawn with a strong initiative. But he misses several opportunities to gain an advantage and is gradually outplayed. Just at the critical moment, when the chances are roughly equal, he commits the same kind of gross oversight that had doomed Fischer against Spassky (game 18). He suffers the same fate. }
1. e4 { Best by test. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4
{ The last time I played this move in a tournament was when I was 12. at the 1955US Junior Championship. }
3... Nf6 $5
{ Steinitz considered this to be an unsound sacrificial continuation! } 4. Ng5
{ Tarrasch branded this a ""duffer's move" and Panov called "primitive." But there is no other way for White to try for an advantage 4 d3 is tame. And after 4 0-0 Nxe4 5 Nc3 Nxc3 6 dxc3 Qe7! White has no compensation for the Pawn. Finally, 4 d4 leads to the Max Lange attack. }
4... d5 5. exd5 Na5
{ 5...Nd4!? (FRITZ) and 5...b5!? (ULVESTAD) are both interesting but unsound. On 5...Nxd5 6 d4! (6 Nxf7!? is the "Fried Liver Attack') is so strong that 5...Nxd5 is practically extinct. }
6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 h6 9. Nh3 $5
{ To my knowledge, this is the first time that this move had been employed in Grandmaster chess for over seventy years. It is one of Steinitz's many unique opening contributions. The famous cable match game in 1891 between Steinitz and Tchigorin, which ended in a victory for Black, apparently caused the chess world to shy away from this variation. }
9... Bc5
{ A] 9...Bd6 (sTEINITz) might be worth investigating. If 10 d4 (TCHIGORIN) then 10...e4 (FISCHER).
B] 9...Bf5 is too crude: 10 0-0 Qd7 11 Re1 Bxh3 12 gxh3 xh3 13 Bfl and Black is busted, e.g., 13...g4+?14 xg4 Nxg4 15 h3 wins a piece (STEINITZ).
c] 9...g5 10 d3 g4 11 Ng1 Bc5 12 Nc3 effectively wards off the threats (12...Qb6 is answered by 13 Na4!). }
10. O-O
{ Played by Steinitz in the 6th game of his second match with Tchigorir in 1892. Better is 10 d3! 0-0 11 Nc3 Re8 12 0-0 Bxh3 13 gxh3 Qd~ 14 Bg4 Nxg4 15 hxg4, etc., as I played vs. Radoicich here in a later round. }
10... O-O
{ Dr. Gottschall, in the 1892 Deutsche Schachzeitung, suggests 10...g5. remarking it strange that a player as aggressive as Tchigorin did not chance it. Gottschall gives 11 Kh1 g4 12 Ng1 Ne4 13 Bxg4! Nxf2-14 xf2 Bxf2 and, although Black has won the exchange, prefers White's practical chances.
After 10...g5 11 Kh1 g4 12 Ng1 Ne4 let us suppose White tries to avert material loss with 13 b4 (of no avail is 13 Qe1 Qd4 14 Bd1 Nxf2+ 15 Rxf2 Qxf2 16 Qxe5+? Be6) 13...Nxf2+ (or Gottschall's 13...Bxf2 14 d3 Qh4 15 dxe4 Bg3 16 Nh3 [if 16 h3 gxh3 17 gxh3 Rg8] 16...Bxh2! 17 Kxh2 g3+ 18 Kg1 Bxh3, etc.) 14 Rxf2 Bxf 15 bxa5 Qh4! 16 Qf1 Bg3 17 h3 Rg8 with a crushing attack. }
11. d3 Bxh3
{ This certainly seems an improvement over the aforementionec Steinitz-Tchigorin match game which continued: 11...Nd5 (Gottschall's 11...Nh7 also merits attention) 12 c4 Ne7 13 Kh1 Bxh3 14 gxh3 Nf5 15 f4 exf4 16 Bxf4 Ne3 17 Bxe3 Bxe3 18 Nc3 anć White won easily with his Q-side majority. }
12. gxh3 Qd7 13. Bf3
{ A difficult choice. I rejected 13 κg2 since this was the square I had reserved for my Bishop. On 13 Bg4 Nxg4 followed by ...f5 gives fair attacking chances. }
13... Qxh3 { So Black regains the Pawn, but I have faith in my two Bishops. }
14. Nd2
{ It would be a mistake to play for the win of a Pawn by 14 Bg2 h4! 15 Qe1 Rfe8! 16 Qxa5 Ng4 17 h3 Bxf2+ 18 Rxf2 (if 18 Kh1 Qg3) 18...xf2+ 19 Kh1 e4! 20 hxg4 (if 20 dxe4 Rxe4) 20...exd3 with a winning attack. }
14... Rad8 { Not 14...e4? 15 Nxe4 Nxe4 16 Bxe4 Bd6 17 f4, etc. } 15. Bg2 Qf5
{ The Queen is forced off the h-file. On 15...Qh4? 16 Nf3 Qh5 17We1 wins a Pawn. }
16. Qe1
{ Perhaps better is 16 Qf3 with possibilities of a slightly favorable ending. }
16... Rfe8 17. Ne4 Bb6 18. Nxf6+
{ I was worried about the maneuver ... Nd5-f4. But sharper is 18 b4 Nb7 19 b5. }
18... Qxf6 19. Kh1 c5
{ Stronger is 19...g5 preventing White's break on f4 once and for all. Then by bringing his Knight to h4(!) Black could get a good game. }
20. Qc3
{ Serves the double purpose of preventing ..c4 and of enforcing f4. For all the good it does, Black's Bishop on b6 might just as well be a Pawr. for the rest of the game. }
20... Nc6 { Too late now is 20...g5? 21 f4! } 21. f4 Nd4 22. Qc4
{ To prepare c3, driving the Knight away from d4. I didn't like the looks of 22 fxe5 Qxe5 23 Bf4 Qe2, etc. }
22... Qg6
{ Intending ..Qh5 followed by ... Nf5. (Not 22...Qe6 23 Qa4 Qd7. 24 Qxd7 Rxd725 c3 Nc2 26 Bc6!) }
23. c3
{ After the game a kibitzer suggested 23 Be4 Qh5 24 f5 but this allows Black to turn the tables by 24...Qe2 25 Rg1 Nf3! }
23... Nf5
{ On 23...Ne224 f5 Qf6(24...Qh5? 25 Bf3!) 25 Be3 Nf4 26 Be4 is tremendous. }
24. fxe5 { After 24 Be4 Qh5 just who has got the attack is not quite clear! }
24... Rxe5 25. Bf4 { 25 Qf4 turns out badly after 25...Bc7! 26 Be4h5! } 25...
Re2
{ Black is playing for an advantage. 25...Ne3 26 Bxe3 Rxe3 is absolutely equal. }
26. Be4 { The critical position. } 26... Rxb2 $2
{ A pity that just when the game was getting interesting, Black has to make this terrible mistake.
Correct is 26...e8! (threatening ..R8xe4). Bad would be 27 Rg1 h5 28 af1 Ne3! 29 b5xe4 30 dxe4 Nxf1 31 Qe8+ (if 31 Rxf1 Qg4 wins) 31...Kh7 32 xg7+ Kxg7 33 Be5+ xe5 34 Qxe5+ f6 35 Qe7+ κg6 36 Qe8+ κg5 escaping the perpetual and should win.
After 26...Re8! therefore, the best White has is 27 Bf3 (to prevent ...h5) 27..xb2 28 Rael with even chances, owing to the Bishop pair. }
27. Be5 $1
{ Bisguier slumped and his chest collapsed, as he saw that Black cannot avert the loss of a piece. }
27... Re8 28. Rxf5 Rxe5 29. Rxe5 1-0

[Event "USA Championship 1963-4"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "46"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Benko"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Pirc -robatsch Defense"]
[Title "Romp"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "287"]

{ Chess Life, January 1964, reported:
"In the later stages of the tournament some of Fischer's opponents did almost as much to guarantee his //-0score as Bobby did. The building tension worked to Fischer's advantage...On Monday, December 30, Fischer won his last game of 1963 - defeating Pal Benko with a neat little combination, after Benko had shown some suicidal tendencies in the management of his defense."
And so, in twenty-one moves, another Grandmaster is demolished. Benko misses a chance to simplify (on move /5) in order to reach an inferior but possibly tenable ending. This is fortunate for the reader, who otherwise would be cheated of White's scintillating 19th move. That alone is worth the price of admission! }
1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3
{ 3 c4 d6 4 Nc3 transposes to a King's Indian. An unorthodox try 3 h4!? } 3...
d6 4. f4 { Sharpest. Another build-up is 4 Be3 Nf6 5 f3, etc. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3
O-O 6. Bd3
{ An improvement over 6 Be2 which I played against Korchnoi Curaçao 1962. That game continued: 6...c5 7 dxc5 a5 8 0-0xc– 9 Kh1 Nc6 10 Nd2 a5! 11 Nb3b6 12 a4 Nb4 13 g4 xg4! wit big advantage. }
6... Bg4 $2
{ Preparing to sac the "minor exchange." Interesting is Valvo's ... Na6!? 7 e5 dxe5 8 fxe5 Nd5 9 Nxd5xd5 about equal. (Bisguier-3enko, match 1964.)
The book gives 6...Nbd7 7 0-0 (7 e5 is best) 7...e5 8 dxe5 dxe5 9 fxe5 xe5 10 Nxe5d4+ 11 Kh1xe5 12 Bf4c5 with a tenable game.
Fischer-Perez, Havana 1965 continued; 6...Nc6!? 7 e5 dxe5 8 fxe5 d5 (Spassky's 8...Ng4 or maybe even 8...Nh5 is better) 9 Nxd5 xd510 c3 Bg4 11 Qe2! with a pull. }
7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3
{ I overheard someone explaining this game to a beginner: "You take off the Knight here, another piece comes out to replace it, so Black hasn't really stopped White's development... }
8... Nc6 9. Be3 e5 { On 9...Nd7 10 e5 keeps Black cramped. } 10. dxe5 dxe5 11.
f5 { Already threatening to obtain a winning bind with g4-g5. } 11... gxf5
{ Best. My original note said: "If immediately 11...Nd4 12 Wf2 gxf5 13 exf5 with a quick crush in sight." This verdict was later confirmed in Bednarsky-Kraidman, Tel Aviv 1964, which continued: 13...b5 14 0-0 c5 15 Ne4 c4 16 Nxf6+ xf6 17 Be4 Rad8 18 c3 fe8 19 Kh: Kh8 20 ae1 b4 21 cxd4 exd4 22 Bc1 d3 23 b3 Bh6 24 xh6 Qxh6 25 f3 Rxe1 26 Qxel c3 27 We7! and White won in ten more moves. }
12. Qxf5 { After 12 exf5 e4! Black gets good counterplay. } 12... Nd4
{ Benko is willing to gamble a Pawn to drive White's Queen from its dominating post. 12...d7 is safer, however. }
13. Qf2
{ I was tempted to accept the dare with 13 xe5! Ng4 14 Qxg7-: κxg7 15 hxg4 with threats all over the place. For example, if 15...Ne 16 e5 h8 17 Bh6+ g8 18 Ne4 wins. But 15...Nc6 is hard to crack. }
13... Ne8
{ More active than 13...Nd7 14 0-0-0 Nc5 15 Kb1 followed by Ne_ and c3 driving out the Knight. Now with ... Nd6 pending, Black threatens either to break with ...f5 or, in some lines, to advance witi: ...c5-c4. }
14. O-O
{ An alternative is 14 0-0-0 Nd6 15 De2. I thought White's Kin§ would be safer after the text –- the drawback is the K-side Pawns can n longer safely advance. }
14... Nd6
{ Sharp! I had expected 14...c6 15 Ne2 after which Black must either exchange his only well-placed piece or allow White's Knight to scramble to g3 followed by h5 or f5. }
15. Qg3
{ The only way to sustain the initiative. On 15 Nd5 f5 16 Bxd4 Nxe4! 17 Bxe4 fxe4 wins a Pawn. And after 15 Ne2 f5 gives Black active counterchances. }
15... Kh8
{ On 15...f5 16 Bh6 f6 17 Bxg7 Qxg7 18 Qxg7+ xg7 19 exf5 N6xf5 20 Rael Rae8 21 Ne4 with a comfortable edge, but certainly no forced win. }
16. Qg4 { To prevent ..f5. } 16... c6
{ Too passive. Black should seize the opportunity for 16...c5! } 17. Qh5
{ Threatening 18 Bxd4 exd4 19 e5. } 17... Qe8 $2
{ Either 17...e6 or ..c5 is essential. } 18. Bxd4 exd4 19. Rf6 $1
{ The zwischenzug Benko missed. He had expected 19 e5 f5! 290 46 / USA CHAMPIONSHIP 1963-4
A bolt from the blue! }
19... Kg8 { Forced. On 19...dxc3 (or 19...Bxf6) 20 e5 mates. } 20. e5 h6 21.
Ne2 $1
{ Black was hoping for 21 Rxd6 Wxe5! and he survives to an ending.
There is no defense to the threat of 22 Rxd6. On 21...b5 22 Qf5 wins. Or 21...Bxf6 22 Wxh6 forces mate. }
1-0

[Event "USA Championship 1963-4"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "47"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Bisguier"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "The Indian sign?"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "292"]

{ Bisguier is the one Grandmaster who consistently obtains decent positions against Fischer, only to throw them away for no apparent reason. Out of something like a dozen encounters, he has squeezed but a single draw.
Here is the only game in which Bisguier is outplayed from the start. Still, he does catch Fischer napping and nearly escapes. Describing his sensations before the game, Bisguier wrote:
"For the first time I was really in doubt as to what Bobby would play against me as White...I was hoping to play the Black side of the King's Gambit or the Two Knights' Defense, but he surprised' me with the Ruy Lopez...taken by surprise I was not so prepared or comfortable as I would like to have been. Now that Bobby has added psychology to his arsenal of weapons he is a much more dangerous opponent than ever before. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3
{ In a previous round, against Evans, I had hazarded a King's Gambit: 2 f4 exf4 3 Bc4 Qh4+ 4 κf1 and White won only after some uneasy moments. }
2... Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6
{ Bernstein tried the Marshall Attack against me in the 1959-60 US Championship, and an interesting struggle developed: 8...d5!? 9 exd5 e4 (in place of the usual 9...Nxd5) 10 dxc6 exf3 11 xf3 Bg4 12 Qg3 Bd6 13 h4 Re8 14 f3 Bf5 15 d4 Bxh2+ 16 Kxh2 Ng4+ 17 Kg3 Qxh4+ 18 Kxh4 Rxe1 19 fxg4 xc1 20 gxf5d8 21 a4! White won shortly. }
9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 { For 11...Nd7 see game 38. } 12. Nbd2 Nc6 13.
dxc5
{ The Rauzer Attack. White gives up the center in order to exploit Black's weakened squares on d5 and f5. }
13... dxc5 14. Nf1 Rd8
{ Risky. Better is the usual 14...Be6 15 Ne3 Rad8 16 Qe2 g6, etc. } 15. Qe2 Nh5
{ An old line rehabilitated by Reshevsky against Bronstein at Zurich 1953. If now 15...Be6 16 Ne3 g6 17 Ng5 Bc8 18 Nd5! Nxd5 19 exd5 Bxg5 (19...Rxd5 20 Qf3! Be6 21 Nxe6 fxe6 22 g4!) 20 Bxg5 Rxd5 21 Rad1 with a plus (LIPNITZKY). }
16. g3 $1
{ Bronstein's move – after first interpolating 16 a4 b8.
This idea bankrupts Black's strategy. The slight weakening of the K-side is inconsequential, but Black's loss of time with his KN is.
Actually the sharpest continuation is 16 a4! as I played against Eliskases at Mar del Plata 1960 (I simply forgot to interpolate it here) 16...Rb8 17 axb5 axb5 18 g3! g6 19 h4! Be6 20 Ne3 c4 21 Ng5 Bxg5 22 hxg5 Na5 23 Ng4 Bxg4 24xg4 Nb3 25 Bxb3 cxb3 26 Be3 with a great advantage. }
16... g6
{ Simply 16...Nf6 may be best. Then on 17 h4 h6 18 Ne3 Be6. 16...Bxh3 is bad owing to 17 Ng5 Bxg5 (if 17...Bxfl 18 xh5 Bxg5 19 Bxg5 f6 20 xf6! gxf6 21 Kxf1 White's better) 18 Bxg5 Nf6 (not 18...Bxf1? 19 Bxd8) 19 Bxf6 gxf6 20 Ne3 White has more than enough for the Pawn. }
17. h4 $1
{ Bronstein's 17 Kh2 and Weinstein's 17 κg2 are time-consuming and hence weaker.
When I told Bronstein (at Mar del Plata 1960) that the text was a tremendous improvement over his game with Reshevsky, he replied: "Of course. After seven years one must find an improvement.' }
17... Be6 18. Ne3 f6
{ Probably best. 18...c4 19 Ng5! is similar to the quoted game with Eliskases. }
19. Nd5 $1
{ Of course! Chess is a matter of timing. Given another move or two Black would be able not only to defend himself against this invasion, but even try for the initiative. }
19... Qb7
{ Prudent. "Winning" the Pawn wouldallow White's Bishop-pair to enter the game with powerful effect: e.g., 19...Bxd5 20 exd5 Rxd5 21 c4! Nd4 22 Nxd4 Rxd4 23 cxb5 axb5 24 Qxb5 and now Eliskases recommends 24...c4 but I fail to see how this improves matters since 25 Be3 Rb8 26 Qa4 Rb4?is answered by 27 Qe8+. }
20. Nxe7+ Qxe7 { White has the two Bishops – or a "half point"advantage. } 21.
Nh2 { This Knight is also bound for d5. } 21... Ng7 22. Ng4 c4 23. Qf3 $1
{ Winning the second "minor exchange." On 23...Rf8 24 Ne3 the Knight is ready to pounce on d5, especially after Black's Rook has been deflected from the d-file. }
23... Bxg4 24. Qxg4 Ne6 25. h5 $2
{ More accurate is 25 Be3 (25...Nc5? 26 Bxc5 Qxc5 27 Qe6+ Kg7 28 Rad1 penetrates decisively). }
25... Kh8 $1
{ Alert. I had expected 25...g5 whereupon 26 Be3 is even more devastating than before. }
26. Kg2 $1 { On 26 hxg6 Rg8 White is in trouble! } 26... g5
{ Forced – eventually. On 26...g8 27 Rh1 gxh5? (27...g5 is better) 28xh5 Nf4+ 29 Bxf4 exf4 30 e5! Rg7 31 exf6 Qxf6 32 Bxh7 wins. }
27. Be3 Nf4+ $1 28. Kh2 $1
{ 28 gxf4? gxf4 threatening 29...Rg8 as well as 29...fxe3 regains the piece advantageously. }
28... Nd3 29. Bxd3 cxd3 $2
{ Now the advanced soldier must fall. Exchanging a pair of Rooks would make it more difficult, but White still maintains his grip after 29...Rxd3 30 Red1 Rad8 31 Rxd3 cxd3 (31...Rxd3? 32 Qc8+ Nd8 33 Qxa6) 32 d1 Rd7 33 Rd2 threatening a winning bind with 34 Bc5. }
30. Red1 Rd7
{ On 30...b4 31 Rd2 bxc3 32 bxc3 Qa3 33 Rad1 Qxc3 34 Qe6 Kg7 35 h6+! wins. }
31. Rd2 Na5
{ A useless excursion, but there was no good defense. Strangely enough, Black's difficulty stems from his third move and its consequent weakening on b6. If the pawn were still on a7 (preventing a later Bb6) he might weli hold.
On 31...ad8 32 ad1f7 33 Bb6 b8 34 Bc5bd8 35 f3 picks up the d-pawn at leisure. }
32. b3 Qd6 { Not 32...c8? 33xd3! } 33. Rad1 Re8
{ On 33...ad834xd3xd3 35xd3xd3 36 Bb6! } 34. Rxd3 Qxd3
{ Black is braced to give up his Queen for two Rooks and keep control of the crucial d-file. }
35. Qxd7 $1
{ A devastating X-ray. After 35...xd7 36 xd7 it's just a matter o time. E.g., 35...e6 37 h3 followed by g4-f5, etc. }
1-0

[Event "USA Championship 1963-4"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "48"]
[White "R.Byrne"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Gruenfeld Defense"]
[Title "The brilliancy prize"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "298"]

{ K. F. Kirby, editor of the South African Chess Quarterly, summed up the astonishment and admiration of the chess world when he wrote:
"The Byrne game was quite fabulous, and I cannot call to mind anything to parallel it. After White's eieventh move I should adjudicate his position as slightly superior, and at worst completely safe. To turn this into a mating position in eleven more moves is more witchcraft than chess! Quite honestly, I do not see the man who can stop Bobby at this time...'
And one can add nothing to Byrne's own words: "And as I sat pondering why Fischer would choose such a line, because it was so obviously lost for Black, there suddenly came 18...Nxg2. This dazzling move came as the shocker...The culminating combination is of such depth that, even at the very moment at which I resigned, both grandmasters who were commenting on the play for the spectators in a separate room believed that I had a won game!" }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 c6 4. Bg2
{ In the 1962-3 US Championship we reached the same position, but Byrne continued 4 d5 b5! 5 dxc6 bxc4 6 cxd7+ Nbxd7 7 Bg2 b8 8 Nf3 Bg7 9 0-0 0-0= Black's weak c-pawn is compensated for by pressure on the open b-file. }
4... d5 5. cxd5 { 5 Qb3 maintains more tension. } 5... cxd5 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. e3
{ Benko-Fischer, US Championship 1962-3, continued 7 Nf3 0-0 8 Ne5 (if 8 0-0 Ne4!=) 8...Bf5 9 0-0 Ne4 10 b3 Nc6 11xd5 Nxc3 12 bxc3 xd5 13 Bxd5 Nxe5 14 dxe5 Bxe5 with a draw shortly. }
7... O-O 8. Nge2 Nc6 9. O-O b6 10. b3
{ It's hard for either side to introduce an imbalance into this essentially symmetrical variation.
Deadeye equality also ensues after 10 Nf4 e6 11 b3 Ba6 12 e1 c8 13 a3e8 14 Rc1, etc. (Stahlberg-Flohr, Kemeri 1937.) }
10... Ba6 11. Ba3 Re8 12. Qd2
{ A good alternative is 12 Rc1. A kibitzer later suggested 12 f4?! to prevent ..e5. But after 12...eć followed by ... Bf8 and eventual doubling on the c-file, Black gets ar advantage. }
12... e5 $1
{ I was a bit worried about weakening my d-pawn, but felt that thë tremendous activity obtained by my minor pieces would permit White no time to exploit it. 12...e6 would probably lead to a draw. }
13. dxe5
{ Passive is 13 Rac1 exd4 (if 13...Rc8 14 Rfd1 e4 15 f3! is tenable 14 exd4 Rc8 15 f3 although Black has difficulty breaking through. }
13... Nxe5 14. Rfd1 $2
{ Add another to those melancholy case histories entitled "the wrong Rook." Correct is 14 Rad1! Originally I gave the following "refutation": "14...Ne4 15 Nxe4 dxe4 16 Bxe4 Qxd2 17 Rxd2 Nc4 18 Bxa8 Nxd2 19 Rd1 Nc4 20 bxc4 (best) 20...xa8 regaining the Pawn with a big endgame advantage." But Averbakh found a hole in my analysis with 20 Bc6! (instead of 20 bxc4 which I had carelessly given as "best"), 20...Nxa3 21 Bxe8 Bxe2 22 Rd7 and White is the one who wins instead of Black!
I spent an evening just staring at the position after 14 Rad1, trying everything, unwilling to let my brilliancy go down the drain. The more I looked, the more I liked White's game! For example, 14...c8 (14...Nd3 is refuted by 15 c2) 15 Nxd5 Nxd5 16 Bxd5 Bd3 17 Bg2 c2 18 xc2! kaput. No better is 14...Qd715 c2 followed by Rd2 and Rfd1 (if 15...ac816 b1!).
Another try which just falls short is 14 Rad1c7 15Wc1! Ne4!? (otherwise 16 Qb1 consolidates) 16 Nxd5! xc1 17 Nxc1 Bxf1 18 Bxe4 Bh3 19 Ne7+ Kh8 20 Bxa8 Rxa8 21 f4 keeping the extra Pawn. Indeed, how does Black even equalize, let alone sustain the initiative?
Finally I found 14...c8! – the only move to keep the pressure. Now on 15 Nxd5xd5 16 Bxd5 Rd8 17 f4xd5!18xd5 Bb7! 19 Qd8+ (if 19 Qd2 Qh3! 20 Nd4 Ng4 21 fe1 [or 21 Nc2 h5 with a strong attack] 21...Nxe3! should win) 19...xd8 20 Rxd8+ Rxd8 21 fxe5 Bxe5 with a better ending. And on 15 c1 Qd7! 16 Rcd1 ad8 Black has finagled a precious tempo, since his Queen is on d7 instead of d8. After 14...Qc8! relatively best is 15 Bb2 (if 15 c1 Ne4 16 Nxd5 Bxe2 17 Bxe4 Kh8! wins the exchange. One possible line is 18 xc8 axc8 19 Ne7 c7 20 c1 Rd7 21 Rfel Bf3!) although Black keeps the initiative with 15...Qf5. }
14... Nd3 $1 15. Qc2
{ There is hardly any other defense to the threat of ... Ne4. A] 15 Nd4 e4 16 Nxe4 dxe4 17 Bb2 Rc8 with a powerful bind. B] 15 Nf4 Ne4 16 Nxe4 dxe4 (not 16...Bxa1? 17 Nd6) 17 ab1 c8 18 Nxd3 Bc3! 19 Qe2 Bxd3 20 g4 f5 21 Qh3 Bxb1! 22 Rxd8 exd8 23 Bf1 d1 24 Kg2 d3! 25 Bxd3 exd3 wins.
C] 15 f3 Bh6 16 f4 (if 16 Nf4? d4!) 16...Bg7! resumes the threat of ... Ne4, only White has weakened himself in the interim. }
15... Nxf2 $1
{ The key to Black's previous play. The complete justification for this sac does not become apparent until White resigns! }
16. Kxf2 Ng4+ 17. Kg1 Nxe3 18. Qd2
{ Forced. Now on 18...Nxd1 19 xd1 White is all right again. } 18... Nxg2 $1
{ Removing this Bishop leaves White defenseless on his light squares. } 19.
Kxg2 d4 $1 20. Nxd4 Bb7+
{ The King is at Black's mercy. BYRNE / GRUENFELD DEFENSE 301 } 21. Kf1
{ Equally hopeless is 21 κg1 Bxd4+ 22 Qxd4 Re1+! 23 Kf2 Qxd4+ 24 Rxd4 Rxa1 25 Rd7 Rc8 26 Rxb7 (if 26 Bb2 Rh1) 26...Rxc3 27 Rb8+κg7 28 Bb2 Rxa2, etc.
Or 21 Kf2 d7! 22 Rac1 Qh3 23 Nf3 Bh6 24 d3 Be3+ 25 Qxe3 Rxe3 26 Kxe3 Re8+ 27 Kf2f5! finis! }
21... Qd7 $1
{ A bitter disappointment. I'd hoped for 22 f2 h3+ 23κg1 Re1+!! 24 Rxel Bxd4 with mate to follow shortly. Also 22 Ndb5 Qh3+ 23 κg1 Bh6 and the curtain comes down. }
0-1

[Event "USA Championship 1963-4"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Round "49"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Steinmeyer"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[Title "A complex trap"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "303"]

{ While generally thought of as "one movers," some opening traps are deeper and more beautiful than others because falling into them requires a certain degree of skill. They might not attract and, if they did, might work for an amateur!
Steinmeyer's concept beginning with 13...f4+ is both subtle and novel. The only trouble is that it meets with a smashing refutation. Instead of simplifying, as Steinmeyer hopes, his variation enmeshes him in complications. The nail in the coffin is 16 Ne5, after which Black's Queen can no longer be extricated without fatal loss of material. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 { For 2 Nc3 d5 3 Nf3 see game 16. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4
Bf5 5. Ng3
{ On tour (1964) I experimented with the weird 5 Nc5!? Most of my opponents countered with 5...e5 6 Nxb7 xd4 (if 6...Qb6 7 Nc5 Bxc5 8 dxc5 Qxc5 9 c3 White's better. Fischer-Petrosian, five-minute game, Bled 1961) 7 Qxd4 exd4 8 Bd3 with the better ending. Some replied with 5...b6 6 Na6 Nxa6 7 Bxa6 d5! Still others played 5...Qc7 6 Bd3 Bxd3 7 Nxd3 e6. White has more space, but only experience can tell whether he has the edge; however the Knight on d3 discourages the normal freeing maneuver ...c5 and/or ...e5. At least it's something to break the monotony. }
5... Bg6 6. Nf3 Nf6 { More usual is the immediate ... Nd7 to prevent Ne5. } 7.
h4
{ 7 Bd3 (if 7 Ne5 Nbd7 8 Nxg6 hxg6 Black is solid), 7...e6 8 0-0 Be7 9 c4 0-0 10 Bxg6 hxg6 leads to equality. (Evans-Benko, US Championship 1962-3.) }
7... h6 8. Bd3
{ White can try to exploit the order of Black's moves by 8 Ne5, but 8...Bh7 9 Bc4 e6 10 Qe2 Nd5! (not 10...Qxd4? 11 Nxf7!) followed by ... Nd7 equalizes.
8 h5 Bh7 9 Bd3 Bxd3 10 Qxd3 e6 11 Bd2 Nbd7 12 0-0-0 Qc7 13 Ne4 (Spassky-Petrosian, 13th match game 1966, continued 13 Qe2 0-0-0 14 Ne5 Nxe5 15 dxe5 Nd7 16 f4 with an edge) 13...0-0-0 14 g3! (Geller-Petrosian, Moscow 1967), and now 14...Nxe4 (instead of 14...Ng4?) 15Wxe4 Bd6 holds White to a minimal pull. }
8... Bxd3 9. Qxd3 e6 10. Bd2 Nbd7
{ Or 10...c7 11 c4 (if 11 0-0-0 Bd6 12 Ne4 Bf4! 13 Nxf6+ gxf6 is satisfactory) 11...Nbd7 12 Bc3 (the whole idea is to prevent Black from swapping Bishops) 12...a5! 13 0-0!? (if 13 0-0-0Bb4!) 13...Bd6 14 Ne4 (Tal suggested 14 d5!? mixing it up, but 14...Bxg3! holds; not 15 dxe6? Ne5; or 15 fxg3 cxd5 16 cxd5 Nxd5 17 Bxg7 Rg8 18 Qh7 N7f619 Bxf6 Nxf6 20 Qxh6 Qxg3 21 Qd2=) 14...Nxe4 15 Qxe4 0-0=. (Fischer-Donner, Varna 1962.) }
11. O-O-O Qc7 12. c4 O-O-O
{ 12...Bd6! 13 Ne4(if 13 Ne2 0-0-0 14 Kb1 e5=) 13...Bf4! leads to immediate simplifications. }
13. Bc3 $1 { Now Black no longer can force the exchange of Bishops. } 13...
Qf4+ $2
{ The start of a faulty concept. After 13...Bd6(on 13...c5 14 d5! 14 Ne4 Bf4+ 15 Kb1 Ne5! 16 Nxe5 Bxe5 produces equality. }
14. Kb1 Nc5 $2 { He still has time to back outwith 14...Qc7. } 15. Qc2 Nce4
{ Now there is no turning back. On 15...cd7 16 Ne5! is very strong: e.g., 16...Nxe5 17 dxe5 Nd7 (or 17...Ng4 18 xd8+ Kxd8 19 Rd1+ K28 20 d4) 18 d4 xe5 19 xd7!, etc. }
16. Ne5 $1
{ A clear refutation. The Queen's retreat is cut off and the ancient weakness on Black's f7 is etched more sharply than ever. Shamkovich-Goldberg, USSR 1961, continued with 16 Ba5? which won only against inferior defense. }
16... Nxf2
{ What else? 16...Nxg3 loses to 17 fxg3xg3 18 d3 Qf4 19 Rf3 Qe4 20 Nxf7. And 16...Nxc3+ is refuted by 17 bxc3! Rg8 (if 17...g4 18 Nh5! Qf5 19 Qxf5 exf5 20 Nxf7) 18 d3 h5 19 Rf3h6 20 Nxf7, etc. }
17. Rdf1 $1
{ Probably what Steinmeyer overlooked when he went into this whole mess. On 17...xg318 Rxf2We3 (otherwise Rf3) 19 e2f4 20 Nxf7 wins at least the exchange.
Motivated by my lopsided result (11-0!), Dr Kmoch congratulated Evans (the runner up) on "winning" the tournament ... and then he congratulated me on "winning the exhibition." }
1-0

[Event "Exhibition Tour 1964"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1964.??.??"]
[Round "50"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Celle"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Title "Tour de force"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "307"]

{ CALIFORNIA 1964: Exhibition Tour
EVANS GAMBIT
Tour de force
As one of ten simultaneous clock games played on tour, at Davis College, this is a perfect example of the precept that if White makes a slip in the opening he is punished by loss of the initiative, while if Black makes a slip (since he is skating on thin ice from the very start) it is likely to be fatal. 6...d6 is the offender.
With 9 h5 Fischer assumes a commanding control of space, but faces a strong defense which compels him to offer a piece in order to maintain pressure. Continuing with restraint and circumspection, he builds up the attack with a series of quiet developing moves – reminiscent of Morphy's famous victory over the Duke of Brunswick at the Paris opera. When the time is ripe, Fischer throws everything at Black's King, including the proverbial kitchen sink. His show of brute force is handsomely rewarded. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 $5
{ The Evans was already analyzed to death by the 'nineties. But it still makes for enterprising chess. }
4... Bxb4 5. c3 Be7
{ Must be the trend. At least, on tour most players answered this way. For 5...Ba5 see game 44. }
6. d4 d6 $2
{ A mistake is usually much more serious in these open games. Black must return the Pawn with 6...Na5! 7 Nxe5 Nxc4 8 Nxc4 d5! }
7. dxe5 Nxe5
{ On 7...Na5? 8 Bxf7+! Kxf7 9 Qd5+ Be6 10 Qxa5 wins a Pawn. 7...dxe5 8 Qb3 Na5 9 Bxf7+ Kf8 10 Qa4 is strong. }
8. Nxe5 dxe5 9. Qh5 $1
{ In an earlier exhibition game I played 9 Qb3 but got nothing after 9...Be6! 10 Bxe6 fxe6 11 Ba3!? (if 11 Qxe6 Qd6=) 11...d3! }
9... g6 10. Qxe5 Nf6 { On 10...f6 11 Qb5+! c6? 12 Qb3 f8 13 Bxg8! wins. } 11.
Ba3 $1 { Incredible how Black is so completely immobilized by this one move! }
11... Rf8
{ The only way to get relief. 11...Kf8? works in all lines except 12 Qxf6! }
12. O-O Ng4
{ 12...Nd7 followed by ... Nb6 might be better, but this certainly looked good at the time. }
13. Qg3 Bxa3 14. Nxa3 Qe7 $1
{ Apparently Black has freed his game. If now 15 Nc2 We5 virtually forces an exchange of Queens. 15 Nb5 is rendered harmless by .. Ne5. How's White to sustain the initiative? }
15. Bb5+ $1
{ That's how. This forces Black to weaken himself on d6, although White must sacrifice a piece to exploit it. }
15... c6 { On 15...Bd7 16 Qxc7 (not 16 xg4 c6!). } 16. Nc4 $1 Qe6 $1
{ Some fascinating possibilities appear after 16...cxb5 17 Nd6+ Kd8 18 Rfd1 d7 19 Nxb7+ Kc8 20 Nd6+ Kd8 21 d4! Ne5 22 Rad1 Kc7 (if 22...g5 23 Nf5 e8 24 xe5! Qxe5 25 Rxd7+ Ke8 [if 25...Kc8 26 Ne7+! wins] 26 e7+! Qxe7 27 Ng7 mate) 23 f4 Ng4 24 h3 Nf6 25 f5 Kb6 26 Qe3 Kc7 (after 26...Ka6 27 a4 smashes Black) 27 Rc4+! bxc4 (if 27...Kd8 28 Qc5 anyway) 28 Qc5+ Bc6 (if 28...Kd8 29 Qa5 mate; or 28...Kb8 29 Rb1+) 29 Nb5+, etc.
Black may not have seen the mate, but he suspected the worst! }
17. Rad1 $1
{ Piling on the pressure. White mustn't amateurishly rush in with 17 Qc7 Qd7! forcing him to simplify by 18 Nd6+ Kə7 19 Nxc8+ Raxc8 20 xd7+ Kxd7, etc., and the advantage has evaporated. }
17... cxb5
{ He might as well take it since after 17...Bd7 18 Nd6+ Ke7 19 Bc4 White wins a Pawn without any risk. }
18. Qc7 Bd7 { Forced. } 19. Nd6+ Ke7 20. Nf5+ $1
{ The attack needs fresh fuel. Material is not what counts now, but open lines. Black is forced to capture against his will. 20...Kə8 is out because of 21 Ng7+. And 20...Kf6 21 Rd6 gxf5 22 Qxd7! wins outright. }
20... gxf5 21. exf5 Rac8
{ On 21...Qxf5 22 Qd6+ Kd8 (2...Ke8 23 Rfe1+ Be6 24 Qd7 mate) 23 Qxf8+ Kc7 24 Qxa8 wins. }
22. Rxd7+ $1 Qxd7 23. f6+ $1
{ Originally I intended 23 Re1+ Ne5 24 xe5+ Kf6 25 xd7 Kxe5 26 Qxb5+ with a won ending. But then I remembered Emanue1 Lasker's maxim: "When you see a good move – wait – don't play it – you might find a better one. }
23... Nxf6 { Not 23...Ke8? 24 Re1+ Qe6 25 Qxc8 mate. On 23...Kxf6 24 Wxd7. }
24. Re1+
{ Note the amusing piece configuration. All Black's pieces are stepping on each other's toes. }
24... Ne4 { The only legal move! } 25. Rxe4+ Kf6 26. Qxd7 Rfd8 27. Qg4
{ Here I forgot Lasker's maxim. 27 Qe7+ would have forced mate in four. } 1-0

[Event "Havana (Capablanca Memorial) 1965"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1965.??.??"]
[Round "51"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Smyslov"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "Squeeze play"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "312"]

{ Fischer competed in this Capablanca Memorial Tournament by longdistance telephone, and his victory over the winner is reminiscent of the famous Lasker-Capablanca duel at St. Petersburg, 1914, where Black was also gradually constricted and strangled.
Taken by surprise with an antiquated line (5 d3), Smyslov soon gets into trouble. He finds a way out, although it burdens him with doubled King Pawns. After the subsequent exchange of Queens he apparently underestimates White's winning chances and permits himself to drift into a cramped ending. Applying persistent pressure, Fischer makes gradual inroads; the defensive task eventually proves too great and Smyslov buckles under the strain. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. d3
{ Steinitz's favorite, long abandoned, and the first time I've employed it in a tournament game. }
5... d6
{ A solid but passive reaction. An alternative is 5...b5 6 Bb3 Be7 7 a4, etc. Anderssen-Morphy, match 1858(!) continued: 5...Bc5 6 c3 b5 7 Bc2 0-0 8 0-0 d5 9 exd5 Nxd5 10 h3 h6 (Steinitz had a field day criticizing White's last two moves) 11 d4 exd4 with a satisfactory game for Black. }
6. c3 Be7
{ On 6...g6 7 Nbd2 (or Bronstein's 7 Bg5) 7...Bg7 8 Nf1 0-0 9 h4 opens fresh vistas. }
7. Nbd2 O-O 8. Nf1
{ One facet of White's strategy is to defer castling and possibly institute a K-side attack with h3, g4, etc. Furthermore, this Knight can be deployed to e3 or g3 immediately without having to waste a tempo (after having castled) with e1. }
8... b5 9. Bb3 d5
{ Inconsistent, after having lost a tempo with ...d6. Right is 9...Na5 10 Bc2 c5 11 Ne3 e8 12 0-0 Bf8 with equal chances. }
10. Qe2 dxe4
{ Opens the position prematurely. No better would be 10...d4 11 Ng3 dxc3 12 bxc3 b4 13 Bd2. Black should keep tension in the center with 10...Be6 11 Ng3 (if 11 Ng5 Bg4 12 f3 Bc8! 13 exd5 Na5!) 11...h6. }
11. dxe4 Be6 $1
{ I was surprised that Smyslov was prepared to saddle himself with doubled King Pawns, but surmised that it must be all right since he doesn't do such things lightly. Anyway Black's game, without this exchange, would remain permanently cramped. }
12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. Ng3
{ This Knight is undeniably misplaced here, but White intends to castle and then regroup his pieces in order to bring maximum pressure to bear on the doubled Pawns. }
13... Qd7
{ Some annotators suggested the obvious 13...Bd6 (followed by Ne7-g6, etc.) but Black has no time for such sophisticated strategy: e.g., 14 0-0 Ne7 15 c4! c6 16 Rd1 winning material (if 16...Qc7 17 Ng5). }
14. O-O
{ During the game I was kicking myself for allowing the subsequent exchange of Queens. Sharper is 14 a4 ad8 (if 14...bxa4 15 Wc4 a3 16 b4) 15 axb5 axb5 16 a6 b4 17 0-0 and Black can no longer ease his burden with ... Qd3. So correct is 14...b4! }
14... Rad8
{ Suddenly Black's plan hit me! At first I thought he just wanted to control the d-file; but now I realized he was scheming to chop wood. }
15. a4 Qd3 $1
{ Of course! With the Queens gone, it's that much harder to strike at Black's weaknesses. }
16. Qxd3 Rxd3 17. axb5 axb5 18. Ra6 $1
{ Forcing Black's reply, and thus preventing the freeing maneuver with ... Bc5. 18 Be3 would be met by ... Ng4. }
18... Rd6 19. Kh1 $1
{ The threat was 19...Nd4 forcing a favorable series of exchanges. } 19... Nd7
{ 19...b4 20 cxb4 Nxb4 21 Ra7 Rc6 22 Nxe5 Rc2 would give Black active play for the Pawn. }
20. Be3 Rd8
{ 20...b4 is still playable. Neither of us realized at this stage how essential this move was. I didn't want to weaken my c3 and c4 squares by playing b4 to prevent it; and Smyslov didn't want to commit himself yet. }
21. h3
{ More accurate is 21 b4, from which I abstained for the reasons already mentioned. }
21... h6 22. Rfa1 Ndb8 23. Ra8 Rd1+ 24. Kh2
{ On 24 xd1 Rxd1+ 25 Kh2 Bd6 holds (26 Ba7?a1!). } 24... Rxa1 25. Rxa1 Nd7 $2
{ When I spoke to Smyslov on the direct phone line immediately after the game, he congratulated me on a beautiful performance and attributed his loss to his reluctance to play ..b4 at some point – and this is his last chance. After 25...b4 26 cxb4 Bxb4 27 Nfl Black obtains much more freedom than in the actual game, and eliminates a weakness (his b-pawn) as well. Perhaps Smyslov feared that in this line White could maneuver his Knight to c4; even so, this is hardly fatal. }
26. b4 $1
{ Smyslov confessed that he felt Black was probably lost after this riposte. But the win is far from easy, and Black might later have improved upon his defense.
26 K17 }
26... Kf7 27. Nf1 Bd6 28. g3
{ Once and for all negating all possible combinations with ... Nd4. } 28... Nf6
29. N1d2 Ke7 30. Ra6 $1 Nb8
{ Very uncomfortable is 30...Kd7 31 Ne1 Nb8 32 Ra5 Kc6. } 31. Ra5 $1
{ Now White strengthens his bind by forcing ...c6 which takes away another breathing space from Black's pieces. }
31... c6 32. Kg2 Nbd7 33. Kf1
{ Preparing to bring the King to e2 where it can support the Nel-d3 maneuver. }
33... Rc8 $2
{ A surprise! I had expected the much stouter defense with 33...Ne8! (intending to exchange Rooks with ... Nc7 and ...Ra8). After 34 a6 c8 35 Nb3 c5 36 bxc5 Bxc5! Black can hold. And there is no time for 34 Ne1 Nc7 35 Nd3 a8 36 Nb3 xa5 37 Nxa5 Nb8 38 Ba7 Nca639 c4 Bc7.
The main line is 33...Ne8 34 Nb3! Nc7 35 a7 Ra8 36 Na5 Nb8 37 Rxa8 (if 37 Rb7 Kd7) 37...Nxa838 Ba7 Kd7 39 Nb7. It looks bad, but Black has chances to survive in the absence of a forced win. }
34. Ne1 $1 Ne8 { Too late now. } 35. Nd3 Nc7 36. c4 $1 bxc4
{ 36...Ra8? is refuted by 37 c5! winning a piece. } 37. Nxc4
{ The ideal position! Finally White has ganged up on Black's venerable weakness - his Pawn on e5. }
37... Nb5
{ On 37...Ra8 38 xa8 Nxa8 39 Na5 Nb8 40 a7 Kd7 41 Nc4 picks off the e-pawn. }
38. Ra6 { Keeping Black tied up some more. } 38... Kf6
{ 38...Nb8 39 Ra8 Nc7 40 Nxd6 xd6 41 Bc5+ wins. } 39. Bc1 $1 Bb8 40. Bb2
{ Threatening f4. } 40... c5 { A desperate bid for counterplay. } 41. Nb6 $1
{ 41 Ra5! also has a nasty sting; for if 41...cxb4 42 Ncxe5! wins outright. }
41... Nxb6 42. Rxb6 c4
{ On 42...Nd4 43 Nxc5 Ba7 44 Nd7+ Kg5 45 h4+ Kh5 46 Rb7 Rc2 47 Rxa7 Rxb2 48 Nxe5 Rxb4 49 Rxg7 is decisive. }
43. Nc5 c3
{ White can win with 44 Bc1 Nd4 45 Nd7+ Ke7 (if 45...Kf7 46 Rxb8 xb8 47 Nxb8 b3 48 a3 c2 49 Nc6, etc.) 46 Nxb8 Nb3 47 Rb7+ Kd8 48 d7+κe8 49 xg7! }
1-0

[Event "USA Championship 1965-6"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1965.??.??"]
[Round "52"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Rossolimo"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[Title "Peekaboo strategy"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "319"]

{ The MacCutcheon Variation gives rise to unusual positions where White is frequently obliged to forfeit the privilege of castling in order to try and wrest an advantage. After some slight but instructive opening inexactitudes on both sides, Rossolimo appears to achieve equality. Indeed, he is constantly on the brink of crashing through with a counter-attack against White's vulnerable King, although his own monarch is also stranded in the center. After 13...f5, which shores up his fortress, it's not clear just whose attack will come first.
In a theoretically important formation – a logical offshoot of this variation - Fischer unearths two fine moves (17 Bb5+ and 19 Ng1) to sustain his flagging initiative. Still, he is forced to wage a running battle, no sooner landing a blow than having to duck. Consequently, the outcome is in doubt until the very last punch. }
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4
{ The MacCutcheon Variation, giving rise to immediate complications. 4...Be7or 4...dxe4 are tamer. }
5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 { 6 exf6 hxg57 fxg7g8 leads to nothing. } 6... Bxc3 7. bxc3
{ Against Petrosian, at Curaçao 1962, I tried the ridiculous 7 Bxc3? Ne4 8 Ba5?? (if 8 Bb4 c5 9 dxc5 Nxf2! 10 Kxf2 Qh4+) 8...0-0 (weaker is 8...b6 9 Bb4 c5 10 Ba3 cxd4 11 Qxd4 Nc6 12 Bb5) 9 Bd3 Nc6 10 Bc3 Nxc3 11 bxc3 f6 and Black already had the initiative. }
7... Ne4 8. Qg4 g6 { More risky is 8...Kf8 9 h4 c5 10 h3. } 9. Bd3 Nxd2 10.
Kxd2 c5
{ Producing a position well-known to theory, but never completely worked out. Not 10...g5+ 11 Qxg5 hxg512 g4! }
11. Nf3 Nc6
{ According to Modern Chess Openings, 11...Wc7 is more accurate; the point being that 12 Qf4 can be met by 12...f5!. 11...Bd7 12 dxc5 deserves testing. }
12. Qf4
{ Possibly better is 12 Rab1, restraining the development of Black's Q-side. }
12... Qc7
{ Better is the natural 12...a5 (if 12...g5 13 Wf6! xf6 14 exf6 g4 15 Ne5 cxd4 16 cxd4 Nxd4 17 h3 with a better ending) 13 ab1 (if 13 hb1 b6 14 a4 a6 15 Bb5 c8 16 dxc5 bxc5 17 Bxc6+ Rxc6 18 Rb8+ Rc8 holds) 13...b6 14 dxc5 xc5 15 Nd4 Nxd4 16 cxd4 a5+ with equality. }
13. h4
{ Sharper is 13 f6! g8 14 h4 and if 14...a5 (14...h5 looks practically forced) 15 h5! gxh5 16 Rxh5 cxd4 17 ah1 yielding good attacking prospects. }
13... f5 $1 { Re-establishing parity. } 14. g4 cxd4 15. cxd4 Ne7 $2
{ After the game Rossolimo suggested 15...Bd7 but White keeps the better of it after 16 gxf5 gxf5 (if 16...exf5 17 Qg3 Ne7 18 e6! Qa5+ 19 c3 xe6 20 hel gives a powerful attack) 17 Rhg1 0-0-0 18 g6. At least Black's King reaches safety in this line. }
16. gxf5 exf5 17. Bb5+ $1 Kf8 $2
{ On 17...Nc6 (if 17...d7 18 Bxd7+ Qxd7 19 e6!) 18 Bxc6+ bxc6 (18...Qxc6 is again met by 19 e6! Bxe6 20 Ne5 Qd6 21 Nxg6 Qxf4+ 22 Nxf4 and the Knight beats the Bishop in the ending) 19 Rhg1, etc.
Black's best chance, however, is to try and reach sanctuary with 17...Kd8! 18 Bd3 Be6. White undoubtedly has the initiative, but it's hard to get at the King. }
18. Bd3 { Mission accomplished. Now Black's King is pinioned to the K-side. }
18... Be6 19. Ng1 $1
{ The key move. This Knight is headed for f4 where it can exert maximum pressure on the g-Pawn.
19 K17 }
19... Kf7 20. Nh3 Rac8 $5
{ Quite rightly. Rossolimo prefers active defense. After 20...ag8 White eventually triples on the g-file (bringing his Knight to f4) with a crushing bind. }
21. Rhg1 b6
{ This takes a5 away from the Queen, eliminating any possible defenses there with check.
But Black also loses after 21...Qc3+ 22 Ke3 Nc6 23 Rab1 b4 24 Rxb4! Qxb4 25 h5 Rcg8 26 hxg6+ =xg6 27 xg6 e1+ 28 Kf3 Kxg6 29 h4!d1+ 30 Kg2 Rg8 31 Kh2! }
22. h5 $1 Qc3+ 23. Ke2 Nc6
{ On 23...Rcg8 (if 23...g5 24 Nxg5+! hxg5 25 xg5 Ke8 26 Wf6 d7 27 Bb5+! c6 28 Qxh8 Qxc2+ 29 Kf1 wins) 24 hxg6+ xg6 (if 24...Nxg625 Qf3) 25 Qh4 is decisive. }
24. hxg6+ Kg7
{ No better is 24...Ke7 25 Qh4+ Kd7 26 ad1! Wxd4 (26...Nxd4+? 27xd4!) 27 Nf4! }
25. Rad1 $1 Nxd4+
{ On 25...Qxd4 26 Kf1! Qxe5 27 e1 Qxf4 28 Nxf4 Bd7 29 Nh5+ wins. } 26. Kf1 Rhe8
27. Rg3
{ Overprotecting the Bishop. After the hasty 27 h4 Nf3! 28 Qf6+ Kg8 29 Bxf5 Nh2+ 30 Kg2f3+ 31 Kxh2 Qxf5 holds! ROSSOLIMO / FRENCH DEFENSE 323 }
27... Nc6 28. Qh4 Nxe5 { After 28...xe5 29 f4 contains too many threats. } 29.
Nf4 Ng4 30. Nxe6+ $1 Rxe6 31. Bxf5 Qc4+ 32. Kg1
{ No credit for other moves!
ROSSOLIMO
If 32...xf2 33xc4xc4 34 xf2 f4+ 35 f3, etc.
A hard-fought game! }
1-0

[Event "Santa Monica 1966"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1966.??.??"]
[Round "53"]
[White "Portisch"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Nimzo Indian Defense"]
[Title "Black magic"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "325"]

{ Here is one of the few instances when Fischer does not employ the fianchetto of his King's Bishop as a defense to the QP. By ignoring White's gambit on move nine he lays the groundwork for the positional trap (11...d7) into which Portisch falls (14xa8). Normally, two Rooks for the Queen is a good trade - better than good when it produces a setting in which the scope and power of the Rooks may be formidable. But Portisch's judgment is faulty, he fails to take into account the weakness of his Pawns. This is one of the rare occasions when the Queen can run rampant, and she does.
Still, the question remains: how did Black manage to weave his spell? To achieve a winning endgame within fifteen moves, against a specialist with White in this opening, is an almost unheard of feat. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 $1
{ To throw White off balance. I felt Portisch was just too well-versed in the K's Indian. }
3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3
{ This has been fashionable for some time. Spassky's offbeat 4 g5 leads to no advantage after 4...h6 5 Bh4 c5 6 d5 d6 7 e3 Bxc3+! 8 bxc3 e5, etc. The two Knights are better than the Bishops in such closed formations. }
4... b6 $1 { Other moves have been analyzed to death. } 5. Ne2
{ Reshevsky-Fischer, US Championship 1966, continued: 5 Bd3 Bb7 6 Nf3 0-0 (sharper is 6...Ne4! 7 0-0 f5 [or 7...Nxc3! 8 bxc3 Bxc3 9 b1 Nc6! and White hasn't got enough for his Pawn] 8 Bxe4 fxe4 9 Nd2 Bxc3 10 bxc3 0-0 11 Qg4 Rf5!= Gligorich-Larsen, Havana 1967. Not 12 Nxe4? h5) 7 0-0 Bxc3 (7...d5 is an alternative) 8 bxc3 Be4 9 Qc2 and now 9...Bxf3! (instead of 9... xd3) 10 gxf3 c5 would lead to an exciting positional struggle – two Knights vs. two Bishops, but White's Pawn formation is shaky. }
5... Ba6
{ Bronstein's active idea, trying to profit from White's last move. I had adopted it with success in the 1966 US Championship.
A very interesting try is 5...Ne4!? as in the 1967 USSR Championship. Taimanov-Levin continued 6 Qc2 Bb7 7 f3 (7 a3 is better), and now instead of 7...xc3+ (as in the game) 7...Nxc3! 8 Nxc3 (8 bxc3 Bd6! 9 e4 Nc6 with good play against White's doubled c-pawn) 8...Qh4+ 9 Qf2 Bxc3+ 10 bxc3 xf2+ 11 Kxf2 Ba6! threatening .. Nc6-a5 with at least equality. }
6. Ng3
{ Somewhat inconsistent. The whole point is to play 6 a3 so that after 6...Bxc3+ (if 6...Be7 7 Nf4 d58 cxd5 xf1 9 Kxf1 exd5 10 g4! gives White a slight edge, as demonstrated in the 1954 Botvinnik-Smyslov match) 7 Nxc3 White can avoid doubling his c-Pawn. After 7...d5 8 b3 0-0 9 a4 Nc6 I reached this position, as Black, twice in the 1966 US Championship. Addison played 10 b2? and got the worst of it after 10...dxc4 11 bxc4 Na512 Nb5 c6 13 Na3 e7! 14 c2 c5 15 Be2 (finally) 15...cxd4 16 exd4 fc8 17 0-0 c6! 18 Bf3 Nd5 and White's c-Pawn falls. Evans chose 10 Be2 dxc4 11 Ba3! e8 12 b4 Ne7 13 0-0? (13 b5 Bb7 14 0-0 permits White to regain his Pawn with a tiny pull) 13...Ned5 14 c1 c6! 15 Bf3 b5 16 a5c7 17 c2 Rad8 18 Rfd1 Bb7 19 Rd2 Nxc3 20xc3 c5! 21 dxc5 Bxf3 22 gxf3 xd2 23xd2d8 24 e1d3 25 Bb2 Nd5 and White soon collapsed. }
6... Bxc3+ $1
{ Inferior is 6...0-0 (not 6...d5?? 7 Qa4+) 7 e4 Nc6 (White keeps his initiative also after 7...c5 8 d5 d6 9 Be2 exd510 exd5 Bxc3+ 11 bxc3 Nbd7 12 0-0 Re8 13 a4, etc. Portisch-Reshevsky, Santa Monica 1966) 8 Bd3! d5 (8...Nxd4? 9 a4 wins a piece) 9 cxd5 Bxd3 10 Qxd3 exd5 11 e5 Ne4 12 a3! with a clear advantage. Portisch-Spassky, Moscow 1967. }
7. bxc3 d5 8. Qf3
{ The whole idea is dubious. No better is 8 Ba3 dxc4! 9 f3 d5 10 e4Wc6 and White hasn't got enough for the Pawn.
Simply 8 cxd5 leads to level play. It is White's insistence on seeking the initiative that lands him in trouble. }
8... O-O 9. e4 $5
{ Right is 9 cxd5 exd5 (not 9...Bxf1? 10 dxe6!) 10 Bxa6 Nxa6 11We2 Qc8 12 0-0 c5 13 dxc5 Nxc5 14 c4=. The text involves a gambit which Portisch probably had expected me to accept. }
9... dxe4 $1
{ An improvement over 9...dxc4!? which I had played against Saidy in the 1966 US Championship: 10 Bg5 h6 11 Bd2? (right is 11 h4! Bb7! 12 Bxf6 Qxf6 13 xf6 gxf6 14 Bxc4 or White might try to continue the attack with 12 Nh5!? Nbd7! – but not 12...hxg5? 13 hxg5 Nxe4 14 Nf6+!! wins – Spassky) 11...Nbd7 12 e5 Nd5 13 Nf5 (if 13 Nh5 h4!) 13...exf5 14 Qxd5 e8! 15 Bxc4 (or 15 0-0-0 c5) 15...Nxe5! 16 Qxd8 Nxc4+ 17 xe8+ Rxe8+ 18 Kd1 Nxd2 19 Kxd2 e2+ with an easily won endgame. }
10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Qxe4 Qd7 $3
{ The finest move in the game, far superior to the "natural" 11...Nd7 12 Bd3 Nf6 13 Qh4 with two Bishops and a beautiful development despite the doubled Pawns.
Black can well afford to give up two Rooks for a Queen (after 12 xa8? Nc6), as will soon become apparent. The text prepares .. Nc6-a5 hitting the "weakling," as Alekhine used to call that kind of a target. }
12. Ba3
{ White gets the worst of it after 12 Bd3 f5 13 We2 Nc6, etc. Still, this was a prudent choice. }
12... Re8 13. Bd3
{ 13 0-0-0 seems more consistent, making a real fight of it. Such double-edged lines, however, are not to Portisch's taste. }
13... f5 14. Qxa8 $2
{ Very bad judgment, White should resist temptation and try to hold on with 14 Qe2. His doubled c-Pawn, though weak, is not fatal. As the game goes, however, it is. }
14... Nc6 15. Qxe8+ Qxe8 16. O-O Na5 17. Rae1 Bxc4
{ Too routine. Crushing is 17...Qa4! E.g., 18 Bb4 (if 18 Bc1 Bxc4 19 Bxc4 Qxc4 should easily win) 18...Bxc4 19 Bxc4 Nxc4 20 Rxe6 a521 Be7 Nd2! 22 Rfe1 Ne423 f3 Qxa2! Curtains. }
18. Bxc4 { If 18 Bxf5? Qa4 wins. } 18... Nxc4 19. Bc1 c5 20. dxc5
{ White cannot hold the ending. If 20 d5? simply ..e5. } 20... bxc5 21. Bf4 h6
$1 { Preparing to expand on the K-side, which cannot be prevented. } 22. Re2
{ If 22 h4 e5! 23 Bxe5xe5 24 f4 Nf3+! 25 gxf3a4 and White's Pawns are too loose. }
22... g5 23. Be5 Qd8 24. Rfe1 { On 24 f4 Nd2! 25 fe1 Ne4 ties White up. } 24...
Kf7 25. h3 f4 26. Kh2 a6
{ Taking care of details, so that the Pawn will not be within the Bishop's reach after 26...Qd5 27 Bb8, etc. }
27. Re4 Qd5 $1
{ The Queen is boss.
Black's superiority is obvious. He has some minor threats, and a major one which cannot be met. To the surprise of no one, Sovietski Sport, a Soviet magazine, reported that Portisch had blundered and thrown away a perfectly even endgame. }
28. h4
{ After the comparatively better 28 R4e2, Black wins more slowly with 28...f3! 29 gxf3 (or 29 Re4 fxg2 followed by ... Nd2 wins) 29...d2, etc. 330 53 / SANTA MONICA 1966 }
28... Ne3 $1 { Wins the exchange, as 29 f3 fails against 29...d2 30 g1f2. } 29.
R1xe3 fxe3 30. Rxe3 Qxa2 31. Rf3+ Ke8 32. Bg7 c4 33. hxg5 hxg5 34. Rf8+
{ A check before dying. } 34... Kd7 35. Ra8
{ The ominous presence of Black's a-pawn is the deciding factor. } 0-1

[Event "Santa Monica 1966"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1966.??.??"]
[Round "54"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Najdorf"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Najdorf's night off from the Najdorf"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "332"]

{ This game follows a loss (with Black) to Najdorf earlier in the tournament. Here, Najdorf adopts the Sicilian but not his Variation — perhaps because he had lost with it previously. (See game 40.) White soon launches a sharp line, a curious violation of principle involving three consecutive Bishop sorties before his other men have been developed. In relatively uncharted terrain, both players miss their way on move twelve. It then becomes a question of whether Najdorf's doubled center Pawns are a mass or a mess. Fischer proceeds to exploit his slight advantage with restraint, gradually building up pressure against Black's uncastled King. At the right moment he offers a stunning Pawn sacrifice (26 c5). Najdorf is compelled to decline, whereupon he lands in a hopeless endgame.
This forceful and resourceful performance typified Fischer's surge throughout the last half of the 2nd Piatigorsky Cup. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Najdorf avoids the Najdorf Variation. } 3. d4 cxd4 4.
Nxd4 e6
{ 4...Nf6 obliges 5 Nc3, which precludes the Maroczy Bind by c4. Ever since ways of combatting the "Bind" have been found, it has become almost an obsession to abstain from 4...Nf6, although the most that can be said for other moves is that some of them may be as good. }
5. Nb5
{ Alekhine was of the opinion that 5 c4 is best, but it has since been discovered that White cannot maintain any advantage after 5...Nf6 6 Nc3 Bb4, etc. }
5... d6 6. Bf4 $5
{ Sharpest. Objectively speaking, it is probably best to establish an immediate Maroczy Bind with 6 c4. }
6... e5
{ After 6...Ne5!? 7 Nla3! (Bronstein's idea) is best. But not Euwe's suggestion to win a Pawn by 7 d4 a6 8 Nxd6+?? Bxd6 9 Bxe5 because of 9...Qa5+! (ZUCKERMAN). }
7. Be3 Nf6
{ Black can avoid the doubling of his Pawns by 7...a6 8 N5c3 f6 9 Bg5 Be7. However, Najdorf may have been worried about 9 Bc4! Fischer-Badilles, Manila 1967, then continued: 9...Be7 10 d5! Nxd5 11 Bxd5 0-0 12 Nc3 with absolute control of d5. After the text, 8 Bc4 is met simply by 8...Be6! (but not 8...Nxe4?? 9 d5 Be6 10xe4 d5 11 Bxd5! and wins) 9 Bxe6 fxe6 10 c4! Be7= (not 10...Nxe4? 11 g4). }
8. Bg5 $5
{ Another of Bronstein's ideas. The customary line was 8 1 c3 and after 8...a6 9 Na3 Black has 3 possibilities:
A] 9...b5 10 Nd5 Nxd5 (or 10...b811 Nxf6+ xf6 12 b1! with an edge for White) 11 exd5 e7 12 c4 with advantage.
B] Simagin gives 9...Be6 10 d5 Bxd5! 11 exd5 Ne7 12 c4 Nf5 with harmonious development for all of Black's pieces. Or 10 c4 b5 11 Nb6 Rb8 12 Nbd5 xd5 13 exd5 Ne7 with a good game. But 13 xd5!? (instead of 13 exd5) launches a promising gambit (13...Nxe4 14 f3 Nc5 15 0-0-0).
c] Best is 9...b8! 10 Bg5 b5= Aronin-Taimanov, USSR Championship 1962. 11 Nd5 is met by 11...a5+ forcing 12 Bd2 then 12...Wd8 and White has made no progress. On 13 Nxf6+ Wxf6 14 Nb1 b4! and White must regroup his forces.
The third consecutive Bishop sortie is well-motivated since White is threatening to double Black's f-Pawn. }
8... Be6 $2
{ Also weak is 8...a6 9 Bxf6 gxf6 10 N5c3 f5? 11 h5! Nd4 12 Bc4 c7 13 Nd2xc2+ 14 Ke2 xa1 (Bronstein-Polugayevsky, USSR Championship 1964) and now simply 15 xa1! must win out. Another try is 11...Bg7!? (instead of 11...Nd4) 12 Bc4 0-0 13 exf5 Nd4 14 Bd3 e8 15 e4! (not 15 f6? as in Estrin-Tcherepkov, Leningrad 1964). For instance, 15...d5 16xd5xf517 xf5xd5 18 Nc3 xg2? 19 Be4 Nxc2+ 20 Ke2 Nd4+ 21 Ke3, etc.
But Black could equalize immediately with 8...a5+! 9 d2 (or 9 Bd2 d8 draws) 9...Nxe4 10xa5 Nxa5 11 Be3 (R.Byrne suggests 11 Nc7+? Kd7 12 Dxa8 Nxg5 13 Bb5+, but after 13...d8! 14 Nc3 Bd7 15 0-0-0 Be7 White's straying Knight is soon lost) 11...d7 12 Nxa7 d5, etc. }
9. N1c3 { After 9 c4 h6! 10 Bxf6 gxf6 Black's position is excellent. } 9... a6
10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Na3 Nd4
{ Other possibilities (all favoring White) are: A]11...b5 12 Nd5! B] 11...Be7 12 c4! c] 1...f5? 12 Bc4 Bxc4 13 xc4 fxe4 14 Nxe4 d5 15 xd5! }
12. Bc4 $2
{ Correct is 12 Nc4 and if 12...c8 13 Ne3 h6 (if 13...b6 simply 14 b1) 14 Bd3 Rg8 15 Qh5! snuffs out Black's initiative. }
12... b5
{ Sharper is 12...d5! 13 xd5 (if 13 exd5 xa3 14 bxa3 Qa5) 13...Bxa3 14 bxa3 a5+ 15 Kfl (or 15 c3 Bxd5 16 Bxd5 xc3+ 17 Kf1 c8! with advantage) 15...0-0-0 with active play: e.g., 16 c3, then ... Nb5, etc. }
13. Bxe6 { Of course not the tempting 13 Bd5? b4. } 13... fxe6
{ All in all this exchange benefits Black since it enables him to protect his weak squares (d5 and f5). However if (as in the sequence) his central Pawn mass can be restrained, then it becomes merely a fixed target subject to constant pressure. }
14. Ne2 Nc6
{ Black should get some scope for his pieces by 14...Nxe2! 15xe2 d5. The check looming on h5 is not to be feared. }
15. Ng3 { 15 c4 at once is met by 15...a5+ } 15... Qd7 $2
{ Dr. Kmoch recommends 15...d5! Or 15...a5+! 16 c3 b4 and if 17 Nc4 Qc5. } 16.
c4 Nd4 17. O-O b4
{ "Castling Q-side is a risk Black's insurance company would not permit him to take." (R.Byrne)
The text later enables White to use this b-Pawn to pry open the a-file (after a3). Better is 17...Bg7. }
18. Nc2 Nxc2 19. Qxc2 h5 20. Rfd1 h4 21. Nf1 Rg8 $2
{ Better is 21...h3 22 g3 Qc6 with reasonable play. } 22. a3 $1 h3 23. g3 bxa3
24. Rxa3 Qc6 25. Qe2 $1 f5
{ Hoping to trade his h-Pawn for the e-Pawn in the event of 26 Wh5+. But White now is ready to exploit Black's poor development. }
26. c5 $1
{ More than a glancing blow. Black can know nothing about the imminent destruction of his compact mass of center Pawns. }
26... Qxe4
{ 26...dxc5 (if 26...xc5 27 Qh5+ followed by exf5, etc.) 27 h5+ e7 28 ad3 fxe4 29 R3d2 is quite hopeless for Black. }
27. Qxe4 fxe4 28. cxd6
{ Black's game is in ruins – note his pathetic triplets on the e-file. The rest is technique. }
28... Bh6 { To prevent Ne3-c4. } 29. Ra5 Kd7 30. Rxe5 Bg7 31. Rxe4 Bxb2 32. Ne3
a5 { A last gasp. Different people feel differently about resigning. } 33. Nc4
Rgb8 34. Rh4 Kc6 35. Rh7 Bd4 36. Rc7+ Kd5 37. d7 a4 38. Nb6+
{ Najdorf was probably hoping for 38 Rc8! Rxc8 39 Nb6+ Kc5 40 dxc8=+?xc8 41 Nxc8 a3 with some practical chances. But we both overlooked the neat Zwischenzug 40 c1+! Kxb6 41 xc8! breaking all further resistance.
The text is good enough, but prolongs the game. }
38... Rxb6 39. Rc8 { The point: on 39...Rbb8 the Pawn queens with check. }
39... Rd6 40. Rxa8 Rxd7 41. Rxa4 e5 42. Kf1 Rb7 43. f4 $1 Ke6 44. fxe5 Rf7+ 45.
Ke2 Rf2+ 46. Kd3 Bxe5 47. Re1 $1
{ After 47...Rf5 48 Ra5 (win by pin!) 48...f6 49 exe5 Rxe5 50 Rxe5 Kxe5 51 Ke3 Kf5 52 Kd4! (but not 52 Kf3 Kg5 53 g4?? Kh4! and draws) 52...g5 53 Ke5 Kg4 54 Ke4 Kg5 55 Kf3 Kf5 56 g4+ followed by kg3 wins. }
1-0

[Event "Havana Olympic 1966"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1966.??.??"]
[Round "55"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Bednarsky"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "The price of incaution"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "339"]

{ Once more Fischer rehabilitates his pet move (6 Bc4) against his favorite Najdorf Variation, and it is remarkable that he should continue to win with such ease. In fact, his opponents do not seem to offer serious resistance. Young Bednarsky apparently is caught napping, improvises, and loses in just 22 moves!
Seeking active counterplay, Bednarsky blunders through bravado. He takes a tainted Pawn (9...fxe4)and impetuously pursues the attack only to find himself in an ambush which, ironically, he had helped to construct. By move 12 Fischer obtains a formation at which he had had success even as a child. Naturally he wins, but the economy with which he does so is delightful. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4
{ Here we go again! } 6... e6
{ Probably best. White's Bishop is made to "bite on granite.' } 7. Bb3
{ Too slow is Bronstein's idea 7 a3. E.g., Robatsch-Fischer, Havana 1965: 7...Be7 8 Ba2 0-0 9 0-0 b5 10 f4 Bb7 11 f5 e5 (Black is healthy as long as White can't exploit his hole on d5) 12 Nde2 Nbd7 13 Ng3 c8 14 Be3 (if 14 Bg5 xc3! 15 bxc3 Nxe4 with advantage -Gligorich) 14...Nb6 15 Bxb6 Qxb6+ 16 Kh1 Qe3! (to prevent Nh5) with the bettergame for Black.
White would of course like to get in f4-f5 as swiftly as possible, but he must exercise some caution. The text is essentially a waiting move which narrows Black's options. After 7 f4 Black has a choice of 7...d5, 7...b5, or 7...Nxe4 8 Nxe4 d5. }
7... Nbd7
{ In order to reach c5 with an attack on the Bishop as well as the e-Pawn. But 7...b5! is better (see game 17). An example of staticWhite playis Garcia-Fischer, Havana Olympic 1966:8 a3 Be7 9 Be3 0-0 10 0-0 Bb711 f3 Nbd7 12 Qd2 (12 Bxe6!? leads to rough equality) 12...Ne5 13 Qf2 Qc714 Rac1 Kh8! 15 Nce2 Rg8! 16 Kh1 g5! 17 h3 Rg6 18 Ng3 Rag8 (White is curiously helpless against the threat of ..h5 and ...g4. His normal break with f4 is restrained by the silent Bishop on b7) 19 Nxe6? fxe620 Bxe6 Nxe4! 21 Nxe4 Rxe6 White resigns.
After 7...b5 recent analysis indicates that Black's best plan is rapid development on the Q-side: 8 f4 Bb7 9 f5 e5 10 Nde2 Nbd7 11 Bg5 Be7. Now on 12 Ng3? (Correct is 12 Bxf6 Nxf6 13 Qd3 Rc8 with even chances – Fischer-Zuckerman, US Championship 1966) 12...Rc8! 13 0-0 h5! White's in trouble, as indicated by the following examples:
A]14 h4 b4 15 Bxf6 Bxf6 16 Nd5 Bxh4 17 Nxh5!? Qg518 f6g6 19 Ng7+ Kd8 20 Rf3 Bg3 21 Qd3 Bh2+ 22 Kf1 Nc5 23 Rh3!? Rh4! 24 Qf3 Nxb3 25 axb3 Rxh3 26 Qxh3 Bxd527 exd5 Qxf6+ 28 Ke1 Qf4 and since there's nothing left – but emptiness – White resigns. (R.Byrne-Fischer, Tunisia Interzonal, 1967)
B] 14 Bxf6 Nxf6 15 Nd5 h4 16 Nxf6+ gxf6 17 Ne2 Bxe4 18 Bd5 b6+ 19 Kh1 Bxd5 20 Qxd5 xc2 21 d3 c6 22 Rac1 h3! (Ciocaltea-Gheorghiu, Bucharest 1967.) }
8. f4 $1 Nc5 $5
{ Too passive is 8...Be7 9 f3 0-0 10 g4. Bad is 8...b5 9 f5! e5 10 Nc6! Qc7 11 Nb4! and White is ready to sit on Black once he occupies the hole on d5. }
9. f5 $1
{ Thematic. Bednarsky told me after the game he had reached this position before, as White, and he had continued 9 e5 dxe5 10 fxe5 Nfd7 11 Bf4 vs. Bogdanovich, East Germany 1964. Obviously that approach is too tame. }
9... Nfxe4 $5
{ Playing with fire. The question is whether 9...e5 is sufficient. Apparently not, after 10 Nde2 Nxb3 (not 10...Ncxe4? 11 Nxe4 Nxe4 12 Wd5 Ng5 13 h4) 11 axb3 h6 12 Ng3 followed by Nh5 with a bind. R.Byrne-Bogdanovich, Sarajevo 1967.
9...Be7 is steadier than the text (for 9...Nxb3 10 axb3 see note to White's 7th move in game 58). But White stays on top after 10 Qf3 0-0 11 Be3. Now on 11...d5 12 exd5 Nxb3 13 Nxb3! exf5 (if 13...Nxd5? 14 0-0-0 wins a Pawn) 14 0-0-0, etc. }
10. fxe6 $1 Qh4+ $2
{ Tempting, but suicidal. Black had two better tries: A] 10...Bxe6 11 Nxe4 Nxe4 12 xe6 fxe6 (not 12...Qh4+ 13 g3 Nxg3 14 Bg5! We4+ 15 d2 Nxh1 16 Nc7+ d7 17 Nxa8 wins) 13Wg4 Nc5 14 Be3! with a strong initiative.
B] 10...fxe6! 11 Nxe4 Nxe4 12 0-0 e7! (weaker is 12...Nc5 13 Wg4 – if 13...Nxb3 14 axb3 e5 15 Wf3). White has good play for the Pawn, but no forced win in view. }
11. g3 Nxg3 12. Nf3 $1
{ This twist is well known: e.g., from the Vienna I e4 e5 2 Nc3 Nf6 3 f4 d54 fxe5 Nxe4 5 d3 h4+ 6 g3 Nxg3 7 Nf3, etc.
Only not 12 exf7+?d813 Nf3 Qe7+! }
12... Qh5 13. exf7+ Kd8 14. Rg1 Nf5 15. Nd5 $1 Qxf7
{ Black's attack has boomeranged. Now his King gets caught in a merciless crossfire.
On 15...h6 16 Nf4! picks off the Queen. No better is 15...Nxb3 16 Bg5+ d7 (or 16...Be7 17 Nxe7! Nxe7 18 xd6+) 17 e5+! }
16. Bg5+ Ke8 17. Qe2+ $1
{ By now I was hunting for bigger game than the paltry win of a Queen after 17 Nf6+ gxf6 18 Bxf7+. }
17... Be6 18. Nf4 Kd7 19. O-O-O { One threat of course is 20 Ne5+. } 19... Qe8
{ Black is helpless. After 19...Nxb3+ 20 axb3 Qe8 21 Rge1 Bg8 22 Qd3 it's also quits. The only way for Black to last is to give up his Queen with 19...Bxb3 20 Ne5+ Kc7 21 Nxf7 Bxf7. }
20. Bxe6+ Nxe6 21. Qe4 $1 { Centralization with a vengeance! } 21... g6 22.
Nxe6 { On 22...Qxe6 23 Qxb7+ Ke8 24 Rge1! wins everything. } 1-0

[Event "Havana Olympic 1966"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1966.??.??"]
[Round "56"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Gligorich"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "The Fischer continuation"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "344"]

{ Fischer's surprising 4 Bxc6, a revival of Emanuel Lasker's Exchange Variation – the one he used at St. Petersburg in 1914 to defeat Alekhine and Capablanca, but which subsequently fell into desuetude because ways to equalize were rapidly discovered – drew from his opponent the obligatory response. However, Fischer's next move, regarded as inferior, and his sixth (the customary follow-up) prepared no one for the gambit which he introduced on move seven. Gligorich reacted with innocent appropriateness until move seventeen, then made a startling blunder that met with speedy retribution. Because, in the course of the tournament, Fischer had played and won two other games with this very same line (demonstrating in each case White's hitherto unsuspected potential), it was promptly dubbed "The Fischer Variation." Of course, sticklers will insist that it should be called the Fischer continuation of the Barendregt Variation. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 $1
{ A surprise! I had introduced this in an earlier game against Portisch (see note to Black's 6th). After sizing up Gligorich over-the-board, I decided he was ripe for a repeat performance. }
4... dxc6
{ This recapture is so automatic that most annotators fail to comment on it. After 4...bxc6 5 d4 exd4 6 Qxd4 White maintains an enduring initiative, If 6...Qf6 7 Qd3! (but not 7 e5 Qg6 8 0-0 Bb7 9 e6? fxe6 10 Ne5 xg2+! 11 Kxg2 c5+ – an old trap.) }
5. O-O $1
{ "The text poses more problems for Black than does an immediate 5 d4 and Nimzovich is once more proved right in his pronouncement that the threat is stronger than its execution. Though White has sold his strong Bishop for a Knight, a Bishop which is usually Black's main strategical problem in many variations of the Lopez, there is no basic flaw in White's tactics. He has gained a tempo for development, somewhat spoiled Black's Pawn structure and revived the threat on Black's e-Pawn." (GLIGORICH).
The text was favored by Emanuel Lasker, Bernstein and also, in recent years, by the Dutch master Barendregt. I had been pondering it for a long time before deciding to include it in my arsenal. }
5... f6 $1
{ "This position has not been seen frequently in the modern grandmaster praxis and, thanks to imaginative Fischer, we have to go back to the 19th century to find the alternatives for Black. It is not clear, however, that Black has any better way of defending the e-Pawn. 95 (GLIGORICH).
Black can defend his e-Pawn in numerous ways. Let's look at the lemons first.
A] 5...Be7? (played by Reshevsky) 6 Nxe5! Qd4 7 Nf3 Qxe4 8 Re1 (instead of 8 d3? as in Malesich-Reshevsky, Maribor 1967) and it's doubtful Black can get out of the opening with equality. One example, 8...f5 9 b3! Nf6 10 Ba3 (or 10 Re5!) 10...Be611 Nd4, etc.
B] The ballet dancer Harmonist showed good sense by trying 5...Qf6 with the threat of ... Bg4 (against Schallopp in Frankfurt 1887) but after 6 d4 exd4 7 Bg5! Qg68xd4 White can get a clear initiative.
C] 5...Bd6? 6 d4 exd4 (not 6...f6? 7 dxe5 fxe5 8 Nxe5! – or 6...Bg4 7 dxe5 Bxf3 8 xf3 with a comfortable K-side Pawn majority. as in Schallopp-Blackburne, Frankfurt 1887) 7 xd4 f6 8 Nbd2! Ne7 9 Nc4, etc.
D] A reasonable try is Bronstein's 5...Qd6!? 6 d3(6 Na3? works well against 6...Be6? 7 Ng5 but 6...b5! strands the Knight) 6...f67 Be3 c5 8 Nbd2 Be6 9 Qe2 0-0-0=. White has possibilities of breaking on the Q-side after a3 followed by Rfb1 and b4, but Black can probably prevent this expansion.
So best is 5...Qd6 6 d4 exd4 7 Nxd4, etc. E] The most ambitious continuation is 5...Bg4!? 6 h3 h5!? (Em. Lasker used to win such positions for White after 6...Bxf3 7 Qxf3. Hort-Kolarov, Poland 1967, continued: 7...Qf68 Qg3 Bd6 9 d3 Qg6 10 Be3!? Qxg311 fxg3 and White managed to grind out a win in the ensuing endgame) 7 d3!(On 7 c3 Qd3! 8 hxg4 hxg4 9 Nxe5 Bd6! 10 Nxd3 Bh2+ draws. A fantasy variation occurs after 8 Qb3? Bxf39 Qxb7 Kd7 10 Qxa8 Bxg2! 11 Kxg2 Rh6! 12 Rg1 Rg6+ 13 Kh2 Rxg1 14 Kxg1 Bc5 with a winning attack) 7...Qf6 8 Nbd2! (Keres in his old book on the open games wrongly praises this whole line for Black, having considered only 8 hxg4? hxg4 9 Ng5 Qh6 10 Nh3 Qh4 11 Kh2 g6 12 Nc3 gxh3 13 g3 Qe7 with advantage) 8...Ne7(now 8...g5 is met by 9 Nc4! Bxf3 10 Qxf3 Qxf311 gxf3 f6 12 h4! gxh4 13 f4 with promising play for the Pawn. But not 9 Re1? Be6 10 d4 g4 11 Nxe5 gxh3 12 g3 h2+ 13 Kg2 h4 with initiative) 9 Re1! (or 9 Nc4! Bxf3 10 Qxf3 Qxf3 11 gxf3 Ng612 Be3c513 a4! White stands better and eventually won. Hort-Sliwa, Poland 1967) 9...Ng6 10 d4! Bd6 11 hxg4 hxg4 12 Nh2 Rxh2! 13 Qxg4! Rh4 14 Qf5 and White is slightly better. }
6. d4 Bg4 $1
{ Best. In our earlier game Portisch had tried 6...exd4 7 Nxd4!c5 (Portisch played the more sensible 7...Bd6 in a subsequent event but it's still inferior after 8 Qh5+! g6 9 Qf3 Bxh2+? 10 Kxh2 Qxd4 11 Rd1!) 8 Nb3 Qxd1 (now 8...Bd6? is met by 9 Nxc5!) 9 Rxd1 Bd6 (the queer-looking 9...b6 as a defensive try scored an unmerited success in a Soviet women's tournament due to White's passive play. But 10 Bf4 Ra7! 11 Nc3 Ne7 12 a4! followed by a5 is almost decisive. (12...a5? is impossiblebecause of 13 Nb5! Polugaievsky's 9...Bd7 offers defensive prospects) 10 Na5! b5(amusing is 10...Bg4? 11 f3 0-0-0?? 12 e5! and Black resigns. Hort-Zelandinow, Havana 1967. Keres tried 10...Nh6 but also failed to equalize after 11 Bxh6 gxh6 12 Nc4 Be7 13 Nc3 Be6 14 Nd5. Bagirov-Keres, Moscow 1967) 11 c4 Ne7 12 Be3 f5 13 Nc3 f4 14 e5! Bxe5 15 Bxc5 and Black's disorganized position soon crumbled. }
7. c3 $1
{ The text involves a gambit. Curiously, this was Gligorich's own published suggestion when he annotated his game against Lee at Hastings 1965-6, which had continued 7 dxe5 Qxd1 8 Rxd1 Bxf3! (Fischer-Smyslov, Monaco 1967, went 8...fxe5? 9 d3! Bxf3 10 xf3 Nf6 11 Nc3 Bb4 12 Bg5! Bxc3 13 bxc3! [was Black playing for the cheap trap 13 Bxf6? Bxb2 14 Bxg7?? Bxa1 15 Bxh8 0-0-0!] 13...Rf8 14 Bxf6 Rxf6 15 Rxf6 gxf6 16 Rd1! and White should have won the ending) 9 gxf3 fxe5 10 f4 Nf6 and now 11 Nc3! (instead of 11 fxe5? Nxe4 12 Be3 Bc5 13 Nd2 Nxd2 14 Bxc5 0-0-0 as in the game) 11...Bd6 12 fxe5 Bxe5 13 Na4! gives White the better of a probable draw.
So the best is 7 dxe5! xd1 8 Rxd1 xf3! 9 gxf3 fxe5 10 Be3! followed by Nd2-c4 with pressure. If 10...Ne7 11 f4! keeps the initiative. }
7... exd4 { An alternative is 7...Bd6 holding the center. } 8. cxd4 Qd7
{ Black dares not accept the Pawn. 8...Bxf3 9 Qxf3 Qxd4 10 Rd1 Qc4 11 Bf4, etc. However, Gligorich said (in Chess Review) he had completely forgotten his Hastings analysis, which indicated Black's best as 8...c5! 9 d5 Bd6. }
9. h3 $1
{ "Putting the question to the Bishop." Nimzovich, Steinitz, Evans, and other theoreticians have pointed out the enormous value of kicking this Bishop before the pin becomes really troublesome. Here, White must exercise care since his h-Pawn could easily become a potential target. }
9... Be6
{ This natural retreat, which releases the tension, gives White too free a hand and is the subsequent cause of Gligorich's difficulties. Better is 9...Bh5! as played against me by Jimenez in a later round. After 10 Ne5! Bxd1 (bad is 10...Qxh3 11 gxh3 Bxd1 12 Rxd1 fxe513 dxe5 Bc5 14 Kg2 with a dangerous preponderance of center Pawns) 11 Nxd7 Kxd7 12 Rxd1 Black should hold the ending, although he found a way to lose: 12...Re813 f3(13 Nc3 is more accurate) 13...Ne7 14 Nc3 Kc8 15 Be3 f516 Rac1 fxe4 17 fxe4g6? (17...Ng6 is correct) 18 Bf4! Bg719 d5! Rd8 20 Na4! Rhf8 21 g3 g5? (cracking under the pressure – 21...Rf7 is more sensible) 22 Bxg5 Rf7 23 Kg2 cxd5 24 exd5 Kb8 25 Rel Bf8 26 Rf1! Rg7 27 Bf6 Rg8 28 Rce1 Rd7 29 d6! cxd6 30 Bxe7 Bxe7 31 Rf7 resigns (if 31...Re8 32 Nb6 Rc7 33 Nd5). }
10. Nc3 O-O-O 11. Bf4 $1 Ne7 $2
{ More solid is 11...Bd6! 12 Bxd6 Qxd6.
The critical line is 11...g5!? 12 Bg3 h513 d5! cxd5 14 Rc1! and now: A] 14...Bd6 15 Na4! Kb8 16 Nc5 Qe7 17 Nxa6+! bxa618 Nd4 Bd7 19 Qb3+ Ka7 20 Rxc7+!! Bxc7 21 Bxc7 Bb5 (if 21...Qc5 22 Qe3!is the simplest win) 22 Nc6+! (Palacio) 22...Bxc6 23 Qb6+ and mate next.
B] The fantastic win is 14...dxe4 15 Na4! Kb8 16 Rxc7!! Qxd1 17 Rc8+!!! Ka7 (or 17...Kxc8 18 Nb6 mate) 18 Bb8+ Ka8 19 Nb6 mate. }
12. Rc1 Ng6 { Black has lost time in order to reach this inferior square. } 13.
Bg3 Bd6 14. Na4 $1 Bxg3 $2
{ Yielding his c5 permanently to the Knight. Correct is14...Kb8 15 Nc5 Qe7. }
15. fxg3 Kb8 { Bad is 15...b6 16 d5! Bf7 (16...cxd5? 17 Nxb6+) 17 Qe2!, etc. }
16. Nc5 Qd6 17. Qa4 $1 Ka7 $4
{ Catastrophic. After 17...Bc8 18 Rc3 (18...Qxg3? 19 Ne5 Qh4 20 Nxc6+), Black might have hung on with 18...Nf8! }
18. Nxa6 $1 { The finishing stroke. } 18... Bxh3
{ Desperation! 18...bxa6 19 xc6 costs Black's Queen to prevent mate. } 19. e5
$1 { The most forceful method. } 19... Nxe5
{ Sheer desperation!! After 19...fxe5 20 Nc5+ Kb8 21 c3! followed by Ra3 is most persuasive. }
20. dxe5 fxe5 21. Nc5+ Kb8 22. gxh3 e4 { Never say die! } 23. Nxe4 Qe7 24. Rc3
b5 25. Qc2
{ Time to consolidate. 25 a6 also does the trick. According to a Havana newspaper, some casual spectators who had just wandered in thought White had merely won two pieces for a Rook. Nobody could believe that Gligorich was playing on two pieces behind! The rude awakening came when – }
1-0

[Event "Monaco 1967"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1967.??.??"]
[Round "57"]
[White "Larsen"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "Change of pace"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "351"]

{ Larsen, uncharacteristically, forces an early exchange of Queens so that he can spring a surprise in the resulting endgame. Fischer beats him to it (13...b6) and proceeds to defend with meticulous care. It looks as if a draw must ensue, but Larsen presses. He is rebuffed, and again a draw seems imminent. Larsen senses no danger and, as if by inertia, continues to play for a win. As the game simplifies, the self-inflicted dark square weaknesses in the Dane's position gradually reveal themselves. By move thirty it becomes Fischer's turn to assume the initiative, and he probes these flaws judiciously. Thrown on the defensive, Larsen makes one or two reckless moves out of which Fischer constructs elegant combinations. Thus, what begins as a barren endgame is transformed into an exhibition of chess sensibility and virtuosity. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2
{ Larsen had won some good games with 5 Nf3 0-0 6 Be3 but after 6...e5! (which no one seems to have played) White gets no advantage. 7 e2 Nc6 transposes into well-known modern lines. And 7 d5 Ng4 8 Bg5 f6 9 h4 Qe8 gives Black dynamic play. Now 10 h3 Nh6 11 g4!? f5 is too risky for White. Finally on 7 dxe5 dxe5 8 Qxd8 Rxd8 9 Nd5(?) Na6! gives Black the better ending. }
5... O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. Be3
{ A bit of a surprise. I had expected 8 d5 Ne7 9 Ne1 Nd7 10 Nd3 f5 11 Bd2. Now I had in mind 11...c5! 12 f3 f4! (but not 12...f6? 13 g4! f4 14 h4! and Black's K-side counterplay is completely stymied) with active chances. Larsen-Najdorf, Santa Monica 1966, had continued 11...Nf6? 12 f3 f4 13 c5! g5 14 c1 Ng6 15 cxd6 cxd6 16 Nb5 Rf7 17 Wc2! Ne8 18 a4 and White came first on the Q-side since Black's attack never got off the ground. }
8... Re8 $1
{ The cleanest way to equalize. Najdorf found this move after some painful experiences with 8...Ng4 in his match vs. Reshevsky. The main point if that 9 d5 Nd4! levels. }
9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8 Nxd8
{ A dubious improvement over 10...Rxd8! as played by Reshevsky in his match with Benko. After 11 Bg5 Black must not play 11...d7? (after which Benko's 12 Bd1!! followed by Ba4 was very strong) but 11...f8! solves all his problems. }
11. Nb5 Ne6 12. Ng5 Re7 13. Rfd1
{ Larsen is attempting to improve on Reshevsky-Fischer, Santa Monica 1966, which continued: 13 Nxe6 Bxe6 14 f3 c6 15 Nc3 Rd7 16 Rfd1 Bf8 17 Kf2 b618 b3 Rb7 19 Na4 Nd7 20 Nb2 b5 with an eventua1 draw. }
13... b6 $1
{ In my 9th match game with Reshevsky, 1961, I tried 13...c6!? Larsen told me he had intended 14 Nxa7!? (instead of 14 Nxe6 Bxe6 15 Nc3 Rd7=) 14...Bd7 15 Nxe6 Bxe616 f3. But after 16...Rd7!(threatening ...Rd4) Black has fair play for the Pawn, considering that the Knight is stranded on a7.
The book text was an improvement that I had hatched some time ago. }
14. c5 $5
{ Typically, Larsen adopts an enterprising continuation. He should settle for 14 Nxe6 Bxe6 15 f3 with a draw in view. By overestimating his chances, he gradually drifts into a losing position. }
14... Nxc5 { Naturally not14...bxc5? 15 Nxe6 Bxe6 16 Bxc5 Rd7 17 f3 wins. } 15.
Rd8+ Bf8
{ No better is 15...Re8 16 Rxe8+ Nxe8 17 Bxc5 bxc5 18 Bc4! Or 15...Ne8? 16 Bxc5 bxc517 Nxc7 Rxc718 Rxe8+ Bf8 19 Nxh7! }
16. Nxa7 Rxa7
{ On 16...Bb717 Rxa8 Bxa818 f3 White has a slight pull despite his misplaced Knight. After18...c619 Nc8 Rb720 Rd1 maintains some pressure. }
17. Rxc8 { White recovers his pawn with even chances. } 17... Kg7
{ Black wisely resists the temptation of 17...Ncxe4?? 18 Nxe4 Nxe4 19 Bh6. But even more accurate than the text is 17...h6! 18 Nf3 Kg7 19 Bxc5 bxc5 20 Bd3 completely neutralizing any initiative for either side. }
18. f3 Ne8 19. a3
{ Larsen's reluctance to simplify will soon backfire. Correct is 19 Bxc5! bxc5 20 Rb8 with theoretical winning chances because of the passed a-Pawn. But it would be difficult to make headway because of the opposite coloredBishops. }
19... Nd6 20. Rd8
{ Optimistic as ever! 20 Rb8 Nd721 Rd8 Nb722 Rc8 Nd6 would lead to a draw by repetition. }
20... h6 21. Nh3 Ne6 22. Rb8 Re8 23. Rxe8 Nxe8
{ "Now White's initiative is over and the position is even but by no means drawish. There is a lot of play."(κMOCH)
White's dark squares, notably his d4, are weak. But it's still not too serious. }
24. Bb5
{ Pointless. White should start bringing his Knight into the game via f2. He can't prevent .. Bc5, gaining control of the dark squares. Of course not 24 b4? Bxb4. }
24... Nd6 25. Bf1 Nb7 $1 26. Nf2 Bc5 $1 27. Bxc5 Nbxc5 28. Rd1 h5 $1
{ To keep the Knight out of g4. This "prophylactic"thrust would have gladdened Nimzovich's heart. Not 28...Nd4? 29 Ng4 f6 30 f4! }
29. Rd5
{ Larsen still has illusions, but his game is fast deteriorating. More prudent is 29 Nd3 Nxd330 Bxd3 Nd4 31 Kf2. White probably should hold the ending despite Black's creeping pressure. }
29... Kf6 30. h4 Ke7 $1 31. Bc4
{ The Pawn is poisoned: 31 Rxe5? c6 followed by ... Nd7 (or ...f6). The mission of the Rook has failed, but no serious harm has been done. }
31... c6 32. Rd2 Nd4 $1
{ Finally the Knight has gained this dominant outpost. } 33. Kf1
{ The more active 33 Nd3 is preferable. Now Black's tactical threats begin to proliferate. }
33... f5 $1 34. b4
{ Meets with a still sharper counter thrust. 34 Nd3 offered a better chance for survival. After the text White's a-Pawn is weakened.
Not 34 exf5 Nxf5 with the double threat of ... Ne3+ or Nxh4 (and if 35 Re2? Ng3+). }
34... b5 $1 35. Bg8
{ 35 bxc5 bxc4 clearly wins for Black. And 35 Bxb5? is refuted by 35...Ncb3. }
35... fxe4 $1 { Fixing White with another weakness. } 36. fxe4
{ Not 36 bxc5 e3 37 Rxd4 (if 37 Rd3 exf2 38 Kxf2 Ra8 39 Ba2 b4 – or 37 Ra2? exf2 38 Kxf2 Kf8!) 37...exd4 38 Nd3 Rxa3 39 Ke2 Rc3, etc. }
36... Nd7 37. Rd3 Ra6 $1
{ Threatening Nc2 which, if played immediately, could have been met by38Rc3. }
38. Rc3 c5 $1
{ This surprising combination apparently confused Larsen, who was in time-pressure. }
39. g4 $2
{ The last blunder. On 39 bxc5 b4! 40 Rc1! (not 40 axb4 a1+) there's still a lot of fight. If 40...xa3 (or 40...bxa3 41 Ba2) 41 c6 Nb6. }
39... c4
{ This protected passed Pawn is just too strong. Not 39...Nf6 40 Rxc5! } 40.
gxh5 gxh5 41. Bd5 Nf6 42. Rg3 Nxd5 43. exd5 Rf6 44. Kg2
{ The sealed move. White is completely tied up. On 44 Kg1? Ne2+ wins. Or if 44 Ke1 Rf4 mops up. }
44... Nf5 45. Rh3 Rg6+ 46. Kf3 Nd4+ 47. Ke3
{ On 47 Ke4 Kd6 White is in zugzwang. If 48 Rh2 (to prevent ..g2) 48...g3. }
47... Rg2 48. Rh1 Kd6 49. Ne4+ Kxd5 50. Nc3+ Ke6 51. Rc1
{ White has to prevent .c2 as then the Knight cannot move because of ...Re2 mate. }
51... Rh2 52. a4 Rh3+ 53. Kf2 Nb3 54. Kg2 Nxc1 55. Kxh3 bxa4 56. Nxa4 Ne2
{ 358 57 / MONACO 1967 } 57. b5 c3 58. b6 c2 59. Nc5+ Kd5 60. Nb3
{ White can choose his own end. If 60 Nd3 (or 60 b7 c1=Q 61 b8= Q Qh1 mate) 60...Nf4+ 61 Nxf4+ exf4 62 b7 c1= Q 63 b8= QQh1 mate.
60 KC6 }
60... Kc6 61. Kg2 Kxb6 0-1

[Event "Skopje 1967"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1967.??.??"]
[Round "58"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Geller"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense"]
[Title "Flawed masterpiece"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "360"]

{ After Fischer dropped this miniature (his third loss in a row to Geller) Kurajica concluded: "He just cannot play against Geller." Another Yugoslav, Trifunovich, opined at greater length:
Geller is one of the best-prepared players in the world as to opening theory, and Fischer cannot be superior in that respect...Fischer [as White] chose a very sharp and modern variation...playing to win in the early stage of the game, as he usually does, and successfully, against weaker opponents. Fischer played better and attained a superior position, but it was very difficult to find the right solution over-the-board...There was his mistake...He has to impose a hard positional game, playing without pretensions for a win in the very opening. Nowhere but in the notes that follow have the above errors been answered. Were it not for a momentary lapse (a3?), Fischer would have won this little gem on move twenty – despite his critics. }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bc4 e6
{ There is no apparent refutation to Benko's roguish 6...Wb6!? Saidy ventured it against me in the 1967 US Championship. After 7 Nb3 e6 8 0-0 Be7 9 Be3Wc7 10 f4 0-0 11 Bd3 it's a mutually hard game. Also see game 11. }
7. Be3
{ 7 Bb3 cuts down Black's options. Fischer-Dely, Skopje 1967, continued: 7...a6 8 f4! Qa5 (8...Na5 9 f5! Nxb3 10 axb3 Be7 11 Qf3 0-0 12 Be3 Bd7 13 g4 e5 14 Nde2 with a crush in sight: Fischer-Bielicki, Mar del Plata 1960. 8...c7 9 f5! Nxd4 10 Qxd4 exf5 11 exf5 Bxf5 12 0-0 yields a strong attack) 9 0-0! Nxd4? (a better try is 9...d5 but after 10 Nxc6! bxc6 11 f5! White's on top. If 11...d4? 12 Ne2 e5 either 13 Ng3 or 13 Qd3 retains the advantage. Also on 11...Be7 12 e5 Nd7 13 fxe6 fxe6 [or 13...Nxe5 14 Bf4!] 14 Qg4. Finally, 11...Bc5+ 12 Kh1 0-0 may be tenable) 10xd4 d5 (10...c5 11 Qxc5 dxc5 12 a4! puts Black in an excruciating bind) 11 Be3! Nxe4 (if 11...Ng4 12 Kh1! Nxe3 13 xe3 dxe4 14 Qxe4! Be7 15 ae1 and mate is lurking in the wings: e.g., 15...g6 16 Nd5! Bd8 17 Qe5 0-0 18 Ne7+! wins. Slightly more accurate, but still bad, is 11...dxe4 12 Nxe4 Be7 13 Nd6+, etc.) 12 Nxe4 dxe4 13 f5! Qb4 (if 13...exf5 14 g4! Be6 [14...b4 15 Ba4+! b5 16 Qd5! wins] 15 gxf5 Bxb3 16 axb3 b4 17 Ra4! Qxd4 18 xd4 and Black is in a curiously hopeless predicament: e.g., 18...Be7 19 xe4 Kf8 20 f6!! Bxf6 21 xf6! gxf6 22 Bh6+ and mates) 14 fxe6 Bxe6 15 Bxe6! fxe6 16 Rxf8+!xf8 17 Qa4+! resigns. On 17...b5 18 Qxe4 Rd8 19 Qc6+! Rd7 20 d1 e7 and now 21 Bb6 (Dely). (About the only move that doesn't win is 21 Bg5? 0-0!) }
7... Be7
{ Too routine. Black should start quicker action on the Q-side. More reasonable is 7...a6 8 Bb3c7 9 e2 (or 9 f4) 9...b5 10 0-0-0Na5 (10...Bb7 is also possible, whereupon White might reply 11 f3). }
8. Bb3
{ Against Pascual, in a clock exhibition game at Davao (Philippines) 1967, I essayed 8 Qe2 a6 9 0-0-0 c7 10 Bb3 Bd7 11 g4 Nxd4 12 Bxd4 (Fishy. Better is 12 xd4) 12...e5 13 g5 exd4 14 gxf6 dxc3 15 fxe7 cxb2+ 16 Kb1 Kxe7? (16...Be6 equalizes) 17 h5! g6 18 h4+ f6 19 e5! dxe5 20 f4 e4 21 h6 Rae8 22 Rd4 Kd8 23hd1 Kc8 (the beauty part is 23...Re7 24 Be6!! xe6 25 g7, mopping up) 24 xd7xd7 25 Rxd7 Kxd7 26 g7+ Kd6 27 xb7 e3 28 b6+ resigns. }
8... O-O 9. Qe2
{ Preparing Q-side castling and disallowing the reply ... Ng4 which would be the case after 9 d2. }
9... Qa5
{ Geller's attempt to improve on the customary 9...a6 10 0-0-0c7 11 g4 Nxd4 12 xd4! b5 (Tal gives 12...e5 13 Rc4! d8 14 g5 e8 15 Rxc8!xc8 16 h4 Nc7 17 g4 followed by h5, with a terrific attack) 13 g5 Nd7 14 Qh5 Ne5 15 f4 Nc6 16 Rd3 Nb4 17 d2 d8 18 f5 g6 19 fxg6 hxg6 20 h4 Nc6 21 g3 Ne5 22 h4 Bb7 23 h5 b4 24 hxg6 Nxg6 25 Rdh2 bxc3 26 Bd4 e5 27 Rh8+!! Nxh8 28 g6! Bf6 29 gxf7++ Kf8 30 Rh7! and White wins (Velimirovich-Nikolich, Belgrade 1964.) }
10. O-O-O { 10 0-0 also comes into consideration. } 10... Nxd4
{ Apparently Geller rejected10...Bd7 11 Ndb5! Ne8 12 Bf4 a6 13 Nxd6 Nxd6 14 Bxd6 Bxd6 15 Rxd6 Qg5+ 16 Qd2 Qxg2 17 Rhd1 Be8 18 Qf4 withgood pressure. }
11. Bxd4 Bd7
{ Black cannot afford to go Pawn-snatching with 11...Qg5+? 12 Kb1 Qxg2? 13 Rhg1! Qh3 (if 13...Qxh2 14 Rh1 Qf4 15 Rdg1! e5 [or 15...g6 16 Be3 Qe5 17 g5]16 Be3 Bg4 17 Qe1 Qf3 18 Rg3 wins the Queen) 14 e5 Ne8 (on 14...dxe5 15 Qxe5 carries too many threats) 15 exd6 Bxd616 Bxg7! Nxg7 17 Rxd6 and it's not a game any more. But interesting is 12...e5 (instead of 12...Qxg2) 13 h4 Qxg2 14 Rdg1 Bg4 15 Rxg2! Bxe2 16 Nxe2 exd4 17 Nxd4 with advantage (if 17...Nxe418 f3! followed byNf5). }
12. Kb1 { A critical position. White's immediate threat is 13 Bxf6. } 12... Bc6
{ In a later round Sofrevsky tried to improve against me with 12...Rad8, but got into trouble after13 Qe3! Black now rejected a dangerous Pawn sac which must be examined very carefully: 13...b5!? But 14 a3! (not 14 Bxa7 Ra8 with active play) 14...b4 15 axb4 xb4 16 Bxa7 Qb7 17 Qb6! a8 18 f3 and Black has no good way to prosecute his attack. Consequently, Sofrevsky chose 13...b6 14 Bxf6! gxf6? (Black should reconcile himself to the loss of a Pawn after 14...Bxf6 15 Rxd6 Bc8) 15 Nd5!! Rfe8 (if 15...exd5 16 Rxd5 Qa6 17 h5! wins – 17...Bg4 18 g3, etc.) 16 Nxe7+ (16 h6! is a quicker kill) 16...Rxe7 17 Rxd6 c8 18 Qd4 Be8? 19 Qxf6 resigns. }
13. f4 Rad8
{ If 13...e5 14 Be3! Bxe4 (not 14...Nxe4? 15 Nxe4 Bxe4 16 Bd2 wins) 15 Nxe4 Nxe4 16 Qf3 with advantage. }
14. Rhf1
{ I already had in mind the ensuing sacrifice. Also strong is 14 g4 – not to mention Trifunovich's post-mortem suggestion 14 f5! exf5 (not 14...e5 15 Bf2 d5? 16 exd5 Nxd5 17 Nxd5 Bxd5 18 Qxe5 wins a piece) 15 exf5 Rfe8 16 Qf2 with positional pressure. }
14... b5 { On 14...d5 15 e5 Ne4 16 f5! maintains the initiative. } 15. f5 $3
{ The die is cast. I didn't want to lose a tempo playing it safe with 15 a3. }
15... b4 16. fxe6 $1 bxc3 17. exf7+ Kh8
{ Not 17...Rxf7 18 Bxf7+ Kxf7 19 Qc4+ d5 20 Qxc6, etc. } 18. Rf5 $1 Qb4
{ On 18...c7 I had intended 19 df1 (threatening Rxf6). If then 19...Nd7 (or on 19...Nxe4 20 Qg4 is bitter) 20 Rh5! (threatening Rxh7+) is decisive. }
19. Qf1 $1
{ A hard move to find – it took around 45 minutes. The threat of xf6 must be attended to. }
19... Nxe4
{ A fighting defense. 19...Nd7 loses immediately to 20 h5 Ne5 21 Qf5 h6 22 g6!! xf7 (22...Nxg6 allows 23 xh6 mate) 23 xe5, etc. On 19... xf7 20 Bxc3 wins. And 19...Bxe4 gives White the pleasant choice of 20 xf6 or 20 b5.
Objectively best is 19...g4. But after 20 Bxc3 (20 Rh5 Bd7! holds) 20...b7 (if 20...Qxe4 21 d4!) 21 f4 with three Pawns for the piece and a winning attack in the offing. }
20. a3 $2
{ Losing! A couple of hours after the game it occurred to me that White has a problem-like win after 20 Wf4!! (with the threat of h5). Black has no adequate defense.
A] 20...d5 21 e5 Nf6 22 xf6 xf6 23 xf6! B] 20...Nd2+ 21 Rxd2 cxd2 22 c3!! xb3 23 Bxg7+! Kxg7 24 g4+ Kh8 25Wd4+ and mates.
C] 20...cxb2 21 h5! (threatening Bxg7+) 21...Nc3+ (if 21...Bf6 22 f5 h6 23 xh6+! gxh6 24 g6!! forces mate) 22 xb2 Nxd1+ (or 22...Rxf7 23 xf7 Nxd1+ 24 Kb1!!xd4 25 xh7+!! Kxh7 26 Qh5 mate) 23 c1 Rxf7 (forced) 24 xf7! (24 xf7?? g5+) and Black has no satisfactory answer to the threat of 25 xh7+ Kxh7 26 Wf5+ and mates. If 24...Bd7 25 Bxg7+ wins Black's Queen. }
20... Qb7 21. Qf4 Ba4 $3
{ I didn't see it! Moreover, the strength of this resource didn't become fully apparent to me for another two moves. }
22. Qg4 { Also futile is 22 h6 Bf6 23 Rxf6xb3. } 22... Bf6 $1 23. Rxf6 Bxb3 $1
{ At long last I saw the point of Geller's clever defense. As I was studying 24 Rf4, it suddenly dawned on me that 24...Ba2+ was curtains. So –
After 24 cxb3 Nxf6! is the quietus. It is not enough to be a good player, observed Dr Tarrasch; you must also play well. }
0-1

[Event "Skopje 1967"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1967.??.??"]
[Round "59"]
[White "Kholmov"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[Title "The erring Bishop"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "367"]

{ In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3, then penetrates ambitiously with 11 Bd6. Fischer, with the routine 1...a5, promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how, from now on, he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions, prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly, whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However, Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves.
Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter, played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here, Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 6. Nc3
{ A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge. }
6... Nbd7
{ More flexible than 6...d5 which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued 7 Ne5 c6 8 e4 and now Black could have equalized easily with 8...dxe4 (instead of 8...Be6?) 9 Nxe4 Nxe4 10 Bxe4 Bh3 (not 10...c5? 11 f3!) 11 Rel Nd7, etc. }
7. b3
{ A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This system is characterized by Pawn symmetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter.
7 e4 e5 also presents Black with no opening problems. }
7... e5 8. dxe5
{ Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after 8 e4 exd4 9 Nxd4 Re8. }
8... dxe5 9. e4
{ "The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even. 99 (TRIFUNOVICH). }
9... Re8 10. Ba3
{ Better is 10 a4 a5 11 Ba3; whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ..Qa5.
"Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto. 10 Bb2 is correct." 99 (TRIFUNOVICH). }
10... c6
{ Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position."(TRIFUNOVICH). } 11. Bd6
$2
{ Kholmov consumed over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for youropponent to get an idea – it's sure to be wrong!" 11 Qe2 was indicated. }
11... Qa5 $1 { This normalfreeing maneuver is now devastating. } 12. Qd3
{ What else? 12 b4 Qa3 leaves White in the same predicament.
Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal). }
12... Re6 $1
{ Springing the trap! White cannot avoid material loss. Geller, who was tied with me for the lead, had displayed great interest in my game – up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again. }
13. b4
{ No matter how White wriggles and squirms, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After 13 Ne2 Black has the pleasant choice of either ...Nxe4 or ...Ne8 – both of which win a Pawn.
After the game Kholmov told me he had originally intended 13 Ng5 Rxd614 Qxd6 Qxc3 15 Qe7 with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14...h6! squelches all such illusions. }
13... Qa3 $1 { Renewing the ancient threat of ...Rxd6. } 14. Bc7
{ The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn. }
14... Qxb4
{ Perhaps White was hoping for 14...Ne815 Ba5b616 Rab1! bxa5 17 Rb3 Nc5! 18 bxc5 Qxc5 19 Rfb1 where Black's technica1 difficulties are great. }
15. Rab1 Qe7 $1
{ A cute tactical point. 15...Qf8? loses the exchange after 16 Ng5 (if the Rook retreats, thenWhite's Bishop returns to d6). Now 16 Ng5 is refuted by 16...Nc5. For all practical purposes the game is over. }
16. Rfd1 Ne8 17. Ba5 { No rest for the weary. } 17... Rd6 18. Qe2 Rxd1+ 19.
Qxd1 Bf8 20. Nd2
{ Bad – as is everything else. 20 Bb4 Qf6 21 Bxf8 Nxf8 leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing. }
20... Qa3 $1 { Winning even more material. } 21. Nc4 Qc5 22. Bf1 b5 23. Nd2
{ 23 Bb4 drops a piece to 23...Qd4. } 23... Qa3 $1
{ This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing. } 24. Nb3 Nc5 25. Bxb5
{ Desperation. After 25 Bd8 Ne6! 26 Ba5 Nd6, Black wins as he pleases. } 25...
cxb5 26. Nxb5 Qa4 27. Nxc5
{ Perhaps White had intended 27 Qd5, but27...Qxe4! spells finis. } 27... Qxa5
28. Qd5 Rb8 29. a4 Bh3 $1 { Quickest. } 30. Qxe5 Rc8 31. Nd3 Qxa4 32. Ne1 a6
{ The Knight has no good square. If 33 Nd4 Bg7. Or 33 Nc3 Qc4. Finally, 33 Na7 Rc7 34 Ra1 Qd7 35 Rxa6 Rxa7 36 Rxa7 Qxa7 37 Qxe8 Qa1 delivers the mate.
Afterwards, Geller tried to offer my opponent some sympathy. I overheard a dismayed Kholmov telling him that I had ""seen everything!" This game was particularly sweet because it was my first win against a Russian in almost a dozen tries (since game 52) – and my first with Black since 1962 (Korchnoi at Curaçao). }
0-1

[Event "Sousse 1967"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1967.??.??"]
[Round "60"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Stein"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez"]
[Title "When champions meet"]
[Annotator "Fischer"]
[BookPage "372"]

{ On his ninth turn Black varies the routine sparring but the game proceeds innocuously until Fischer veers with 14 b4, intensifying the struggle. If, in the ensuing slugfest, Stein can be said to have made an error, it is the strategic one of so pressing on the Q-side as to allow White to become entrenched on the opposite wing. Fischer's prosecution of the attack is crowned by a brilliant offer of a piece (29 Bxf5) which the Soviet champion declines. Had Fischer then renewed the sacrifice, the end would have come sooner. In his detailed notes Fischer refers to this oversight, reveals some important thoughts on the Ruy, pinpoints "the losing move' (21...Nb6), criticizes a second subtle mistake of his own (26 Nf3), and offers a possible defense for Stein (28...Bf6), which other commentators have failed to note.
It is unfortunate that this interesting and most instructive game was expunged from the official records due to Fischer's withdrawa1 before having completed half his playing schedule. }
1. e4 { I have never opened with the d-pawn – on principle. } 1... e5
{ I had expected the Sicilian, with Stein's favorite accelerated Dragon (2...g6). I suspect that the Russians "group-think" before important games to decide which openings will upset their opponents psychologically. }
2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 { Possibly Stein was braced for 4 Bxc6, as in game 56. }
4. Ba4 { Relieving the suspense. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6
{ In the event the reader is interested in what I may have had in mind against the Marshall Attack, he is referred to my game against Spassky at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966, which continued: 7...0-0 8 c3 d5 9 exd5 Nxd5 10 Nxe5 Nxe5 11 Rxe5 c6 12 g3!? Bd6 13 Re1 Nf6 14 d4 g4 15 Qd3 (15 f3 might be better) 15...c5 and now 16 Bc2! (instead of 16 dxc5?) allows Black insufficient compensation for his Pawn. }
8. c3 O-O 9. h3 { For 9 d4 see game 36. } 9... Bb7
{ A rare side line. 9...Na5 10 Bc2 c5 is a better-known sequence. The text is somewhat passive and commits the Bishop perhaps prematurely. Usually Stein continues 9...Nd7 10 d4 Bf6; then 11 a4 is slightly better for White.
The line chosen in the game comes to resemble Breyer's Defense (9...Nb8 10 d4 Nbd7 11 Nbd2 Bb7 12 Bc2! e8 13 b4 exd4 14 cxd4 a5 15 bxa5 c5) – see note to White's 17th move. }
10. d4 Na5
{ Believe it or not, this Knight is headed for d7! Black may prefer the shorter route with 10...Nb8; but experience has shown that after 11 dxe5 dxe5 12 xd8, Black is obliged to recapture with his Bishop, which interferes with his development and produces endgame difficulties. }
11. Bc2 Nc4
{ Unsound is 11...exd4 12 cxd4 d5 13 e5 e4 14 Nc3 f5 15 exf6 e.p. Bxf6 16 Nxe4 dxe4 17 Bxe4 Bxe4 18 Rxe4 c5 19 d5 and Black remains a Pawn down.
Another possibility is /...exd4 12 cxd4 c5 but White holds the edge with 13 Nbd2. On 11...c5 immediately, White replies 12 Nbd2, retaining the option of d5, locking in Black's Bishop on b7. }
12. b3 Nb6 13. Nbd2 { Not 13 dxe5 dxe5 14 xd8 axd8 15 Nxe5xe4!=. } 13... Nbd7
{ Black's 5th move with this Knight! 13...exd4 14 cxd4 c5 seems more active.
Stein-Lutikov, Moscow 1966 continued 13...Re8? 14 f1? and Black equalized easily. However, White should vary with 14 dxe5 dxe5 15 Nxe5 Bd6 16 ef3 Bxe4 (16...Nxe4 17 Nxe4 Bxe4 18 Bg5! busts Black) 17 Nxe4 Nxe4 18 d3! (White can't win a piece because his Queen hangs at the end after .. Bh2+), with initiative. }
14. b4 $1
{ Prevents ...c5 and prepares a dominating build-up with 15 Bb2 followed by c4. The routine continuation 14 Bb2 (Keres-Gligorich. Zurich 1959) gives nothing. }
14... exd4
{ Stein makes his bid for active counterplay even though it involves abandoning his "strong point" (e5-Pawn). If 14...a5 15 Nb3! and Black might find himself in straightened circumstances after the Knight reaches a5. }
15. cxd4 a5
{ On 15...c5 16 bxc5 dxc5 17 d5 White's steamroller in the center is more formidable thanBlack's Q-side majority. }
16. bxa5 c5
{ Inferior would be 16...Rxa5 17 d5! c5 18 dxc6e.p. Bxc619 Nd4, after which White, among other things, goes to work on the isolated b-Pawn. }
17. e5 $1
{ This identical position was reached by transposition, with Black's Rook on e8, in Cirich-Robatsch, Beverwijk 1967, which continued: 17 Bb2 Qxa518 a4 b4 19 Nc4 Qc7 20 e5 dxe5 21 dxe5 Nd5 22 Nfd2 N7b6 and now Spassky's recommendation of 23 e6! is unpleasant for Black.(See note to Black's9th move.) }
17... dxe5
{ Another line of defense is 17...Ne8 with the idea of eliminating both of White's center Pawns. "The consequences are very ramified, and there is some danger that Black may fail to recover White's Pawn on its a5or may lose his own on b5, or both. The text is more active but also more dangerous for Black's King."(κMoCH). }
18. dxe5 Nd5 19. Ne4 Nb4 $1
{ The idea is to force the Bishop to retreat and thereby hem in White's QR. On 19...Rxa5 20 Neg5! h6 21 Qd3!g6 22 Ne6! wins. }
20. Bb1 Rxa5 21. Qe2 $1
{ Increasing the pressure. Not 21 e6 fxe6 22 Neg5?(or 22 Nfg5 Bd5 23 Nxh7 Rf5! holds) 22...Bxf3! 23 Nxf3 Bf6 wins.
One can sense the storm looming against Black's King. }
21... Nb6 $2
{ Quite possibly "the losing move." It is better to reserve this Knight for the defense of the K-side.More prudent is 21...Re8! with ...Nf8 in the offing. 22 Rd1 Qc7 leads to nought. And 22 e6 leads to no demonstrable advantage after 22...fxe6 23 Neg5 Bxg5 24 Nxg5 Nf8 25 Qh5 g6, etc. }
22. Nfg5 $1 { Now the threats are beginning to jell. } 22... Bxe4 $1
{ Forced, because if 22...h6 23 Nh7!! stands Black up.On 23...Re8 (23...Kxh7 24 Nxc5 dis.+ followed by Nxb7 leads to a small fork) 24 Nhf6+! Bxf6 (24...gxf6 25 Qg4+ Kh8 26 Nd6! Bxd6 27 Qf5! Kg7 28 Bxh6+ leads to mate) 25 Nxf6+ Qxf6 (again if 25...gxf6 26 Qg4+ Kf8 27 Bxh6+ Ke7 28 e6! Kd629 Qg3+ Kc6 30 Be4+ N4d5 31 exf7 Rh8 32 Bxd5+ wins) 26 exf6 wins the exchange.
Also insufficient is 22...g623 e6! f5 24 Nf7! followed byBb2 with a crushing attack. }
23. Qxe4 g6 24. Qh4 h5 25. Qg3 $1
{ Now White threatens 26 Ne6! Bh4! 27 Nxd8 Bxg3 28 Nb7 Ra7 29 Nxc5. After Black's next move, this variation fails against 29...Bxe5.
Impetuous would be 25 g4?? Qd4. }
25... Nc4 $1 26. Nf3 $2
{ More forcing is 26 e6!f5 27 Nf3 (not 27 Nf7 Rxf7! 28 exf7+ Kxf7 29 Bxf5! gxf5 30 Qf3 κg6 31 g4 Qd5 and a draw appears likely) 27...Kg7 28 Qf4 Rh8 transposing into the game (but not 27...Rf6 28 Bg5 Kh7 29 Bxf6 Bxf630 Bxf5! gxf5 31 Rad1 Nd5 32 e7! Bxe7 33 Rxd5 is decisive.). This order of moves would prohibit the defense mentioned in the note to Black's 28th; after being forced to play 26...f5, Blackloses his options.
At this stage the power failed. In the dark I began to worry about 26...Nd3! (if 27 Rd1 Nxc1! and White has nothing). Then the lights came on again and I saw clearly that 26...Nd3? was crushed by 27 Bxd3! Qxd3 28 Bg5! and White penetrates decisively on the weak dark squares. }
26... Kg7 27. Qf4 Rh8 28. e6 $1
{ "This blow rocks the remnants of the tower around the Black King. 99 (GLIGORICH). }
28... f5
{ Much stiffer resistance (taking advantage of White's inaccurate 26th move) is offered by 28...Bf6! (not 28...f6 29 Nh4) 29 exf7 Bxa1 (29...Qd6? 30 f8=Q+! Kxf831 Qe4 Qd7 32 Bg5! Ra6 33 Bxf6 Rxf6 34 Ne5! winning a Pawn and eventually the game) 30 f8=Q+! Qxf8 31 Qc7+ Kg8 32 Bxg6 Nd5 (if 32...Ra6 33 Re8!) 33 Qb7 Nf6 34 Bf4 (threatening 35 Ng5 and Bf7+) 34...Rh7! White now appears to have nothing better than 35 Bxh7+ Nxh7 36 Qd5+ Qf7(worse is 36...Kh8? 37 Qxh5!) 37 Qxf7+ Kxf738 Rxa1 with chances by virtue of the extra Pawn. But this would be a tough ending to win! }
29. Bxf5 $1 Qf8
{ The only reasonable way to decline the sacrifice. On 29...Bd6 30 e7! Bxf4 (or 30...Bxe7 31 Qg3 Ra6 32 Ng5, etc.) 31 exd8=Q Rxd8 32 Bxf4 gxf5 33 Bc7!(R.Byrne).
Kmoch suggests that "Leonidas might even have better taken a chance and faced the storm by playing 29...gxf5."But the Bishop is tabu, for White wins quickly with 30 Qg3+. Black now has two defenses which fail:
A] 30...Kf8 31 Qg6 Qe8 (if 31...Nd6 32 Ne5!) 32 Bh6+ Rxh6 33 Qxh6+ Kg8 34 Ng5.
B] 30...Kh7 31 Ng5+! Bxg5 32 Bxg5 Qd3 (if 32...Qb8 33 Qh4! Kg6 34 Bf6–or 32...Qe8 33 Rad1 Ra7 34 Rd8! Qxd8 35 Bxd8 Rxd8 36 e7 Re837 Re6! Rexe7 38 Qg6+ Kh8 39 Qf6+ Rg7 40 Qh6+ and mates) 33 Qc7+ Kg6 34 Qf7+! Kxg5 35 Qg7+ Kf4 36 Rad1!, etc. }
30. Be4 $2
{ Littlewood indicates 30 Nh4! as a quick win for White. He's right. The main line is 30...Bxh431 Qxh4 Qxf5(if31...Qf6 32 Qg3! – or 31...gxf5 32 Qg5+ Kh7 33 e7 Qe8 34 Re6!) 32 Qe7+ Kg8 33 Qd8+ Kg7 34 Qc7+ Kg8 35 e7, etc. }
30... Qxf4 31. Bxf4 Re8 $2
{ Stein's post-mortem suggestion of 31...Ra6 is met by 32 Rad1 Rxe6 33 Rd7(threateningNg5), etc.
But the best try is 31...Rxa2! On 32 Rad1 Ra7 holds. And if 32 Rxa2 Nxa2 33 Ne5 g5 34 Bg3 maintains the initiative, but Black has drawing chances.
Black, however, was in extreme time-pressure. }
32. Rad1 Ra6 33. Rd7 { Even more convincing is 33 Bb7! Ra7 34 Rd7. } 33... Rxe6
34. Ng5 Rf6
{ Costs the exchange. But no better is 34...Ra6 35 Bb1 Kf6 36 Ne4+ Kf7 37 Nxc5, etc. }
35. Bf3 $1 Rxf4 { Of course 35...Kf8 is refuted by 36 Nh7+. } 36. Ne6+ Kf6 37.
Nxf4 Ne5 38. Rb7 Bd6 39. Kf1
{ Puts an end to all effective resistance. White's material superiority must tell. }
39... Nc2
{ A pretty point is revealed after 39...Nxf3 40 Rxe8 Nd2+ 41 Ke2 Bxf4 42 Rf8+ Kg5 43 Rxf4 Kxf4 44 Kxd2, resigns. }
40. Re4 Nd4 41. Rb6 Rd8 42. Nd5+ Kf5 43. Ne3+
{ The sealed move seals Black's fate. Not only is White an exchange ahead, but his attack still rages. }
43... Ke6 { Equally hopeless is 43...Kf6 44 Be2 b4 45 f4 followed by Bc4. } 44.
Be2 $1
{ "The double threat of 45 f4 and 45 Bxb5 clears the last stone from the road to victory." (κmoCí). }
44... Kd7 45. Bxb5+ Nxb5 46. Rxb5 Kc6 47. a4 Bc7 48. Ke2 g5 49. g3 Ra8 50. Rb2
Rf8 51. f4 { The beginning of the end. } 51... gxf4 52. gxf4 Nf7 53. Re6+ Nd6
{ If 53...Bd6 54 Rf6! is powerful. } 54. f5 Ra8 55. Rd2 $1 Rxa4 56. f6
{ On 56...Rf4 57 Nd5 wins the house. A stubborn fight!
